10.31820/pt.28.3.7,5435dc83d0805ac2,1,10.1111/j.2044-8309.1990.tb00892.x,"Knowing what to think by knowing who you are: Self-categorisation and the nature of norm formation, conformity and group polarisation","Abrams, D., Wetherell, M., Cochrane, S., Hogg, M. A., & Turner, J. C.",1990,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": ""10.1111/j.2044-8309.1990.tb00892.x"", ""title"": ""Knowing what to think by knowing who you are: Self-categorisation and the nature of norm formation, conformity and group polarisation"", ""authors"": ""Abrams, D., Wetherell, M., Cochrane, S., Hogg, M. A., & Turner, J. C."", ""year"": ""1990"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""successful""}, ""quote"": ""Consistent with the assumption of the SCT (Turner, 1991;Turner et al., 1987), participants exposed to the in-group majority showed the increase in conformity in comparison to the standard condition in which group identity was not salient. On the opposite, when the majority was presented as the out-group, the conformity effect significantly dropped. Thus, we replicated and extended past research (Abrahams et al., 1990), showing that self-categorization could play a determining role in conformity even in more minimal conditions, in which salient in-and out-group characteristics (i.e., ethnicity) were completely irrelevant for the task at hand."", ""location"": ""Discussion, line 22"", ""reason"": ""In addition to replicating the classic Asch conformity effect, the study explicitly adapts the design of Abrams et al. (1990) to re-test their finding that self-categorization (in-group vs. out-group majority membership) moderates conformity in the Asch paradigm without confounding similarity and competence (lines 6-7), and explicitly concludes that it replicated and extended Abrams et al. (1990) (line 22). This is an omitted target in the shipped dataset.""}","{""authors"": ""Abrams, D., Wetherell, M., Cochrane, S., Hogg, M. A., & Turner, J. C."", ""year"": ""1990"", ""title"": ""Knowing what to think by knowing who you are: Self-categorisation and the nature of norm formation, conformity and group polarisation"", ""doi"": ""10.1111/j.2044-8309.1990.tb00892.x"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Thus, we replicated and extended past research (Abrahams et al., 1990), showing that self-categorization could play a determining role in conformity even in more minimal conditions, in which salient in-and out-group characteristics (i.e., ethnicity) were completely irrelevant for the task at hand."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Thus, we replicated and extended past research (Abrahams et al., 1990), showing that self-categorization could play a determining role in conformity even in more minimal conditions, in which salient in-and out-group characteristics (i.e., ethnicity) were completely irrelevant for the task at hand."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true}"
10.1080/10409289.2018.1475202,d454c55ff4e3548b,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Three-level, linear growth models (time-student-school) were used to estimate treatment effects, found for print awareness, elision, rhyming, and the screener (print and letter knowledge, phonological awareness), replicating previous findings for the screener, rhyming, and print knowledge, and extending them to elision."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Three-level, linear growth models (time-student-school) were used to estimate treatment effects, found for print awareness, elision, rhyming, and the screener (print and letter knowledge, phonological awareness), replicating previous findings for the screener, rhyming, and print knowledge, and extending them to elision."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract explicitly states agreement with previous findings on the three previously studied outcomes. Elision extends rather than contradicts those findings. Target identity and number cannot be resolved from this abstract.""}"
10.1037/fam0000034,61bcdf15a8843c7f,1,10.3389/fnbeh.2014.00021,Genetic imaging of the association of oxytocin receptor gene (OXTR) polymorphisms with positive maternal parenting,Michalska KJ; Decety J; Liu C; Chen Q; Martz ME; Jacob S; Lahey BB,2014,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Michalska KJ; Decety J; Liu C; Chen Q; Martz ME; Jacob S; Lahey BB"", ""doi"": ""10.3389/fnbeh.2014.00021"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Genetic imaging of the association of oxytocin receptor gene (OXTR) polymorphisms with positive maternal parenting"", ""year"": ""2014""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""61bcdf15a8843c7f"", ""location"": ""Discussion, lines 309-318 (see also Abstract, lines 13-17; Introduction, lines 93-108; Works Cited, lines 517-518)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""The current study investigated the association between child and parent OXTR genotype ( OXTR rs53576) and observed\nparental warmth, control, and negativity in both mothers and fathers in a\npopulation-based sample of 500 families with twin children between the ages of 6 and\n10, in an attempt to replicate and extend prior research ( Bakermans-Kranenburg & van IJzendoorn, 2008 ; Michalska et al., 2014 ). Although only\npreliminary given the sample size, our results revealed a significant association\nbetween maternal OXTR rs53576 genotype and warmth, even when\ncontrolling for the effects of child age, gender, and ethnicity, as well as\nchild-driven evocative effects. This finding not only serves as a constructive\nreplication of previous research examining the relationship between variants within OXTR and parenting ( Bakermans-Kranenburg & van IJzendoorn, 2008 ; Feldman et al., 2012 ; Michalska et al., 2014 ), but also adds to the growing body of research\nsupporting a link between oxytocin functioning and parenting ( Feldman et al., 2012 ; Weisman, Zagoory-Sharon, & Feldman, 2012 )."", ""reason"": ""The study empirically tested the association between OXTR rs53576 genotype and observed parenting in 500 twin families and explicitly states in the Abstract, Introduction, and Discussion that it was conducted to constructively replicate and extend Michalska et al. (2014), which examined OXTR rs53576 and observed positive maternal parenting in 40 mothers. The Works Cited section confirms the exact identity: Michalska KJ, Decety J, Liu C, Chen Q, Martz ME, Jacob S, Lahey BB (2014), 'Genetic imaging of the association of oxytocin receptor gene (OXTR) polymorphisms with positive maternal parenting', Frontiers in Behavioral Neuroscience 8:21, DOI 10.3389/fnbeh.2014.00021. It is completely distinct from shipped row 9 (Bakermans-Kranenburg & van IJzendoorn, 2008; 10.1093/scan/nsn004) and sibling target 2 (Feldman et al., 2012; 10.1016/j.biopsych.2011.12.025). Prior adjudication and state files were checked and are legitimately absent."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0047_61bcdf15a8843c7f"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Michalska KJ; Decety J; Liu C; Chen Q; Martz ME; Jacob S; Lahey BB"", ""year"": ""2014"", ""title"": ""Genetic imaging of the association of oxytocin receptor gene (OXTR) polymorphisms with positive maternal parenting"", ""doi"": ""10.3389/fnbeh.2014.00021"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""This finding not only serves as a constructive\nreplication of previous research examining the relationship between variants within OXTR and parenting ( Bakermans-Kranenburg & van IJzendoorn, 2008 ; Feldman et al., 2012 ; Michalska et al., 2014 ), but also adds to the growing body of research\nsupporting a link between oxytocin functioning and parenting ( Feldman et al., 2012 ; Weisman, Zagoory-Sharon, & Feldman, 2012 )."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""This finding not only serves as a constructive\nreplication of previous research examining the relationship between variants within OXTR and parenting ( Bakermans-Kranenburg & van IJzendoorn, 2008 ; Feldman et al., 2012 ; Michalska et al., 2014 ), but also adds to the growing body of research\nsupporting a link between oxytocin functioning and parenting ( Feldman et al., 2012 ; Weisman, Zagoory-Sharon, & Feldman, 2012 )."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly characterize the finding as a constructive replication of the cited set. Their overall verdict controls despite the opposite allele direction described for Michalska et al.""}"
