10.1007/s12186-012-9090-0,6779252b07407f5b,0,,Modelling learning from errors in daily work.,"Bauer, J., & Mulder, R. H.",2007,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Bauer, J., & Mulder, R. H."", ""year"": ""2007"", ""title"": ""Modelling learning from errors in daily work."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The goal of our study was to investigate whether exploratory findings from an earlier study on hospital nurses’ ESLA (Bauer and Mulder Learning in Health and Social Care 6:121–133, 2011) replicate and generalise to the domain of elder care nursing."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""These results entirely cross-validate the exploratory findings of Bauer and Mulder (Learning in Health and Social Care 6:121–133, 2011) on hospital nurses’ ESLA and show that they generalise to the domain of elder care nursing."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly say the results entirely cross-validate the earlier exploratory findings.""}"
10.1002/bin.70067,047db07d0bc22a78,0,,,Becraft et al.,,abstract,,correct,argon,"{""corrected_value"": null, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""047db07d0bc22a78"", ""location"": ""Abstract (line 5)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""A recent preliminary study suggested that some caregivers' data may correspond well with trained observers and that caregivers can detect large changes in challenging behavior. In the current investigation, we replicated and extended Becraft et al. with 8 caregivers of children receiving treatment for pediatric feeding disorder."", ""reason"": ""The primary source abstract (047db07d0bc22a78.txt) explicitly states that a 'recent preliminary study suggested that some caregivers' data may correspond well with trained observers and that caregivers can detect large changes in challenging behavior' and that '[i]n the current investigation, we replicated and extended Becraft et al. with 8 caregivers of children receiving treatment for pediatric feeding disorder.' No prior adjudication or state file exists for this work. Because the primary source text contains only the title, author list, DOI, and abstract (without the bibliography), the blind coder recorded authors as 'Becraft et al.' and left year, title, and DOI blank. Shipped row input_row_index 13 is Becraft, Cataldo, Yu-Lefler, Schenk, Edelstein, and Kurtz (2023), 'Correspondence between data provided by parents and trained observers about challenging behavior.' (10.1037/bar0000276, verified in bibliography_metadata.json). Both the explicit citation to 'Becraft et al.' (with Jessica L. Becraft also serving as senior author of the replicating study) and the exact substantive description of the original study's findings (correspondence between parent/caregiver data and trained observers and detection of changes in challenging behavior) establish that target_index 0 is already represented by shipped row input_row_index 13."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0002_047db07d0bc22a78"", ""target_index"": 0, ""verdict"": ""shipped_right""}","{""authors"": ""Becraft et al."", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In the current investigation, we replicated and extended Becraft et al. with 8 caregivers of children receiving treatment for pediatric feeding disorder."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""A recent preliminary study suggested that some caregivers' data may correspond well with trained observers and that caregivers can detect large changes in challenging behavior. In the current investigation, we replicated and extended Becraft et al. with 8 caregivers of children receiving treatment for pediatric feeding disorder. Overall, caregiver data were significantly correlated with observer data for inappropriate and appropriate behavior for seven out of eight caregivers."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Implicit comparison: the abstract states the earlier finding of correspondence and detection of changes and reports the corresponding agreement in the new sample. The final abstract conclusion also supports caregiver monitoring. Variation among caregivers is not itself a mixed replication verdict.""}"
10.3102/0013189x20940304,15b01ee69a8e149c,0,,Equality of educational opportunity study.,"Coleman, J. S., Campbell, E., Hobson, C., McPartland, F., Mood, A., & Weinfeld, F.",1966,full_text,,error,obvious,"{""target_index"": 0, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""obvious"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Coleman, J. S., Campbell, E., Hobson, C., McPartland, F., Mood, A., & Weinfeld, F."", ""year"": ""1966"", ""title"": ""Equality of educational opportunity study."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined""}, ""quote"": ""Our results replicate both the original Coleman Report\nICCs, as well as those from Bryk and Raudenbush’s study."", ""location"": {""start_line"": 140, ""end_line"": 141, ""form_feed_page"": 5}, ""reason"": ""The results explicitly replicate Coleman Report ICCs and name the distinct 1966 report in the references. The sole shipped original is Bryk and Raudenbush (1988). This is a direct empirical retest, so Luna's background label is contradicted by the source. Parent reviewed the cited results/purpose and reference identity against all supplied shipped originals. This decision confirms the missing paper-level pair only; its overall outcome has not been adjudicated."", ""quote_provenance"": {""kind"": ""new_independent_literal_primary_quotation"", ""inspection_result"": ""evidence_followup/integration/strict_literal_quote_inspection.result.json"", ""issue_index"": 156, ""source_path"": ""/home/lukas/.t3/worktrees/fred-data/audit-flora-blind-2026-10/audit/flora_blind_2026-10/texts/15b01ee69a8e149c.txt"", ""source_sha256"": ""8458e7e97b441cfae698f46ff12f63668210533566b13187cb1734be0becdcfc"", ""raw_start_offset"": 10795, ""raw_end_offset"": 10906, ""scientific_verdict_unchanged"": true, ""reason"": ""Parent verified exact raw slice and semantic identity with the earlier accepted passage under its recorded display-normalisation policy. Independent primary-context inspector supported this passage. Original provider wording and whole prior decision retained in history; raw provider reply unchanged.""}}","{""authors"": ""Coleman, J. S., Campbell, E., Hobson, C., McPartland, F., Mood, A., & Weinfeld, F."", ""year"": ""1966"", ""title"": ""Equality of educational opportunity study."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Our results replicate both the original Coleman Report ICCs, as well as those from Bryk and Raudenbush’s study."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Our results replicate both the original Coleman Report ICCs, as well as those from Bryk and Raudenbush’s study. In the left column of Table 1 (variation in achievement levels), our results are largely consistent with the Coleman Report: Only 23.7% of the variance in math achievement levels lies between schools (21.4% in ELA)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Explicit overall replication verdict covers both target studies, supported by target-specific comparisons. Scaling sensitivity and cautious interpretation do not negate the stated successful replication.""}"
10.1007/s10864-018-9302-2,f61c7138c56eec1c,0,10.1007/BF03395755,A relational frame training intervention to raise Intelligence Quotients: A pilot study.,"Cassidy, S., Roche, B. & Hayes, S. C.",2011,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Cassidy, S., Roche, B. & Hayes, S. C."", ""year"": ""2011"", ""title"": ""A relational frame training intervention to raise Intelligence Quotients: A pilot study."", ""doi"": ""10.1007/BF03395755"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Previous analyses have reported increases in verbal intelligence following relational skills training interventions, a finding which is replicated in the current analysis (Cassidy et al., 2011; 2016; Hayes & Stewart, 2016)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Previous analyses have reported increases in verbal intelligence following relational skills training interventions, a finding which is replicated in the current analysis (Cassidy et al., 2011; 2016; Hayes & Stewart, 2016)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The discussion explicitly names this paper among findings replicated in the current analysis.""}"
10.1007/s10864-018-9302-2,f61c7138c56eec1c,2,10.1111/bjep.12114,Comparing the effects of derived relational training and computer coding on intellectual potential in school-age children.,"Hayes, J. & Stewart, I.",2016,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Hayes, J. & Stewart, I."", ""year"": ""2016"", ""title"": ""Comparing the effects of derived relational training and computer coding on intellectual potential in school-age children."", ""doi"": ""10.1111/bjep.12114"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Previous analyses have reported increases in verbal intelligence following relational skills training interventions, a finding which is replicated in the current analysis (Cassidy et al., 2011; 2016; Hayes & Stewart, 2016)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Previous analyses have reported increases in verbal intelligence following relational skills training interventions, a finding which is replicated in the current analysis (Cassidy et al., 2011; 2016; Hayes & Stewart, 2016)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Named in the explicit verbal-intelligence replication comparison.""}"
10.1007/s10864-018-9302-2,f61c7138c56eec1c,3,,Assessing the Effects of a Relational Training Intervention on Fluid Intelligence Among a Sample of Socially Disadvantaged Children in Bangladesh.,"Amd, M. & Roche, B.",in press,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Amd, M. & Roche, B."", ""year"": ""in press"", ""title"": ""Assessing the Effects of a Relational Training Intervention on Fluid Intelligence Among a Sample of Socially Disadvantaged Children in Bangladesh."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The current study is the first to implement blind testing in the study of relational skills training programs in a randomized controlled trial of the SMART method. Thus, it does not aim to replicate previous studies precisely but aims to interrogate the reported effects using more stringent methodologies. Indeed, this is the optimal way in which to test the theoretical hypotheses underlying an intervention rather than the methodologies per se (see Crandall & Sherman, 2016 for a more complete discussion of the relative merits of direct and conceptual replication). In this study, the relational skills training intervention used by Amd and Roche (in press), Cassidy et al. (2016), Hayes and Stewart (2016), and Thirus et al. (2016) was administered to a group of 15 to 17-year-old children over a period of three months using a single-blind randomized controlled design. Scores on a standardized assessment of intelligence (Wechsler Abbreviated Scale of Intelligence, WASi, Wechsler, 1999) were administered to all participants before and after completing the training program, in order to assess its impact on intellectual performance. The training program was administered entirely by independent parties (school teachers) and the researchers had no role in the administration of the training program or in participant assignment."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""However, a post-hoc analysis found that the collective Full Scale IQ gain found for participants who failed to complete the training program was 18.2 points, an average increase only marginally lower than that found for those who completed all 55 levels (M = 19.1 points). As such, the number of training levels completed was not a predictor of subsequent IQ gains (as confirmed by correlational analyses), which is inconsistent with the “dosage effects” reported in the Amd & Roche (in press) study."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The current study supports the same IQ-improvement finding but explicitly contrasts its absence of a dosage effect with this original.""}"
10.1007/s10864-018-9302-2,f61c7138c56eec1c,4,,"Relational frame theory, mathematical and logical skills: A multiple exemplar training intervention to enhance intellectual performance.","Thirus, J., Starbrink, M. & Jansson, B.",2016,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Thirus, J., Starbrink, M. & Jansson, B."", ""year"": ""2016"", ""title"": ""Relational frame theory, mathematical and logical skills: A multiple exemplar training intervention to enhance intellectual performance."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The current study is the first to implement blind testing in the study of relational skills training programs in a randomized controlled trial of the SMART method. Thus, it does not aim to replicate previous studies precisely but aims to interrogate the reported effects using more stringent methodologies. Indeed, this is the optimal way in which to test the theoretical hypotheses underlying an intervention rather than the methodologies per se (see Crandall & Sherman, 2016 for a more complete discussion of the relative merits of direct and conceptual replication). In this study, the relational skills training intervention used by Amd and Roche (in press), Cassidy et al. (2016), Hayes and Stewart (2016), and Thirus et al. (2016) was administered to a group of 15 to 17-year-old children over a period of three months using a single-blind randomized controlled design. Scores on a standardized assessment of intelligence (Wechsler Abbreviated Scale of Intelligence, WASi, Wechsler, 1999) were administered to all participants before and after completing the training program, in order to assess its impact on intellectual performance. The training program was administered entirely by independent parties (school teachers) and the researchers had no role in the administration of the training program or in participant assignment."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""For Matrix Reasoning scores, there was a significant within-subjects effect of time F(1, 24) = 32.98, p < .001, ƞp 2 = .579, and an interaction of time*condition, F(1, 24) =13.58, p < .005, ƞp 2 = .361. The between-subjects effect of condition was significant, F(1, 24) = 5.08, p = .034, ƞp 2 = .175. Follow-up paired samples t-tests found a significant increase in Matrix Reasoning scores from Time 1 (M = 47.92 , SD = 9.08) to Time 2 (M = 56.75, SD = 7.03) for the Experimental Condition, t(11) = -5.1, p < .001, CI [-12.64; -502], but no significant difference between Time 1 (M = 45.64, SD = 6.2) and Time 2 (M = 47.57 , SD = 5) for the Control Condition, t(13) = -2.13, p = .053, 95% CI [-3.88; .026]."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The original is described as finding improved Raven matrix reasoning following training; current matrix reasoning improves significantly only in the trained group. This supports the same intellectual-performance finding; mathematics is not re-tested.""}"
10.1007/s10857-019-09443-2,91fc31766a79dc6d,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,"{""corrected_value"": null, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""91fc31766a79dc6d"", ""location"": ""abstract"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""However, empirical findings on the distinguishability of these two knowledge components, and their relationship with student outcomes, are mixed. In this replication and extension study, we explore these issues, drawing on evidence from a multi-year study of over 200 fourth- and fifth-grade US teachers."", ""reason"": ""No prior adjudication or state files exist at the listed paths. The supplied source text is a publisher preview containing only the title, abstract, endnotes, and reference list, omitting the main text body. The abstract and endnotes characterize the work as a replication and extension study examining the dimensionality of teacher content knowledge (CK) and pedagogical content knowledge (PCK) / mathematical knowledge for teaching (MKT) and their contribution to student learning, without naming the specific prior study or studies being replicated. The reference list includes shipped row input_row_index 29 (Copur-Gencturk, Tolar, Jacobson, & Fan, 2018, DOI 10.1177/0022487118761860) as well as multiple other empirical studies of CK/PCK/MKT dimensionality and student achievement (e.g., Hill, Schilling, & Ball, 2004; Hill, Rowan, & Ball, 2005; Krauss et al., 2008; Baumert et al., 2010; Kleickmann et al., 2013, 2015; Campbell et al., 2014). Without the primary article body, the available evidence does not settle whether the replication target is already represented by shipped row 29 or includes another specific study from the reference list."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0073_91fc31766a79dc6d"", ""target_index"": 0, ""verdict"": ""undeterminable""}","{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In this replication and extension study, we explore these issues, drawing on evidence from a multi-year study of over 200 fourth- and fifth-grade US teachers."", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": """", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract reports a single teacher-knowledge factor and positive student achievement predictions, but does not identify which earlier paper is retested or state that paper’s findings. A target-specific comparison cannot be established.""}"
10.3109/03014460.2011.614278,118e48f49bc02bfd,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,"{""file_key"": ""118e48f49bc02bfd"", ""target_index"": 0, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""obvious"", ""verdict"": ""undeterminable"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""This study replicated the genetic association of SNP of FTO (rs17817449) with obesity in a north Indian population and, to the authors' knowledge; this is the first such association study in a north Indian population."", ""location"": ""Complete available primary abstract, Conclusions"", ""reason"": ""The available complete primary abstract specifies rs17817449 association and a new north-Indian sample but names no earlier original. Dina2007 is compatible with this discovery context; multiple early FTO reports concern associated intron1 variants. Original publisher/primary abstracts establish plausibility, not which particular earlier report the authors retested. Full replicating text was unavailable after the documented acquisition routes. The exact original identity remains an actual evidence-access limit; no additional genuinely absent paper can be certified and no categorical represented exclusion is forced.""}","{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""This study replicated the genetic association of SNP of FTO (rs17817449) with obesity in a north Indian population and, to the authors' knowledge; this is the first such association study in a north Indian population."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""This study replicated the genetic association of SNP of FTO (rs17817449) with obesity in a north Indian population and, to the authors' knowledge; this is the first such association study in a north Indian population."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract explicitly states replication of the association. Original-paper identity and number cannot be recovered from the abstract; this unnamed entry records the stated target association without guessing a citation.""}"
10.1017/s000305540505166x,7ca2f3f9da21d2d9,1,,Do Get-Out-the-Vote Calls Reduce Turnout? The Importance of Statistical Methods for Field Experiments.,"Imai, Kosuke.",2005,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": """", ""title"": ""Do Get-Out-the-Vote Calls Reduce Turnout? The Importance of Statistical Methods for Field Experiments."", ""authors"": ""Imai, Kosuke."", ""year"": ""2005""}, ""quote"": ""Replication of Professor Imai’s (2005) analysis reveals\na series of errors that call into question the magnitude,\nsignificance, and robustness of his matching estimates."", ""location"": {""start_line"": 47, ""end_line"": 49, ""form_feed_page"": 1}, ""reason"": ""Omitted target: Under the codebook rules for replication chains and earlier replication/reanalysis attempts, an earlier reanalysis is a target when the current paper directly re-tests its findings. Gerber and Green (2005) directly re-runs Imai (2005)'s propensity score matching code and analysis on the dataset Imai analyzed (Table 2, pp. 308-310), demonstrating computational discrepancies and robustness failures, and also tests Imai's 6.5 percentage point estimate in the 2002 replication experiment."", ""quote_provenance"": {""kind"": ""new_independent_literal_primary_quotation"", ""inspection_result"": ""evidence_followup/integration/strict_literal_quote_inspection.result.json"", ""issue_index"": 157, ""source_path"": ""/home/lukas/.t3/worktrees/fred-data/audit-flora-blind-2026-10/audit/flora_blind_2026-10/texts/7ca2f3f9da21d2d9.txt"", ""source_sha256"": ""202e3db77dd899e088b76ec3bb91ef632ee392f68a35a0a6cab63c3b212edf6a"", ""raw_start_offset"": 2530, ""raw_end_offset"": 2699, ""scientific_verdict_unchanged"": true, ""reason"": ""Parent verified exact raw slice and semantic identity with the earlier accepted passage under its recorded display-normalisation policy. Independent primary-context inspector supported this passage. Original provider wording and whole prior decision retained in history; raw provider reply unchanged.""}}","{""authors"": ""Imai, Kosuke."", ""year"": ""2005"", ""title"": ""Do Get-Out-the-Vote Calls Reduce Turnout? The Importance of Statistical Methods for Field Experiments."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Replication of Professor Imai’s (2005) analysis reveals a series of errors that call into question the magnitude, significance, and robustness of his matching estimates."", ""outcome"": """", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": ""computational issues"", ""outcome_computational_quote"": ""Table 9 of Imai 2005 exaggerates the significance of the phone effect by reporting a coefficient of 6.5 with a standard error of 3.2. These numbers imply a t-ratio of 2.03, which tells the reader at a glance that the effect is statistically significant using a two-sided test (p < .05). When these numbers are replicated using 500,000 bootstrap samples rather than 500, the correct estimate is 6.2, with a t-ratio of 1.87 (see Table 2)."", ""outcome_robustness"": ""robustness challenges"", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": ""The estimated effects of phone calls fall to insignificance when based on the actual sample. His matching program generates an estimate of 5.1 (SE = 3.3) when performing one-to-five matching on the actual sample, and this number drops to 3.6 (SE = 4.5) when one-to-five matching is performed with replacement."", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Re-running the original matching analysis yields substantive numerical discrepancies and inflated reported significance. Alternative matching specifications and analysis of actual rather than bootstrap samples remove significance. This target is itself an earlier reanalysis, directly re-tested here.""}"
10.1080/23251042.2021.1975350,cf6414831a5988a8,0,,,"Fitzgerald, Schor, and Jorgenson",,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Fitzgerald, Schor, and Jorgenson"", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Using 2007–2013 data from the United States, research by Fitzgerald, Schor, and Jorgenson lent support to this policy option by finding a positive and statistically significant relationship between average work hours and state-level CO2 emissions. We replicated their analyses by including more recent data and confirmed the linkage between the average hours worked in a state and annual overall CO2 emissions."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We replicated their analyses by including more recent data and confirmed the linkage between the average hours worked in a state and annual overall CO2 emissions."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract explicitly confirms the same relationship in additional years; the stronger relationship does not make this mixed.""}"
10.1086/599622,04d2019632768708,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,"{""corrected_value"": null, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""04d2019632768708"", ""location"": ""Abstract (line 5)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""In our embedded ultimatum game, the median offer contains only 8 percent of the joint surplus from settlement. When we replicate the simple ultimatum game, we find that 50 percent of the joint surplus is contained in the median offer."", ""reason"": ""The supplied primary source text (04d2019632768708.txt) consists only of the title, authors, and abstract, and no prior adjudication or state file exists for this work. In the abstract, Pecorino and Van Boening state: 'When we replicate the simple ultimatum game, we find that 50 percent of the joint surplus is contained in the median offer.' However, the abstract does not cite any specific original study, authors, year, or title. Shipped row input_row_index 51 is Güth, Schmittberger, and Schwarze (1982), 'An experimental analysis of ultimatum bargaining' (10.1016/0167-2681(82)90011-7, verified in bibliography_metadata.json), the classic ultimatum bargaining experiment. Without the full text or reference list, the precise unresolved question is whether the 'simple ultimatum game' replication in Pecorino and Van Boening cites and retests Güth, Schmittberger, and Schwarze (1982) (input_row_index 51) or a different prior ultimatum game study, so original identity and representation cannot be settled from the abstract alone."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0002_04d2019632768708"", ""target_index"": 0, ""verdict"": ""undeterminable""}","{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""When we replicate the simple ultimatum game, we find that 50 percent of the joint surplus is contained in the median offer."", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Abstract identifies a simple ultimatum-game replication but no particular original paper or its finding; a positive current result alone cannot settle a target-specific outcome.""}"
