10.1177/2053168016652420,216578f767969de4,1,,African American Power and Politics: The Political Context Variable,"Walton H, Jr.",1997,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""Like Walton (1997: Table 4.3), we trace racial discourse over time and across journals. Figure 2 contains separate scatterplots for each journal."", ""location"": ""Analysis and discussion (lines 181–183, 208–210, 234, 269) and Conclusion (lines 326–333)"", ""reason"": ""Walton (1997, Chapter 4) is a companion book chapter by the first author covering the same PSQ/APSR research program as Walton et al. (1995) and is cited only for conceptual background and as an analytical parallel ('Like Walton (1997: Table 4.3), we trace racial discourse over time and across journals'). Both Figure 1 and Figure 2 explicitly identify the 54-article corpus as the 'Walton et al. Replication Dataset, 1894–1990 (N = 54)' from Walton et al. (1995: Appendix 1), and the paper neither states a distinct empirical finding from Walton (1997) nor reports a separate replication verdict for it. Omitting a second row for Walton (1997) is therefore not an error.""}","{""authors"": ""Walton H, Jr."", ""year"": ""1997"", ""title"": ""African American Power and Politics: The Political Context Variable"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Like Walton (1997:\nTable 4.3), we trace racial discourse over time and across\njournals."", ""outcome"": """", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""introduction"", ""outcome_computation"": ""not checked"", ""outcome_computational_quote"": ""By building an instrument that gets at\nwhat Walton and his colleagues observe, we re-analyze\ntheir corpus of text in novel ways and apply our instrument\nto current articles in top-tier political science journals."", ""outcome_robustness"": ""robust"", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": ""This\nreplication-and-extension confirms not only that ours and\nWalton’s operationalizations are empirically comparable\nbut also that the former can serve as a proxy for the\nlatter when reviewing the race literature."", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The original 54-publication corpus is re-analyzed using an alternative automated measure of centrality, rather than rerunning the original numerical analysis. The authors explicitly conclude the operationalizations are comparable and the original findings hold up to re-examination. New journal articles in the extension are not treated as robustness tests of original data.""}"
10.1101/144733,2a634d89a5ebc169,1,,Response latencies in discriminations of recency.,"Muter, P.",1979,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""corrected_value"": null, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""2a634d89a5ebc169"", ""location"": ""Title, Lines 1-3; Abstract, Lines 14-29; Introduction, Lines 47-54 and 144-149; Method (Procedure), Lines 168-171 and 195-208"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""The procedure of this experiment follows the procedure of Experiment 2 of Hacker\n\n(1980) closely. We describe the procedures in detail here, noting di↵erences between this\nexperiment and prior work where relevant."", ""reason"": ""The primary source text explicitly identifies Hacker (1980) as the empirical study being replicated and extended—in its title ('Recency order judgments in short term memory: Replication and extension of Hacker (1980)'), abstract ('The classic ﬁnding from short-term relative JOR tasks is that correct response time (RT) depends on the lag to the more recent item but not to the less recent item (Hacker, 1980)'), introduction ('This study describes ﬁndings from a short-term JOR task very similar to the procedure used in (Hacker, 1980)'), and Method section ('The procedure of this experiment follows the procedure of Experiment 2 of Hacker (1980) closely'), where every procedural detail and methodological difference is compared specifically against Experiment 2 of Hacker (1980). Hacker (1980) is already represented in shipped_rows (input_row_index 90, DOI 10.1037/0278-7393.6.6.651). By contrast, Muter (1979) is cited only in parenthetical literature groupings ('Muter, 1979; Hacker, 1980; Hockley, 1984') as background context for the canonical short-term JOR pattern and self-terminating backward scanning model, without independent empirical retesting of Muter's (1979) study design or specific claims. Under the codebook's target-eligibility and replication-chain rules, only Hacker (1980) is an eligible target. Prior adjudication and state files do not exist for this work."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0019_2a634d89a5ebc169"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""shipped_right""}","{""authors"": ""Muter, P."", ""year"": ""1979"", ""title"": ""Response latencies in discriminations of recency."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The classic ﬁnding from short-term relative JOR tasks is that correct response time (RT) depends on the lag to the more recent item but not to the less recent item (see Fig. 1, Muter, 1979; Hacker, 1980; Hockley, 1984)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""This study replicated the classical ﬁnding in a relative JOR task that correct RT depends on the lag to the more recent probe but not on the lag to the less recent probe (Fig. 2b)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The paper explicitly replicates the classic correct-RT finding attributed jointly to these papers and confirms it in the discussion.""}"
10.1101/144733,2a634d89a5ebc169,2,,Analysis of response time distributions in the study of cognitive processes.,"Hockley, W. E.",1984,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Hockley, W. E."", ""year"": ""1984"", ""title"": ""Analysis of response time distributions in the study of cognitive processes."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The classic ﬁnding from short-term relative JOR tasks is that correct response time (RT) depends on the lag to the more recent item but not to the less recent item (see Fig. 1, Muter, 1979; Hacker, 1980; Hockley, 1984)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""This study replicated the classical ﬁnding in a relative JOR task that correct RT depends on the lag to the more recent probe but not on the lag to the less recent probe (Fig. 2b)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The paper explicitly replicates the classic correct-RT finding attributed jointly to these papers and confirms it in the discussion.""}"
10.1037/edu0000146,701e7e90a9964039,1,10.1037/a0019506,Enhancing interest and performance with a utility value intervention.,"Hulleman, C. S., Godes, O., Hendricks, B. L., & Harackiewicz, J. M.",2010,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": ""10.1037/a0019506"", ""title"": ""Enhancing interest and performance with a utility value intervention."", ""authors"": ""Hulleman, C. S., Godes, O., Hendricks, B. L., & Harackiewicz, J. M."", ""year"": ""2010"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""mixed""}, ""quote"": ""The utility value interventions utilized in the current studies\n\nwere based on the self-generated utility value interventions\n\nused by Hulleman and colleagues (e.g., Hulleman et al., 2010;\nHulleman & Harackiewicz, 2009). In addition to replicating this\n\nprior research, we extend it in five ways."", ""location"": {""start_line"": 517, ""end_line"": 524, ""form_feed_page"": 1}, ""reason"": ""Omitted target: Hulleman et al. (2010) (DOI: 10.1037/a0019506) is an independent original paper explicitly cited throughout the introduction, methods, and discussion alongside Hulleman & Harackiewicz (2009) as prior utility value intervention research replicated in Study 2, and shares the same 'mixed' partial-replication verdict in the Study 2 Discussion."", ""quote_provenance"": {""kind"": ""new_independent_literal_primary_quotation"", ""inspection_result"": ""evidence_followup/integration/strict_literal_quote_inspection.result.json"", ""issue_index"": 158, ""source_path"": ""/home/lukas/.t3/worktrees/fred-data/audit-flora-blind-2026-10/audit/flora_blind_2026-10/texts/701e7e90a9964039.txt"", ""source_sha256"": ""13a618b22b0177a74c3fc4b483f978ee6961d2bda1dae6c2df79687019f1d547"", ""raw_start_offset"": 18248, ""raw_end_offset"": 18544, ""scientific_verdict_unchanged"": true, ""reason"": ""Parent verified exact raw slice and semantic identity with the earlier accepted passage under its recorded display-normalisation policy. Independent primary-context inspector supported this passage. Original provider wording and whole prior decision retained in history; raw provider reply unchanged.""}}","{""authors"": ""Hulleman, C. S., Godes, O., Hendricks, B. L., & Harackiewicz, J. M."", ""year"": ""2010"", ""title"": ""Enhancing interest and performance with a utility value intervention."", ""doi"": ""10.1037/a0019506"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""2"", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Although the research generated by the expectancy-value framework has been largely correlational (Wigfield & Cambria, 2010), recent classroom studies reveal that interventions designed to enhance perceptions of value can increase both interest and course perfor- mance (e.g., Hulleman & Harackiewicz, 2009; Hulleman, Godes, Hendricks, & Harackiewicz, 2010). The research presented herein replicates and extends this prior work by further investigating a theory-based intervention designed to enhance student motivation and performance."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The results of Study 2 partially replicated prior research dem- onstrating that a theoretically guided intervention based on the expectancy-value framework could enhance student learning out- comes (Hulleman & Harackiewicz, 2009; Hulleman et al., 2010)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors explicitly give a partial-replication verdict over both cited prior intervention papers; interest/performance benefits coexist with an unexpected mechanism.""}"
10.1037/edu0000095,75424acd1af13f1d,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,"{""corrected_value"": null, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""75424acd1af13f1d"", ""location"": ""Abstract (line 4)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""This study reports 2 years of findings from a randomized controlled trial designed to replicate and demonstrate the efficacy of an existing, successful professional development program, the Pathway Project, that uses a cognitive strategies approach to text-based analytical writing. Building on an earlier randomized field trial in a large, urban, low socioeconomic status (SES) district in which 98% of the students were Latino and 88% were mainstreamed English learners (ELs) at the intermediate level of fluency, the project aimed to help secondary school students, specifically Latinos and mainstreamed ELs, in another large, urban, low-SES district to develop the academic writing skills called for in the rigorous Common Core State Standards for English Language Arts."", ""reason"": ""No prior adjudication or state file exists at the supplied context paths. Although the supplied abstract states that the randomized controlled trial was designed to replicate the Pathway Project and builds on 'an earlier randomized field trial in a large, urban, low socioeconomic status (SES) district', the source text is abstract-only and prints no authors, year, title, DOI, or reference list entry for that earlier trial. Consequently, the available primary evidence and Crossref metadata for shipped row input_row_index 94 (10.58680/rte20076014, Olson & Land, 2007, Research in the Teaching of English) cannot settle the source-backed bibliographic identity of the target trial or whether it is represented by shipped row 94 versus a distinct unshipped report."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0058_75424acd1af13f1d"", ""target_index"": 0, ""verdict"": ""undeterminable""}","{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""This study reports 2 years of findings from a randomized controlled trial designed to replicate and demonstrate the efficacy of an existing, successful professional development program, the Pathway Project, that uses a cognitive strategies approach to text-based analytical writing. Building on an earlier randomized field trial in a large, urban, low socioeconomic status (SES) district in which 98% of the students were Latino and 88% were mainstreamed English learners (ELs) at the intermediate level of fluency, the project aimed to help secondary school students, specifically Latinos and mainstreamed ELs, in another large, urban, low-SES district to develop the academic writing skills called for in the rigorous Common Core State Standards for English Language Arts."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Difference-in-differences and regression analyses revealed significant effects on student writing outcomes in both years of the intervention (Year 1, d = 0.48; Year 2, d = 0.60). Additionally, Pathway students had higher odds than control students of passing the California High School Exit Exam in both years. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2017 APA, all rights reserved)"", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Abstract identifies an existing successful Pathway intervention, then reports positive effects on the writing outcome it was designed to replicate. Original citation not available in abstract.""}"
10.1002/dev.21515,53850ba7cc0a14c2,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,"{""corrected_value"": null, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""53850ba7cc0a14c2"", ""location"": ""Abstract (line 3); new primary identities/access receipts in original_identity_0008.result.json"", ""method"": ""obvious"", ""quote"": ""In Study 1 ( n = 32), we replicated previous findings showing an asymmetrical pattern of categorization of happy and fearful faces in 6.5‐month‐old infants, and extended these findings by demonstrating that infants’ categorization did not differ when emotions were expressed by familiar (i.e., caregiver) faces."", ""reason"": ""New primary reference deposits and original author abstracts narrow the actual candidates but do not uniquely map current Study1/2 to an original. Nelson/Dolgin1985contains BOTH asymmetrical happy/fear categorization and a fear-preference experiment; Nelson/Morse/Leavitt1979andKotsoni2001also supply plausible categorization/order findings, while shippedPeltola2009is a related attention comparison. Reference-list inclusion plus matching phenomenon is not enough to identify the exact current empirical target. The current full body remains inaccessible after the documented free publisher, repository, Wayback and fresh collaborative-browser routes. The complete Safar2012thesis is a DIFFERENT candidate report: studies1a/1b/1c and study2at8–9months, versus current two6.5month studiesN32/N34with spontaneous-preference Study2. Parent rejects its substitution or source promotion; no experiment numbering or outcome is transferred. Thus actual experiment-to-reference identity remains unknown, not a positive omission or exclusion. JSTOR/Wiley DOI aliases of Nelson papers are one work each, not extra originals."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0039_53850ba7cc0a14c2"", ""target_index"": 0, ""verdict"": ""undeterminable""}","{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1"", ""evidence_quote"": ""In Study 1 ( n = 32), we replicated previous findings showing an asymmetrical pattern of categorization of happy and fearful faces in 6.5‐month‐old infants, and extended these findings by demonstrating that infants’ categorization did not differ when emotions were expressed by familiar (i.e., caregiver) faces."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In Study 1 ( n = 32), we replicated previous findings showing an asymmetrical pattern of categorization of happy and fearful faces in 6.5‐month‐old infants, and extended these findings by demonstrating that infants’ categorization did not differ when emotions were expressed by familiar (i.e., caregiver) faces."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract expressly states that this study replicated the prior finding; the original citation is not named in the available evidence.""}"
