 ""year"": ""2012"", ""title"": ""Replications in Psychology Research"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""To complement the survey results, a bibliometric approach was used to identify replications in the TIS\n\nliterature by carrying out a conceptual replication of Makel, Plucker, and Hegarty’s (2012) study on\nreplication in psychology research."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In psychology,\n\na discipline in which replication is more common than in empirical TIS, the percentage of replications\n\npublished in journals was 1.07% (Makel, Plucker, and Hegarty 2012)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The quoted sentence marks one difference. Elsewhere the authors describe exact-replication proportions as not very different from Makel et al.; hence mixed.""}"
