10.3758/s13428-023-02329-5,49c906aa1fa610be,1,10.1037/xge0000037,When goals conflict with values: Counterproductive attentional and oculomotor capture by reward-related stimuli.,"Le Pelley, M. E., Pearson, D., Griffiths, O., & Beesley, T.",2015,full_text,,error,obvious,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""obvious"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Le Pelley, M. E., Pearson, D., Griffiths, O., & Beesley, T."", ""year"": ""2015"", ""title"": ""When goals conflict with values: Counterproductive attentional and oculomotor capture by reward-related stimuli."", ""doi"": ""10.1037/xge0000037"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined""}, ""quote"": ""These two results replicate previous findings with Le Pelley’s paradigm (Le Pelley et al.,\n2015\n), where the VMAC effect emerged over trials, even when the reward-signals are always task irrelevant, and also replicate Watson et al. (\n2019a\n), who showed that once established, the VMAC effect remains present regardless of the current informational value of the reward-related stimuli."", ""location"": ""Discussion"", ""reason"": ""The discussion expressly attributes the reproduced trial-wise reward capture result to Le Pelley et al. (2015), separately from Watson et al. (2019). The references identify DOI 10.1037/xge0000037; the only shipped pair is Watson (10.1371/journal.pone.0226284). Parent reviewed the cited results/purpose and reference identity against all supplied shipped originals. This decision confirms the missing paper-level pair only; its overall outcome has not been adjudicated.""}","{""authors"": ""Le Pelley, M. E., Pearson, D., Griffiths, O., & Beesley, T."", ""year"": ""2015"", ""title"": ""When goals conflict with values: Counterproductive attentional and oculomotor capture by reward-related stimuli."", ""doi"": ""10.1037/xge0000037"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The first aim of the present study was to replicate and extend the results of Watson et al. (\n2019a\n), which demonstrated that the VMAC effect could be observed in conditions where a high reward predicting singleton had always been response irrelevant and held no informational value. In our study, we showed that the VMAC effect increased over time in the rewarded phase, that is, we observed a progressive increase in RTs when the high valued singleton acted as singleton distractor compared to the low valued singleton. Furthermore, when reward feedback was omitted in the unrewarded phase, the VMAC effect remained constant, without any evidence of reduction over trials. These two results replicate previous findings with Le Pelley’s paradigm (Le Pelley et al.,\n2015\n), where the VMAC effect emerged over trials, even when the reward-signals are always task irrelevant, and also replicate Watson et al. (\n2019a\n), who showed that once established, the VMAC effect remains present regardless of the current informational value of the reward-related stimuli."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The first aim of the present study was to replicate and extend the results of Watson et al. (\n2019a\n), which demonstrated that the VMAC effect could be observed in conditions where a high reward predicting singleton had always been response irrelevant and held no informational value. In our study, we showed that the VMAC effect increased over time in the rewarded phase, that is, we observed a progressive increase in RTs when the high valued singleton acted as singleton distractor compared to the low valued singleton. Furthermore, when reward feedback was omitted in the unrewarded phase, the VMAC effect remained constant, without any evidence of reduction over trials. These two results replicate previous findings with Le Pelley’s paradigm (Le Pelley et al.,\n2015\n), where the VMAC effect emerged over trials, even when the reward-signals are always task irrelevant, and also replicate Watson et al. (\n2019a\n), who showed that once established, the VMAC effect remains present regardless of the current informational value of the reward-related stimuli."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly state that their new-sample results replicate this finding.""}"
10.1027/1864-9335/a000188,fe0e494e6758464b,1,,,Gong and Medin,2012,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Gong and Medin"", ""year"": ""2012"", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Eyal, Liberman, and Trope (2008) established that people judged moral transgressions more harshly and virtuous acts more positively when the acts were psychologically distant than close. In a series of conceptual and direct replications, Gong and Medin (2012) came to the opposite conclusion. Attempting to resolve these inconsistencies, we conducted four high-powered replication studies in which we varied temporal distance (Studies 1 and 3), social distance (Study 2) or construal level (Study 4), and registered their impact on moral judgment."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Eyal, Liberman, and Trope (2008) established that people judged moral transgressions more harshly and virtuous acts more positively when the acts were psychologically distant than close. In a series of conceptual and direct replications, Gong and Medin (2012) came to the opposite conclusion. Attempting to resolve these inconsistencies, we conducted four high-powered replication studies in which we varied temporal distance (Studies 1 and 3), social distance (Study 2) or construal level (Study 4), and registered their impact on moral judgment. We found no systematic effect of temporal distance, the effect of social distance consistent with Eyal et al., and the reversed effect of direct construal level manipulation, consistent with Gong and Medin. Possible explanations for the incompatible results are discussed."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The construal effect agrees with Gong and Medin, while the social distance result agrees with their stated opposite Eyal finding. The abstract does not map original study numbers.""}"
10.1155/2013/639702,0ab1768ae9c6e310,1,,Object individuation in young infants: further evidence with an event-monitoring paradigm,"Wilcox, T., and Baillargeon, R.",1998,full_text,same_author_year_siblings,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Wilcox, T., and Baillargeon, R."", ""year"": ""1998"", ""title"": ""Object individuation in young infants: further evidence with an event-monitoring paradigm"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The direct Narrow-Control test                  Our answer to this question is twofold. First, this phe-\nthat we employed has been used in studies by Wilcox and                nomenon is actually not rare at all in the infant research\nher collaborators as well. For example, in their followup              literature. For example, when two distinct and different-\nto the reference study, Wilcox and Baillargeon [5] obtained"", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Resembling the results obtained through the\nSLTP, the eye tracking data failed to provide evidence that the                               15.00\ninfants used featural information to discriminate between the\n\n\n\n\n                                                                     Mean fixation time (s)\nobjects involved."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The paper implements the direct narrow-control comparison previously used in reference [5] and explicitly contrasts its null discrimination comparison with that paper’s shorter ball-ball than ball-box looking times. This is a retested finding with a new sample, despite the age difference. The evidence quote preserves the interleaved text exactly as supplied, without joining columns or repairing text.""}"
10.1097/j.pain.0000000000000657,1f0d03bc2043e5b8,1,,Pharmacological characterization of intraplantar Complete Freund's Adjuvant-induced burrowing deficits.,"Gould SA, Doods H, Lamla T, Pekcec A.",2016,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""corrected_value"": null, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""1f0d03bc2043e5b8"", ""location"": ""introduction"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Although burrowing is a relatively novel pain outcome measure, a few studies have reported decreased burrowing in rat models of inflammatory and neuropathic pain; importantly, burrowing deficits were reversed by known analgesics, suggesting a degree of predictive validity for this outcome measure.2,9,20,35,61,62\nWe hypothesise that by using a prospective multicentre design, the reliability of novel outcome measures can be efficiently and rapidly evaluated. As part of the IMI Europain collaboration (http://www.imieuropain.org), we investigated rat burrowing behaviour after induction of complete Freund adjuvant (CFA)-associated inflammation across 8 centres to assess the reliability of suppressed burrowing as a pain-related outcome measure."", ""reason"": ""Prior adjudication and state files are absent. This IMI Europain multicentre validation study retests CFA-induced suppression of rat burrowing originally reported by Andrews et al. (2012) [2] (already represented by shipped row 182, 10.1016/j.ejpain.2011.07.012), from which the experimental protocol was taken ('Burrowing experiments were performed as previously described.2,62', where [62] is Rutten et al. 2014 in a knee-joint inflammation model). Gould et al. (2016) [20] ('Pharmacological characterization of intraplantar Complete Freund's Adjuvant-induced burrowing deficits', Behav Brain Res 2016;301:142–51) was published in 2016—after all 11 multicentre experiments in the present study were completed between June 2013 and September 2014 (Results 3.1)—by co-author A. Pekcec's Boehringer Ingelheim group, and is cited only in a six-reference background cluster [2,9,20,35,61,62] and Discussion remarks [2,20,62] on analgesic reversal (ibuprofen vs. gabapentin), which this non-pharmacological study did not test."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0009_1f0d03bc2043e5b8"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""shipped_right""}","{""authors"": ""Gould SA, Doods H, Lamla T, Pekcec A."", ""year"": ""2016"", ""title"": ""Pharmacological characterization of intraplantar Complete Freund's Adjuvant-induced burrowing deficits."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Although burrowing is a relatively novel pain outcome measure, a few studies have reported decreased burrowing in rat models of inflammatory and neuropathic pain; importantly, burrowing deficits were reversed by known analgesics, suggesting a degree of predictive validity for this outcome measure.2,9,20,35,61,62"", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Overall, reduced burrowing was partially replicated at 6 of the 8 participating centres with an element of variability between and within centres."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors give an overall partial replication verdict for CFA-associated suppressed burrowing across centres.""}"
10.1177/20531680231205157,6d33998f7d1c5c95,1,10.1257/aer.20191435,The welfare effects of social media,"Allcott H, Braghieri L, Eichmeyer S, et al.",2020,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""In addition to following the same process as in the original study (Asimovic et al., 2021), we are encouraged by the fact that we were able to replicate secondary findings from the previous study in terms of deactivation reducing users’ anxiety."", ""location"": ""discussion (lines 465-469); introduction (lines 88-96, 135-139)"", ""reason"": ""Wrong original / not an independent target under the replication-chain rule. The paper explicitly identifies Asimovic et al. (2021) in the singular as 'the original study' and describes reduced anxiety and reduced news knowledge as 'secondary outcomes from the original study' (lines 135-136) and 'secondary findings from the previous study' (line 468) that Asimovic et al. (2021) had already tested following Allcott et al. (2020) (lines 88-93, 389-392). Under the codebook rule on replication chains, the target is the study closest to this paper in the chain that it actually re-tests (Asimovic et al., 2021), not the earlier U.S. deactivation study (Allcott et al., 2020).""}","{""authors"": ""Allcott H, Braghieri L, Eichmeyer S, et al."", ""year"": ""2020"", ""title"": ""The welfare effects of social media"", ""doi"": ""10.1257/aer.20191435"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We also find support for recent findings (Allcott et al., 2020; Asimovic et al., 2021) that Facebook deactivation leads to a reduction in anxiety levels and suggestive evidence of a reduction in knowledge of current news, though the latter is again limited to our 2021 subsample."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We also find support for recent findings (Allcott et al., 2020; Asimovic et al., 2021) that Facebook deactivation leads to a reduction in anxiety levels and suggestive evidence of a reduction in knowledge of current news, though the latter is again limited to our 2021 subsample."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Anxiety agrees, but news knowledge agrees only in 2021; the pooled and 2020 news effects are absent. These secondary findings are explicitly preregistered replication targets.""}"
10.3233/jad-2010-1357,5b3bc0521d2f9aa1,1,,No association between CALHM1 and Alzheimer’s disease risk.,"Bertram L, Schjeide BM, Hooli B, Mullin K, Hiltunen M, Soininen H, Ingelsson M, Lannfelt L, Blacker D, Tanzi RE",2008,full_text,competing_supported_matches,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""Unfortunately, original ﬁndings, obtained in ﬁve different case-control series, were not replicated in a large-scale study of familial AD [2] and in six additional studies conducted in AD patients and controls from different populations (Sweden [2], Finland [2], Belgium [3], Caucasian Americans [4,5], and Asians [6])."", ""location"": ""introduction"", ""reason"": ""Wrong original / non-target prior replication study. Reference [2] (Bertram et al., 2008, including the original authors' reply in Cell 135: 993-996) is explicitly introduced as an earlier replication study of the original findings [1] and included in the meta-analysis; its mention alongside [1, 4, 11] in Results serves to motivate testing [1]'s P86L variant under a recessive model for age at onset rather than establishing an independent replication target omitted by the shipped data.""}","{""authors"": ""Bertram L, Schjeide BM, Hooli B, Mullin K, Hiltunen M, Soininen H, Ingelsson M, Lannfelt L, Blacker D, Tanzi RE"", ""year"": ""2008"", ""title"": ""No association between CALHM1 and Alzheimer’s disease risk."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Several independent research groups have observed\nthat P86L and other CALMH1 markers modulate the\n\nAAO only under a recessive model [1,2,4,11]. These\n\nobservations reinforce a hypothetical recessive model\n\nfor the P86L variant in AD. So, we decided to evalu-\n\nate this model in AAO in Spanish AD patients."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We have provided new evidence for the role of the\n\nCALHM1P86L marker in ADrisk. Infact,wehaveob-\n\nserved a trend toward association in case-control stud-\n\nies and a full independent replication of the P86L ef-\n\nfect in AAO. Intriguingly, both results are evidentonly\n\nwhen EOAD patients are eliminated from the pool and\n\nwhen a recessive model is applied to analyses."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Outcome follows the explicit comparisons; the disease-risk nulls of prior replication papers are not themselves the age-at-onset claims re-tested here.""}"
10.3233/jad-2010-1357,5b3bc0521d2f9aa1,2,,No association between CALHM1 variation and risk of Alzheimer disease.,"Minster RL, Demirci FY, DeKosky ST, Kamboh MI",2009,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 2, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""Unfortunately, original ﬁndings, obtained in ﬁve different case-control series, were not replicated in a large-scale study of familial AD [2] and in six additional studies conducted in AD patients and controls from different populations (Sweden [2], Finland [2], Belgium [3], Caucasian Americans [4,5], and Asians [6])."", ""location"": ""introduction"", ""reason"": ""Wrong original / non-target prior replication study. Reference [4] (Minster et al., 2009) is explicitly introduced as one of the prior negative replication studies of the original findings [1] and is cited alongside [1, 2, 11] only as supporting the hypothetical recessive model for P86L in age at onset; per the codebook, an earlier replication attempt cited as background/context for model specification is not a separate target.""}","{""authors"": ""Minster RL, Demirci FY, DeKosky ST, Kamboh MI"", ""year"": ""2009"", ""title"": ""No association between CALHM1 variation and risk of Alzheimer disease."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Several independent research groups have observed\nthat P86L and other CALMH1 markers modulate the\n\nAAO only under a recessive model [1,2,4,11]. These\n\nobservations reinforce a hypothetical recessive model\n\nfor the P86L variant in AD. So, we decided to evalu-\n\nate this model in AAO in Spanish AD patients."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We have provided new evidence for the role of the\n\nCALHM1P86L marker in ADrisk. Infact,wehaveob-\n\nserved a trend toward association in case-control stud-\n\nies and a full independent replication of the P86L ef-\n\nfect in AAO. Intriguingly, both results are evidentonly\n\nwhen EOAD patients are eliminated from the pool and\n\nwhen a recessive model is applied to analyses."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Outcome follows the explicit comparisons; the disease-risk nulls of prior replication papers are not themselves the age-at-onset claims re-tested here.""}"
10.3233/jad-2010-1357,5b3bc0521d2f9aa1,3,,Candidate single-nucleotide polymorphisms from a genomewide association study of Alzheimer disease.,"Li H, Wetten S, Li L, St Jean PL, Upmanyu R, Surh L",2008,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 3, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""Several independent research groups have observed that P86L and other CALMH1 markers modulate the AAO only under a recessive model [1,2,4,11]. These observations reinforce a hypothetical recessive model for the P86L variant in AD. So, we decided to evaluate this model in AAO in Spanish AD patients."", ""location"": ""results"", ""reason"": ""Wrong original / background citation. Reference [11] (Li et al., 2008) is cited only once in passing as part of the background observation that 'P86L and other CALMH1 markers modulate the AAO only under a recessive model [1,2,4,11]' to motivate evaluating the recessive model for P86L in the authors' replication of [1]; the present paper only genotyped P86L and P85P and did not re-test Li et al.'s GWAS candidate SNPs.""}","{""authors"": ""Li H, Wetten S, Li L, St Jean PL, Upmanyu R, Surh L"", ""year"": ""2008"", ""title"": ""Candidate single-nucleotide polymorphisms from a genomewide association study of Alzheimer disease."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Several independent research groups have observed\nthat P86L and other CALMH1 markers modulate the\n\nAAO only under a recessive model [1,2,4,11]. These\n\nobservations reinforce a hypothetical recessive model\n\nfor the P86L variant in AD. So, we decided to evalu-\n\nate this model in AAO in Spanish AD patients."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We have provided new evidence for the role of the\n\nCALHM1P86L marker in ADrisk. Infact,wehaveob-\n\nserved a trend toward association in case-control stud-\n\nies and a full independent replication of the P86L ef-\n\nfect in AAO. Intriguingly, both results are evidentonly\n\nwhen EOAD patients are eliminated from the pool and\n\nwhen a recessive model is applied to analyses."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""New Spanish sample tests the published CALHM1 association and age-at-onset findings. The dominant versus recessive contrast makes the first target mixed; age-at-onset replication supports the remaining targets. Li includes other CALHM1 markers, leaving some target specificity uncertainty.""}"
10.1002/pits.22106,a9fa5691229969ab,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In this study, we replicated a targeted professional development approach that included a brief one‐on‐one training session and emailed visual performance feedback to increase novice teachers’ use of behavior‐specific praise, an evidence‐based classroom management skill."", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Abstract reports the new intervention results but neither names the earlier paper nor states its finding sufficiently to compare outcomes. Do not infer mixed from small effects or variable teacher response.""}"
