10.18148/srm/2012.v6i1.5092,b77773abf87cb45c,0,,Do we have to combine values in the Schwartz’ human values scale? A comment on the Davidov studies.,"Knoppen, D., & Saris, W.",2009,full_text,same_author_year_siblings,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 0, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""Finally, we compared the outcomes of our analysis with those reported by Knoppen and Saris (2009b)."", ""location"": ""methods"", ""reason"": ""Although the paper cites Knoppen and Saris (2009a,b) jointly in headings and general discussion when referring to their student-sample research, the title frames the study as a replication of a single work ('Knoppen and Saris (2009)') and Section 3.3 explicitly states that Study 1 followed the three-step procedure of and compared its analysis outcomes with those reported by Knoppen and Saris (2009b). Every specific empirical comparison in Results (Section 3.4) and Discussion (Section 3.5) is cited exclusively to 2009b, with no separate findings from 2009a independently tested or compared. Thus target 0 (2009a) is a companion citation rather than a separate omitted replication target.""}","{""year"": ""2009"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Study 1 examines whether the Knoppen and Saris (2009a,b) findings generalize to a representative sample of the German population."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Study 1 followed the procedures of Knoppen and Saris (2009a,b) to investigate the measurement models of the ten values as measured by the 40-item PVQ. Our findings in a representative German national sample largely support those that Knoppen and Saris reported for homogeneous samples of German students. But there were notable exceptions, too."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors explicitly characterize support with notable exceptions; detailed results include replicated sub-dimensions and cross-loadings alongside contradictory conformity and tradition results."", ""authors"": ""Knoppen, D., & Saris, W."", ""title"": ""Do we have to combine values in the Schwartz’ human values scale? A comment on the Davidov studies.""}"
10.1111/j.1365-2265.2011.04175.x,f38bdcc69a2f8ac6,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""A recent genome-wide association study for type 2 diabetes in Han Chinese identified several novel genetic variants. We investigated their associations with quantitative measures to explore the mechanism by which these variants influence glucose homoeostasis."", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract reports associations but does not give the original study citation or a verdict comparing all retested signals to its findings.""}"
10.1016/j.rasd.2014.01.006,f05d51a3482f3343,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We investigated visual scanning behaviour in children with ASD during an EFT in an attempt replicating a previous study examining differences in visual search behaviour."", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": """", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Abstract gives the present results but does not state the original result or identify its citation; comparison cannot be established.""}"
10.1080/22243534.2015.11828344,1c44b19bafd0d2c9,0,,,Hofstede,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,"{""corrected_value"": null, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""1c44b19bafd0d2c9"", ""location"": ""Abstract (line 5)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""In the present study the cultural dimensions of the well known Dutch researcher Geert Hofstede were tested on a sample of international business students. The sample consisted of 1 033 students from the Netherlands, Germany, China, South Africa and Qatar. The findings of the present research contest many of Hofstede's original findings. The dimensions power distance and long-term orientation show significant differences with Hofstede's original country values."", ""reason"": ""No prior adjudication or state file exists for this work, and Luna's prior spliced quote is replaced with an intact contiguous passage. The available primary source consists only of the title, author list, and abstract, which reports testing Geert Hofstede's cultural dimensions on 1,033 international business students from five countries and comparing them against Hofstede's original country values. Because the abstract gives no publication year, title, or reference list for the Hofstede work(s) supplying those original country values, the evidence cannot resolve which specific Hofstede publication is retested or whether shipped row 231 (Hofstede, 1999, 'Culture's consequences: International differences in work-related values') already represents the target work."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0008_1c44b19bafd0d2c9"", ""target_index"": 0, ""verdict"": ""undeterminable""}","{""authors"": ""Hofstede"", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In the present study the cultural dimensions of the well known Dutch researcher Geert Hofstede were tested on a sample of international business students."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The findings of the present research contest many of Hofstede's original findings. The dimensions power distance and long-term orientation show significant differences with Hofstede's original country values. The dimensions individualism, masculinity and uncertainty avoidance show significant differences for half of the countries."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Abstract contests many original findings, with discrepancies on two dimensions and only half the countries on three dimensions; coded as partial agreement/disagreement with uncertainty because no full results are available. No reference list supplied, so cannot resolve whether the underlying original findings belong to one or multiple Hofstede publications.""}"
10.1186/s13195-015-0154-x,1c4983e63889f830,1,10.1038/ng.2802,"Meta-analysis of 74,046 individuals identifies 11 new susceptibility loci for Alzheimer's disease.","Lambert JC, Ibrahim-Verbaas CA, Harold D, Naj AC, Sims R, Bellenguez C, et al.",2013,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""Additionally, none of these loss-of-function variants were in linkage disequilibrium (LD) with the ABCA7 common variant identified by GWAS [ 2 ], suggesting that there are multiple and independent mechanisms throughout the ABCA7 region that increase risk for AD. Based on these results, we tried to replicate the association of the ABCA7 loss-of-function variants in a large cohort of European–Americans."", ""location"": ""Introduction"", ""reason"": ""Lambert et al. (2013) [2] is cited only once in the Introduction as background identifying the previously reported GWAS common variant (rs4147929). The paper explicitly identifies Steinberg et al. [1] as 'the original study' and genotypes rs4147929 as a power check and to confirm Steinberg et al.'s finding that the ABCA7 loss-of-function variants are independent of (not in LD with) the common GWAS signal, rather than as an independent target replication of Lambert et al. [2].""}","{""authors"": ""Lambert JC, Ibrahim-Verbaas CA, Harold D, Naj AC, Sims R, Bellenguez C, et al."", ""year"": ""2013"", ""title"": ""Meta-analysis of 74,046 individuals identifies 11 new susceptibility loci for Alzheimer's disease."", ""doi"": ""10.1038/ng.2802"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The ABCA7 GWAS common variant (rs4147929) as well as the loss-of-function variants (p.Glu709Alafs*86, p.Leu1403Argfs*7, rs200538373, and rs113809142) reported by Steinberg et al. [ 1 ] were genotyped in our dataset using competitive allele-specific polymerase chain reaction KASPar genotyping technologies as described previously [ 3 , 4 ]."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In our dataset, the common variant rs4147929[A] was significantly associated with AD risk (Table 1 ; OR = 1.162, P = 0.022), suggesting that we have enough power to replicate known associations for common variants."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The GWAS ABCA7 common-variant association is deliberately genotyped and independently replicated; citation 2 identifies the publication that reported that signal.""}"
10.1002/jocb.350,0f58697d4f41df92,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,"{""corrected_value"": null, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""0f58697d4f41df92"", ""location"": ""Abstract (line 3)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""We could also replicate a positive relationship of mental speed with speeded divergent thinking (r = .21) and mental speed with unspeeded divergent thinking (r = .25)."", ""reason"": ""No prior adjudication or state files exist for this request. The supplied source text consists solely of an abstract (evidence_level: abstract) containing no citations or references. Although the abstract explicitly states that the authors replicated a positive relationship of mental speed with speeded and unspeeded divergent thinking, the abstract alone does not identify the original work(s) and cannot establish whether the retested finding is exclusively shipped row 246 (Preckel, Wermer, & Spinath, 2011, 10.1016/j.intell.2011.06.007) or an omitted original paper."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0003_0f58697d4f41df92"", ""target_index"": 0, ""verdict"": ""undeterminable""}","{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We could also replicate a positive relationship of mental speed with speeded divergent thinking (r = .21) and mental speed with unspeeded divergent thinking (r = .25)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We could also replicate a positive relationship of mental speed with speeded divergent thinking (r = .21) and mental speed with unspeeded divergent thinking (r = .25)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract explicitly states the positive relationships replicated, but does not identify the earlier paper(s); target identity and number remain uncertain.""}"
10.1002/per.2176,04117bda0ee9a4bf,1,10.1016/j.jrp.2011.12.003,From madness to genius: The Openness/Intellect trait domain as a paradoxical simplex,"DeYoung, C. G., Grazioplene, R. G., & Peterson, J. B.",2012,full_text,,error,obvious,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""obvious"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""DeYoung, C. G., Grazioplene, R. G., & Peterson, J. B."", ""year"": ""2012"", ""title"": ""From madness to genius: The Openness/Intellect trait domain as a paradoxical simplex"", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.jrp.2011.12.003"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined""}, ""quote"": ""Conceptually similar traits within the\n      Openness/Intellect (O/I) domain form a simplex, whereby traits are arranged along a single dimension from intelli-\n      gence to apophenia (i.e. observing patterns or causal connections were none exist). Across two studies (total\n      N = 297), we attempted to replicate the O/I simplex and determine how it relates to perceiving PPBS as profound.\n      Participants completed questionnaires measuring traits from the O/I simplex and provided profundity ratings for\n      PPBS."", ""location"": ""Single-column abstract; primary original identified in Introduction and References, p.190"", ""reason"": ""Parent independently inspected the new-sample O/I-simplex retest across both studies, the two explicit Confirming the Openness/Intellect simplex model results sections and the printed DeYoung/Grazioplene/Peterson2012 reference. The sole shipped original is Pennycook et al.2015 on pseudo-profound-bullshit receptivity, a distinct paper and finding. Both are retested; DeYoung is genuinely absent. This is a NEW parent primary-source decision with raw literal quotation, not salvage or repair of the rejected CLI bundle. No target outcome is adjudicated here.""}","{""authors"": ""DeYoung, C. G., Grazioplene, R. G., & Peterson, J. B."", ""year"": ""2012"", ""title"": ""From madness to genius: The Openness/Intellect trait domain as a paradoxical simplex"", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.jrp.2011.12.003"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""The O/I simplex reported in Study 1 and by DeYoung and colleagues (2012) was approximately replicated, albeit imperfectly, and a simplex model was adequate for describing relations among the O/I constructs."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The results were also consistent with the DeYoung and colleagues (2012) ﬁnding that the O/I domain is well described by a simplex. In both studies, a simplex model ﬁt the data well, with intelligence at one end, apophenia at the other end, and the remaining constructs falling in between."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true}"
10.1016/j.system.2020.102377,f133a7e9cc8706dc,0,,,Henry and Cliffordson,2017,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Henry and Cliffordson"", ""year"": ""2017"", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Results replicated those of Henry and Cliffordson (2017) for initial hypotheses related to motivational selves; current and ideal selves were statistically significant predictors of effort, in line with prior studies in the Chinese context."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Results replicated those of Henry and Cliffordson (2017) for initial hypotheses related to motivational selves; current and ideal selves were statistically significant predictors of effort, in line with prior studies in the Chinese context. However, results among this sample supported discrete constructs for ideal L2 and current L2 self and provide evidence of a separable sub-component of self-authenticity, digital self-authenticity."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Initial hypotheses replicated but the abstract frames construct results as contrasting with them. Publisher conclusion preview also explicitly refers to discrepancies with Henry and Cliffordson. Full results unavailable; mixed is uncertain.""}"
10.1017/s0033291720001919,7252b65650fbd13d,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,"{""corrected_value"": null, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""7252b65650fbd13d"", ""location"": ""Abstract and Note (lines 4-6); References (line 7)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""This study aimed to replicate research behind Climate Schools: Alcohol and Cannabis course among a large cohort of adolescents. METHODS: Seventy-one secondary schools across three States participated in a cluster-randomised controlled trial. Year 8 students received either the web-based Climate Schools: Alcohol and Cannabis course (Climate, n = 3236), or health education as usual (Control, n = 3150)."", ""reason"": ""The supplied source text (7252b65650fbd13d.txt) contains only the title, author list, abstract, and publisher reference list, with the note 'Publisher reference list (article body unavailable)' at line 6 (and no prior adjudication file existed). Although the abstract states that 'This study aimed to replicate research behind Climate Schools: Alcohol and Cannabis course among a large cohort of adolescents' and 'replicate alcohol effects in a large and diverse sample of students', it provides no in-text citations, while the reference list contains multiple distinct primary trials of the Climate Schools program by the same research group—including Newton et al. (2009) (10.1080/00048670802653364, Alcohol module, already present as shipped row 258), Newton et al. (2010) (10.1111/j.1360-0443.2009.02853.x, final results of the combined Alcohol and Cannabis course), Champion et al. (2015) (10.1177/0004867415577435, cross-validation trial of the internet-based alcohol and cannabis program), Teesson et al. (2017) (10.1017/S0033291717000198), and Newton et al. (2018) (10.1186/s13011-018-0171-4). Without the article body, the available evidence cannot establish which specific original work(s) were empirically retested as replication targets or whether any target beyond shipped row 258 was omitted."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0056_7252b65650fbd13d"", ""target_index"": 0, ""verdict"": ""undeterminable""}","{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""This study aimed to replicate research behind Climate Schools: Alcohol and Cannabis course among a large cohort of adolescents."", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Abstract explicitly reports replicating alcohol effects but names no original paper or uniquely bounded set of papers. It does not establish which findings from which original were re-tested, so a per-original verdict cannot be assigned. An unresolved target placeholder represents missing identification, not a claim that only one original exists.""}"
10.3390/jcm10010052,0e4704e600fb03ca,0,10.1192/bjo.2020.109,"Anxiety, depression, traumatic stress and COVID-19-related anxiety in the UK general population during the COVID-19 pandemic.","Shevlin, M.; McBride, O.; Murphy, J.; Miller, J.G.; Hartman, T.K.; Levita, L.; Mason, L.; Martinez, A.P.; McKay, R.; Stocks, T.V.A.; et al.",2020,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 0, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": ""10.1192/bjo.2020.109"", ""title"": ""Anxiety, depression, traumatic stress and COVID-19-related anxiety in the UK general population during the COVID-19 pandemic."", ""authors"": ""Shevlin, M.; McBride, O.; Murphy, J.; Miller, J.G.; Hartman, T.K.; Levita, L.; Mason, L.; Martinez, A.P.; McKay, R.; Stocks, T.V.A.; et al."", ""year"": ""2020"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""mixed""}, ""quote"": ""Summarizing, these results are in line with those of the UK study about anxiety, depression, and traumatic stress [19]. The UK and Italian data identify factors associated with worst psychological health: being a woman, younger age, having children, preexisting health issues of self or someone close, and the moderate to high perceived risk of contracting COVID-19 within one month. Unlike Italy, in the UK lower household incomes and having (had) the COVID-19 were associated with worst psychological health outcomes."", ""location"": ""Discussion, line 49 (see also Aims, line 14; Analytic Methods, line 34; Discussion, line 44; References, line 288)"", ""reason"": ""Omitted target. The paper explicitly re-tests two distinct UK C19PRC empirical papers [19,20]. In Section 3.2 (line 34, 41) and the Discussion (line 44: 'In a second part of the analysis, we replicated the approach by Shevlin et al. [19]'), the authors replicate the three binary logistic regression models from Shevlin et al. (2020) [19] (10.1192/bjo.2020.109) in the Italian sample and report mixed results (line 49: several risk factors matched the UK study, whereas lower household income and having had COVID-19 were associated with worse outcomes in the UK unlike Italy). Because [19] is an independent published paper whose findings are directly re-tested, it must be coded as a separate replication target.""}","{""authors"": ""Shevlin, M.; McBride, O.; Murphy, J.; Miller, J.G.; Hartman, T.K.; Levita, L.; Mason, L.; Martinez, A.P.; McKay, R.; Stocks, T.V.A.; et al."", ""year"": ""2020"", ""title"": ""Anxiety, depression, traumatic stress and COVID-19-related anxiety in the UK general population during the COVID-19 pandemic."", ""doi"": ""10.1192/bjo.2020.109"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In a second part of the analysis, we replicated the approach by Shevlin et al. [19]."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Summarizing, these results are in line with those of the UK study about anxiety, depression, and traumatic stress [19]. The UK and Italian data identify factors associated with worst psychological health: being a woman, younger age, having children, preexisting health issues of self or someone close, and the moderate to high perceived risk of contracting COVID-19 within one month. Unlike Italy, in the UK lower household incomes and having (had) the COVID-19 were associated with worst psychological health outcomes."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly identify matching risk factors and contrasting findings; the first sentence concerns these results rather than an overall verdict on the whole replication.""}"
10.1108/ijpdlm-04-2021-0121,a15282356e0c998b,1,,Power and relationship commitment: Their impact on marketing channel member performance.,"Brown, J., Lusch, R. & Nicholson, C. Y.",1995,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Brown, J., Lusch, R. & Nicholson, C. Y."", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Power and relationship commitment: Their impact on marketing channel member performance."", ""year"": ""1995""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""a15282356e0c998b"", ""location"": ""Section 5.3 (The comparison of the results in the United States and those of Brown et al. (1995)), lines 1252-1274; References, lines 1447-1449"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""We find that the impact of non-mediated power  on normative relationship commitment and \n\nthat  of  mediated  power  on  instrumental  relationship  commitment  in  the  United  States  are \n\nconsistent with the findings of Brown et al. (1995). Moreover, the results show that expert and \n\nlegitimate power are negatively associated with whereas referent power is positively associated \n\nwith  instrumental  relationship  commitment,  which  provides  a  possible  explanation  of  the \n\nfinding  of  the  insignificant  effect  of  non-mediated  power  on  instrumental  relationship \n\ncommitment  (Brown  et  al.,  1995).  The  result  reveals  that  different  bases  of  non-mediated \n\npower  may  have  conflicting  effects  on  instrumental  relationship  commitment  and  hence \n\nresearchers need to consider the individual effects of different bases of non-mediated power. \n\nMoreover, we find that reward, coercive and legal legitimate power do not significantly affect \n\nnormative relationship  commitment,  whereas Brown et  al. (1995) find that mediated power \n\nsignificantly reduces normative relationship commitment."", ""reason"": ""1. Empirical retesting: Zhang, Zhao, Huo, & Flynn (2022) collect new survey data from United States manufacturers (N = 202) to test the power-relationship commitment hypotheses originally tested in the United States by Brown, Lusch, & Nicholson (1995) (Introduction, lines 115-129, 207-210) and dedicate Section 5.3 (lines 1250-1286) to directly comparing their US empirical findings against Brown et al.'s (1995) findings on mediated/non-mediated power, instrumental/normative commitment, and power-symmetry moderation. Consistent with the Coleman pilot objection, explicit empirical retesting and results comparison in a dedicated results/discussion subsection establishes a genuine replication target rather than mere background. 2. Source-backed identity: The reference list (lines 1447-1449) identifies the work as Brown, J., Lusch, R. & Nicholson, C. Y. (1995), 'Power and relationship commitment: Their impact on marketing channel member performance', Journal of Retailing, 71, 363-392 (no DOI is printed in the primary source). 3. Absence and non-equivalence: The sole shipped row (input_row_index 270, 10.1016/j.jom.2007.08.002) is Zhao, Huo, Flynn, & Yeung (2007/2008) in Journal of Operations Management (represented by sibling target_index 0), a separate study of Chinese manufacturers by a different author team. Report equivalence does not apply. 4. Prior adjudication: Neither prior_adjudication_path nor prior_state_path exists for this work."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0081_a15282356e0c998b"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Brown, J., Lusch, R. & Nicholson, C. Y."", ""year"": ""1995"", ""title"": ""Power and relationship commitment: Their impact on marketing channel member performance."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We find that the impact of non-mediated power  on normative relationship commitment and \n\nthat  of  mediated  power  on  instrumental  relationship  commitment  in  the  United  States  are \n\nconsistent with the findings of Brown et al. (1995). Moreover, the results show that expert and \n\nlegitimate power are negatively associated with whereas referent power is positively associated \n\nwith  instrumental  relationship  commitment,  which  provides  a  possible  explanation  of  the \n\nfinding  of  the  insignificant  effect  of  non-mediated  power  on  instrumental  relationship \n\ncommitment  (Brown  et  al.,  1995).  The  result  reveals  that  different  bases  of  non-mediated \n\npower  may  have  conflicting  effects  on  instrumental  relationship  commitment  and  hence \n\nresearchers need to consider the individual effects of different bases of non-mediated power. \n\nMoreover, we find that reward, coercive and legal legitimate power do not significantly affect \n\nnormative relationship  commitment,  whereas Brown et  al. (1995) find that mediated power \n\nsignificantly reduces normative relationship commitment. Brown et al. (1995) also investigate \n\nthe moderating effect of symmetry of power and find that a supplier’s use of mediated power \n\ndoes  not  significantly  affect  normative  relational  commitment  when  the  supplier  is  more \n\npowerful. We focus on a manufacturer’s major customers and hence the customers usually are \n\nmore powerful. Thus, the result supports Brown et al.’s (1995) finding on the moderating effect \n\nof symmetry of power. The finding suggests that researchers should consider the moderating \n\neffect of symmetry of power when investigating the impacts of mediated power."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We find that the impact of non-mediated power  on normative relationship commitment and \n\nthat  of  mediated  power  on  instrumental  relationship  commitment  in  the  United  States  are \n\nconsistent with the findings of Brown et al. (1995). Moreover, the results show that expert and \n\nlegitimate power are negatively associated with whereas referent power is positively associated \n\nwith  instrumental  relationship  commitment,  which  provides  a  possible  explanation  of  the \n\nfinding  of  the  insignificant  effect  of  non-mediated  power  on  instrumental  relationship \n\ncommitment  (Brown  et  al.,  1995).  The  result  reveals  that  different  bases  of  non-mediated \n\npower  may  have  conflicting  effects  on  instrumental  relationship  commitment  and  hence \n\nresearchers need to consider the individual effects of different bases of non-mediated power. \n\nMoreover, we find that reward, coercive and legal legitimate power do not significantly affect \n\nnormative relationship  commitment,  whereas Brown et  al. (1995) find that mediated power \n\nsignificantly reduces normative relationship commitment."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The US sample directly tests the power-commitment findings of Brown et al.; the dedicated comparison states both agreements and a contrast for mediated power and normative commitment. The later moderation comparison supports one part, not an overall verdict.""}"
