istic regression Model 6C specifically to re-test the published moderation finding of Turri (2016, Experiment 7) that attributing true beliefs to luck rather than ability moderates Gettier intuitions (ηp2 = .353), explicitly contrasting its null moderation of the Gettier-vs.-knowledge difference with Turri (2016, 2017). The Reference list confirms the exact identity: Turri, J. (2016), 'Vision, knowledge, and assertion.', Consciousness and Cognition, 41, 41–49, DOI 10.1016/j.concog.2016.01.004. The only shipped row (input_row_index 277, confirmed in prior adjudication) is Turri, Buckwalter, and Blouw (2015, online 2014; 10.3758/s13423-014-0683-5), 'Knowledge and luck' in Psychonomic Bulletin & Review, and sibling target 2 is Turri (2017; 10.1093/analys/anx036) in Analysis; all three are distinct empirical articles reporting separate studies, so report equivalence does not apply."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0045_5f752faa16b30af3"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Turri, J."", ""year"": ""2016"", ""title"": ""Vision, knowledge, and assertion."", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.concog.2016.01.004"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""Experiment 7"", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Next, we explored whether attributions of outcomes to luck versus ability influence knowledge attributions, as suggested by prior research (Turri, 2016, 2017)."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Thus, unlike in previous research (Turri, 2016, 2017), luck attributions did not decrease the likelihood of participants demonstrating Gettier intuitions."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Explicit comparisons to the stated original finding determine the verdict.""}"
