) and Discussion ('Luck attributions'), the authors empirically tested whether attributing a protagonist's true belief to luck vs. ability moderates Gettier intuitions and explicitly compared their finding ('unlike in previous research (Turri, 2016, 2017), luck attributions did not decrease the likelihood of participants demonstrating Gettier intuitions') against the finding attributed to Turri (2017). The Reference list confirms the exact identity: Turri, J. (2017), 'Knowledge attributions in iterated fake barn cases.', Analysis, 77(1), 104–115, DOI 10.1093/analys/anx036. This article is an independent publication distinct from shipped row 277 (Turri, Buckwalter, & Blouw, 2014/2015; 10.3758/s13423-014-0683-5) and from sibling target 1 (Turri, 2016; 10.1016/j.concog.2016.01.004), with no report equivalence."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0045_5f752faa16b30af3"", ""target_index"": 2, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Turri, J."", ""year"": ""2017"", ""title"": ""Knowledge attributions in iterated fake barn cases."", ""doi"": ""10.1093/analys/anx036"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Next, we explored whether attributions of outcomes to luck versus ability influence knowledge attributions, as suggested by prior research (Turri, 2016, 2017)."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Thus, unlike in previous research (Turri, 2016, 2017), luck attributions did not decrease the likelihood of participants demonstrating Gettier intuitions."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Explicit comparisons to the stated original finding determine the verdict.""}"
10.1080/01973533.2020.1806845,da281f858e509152,0,10.1006/jesp.2001.1491,Reducing the effects of stereotype threat on African American college students by shaping theories of intelligence.,"Aronson, J., Fried, C. B., & Good, C.",2002,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 0, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""Growth mindset interventions have been effective at improving students’grades and/or GPA (Aronson et al., 2002; Yeager, Walton, et al., 2016), increasing student retention (Yeager, Walton, et al., 2016), and enjoyment and/or engagement in academics (Aronson et al., 2002)."", ""location"": ""introduction (p. 2)"", ""reason"": ""Wrong original by blind coder (no omitted target): Aronson et al. (2002) is cited only in multi-work literature review sentences in the Introduction alongside six other studies and is never mentioned in the Methods, Results, or Discussion. Under the codebook, a work cited for background or motivation without the paper re-testing its specific finding is not a replication target.""}","{""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The proposition that a one-time intervention at the postsecondary level will result in long-term measurable student outcomes was not supported in the present study."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors conclude the college intervention does not improve academic outcomes; their new university sample re-tests prior college effects."", ""authors"": ""Aronson, J., Fried, C. B., & Good, C."", ""year"": ""2002"", ""title"": ""Reducing the effects of stereotype threat on African American college students by shaping theories of intelligence."", ""doi"": ""10.1006/jesp.2001.1491"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Growth mindset interventions have been effective at improving students’grades and/or GPA (Aronson et al., 2002; Yeager, Walton, et al., 2016), increasing student retention (Yeager, Walton, et al., 2016), and enjoyment and/or engagement in academics (Aronson et al., 2002).""}"
10.1080/01973533.2020.1806845,da281f858e509152,1,10.1073/pnas.1524360113,Teaching a lay theory before college narrows achievement gaps at scale.,"Yeager, D. S., Walton, G. M., Brady, S. T., Akcinar, E. N., Paunesku, D., Keane, L., Kamentz, D., Ritter, G., Duckworth, A. L., Urstein, R., Gomez, E. M., Markus, H. R., Cohen, G. L., & Dweck, C. S.",2016,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""The research design followed protocols used in earlier mindset intervention research; however, the student population involved in the present study had not been addressed in the previous studies. Additionally, we included first-time college students, a time of transition when interventions are purported to be especially applicable (Yeager, Walton, et al., 2016). Despite previous findings showing the effectiveness of a one-time intervention for this transition time (Yeager & Walton, 2011), our data did not support this conclusion."", ""location"": ""discussion (p. 5)"", ""reason"": ""Wrong original by blind coder (no omitted target): Yeager, Walton, et al. (2016) is not cited in Materials and Methods (where only Blackwell et al., 2007 is cited for the intervention), and three of the five academic terms of data collection (Spring 2015, Fall 2015, and Spring 2016, p. 3) took place before Yeager, Walton, et al. (2016) was published. In the Introduction and Discussion it is cited only as broader literature context on college transition and disadvantaged student populations.""}","{""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The proposition that a one-time intervention at the postsecondary level will result in long-term measurable student outcomes was not supported in the present study."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors conclude the college intervention does not improve academic outcomes; their new university sample re-tests prior college effects."", ""authors"": ""Yeager, D. S., Walton, G. M., Brady, S. T., Akcinar, E. N., Paunesku, D., Keane, L., Kamentz, D., Ritter, G., Duckworth, A. L., Urstein, R., Gomez, E. M., Markus, H. R., Cohen, G. L., & Dweck, C. S."", ""year"": ""2016"", ""title"": ""Teaching a lay theory before college narrows achievement gaps at scale."", ""doi"": ""10.1073/pnas.1524360113"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Additionally, we included first-time college students, a time of transition when interventions are purported to be especially applicable (Yeager, Walton, et al., 2016).""}"
10.32872/cpe.v2i4.2593,8ac348d62cc5f33c,1,10.1080/02699931.2016.1154817,The beneficial and detrimental effects of major depression on intuitive decision-making.,"Remmers C., Topolinski S., Buxton A., Dietrich D. E., Michalak J.",2017,full_text,,error,obvious,"{""field"": ""missing_row"", ""target_index"": 1, ""method"": ""obvious"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Remmers C., Topolinski S., Buxton A., Dietrich D. E., Michalak J."", ""year"": ""2017"", ""title"": ""The beneficial and detrimental effects of major depression on intuitive decision-making."", ""doi"": ""10.1080/02699931.2016.1154817"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined""}, ""quote"": ""The first hypothesis (H1) refers to the replicability of recently found intuition deficits in depression ( Remmers et al., 2015 ; Remmers et al., 2017 ). We hypothesized that patients with an acute episode of major depression are less able to intuitively discriminate semantic coherence from semantic incoherence in the JSCT than healthy controls. "", ""location"": ""Independent parent primary-source check; exhausted_candidate_0056.parent.json offsets"", ""reason"": ""Parent independently read H1, the new clinical/control Methods and own Results, plus the complete printed reference. Remmers2017 is explicitly re-tested alongside Remmers2015; the former has its own full title, coauthor Buxton and different findings/publication, rather than an online/print variant of the shipped2014/2015 paper. Existing295 represents only Remmers2015. This source-confirmed extra empirical original has no unrequested outcome assigned.""}","{""authors"": ""Remmers C., Topolinski S., Buxton A., Dietrich D. E., Michalak J."", ""year"": ""2017"", ""title"": ""The beneficial and detrimental effects of major depression on intuitive decision-making."", ""doi"": ""10.1080/02699931.2016.1154817"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The first hypothesis (H1) refers to the replicability of recently found intuition deficits in depression ( Remmers et al., 2015 ; Remmers et al., 2017 )."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Overall the current study did not replicate findings from previous research regarding intuitive semantic coherence detection deficits in depression."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The overall deficit replication verdict covers the two originals explicitly named for H1.""}"
10.32872/cpe.v2i4.2593,8ac348d62cc5f33c,2,10.1037/a0014678,The architecture of intuition: Fluency and affect determine intuitive judgments of semantic and visual coherence and judgments of grammaticality in artificial grammar learning.,"Topolinski S., Strack F.",2009,full_text,,error,obvious,"{""field"": ""missing_row"", ""target_index"": 2, ""method"": ""obvious"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Topolinski S., Strack F."", ""year"": ""2009"", ""title"": ""The architecture of intuition: Fluency and affect determine intuitive judgments of semantic and visual coherence and judgments of grammaticality in artificial grammar learning."", ""doi"": ""10.1037/a0014678"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined""}, ""quote"": ""Results did not reveal any effect of our fluency manipulation, and thus our hypotheses regarding fluency (H2) were not supported. As such, the current study could not replicate previous results that bolstered the fluency model proposed by Topolinski and Strack (2009a , 2009b ). "", ""location"": ""Independent parent primary-source check; exhausted_candidate_0056.parent.json offsets"", ""reason"": ""Parent independently inspected pre-registered H2contrast manipulation, H3positive/negative triads, AppendixB and exact references. The new experiment actively tests the fluency/coherence finding and expressly compares its own failure with results of both Topolinski/Strack2009a and2009b; H3 also specifically retests2009aExperiment8. The two separately titled empirical papers in different journals are distinguished in this source, both absent from shipped295Remmers2015; shared authors/year do not collapse the two publications. The identity is source-confirmed; no outcome beyond this missing-pair decision is assigned.""}","{""authors"": ""Topolinski S., Strack F."", ""year"": ""2009"", ""title"": ""The architecture of intuition: Fluency and affect determine intuitive judgments of semantic and visual coherence and judgments of grammaticality in artificial grammar learning."", ""doi"": ""10.1037/a0014678"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""Experiment 8 (valence); fluency experiment unspecified"", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Building up on basic research ( Topolinski & Strack, 2009a ; Experiment 8) showing that healthy subjects are more likely to judge triads to be coherent that consist of positive as compared to negative words, we expected that this effect would also emerge for depressed patients."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Results did not reveal any effect of our fluency manipulation, and thus our hypotheses regarding fluency (H2) were not supported. As such, the current study could not replicate previous results that bolstered the fluency model proposed by Topolinski and Strack (2009a , 2009b )."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Fluency comparison fails, while positive-valence comparison agrees. The fluency passage explicitly contrasts prior results. Additional agreement passage: The results further showed that positive (vs. negative) valence triggered coherence judgments (H3a) and that this effect was – in contrast to our hypothesis (H3b) – not moderated by depression.""}"
