e, while fabricated current author/DOI/sample/section claims are expressly rejected; immutable provider replies retained. This factual correction does not change blind coding or create a new model attempt."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0057_7399924f3ac7dc01"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Childers, Jane B & Tomasello, Michael."", ""year"": ""2001"", ""title"": ""The role of pronouns in young children’s acquisition of the English transitive construction."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The present study differs from Childers and Tomasello (2001) because only proforms such as he were provided in the noun phrases during the training session."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Hypothesis 2 was also disproved. The results showed that input consistency played a negative role for adult L2 construction learning."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""This paper explicitly retests the input-consistency benefit; the reported effect does not agree.""}"
10.1515/iral-2012-0001,7399924f3ac7dc01,2,,Constructions as categories of language: The role of order on construction learning.,"Goldberg, Adele E., Casenhiser, Devin & White, Tiffani.",2007,full_text,,error,obvious,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Goldberg, Adele E., Casenhiser, Devin & White, Tiffani."", ""doi"": """", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Constructions as categories of language: The role of order on construction learning."", ""year"": ""2007""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""7399924f3ac7dc01"", ""location"": ""Section 4 (Purpose and hypotheses of the present study), Hypothesis 3 & Hypothesis 5; Sections 6.4–6.5; Sections 8.3–8.4, line 1"", ""method"": ""obvious"", ""quote"": ""Goldberg et al. (2007) showed that initially providing skewed input facilitated rapid construction generalization. The present study investigates this effect for the position of verbs with relative high token frequency. Relative here denotes that the high token frequency of verbs is adjusted for the experimental groups (four times for +the skewed group or twice for the −skewed group). We expect significant positive effects for the forward order of frequent verbs and positive main effects for order of frequent verbs."", ""reason"": ""Parent independently verified the primary source and exact empirical relationship. Actual Nakamura2012doi10.1515/iral-2012-0001 primary abstract reports137adultL2learners, not72as expert reason says; headings are5.2.2/5.2.3and8.2/8.3. Parent independently read exact distinct input-consistency and high-frequency-verb-order hypotheses, manipulated factorial conditions, Results and original-specific Discussion. Childers/Tomasello2001pronoun-consistency finding andGoldberg/Casenhiser/White2007presentation-order finding are actively retested separately from shipped299Casenhiser/Goldberg2005skewed-input finding. Original citation identities are in complete references; newpopulation/pronounchanges qualify directness, not empirical eligibility. Both genuine missingpairs; no unrequested outcomes inferred. Initial expert original-identity conclusion corroborated from primary evidence, while fabricated current author/DOI/sample/section claims are expressly rejected; immutable provider replies retained. This factual correction does not change blind coding or create a new model attempt."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0057_7399924f3ac7dc01"", ""target_index"": 2, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Goldberg, Adele E., Casenhiser, Devin & White, Tiffani."", ""year"": ""2007"", ""title"": ""Constructions as categories of language: The role of order on construction learning."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Goldberg et al. (2007) showed that initially providing skewed input facilitated rapid construction generalization. The present study investigates this effect for the position of verbs with relative high token frequency."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""There was a significant main effect for order of frequent verbs in the comprehension of the APPEARANCE construction. Figure 1 shows that the (+forward order) group outperformed the (−forward skewing) group. No significant effects were found in the guided production of the APPEARANCE construction and the comprehension of the Samoan ergative construction."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors present an agreeing comprehension effect and explicitly absent effects in the other tasks; no overall verdict supersedes this comparison.""}"
10.1037/edu0000141,5a644ad030ba92a5,1,10.3102/0162373715581709,An Effort to Close Achievement Gaps at Scale through Self-Affirmation.,"Borman, G. D., Grigg, J., & Hanselman, P.",2016,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Borman, G. D., Grigg, J., & Hanselman, P."", ""doi"": ""10.3102/0162373715581709"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""An Effort to Close Achievement Gaps at Scale through Self-Affirmation."", ""year"": ""2016""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""5a644ad030ba92a5"", ""location"": ""The Current Study, lines 345-346"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""The current study was designed to replicate both the original self-affirmation study\n(Cohen et al., 2006) and the previous successful independent replication (Borman et al., 2016)."", ""reason"": ""Neither prior_adjudication_path nor prior_state_path exists on disk (never adjudicated). Hanselman, Rozek, Grigg, & Borman (2017) explicitly state (e.g., lines 345-346, 355-359, 681-686, 868-928) that their study was designed to replicate both the original self-affirmation study by Cohen et al. (2006) (shipped row 301, 10.1126/science.1128317, coded at target_index 0) and the previous independent scale-up replication in the same school district by Borman, Grigg, & Hanselman (2016) ('An Effort to Close Achievement Gaps at Scale through Self-Affirmation', Educational Evaluation and Policy Analysis, 38(1), 21-42, 10.3102/0162373715581709; References lines 1076-1078). Using a new randomized trial in Cohort 2 (N = 1,269 seventh-grade students across all 11 district middle schools, including 449 Black and Hispanic students), the authors retest the district-wide self-affirmation GPA and achievement effects reported in Cohort 1 by Borman et al. (2016) and formally test for treatment-effect differences across the two cohorts (Table 2, Table 3, and Section 'Comparison to Previous Cohort', lines 868-928). Borman et al. (2016) is a distinct primary empirical paper from Cohen et al. (2006) and is absent from shipped_rows."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0042_5a644ad030ba92a5"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Borman, G. D., Grigg, J., & Hanselman, P."", ""year"": ""2016"", ""title"": ""An Effort to Close Achievement Gaps at Scale through Self-Affirmation."", ""doi"": ""10.3102/0162373715581709"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The current study was designed to replicate both the original self-affirmation study\n(Cohen et al., 2006) and the previous successful independent replication (Borman et al., 2016)."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We found no evidence of\neffects in this replication study and estimates were precise enough to reject benefits larger than\nan effect size of 0.10. These null effects were significantly different from persistent benefits in\nthe prior study in the same setting, and extensive testing revealed that currently theorized\nmoderators of self-affirmation effects could not explain the difference."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The new cohort produced no benefits; the paper explicitly describes its failure to replicate. Previously reported cohort results are the comparison, not new successful replications.""}"
10.1080/00221546.2019.1650582,47a8be34f8916560,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,"{""corrected_value"": null, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""47a8be34f8916560"", ""location"": ""Abstract (line 5) of 47a8be34f8916560.txt"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Though low-cost value affirmation interventions have been shown to reduce achievement gaps in some classroom settings, these findings have not been consistently replicated. In this study, we test the efficacy of this intervention among a new sample of students enrolled in a community college in the Midwest."", ""reason"": ""The primary source file (47a8be34f8916560.txt, Baker, Skinner, & Redding, DOI 10.1080/00221546.2019.1650582) contains only the title and a 6-sentence abstract with no in-text citations, methods/results comparisons, or reference list. While the abstract states that low-cost value affirmation interventions have been shown to reduce achievement gaps in some classroom settings and tests the efficacy of this intervention in a new community college sample, it does not name any specific original study. Consequently, it cannot be verified from the available source text which specific published work is empirically retested or whether the unnamed candidate in target_index 0 is already represented by shipped row 302 (Cohen, Garcia, Apfel, & Master, 2006, Science, DOI 10.1126/science.1128317) versus a distinct omitted original study. Prior adjudication and state files were checked and do not exist."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0033_47a8be34f8916560"", ""target_index"": 0, ""verdict"": ""undeterminable""}","{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Though low-cost value affirmation interventions have been shown to reduce achievement gaps in some classroom settings, these findings have not been consistently replicated. In this study, we test the efficacy of this intervention among a new sample of students enrolled in a community college in the Midwest."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Overall, we find little evidence of a positive effect of this one-time affirmation of social identity."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Overall verdict indicates little evidence for the intervention effect. Abstract does not identify the original paper; no citation guessed.""}"
10.1371/journal.pone.0220419,d54c6bf1f5190f00,0,,"Interfering with theories of sleep and memory: sleep, declarative memory, and associative interference.","Ellenbogen JM, Hulbert JC, Stickgold R, et al.",2006,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Ellenbogen JM, Hulbert JC, Stickgold R, et al."", ""year"": ""2006"", ""title"": ""Interfering with theories of sleep and memory: sleep, declarative memory, and associative interference."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""However, two studies by Ellenbogen, et al. [\n8\n,\n9\n] have been by far the most cited and most influential evidence for sleep’s proposed role in protecting against interference. These studies form the basis for the present replication."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""However, we did not replicate Ellenbogen et al.’s observation of a large effect of sleep in protecting memories against interference learning. Instead, we observed that the Sleep and Wake groups both experienced a significant and very similar retroactive interference effect."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The central protective effect failed; title and abstract give the overall failure verdict.""}"
10.1371/journal.pone.0220419,d54c6bf1f5190f00,2,,Scopolamine does not impact declarative and motor memory consolidation across a night of sleep or a day of wakefulness.,"Tucker MA, Taylor K, Merchant R, et al.",2018,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Tucker MA, Taylor K, Merchant R, et al."", ""year"": ""2018"", ""title"": ""Scopolamine does not impact declarative and motor memory consolidation across a night of sleep or a day of wakefulness."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We have also shown that increased intrinsic motivation (the desire to do well even in the absence of extrinsic rewards), measured by responses to items from the Intrinsic Motivation Inventory [\n27\n] used in the present study, correlated with better acquisition of visual paired associates (picture-object pairs) at training, and also with change in performance from training to retest (retention) [\n26\n]."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In the present study, we observed a similar positive correlation between intrinsic motivation and training and retest performance. However, motivation did not correlate with memory consolidation (change in performance from training to retest), nor did intrinsic motivation favor performance in the sleep (v. wake) participants."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The separately hypothesized re-test of a published intrinsic-motivation finding agrees on acquisition but explicitly differs on consolidation.""}"
10.31219/osf.io/x394e_v3,78fb9fef3070ea73,1,10.1037/a0021971,Hindsight bias from 3 to 95 years of age.,"Bernstein, D. M., Erdfelder, E., Meltzoff, A. N., Peria, W., & Loftus, G. R.",2011,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""We conducted an open data and open material pre-registered replication of Experiment 2 from Fischhoff (1975) with extensions investigating the associations between the hindsight bias with sociodemographic variables and depressive symptoms, and the moderation role of depressive symptoms on hindsight bias."", ""location"": ""introduction"", ""reason"": ""Wrong original: the paper only replicates Experiment 2 of Fischhoff (1975), while Bernstein et al. (2011) is cited only as background literature motivating the novel extension hypothesis on age.""}","{""authors"": ""Bernstein, D. M., Erdfelder, E., Meltzoff, A. N., Peria, W., & Loftus, G. R."", ""year"": ""2011"", ""title"": ""Hindsight bias from 3 to 95 years of age."", ""doi"": ""10.1037/a0021971"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""For the extensions, we predict that age will positively correlate with hindsight bias, whereas other socio-demographic variables (such as socioeconomic status, gender, occupation, and education) will not (Hypothesis 2). This investigation will provide further evidence for the claim that populations of older ages are more susceptible to hindsight bias (Bernstein et al., 2011; Groß & Bayen, 2021; Pohl et al., 2018), whereas evidence for gender, occupation, and education is not consolidated yet."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We expected to find a positive correlation between age and hindsight bias, and no correlation with other socio-demographic variables. Also, we expected to find a positive correlation between depressive symptoms and hindsight bias. Contrary to our expectations, no correlation was found between hindsight bias and sociodemographic or depressive symptoms. These results contrast with previous findings showing a higher magnitude of hindsight bias for older adults (Groß, 2013) and a positive correlation between depressive symptoms and hindsight bias (Groß et al., 2017; Groß & Bayen, 2017)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Specific earlier finding is explicitly tested and the authors report its absence. Differences in design do not remove the comparison.""}"
10.31219/osf.io/x394e_v3,78fb9fef3070ea73,2,10.3758/s13421-021-01195-w,Older and younger adults’ hindsight bias after positive and negative outcomes.,"Groß, J., & Bayen, U. J.",2021,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 2, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""We conducted an open data and open material pre-registered replication of Experiment 2 from Fischhoff (1975) with extensions investigating the associations between the hindsight bias with sociodemographic variables and depressive symptoms, and the moderation role of depressive symptoms on hindsight bias."", ""location"": ""introduction"", ""reason"": ""Wrong original: the paper only replicates Experiment 2 of Fischhoff (1975), while Groß and Bayen (2021) is cited only as background literature motivating the novel extension hypotheses on age and depressive symptoms.""}","{""authors"": ""Groß, J., & Bayen, U. J."", ""year"": ""2021"", ""title"": ""Older and younger adults’ hindsight bias after positive and negative outcomes."", ""doi"": ""10.3758/s13421-021-01195-w"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""For the extensions, we predict that age will positively correlate with hindsight bias, whereas other socio-demographic variables (such as socioeconomic status, gender, occupation, and education) will not (Hypothesis 2). This investigation will provide further evidence for the claim that populations of older ages are more susceptible to hindsight bias (Bernstein et al., 2011; Groß & Bayen, 2021; Pohl et al., 2018), whereas evidence for gender, occupation, and education is not consolidated yet. Also, we predict that depressive symptoms will be positively correlated with hindsight bias (Hypothesis 3), thus suggesting an interaction between the cognitive mechanisms in depression and the magnitude of the hindsight bias. Finally, considering the previous evidence of higher levels of hindsight bias associated with higher levels of depressive symptoms (Groß et al., 2017; Groß & Bayen, 2021; Haslam & Jayasinghe, 1995), we expect that depressive symptoms will act as a moderator variable of the hindsight bias effect (Hypothesis 4)."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We expected to find a positive correlation between age and hindsight bias, and no correlation with other socio-demographic variables. Also, we expected to find a positive correlation between depressive symptoms and hindsight bias. Contrary to our expectations, no correlation was found between hindsight bias and sociodemographic or depressive symptoms. These results contrast with previous findings showing a higher magnitude of hindsight bias for older adults (Groß, 2013) and a positive correlation between depressive symptoms and hindsight bias (Groß et al., 2017; Groß & Bayen, 2017)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Specific earlier finding is explicitly tested and the authors report its absence. Differences in design do not remove the comparison.""}"
