10.1016/s0163-6383(83)80012-5,592d372d41a77ec8,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,"{""corrected_value"": null, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""592d372d41a77ec8"", ""location"": ""Abstract, line 4"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""The purpose of the present research was to replicate two experiments in which neonatal imitation was reported. In replicating the first experiment tongue- and lip-protrusion, mouth-opening, and sequential finger movement were modeled for six 2-week-old babies."", ""reason"": ""Neither prior_adjudication_path nor prior_state_path exists on disk (never adjudicated). The supplied source text (592d372d41a77ec8.txt) contains only the 4-line title, authors, and abstract of Koepke, Hamm, Legerstee, & Russell (1983) ('Neonatal imitation: Two failures to replicate', 10.1016/s0163-6383(83)80012-5). The abstract states that the research replicated two experiments in which neonatal imitation was reported (the first modeling tongue- and lip-protrusion, mouth-opening, and sequential finger movement for six 2-week-old babies; the second modeling mouth-opening and tongue-protrusion for 14 babies aged 17-21 days) and that both replications failed to yield evidence of neonatal imitation. However, the abstract never names or cites the original authors, year, or paper(s). While shipped row 319 is Meltzoff & Moore (1977) ('Imitation of Facial and Manual Gestures by Human Neonates', Science, 10.1126/science.198.4312.75), which reported two experiments matching these procedures, the abstract text alone cannot verify the target identity or rule out whether both replicated experiments come solely from shipped row 319 versus an additional unshipped paper."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0042_592d372d41a77ec8"", ""target_index"": 0, ""verdict"": ""undeterminable""}","{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""The purpose of the present research was to replicate two experiments in which neonatal imitation was reported."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The results of both replications failed to yield evidence of neonatal imitation and thus raise questions about the neonate’s capacity to imitate."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract gives an explicit verdict covering both attempts, but names neither original. This unnamed entry records the unresolved target relationship; the number of distinct original papers cannot be established from abstract evidence.""}"
10.2307/2586310,b91a64486d8bd684,1,,,"MacKuen, Erikson, and Stimson",1992,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""undeterminable"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""MacKuen, Erikson, and Stimson (1989, 1992) argue that the aggregate distribution of party identification, or macropartisanship, shifts significantly over short periods in response to changes in presidential popularity and consumer sentiment. Their results are based on a subset of Gallup surveys available from 1953 to 1988 and only those CBS/New York Timessurveys conducted during the Reagan administration. We replicate this analysis using a more extensive inventory of Gallup and CBS/New York Timesdata and find considerably less evidence of partisan fluctuation. The amount of partisan change caused by short-term movements in consumer sentiment and presidential popularity is found to be two to three times smaller than initially reported by MacKuen et al. (1989)."", ""location"": ""abstract"", ""reason"": ""The abstract jointly cites MacKuen, Erikson, and Stimson (1989, 1992) before referring to '[t]heir results' and stating '[w]e replicate this analysis', while explicitly quantifying the comparison only against MacKuen et al. (1989). Because only the abstract is available and the full text and reference list are unseen, the supplied source neither establishes that the 1992 study is independently re-tested nor proves its exclusion from the full-text replication.""}","{""authors"": ""MacKuen, Erikson, and Stimson"", ""year"": ""1992"", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""MacKuen, Erikson, and Stimson (1989, 1992) argue that the aggregate distribution of party identification, or macropartisanship, shifts significantly over short periods in response to changes in presidential popularity and consumer sentiment. We replicate this analysis using a more extensive inventory of Gallup and CBS/New York Timesdata and find considerably less evidence of partisan fluctuation."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The amount of partisan change caused by short-term movements in consumer sentiment and presidential popularity is found to be two to three times smaller than initially reported by MacKuen et al. (1989). Our results indicate that macropartisanship adjusts to short-term shocks in a limited and gradual fashion, consistent with traditional views of partisan realignment."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract describes both cited originals as reporting responsiveness to these inputs and reports responsiveness in the same direction but smaller. Reduced magnitude alone does not imply mixed. Full-text verdict and reference entries unavailable.""}"
10.1080/0003684042000229532,236d200a82c3a67f,1,,"Career mobility, job match, and overeducation",Robst J.,1995,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Robst J."", ""year"": ""1995"", ""title"": ""Career mobility, job match, and overeducation"", ""doi"": """", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""successful""}, ""quote"": ""In Section 4.3, we extend Robst’s\n\n(1995) test for robustness by using an objective, as well as a subjective, measure of\n\ntraining participation. Both measures confirm Robst’s findings that overeducated\n\n(undereducated) workers indeed receive less (more) training than correctly allocated\n\nones."", ""location"": {""start_line"": 190, ""end_line"": 198, ""form_feed_page"": 4}, ""reason"": ""Omitted target in shipped data. Beyond citing Robst (1995) as background, the paper explicitly re-tests Robst's (1995) empirical finding on training participation in Section 4.3 (Tables 5 and 6) using the same subjective survey question in the German GSOEP sample as well as an objective formal training indicator, concluding that both measures confirm Robst's findings."", ""quote_provenance"": {""kind"": ""new_independent_literal_primary_quotation"", ""inspection_result"": ""evidence_followup/integration/strict_literal_quote_inspection.result.json"", ""issue_index"": 160, ""source_path"": ""/home/lukas/.t3/worktrees/fred-data/audit-flora-blind-2026-10/audit/flora_blind_2026-10/texts/236d200a82c3a67f.txt"", ""source_sha256"": ""d965af0966f6a1bdcc7504a2b045040b46bd1389883fbb825b710fad618970a1"", ""raw_start_offset"": 8080, ""raw_end_offset"": 8375, ""scientific_verdict_unchanged"": true, ""reason"": ""Parent verified exact raw slice and semantic identity with the earlier accepted passage under its recorded display-normalisation policy. Independent primary-context inspector supported this passage. Original provider wording and whole prior decision retained in history; raw provider reply unchanged.""}}","{""authors"": ""Robst J."", ""year"": ""1995"", ""title"": ""Career mobility, job match, and overeducation"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In Section 4.3, we extend Robst’s (1995) test for robustness by using an objective, as well as a subjective, measure of training participation."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Both measures confirm Robst’s findings that overeducated (undereducated) workers indeed receive less (more) training than correctly allocated ones."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""introduction"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true}"
10.1016/j.rasd.2013.02.015,6c43f57b16329dce,0,,,Reichow and Wolery,,abstract,,correct,argon,"{""corrected_value"": null, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""6c43f57b16329dce"", ""location"": ""Abstract, line 3"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Only one published study has focused specifically on the effectiveness of IF for children with autism, and demonstrated that the inclusion of IF resulted in more efficient teaching during 1:1 instruction for four preschoolers with autism/developmental delay. The current pair of studies seeks to replicate and extend the findings of Reichow and Wolery."", ""reason"": ""Prior adjudication and state files were checked and do not exist (never-adjudicated work). The supplied source text is an abstract and publisher highlight only. The abstract states that only one published study focused specifically on the effectiveness of instructive feedback (IF) for children with autism (four preschoolers during 1:1 instruction) and that the current pair of studies seeks to replicate and extend the findings of Reichow and Wolery. This single target study is already represented by shipped row 329 (Reichow & Wolery, 2011, 'COMPARISON OF PROGRESSIVE PROMPT DELAY WITH AND WITHOUT INSTRUCTIVE FEEDBACK', 10.1901/jaba.2011.44-327); the blind coder lacked the reference list in the abstract-only source and therefore recorded only the author surnames."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0052_6c43f57b16329dce"", ""target_index"": 0, ""verdict"": ""shipped_right""}","{""authors"": ""Reichow and Wolery"", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""The current pair of studies seeks to replicate and extend the findings of Reichow and Wolery."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Results were mixed and do not match previously published robust findings."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""fulltext"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Publisher highlight explicitly states mixed results relative to earlier robust findings. Abstract describes efficient acquisition for only one of four participants and no extension to dyad instruction. Bibliographic fields unavailable in abstract are left blank; in-text author form retained.""}"
10.1016/j.jml.2018.07.004,a58db421709e9509,1,,Contrasting intrusion profiles for agreement and anaphora: Experimental and modeling evidence.,"Dillon, B., Mishler, A., Sloggett, S., & Phillips, C.",2013,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""In the number agreement experiments, study 1 is the ungrammatical agreement data from Experiment 1 of Dillon et al. (2013); studies 2-5 are the experiments reported in Lago et al. (2015), and 6-10 are from Wagers et al. (2009). Studies 11 and 12 are from Cunnings and Sturt (2018). The estimates shown in the figure were computed by fitting a linear mixed model (with full covariance matrices for random effects) in Stan using log-transformed reading times, and then by back-transforming the estimate of the facilitation effect to milliseconds. Our estimates may be slightly different from the original published estimates in some cases because we did not remove any data."", ""location"": ""Appendix C, p. 72"", ""reason"": ""Wrong original / insufficient evidence from source: Dillon et al. (2013) is one of 12 datasets (10 from the prior meta-analysis of Jäger et al., 2017) plotted in the General Discussion and Appendix C merely as an incidental background illustration that wide credible intervals are common in psycholinguistics, rather than a central target the paper set out to test. Moreover, even in the blind coding, computational reproducibility was 'not checked' and substantive robustness for this specific study is 'cannot_be_determined' (the text reports only pooled summary ranges and an unattributed count across the 10 studies). Under the audit rules, incidental re-estimations with 'not checked' and 'cannot_be_determined' outcomes establish no omitted-target error.""}","{""authors"": ""Dillon, B., Mishler, A., Sloggett, S., & Phillips, C."", ""year"": ""2013"", ""title"": ""Contrasting intrusion profiles for agreement and anaphora: Experimental and modeling evidence."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""Experiment 1"", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In the number agreement experiments, study 1 is the ungrammatical agreement data from Experiment 1 of Dillon et al. (2013); studies 2-5 are the experiments reported in Lago et al. (2015), and 6-10 are from Wagers et al. (2009)."", ""outcome"": """", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""fulltext"", ""outcome_computation"": ""not checked"", ""outcome_computational_quote"": ""Our estimates may be slightly different from the original published estimates in some cases because we did not remove any data."", ""outcome_robustness"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Original data were reanalysed with alternative models/inclusion rules. No target-specific substantive robustness verdict can be assigned from the pooled presentation; numerical reproduction was not the analysis goal.""}"
