10.46867/ijcp.2018.31.01.13,12db194db4781556,0,,‘Mind-reading’ mammals: Attribution of belief tasks with dolphins.,"Tschudin, A.",2001,full_text,competing_supported_matches,correct,obvious,"{""field"": ""missing_row"", ""target_index"": 0, ""method"": ""obvious"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""In 2012, our research team initiated an effort to replicate Tschudin’s follow-up experiments. The goal\nwas to replicate as much of Tschudin’s methodology as could be determined from the summary in his 2006\npaper given that the training protocol used to teach the dolphins, the various aspects of the task, and the timeline\nit took to prepare the dolphins for the task, were not provided (Table 3). To fill any gaps in Tschudin’s\nmethodology, we followed the descriptions for the standardized testing procedure presented in Call and\nTomasello (1999), including limiting the number of FBTs presented so as to control for the effect of experience."", ""location"": ""CaseStudyMethods, closest Tschudin2006 follow-up; native0015finalscope"", ""reason"": ""Final independent scope review and primary methods establish a single closest target: Tschudin2006follow-up with added controls. Tschudin2001is an earlier link whose unstated procedural details fill gaps; it is not independently empirically re-tested as a second original. Row349is corrected to2006target1. Exclude obsolete2001target0from additional missing references, without deleting either blind target or prior sources. Not based merely on publication year or shared authors.""}","{""authors"": ""Tschudin, A."", ""year"": ""2001"", ""title"": ""‘Mind-reading’ mammals: Attribution of belief tasks with dolphins."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""When we designed our FBT study, our goal was to replicate the study conducted by Tschudin (2001,\n2006) using the same methodology. For this reason, we did not seek to modify or improve the methodology."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""After a total of nine months with over 1000 training and probe trials, the dolphin never achieved the\nstandard criterion set for acceptable performance (Table 3, Figure 1). Despite all of these trials, trying to train\na concept- naïve dolphin resulted in a failure to demonstrate competency at invisible displacement of a fish in\ncontainers and to provide experimental evidence of ToM using the FBT."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors report failed preliminary performance and failed false-belief trials. They critique validity but do not explicitly declare this attempt incapable of adjudication. Both cited Tschudin works are explicitly named as replication targets; Call and Tomasello supplies protocol details and is not a separately retested original.""}"
10.1016/j.learninstruc.2018.10.002,f62411717f6082b5,0,,,,2008,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": ""2008"", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""3"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Lastly, in Experiment 3, we attempted to replicate the work of Palm (2008) who was able to triple the number of realistic responses by creating problems with a richer backstory and providing more detail. Our sample of sixth-grade students failed to show an increase in realistic responses to these, enhanced, versions of realistic word problems."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Lastly, in Experiment 3, we attempted to replicate the work of Palm (2008) who was able to triple the number of realistic responses by creating problems with a richer backstory and providing more detail. Our sample of sixth-grade students failed to show an increase in realistic responses to these, enhanced, versions of realistic word problems."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Abstract explicitly reports an unsuccessful replication of Palm (2008). The reference list is unavailable, so citation fields beyond the printed year cannot be recovered.""}"
10.1080/10573569.2020.1860841,5e1e14843d69c7cd,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,"{""corrected_value"": null, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""5e1e14843d69c7cd"", ""location"": ""Abstract, line 4"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""This study conducted a conceptual replication examining the reliability, stability, and sensitivity to growth of slopes produced from CBM-W. Eighty-nine (N = 89) eighth-grade students responded to one CBM-W probe weekly for 11 weeks."", ""reason"": ""The supplied source text (5e1e14843d69c7cd.txt) consists only of a 4-line title, author line, and abstract for Romig & Olsen (2020) (10.1080/10573569.2020.1860841). Neither prior_adjudication_path nor prior_state_path exists on disk (never adjudicated). While the abstract explicitly states that the study 'conducted a conceptual replication examining the reliability, stability, and sensitivity to growth of slopes produced from CBM-W' with N = 89 eighth-grade students over 11 weeks, it contains no citation, author name, publication year, or title identifying the original study or studies being replicated. Consequently, from the available source text alone, it is impossible to determine whether this target is already captured by shipped row 363 (McMaster, Du, Yeo, Deno, Parker, & Ellis, 2011, 10.1177/001440291107700203, 'Curriculum-Based Measures of Beginning Writing: Technical Features of the Slope') or represents a genuinely missing unshipped paper."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0043_5e1e14843d69c7cd"", ""target_index"": 0, ""verdict"": ""undeterminable""}","{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""This study conducted a conceptual replication examining the reliability, stability, and sensitivity to growth of slopes produced from CBM-W."", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Abstract calls this a conceptual replication but does not name the original paper or state its findings. Negative results alone cannot establish disagreement with an unstated original finding; target identity and count remain unresolved.""}"
10.5964/jnc.7029,7113daab150388e1,0,10.1016/0010-0277(90)90003-3,Children's understanding of counting,"Wynn, K.",1990,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 0, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""classification"": ""omitted target"", ""doi"": ""10.1016/0010-0277(90)90003-3"", ""title"": ""Children's understanding of counting"", ""authors"": ""Wynn, K."", ""year"": ""1990"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""mixed""}, ""quote"": ""The present work focused on the GaN from Wynn's (1990Wynn's ( , 1992) ) studies with no intention to test the replicability of results coming from the other tasks (i.e., the Point-to-x task or the colour control task were not administered)."", ""location"": ""Study 1, Materials (line 17)"", ""reason"": ""Omitted target. The authors explicitly frame their work as a conceptual replication of the Give-a-Number (GaN) observations from both Wynn (1990) and Wynn (1992), and in Study 1 they directly re-test Wynn's (1990) counter vs. grabber strategy predictions (line 30) as well as the all-at-once generalization assumption attributed to Wynn (1990, 1992) (line 28), finding mixed support (confirming that large-number knowers count more frequently for large than small numbers, but finding sequential acquisition of large number words and no difference between terminal-counters and terminal-grabbers in reaching large-number knowledge).""}","{""authors"": ""Wynn, K."", ""year"": ""1990"", ""title"": ""Children's understanding of counting"", ""doi"": ""10.1016/0010-0277(90)90003-3"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1"", ""evidence_quote"": ""The present work focused on the GaN from Wynn's (1990Wynn's ( , 1992) ) studies with no intention to test the replicability of results coming from the other tasks (i.e., the Point-to-x task or the colour control task were not administered)."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Supporting this view, Large-numbers knowers (4-to 7-knowers) were found to be more likely to resort to counting for large number words than for small ones in study 1. In addition, several children switched from a grabbing to a counting strategy around four. However, the majority of participants kept a consistent strategy all along the task and many of them still succeeded at giving large numbers with a grabbing strategy. Thus, the strategy, at least as coded here, could not be considered as what makes the difference to acquire the cardinal knowledge of large number words."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly report agreement with some original observations and disagreement with others; no overall replication verdict supersedes those comparisons.""}"
10.1353/csd.2014.0018,7f3121c09ebf8efd,0,,,,1979,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,"{""corrected_value"": null, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""7f3121c09ebf8efd"", ""location"": ""Abstract, line 4"", ""method"": ""obvious"", ""quote"": ""This study examines career planning programs and career services offices at colleges and universities in the United States as viewed by senior student affairs officers (SSAOs). Findings from a 1979 study of career services offices (CSOs) were compared to the current findings."", ""reason"": ""Parent inspected the complete available four-line current abstract. It describes a1979career-services study but supplies no exact citation, original author list, or uniquely identified report. SharedReardoncoauthor, topic and1979date make shipped382plausible but do not independently certify its identity or exclude alternate reports. Replace unsupported exact-identity assertion with actual citation-access ambiguity. Separate source-confirmed deposited-reference enrichment and fresh blind coding may resolve this; no false missing pair or definitive exclusion now."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0061_7f3121c09ebf8efd"", ""target_index"": 0, ""verdict"": ""undeterminable""}","{""authors"": """", ""year"": ""1979"", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Findings from a 1979 study of career services offices (CSOs) were compared to the current findings."", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": """", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract states that findings were compared, but neither identifies the original citation nor states its findings or the replication comparison outcome.""}"
10.1007/s11002-025-09794-2,9e5e8fb1308ca417,1,,Misery is not miserly: Sad and self-focused individuals spend more,"Cryder, C. E., Lerner, J. S., Gross, J. J., & Dahl, R. E.",2008,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Cryder, C. E., Lerner, J. S., Gross, J. J., & Dahl, R. E."", ""doi"": """", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Misery is not miserly: Sad and self-focused individuals spend more"", ""year"": ""2008""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""9e5e8fb1308ca417"", ""location"": ""Section 4.2 Results (lines 225-241); References (lines 388-391)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""This value can be above zero (indicating a positive association as per Babin and Darden,\n1996\n) or below zero (indicating a negative association as per Cryder et al.,\n2008\nand Lerner et al.,\n2004\n). The results show that when products have a 4.03 (raw) utilitarian/indulgent rating or higher, the coefficient of happiness on spending is significantly\npositive\n. Additionally, when products have a 2.34 (raw) utilitarian/indulgent rating or lower, the coefficient of happiness on spending is significantly\nnegative\n.\nFig. 1\nFull size image\nFloodlight test of happiness and spending by utilitarian/indulgence rating\nThese results offer a cohesive perspective on how both happiness and sadness relate to increased spending. Specifically, we provide support for a novel contingency that demonstrates\nwhen\nhappy people spend more. In this vein, our study replicates both Babin and Darden’s findings and Lerner et al.’s and Cryder et al.’s findings."", ""reason"": ""No prior adjudication or state file exists for this work. In Section 4.2 (Results), Polman (2025) empirically tests whether the happiness-spending association in the new field sample (N = 307 undergraduates given $10 to spend) is moderated by utilitarian vs. indulgent product ratings, directly testing the negative association (sadder/less happy individuals spending more on utilitarian products such as a water bottle) reported by Cryder et al. (2008). Using floodlight analysis, the study finds a significant negative association between happiness and spending for utilitarian products (rating <= 2.34) and explicitly concludes in Results that 'our study replicates both Babin and Darden’s findings and Lerner et al.’s and Cryder et al.’s findings.' The reference list (lines 388-391) identifies Cryder, C. E., Lerner, J. S., Gross, J. J., & Dahl, R. E. (2008), 'Misery is not miserly: Sad and self-focused individuals spend more,' Psychological Science, 19, 525-530 (no DOI printed in source). The only shipped row (input_row_index 388) is Babin & Darden (1996, Journal of Business Research, 35, 201-206, DOI 10.1016/0148-2963(95)00125-5), and sibling target_index 2 is Lerner, Small, & Loewenstein (2004, Psychological Science, 15, 337-341); both are distinct independent studies rather than alternate reports of Cryder et al. (2008)."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0077_9e5e8fb1308ca417"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Cryder, C. E., Lerner, J. S., Gross, J. J., & Dahl, R. E."", ""year"": ""2008"", ""title"": ""Misery is not miserly: Sad and self-focused individuals spend more"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""These results offer a cohesive perspective on how both happiness and sadness relate to increased spending. Specifically, we provide support for a novel contingency that demonstrates\nwhen\nhappy people spend more. In this vein, our study replicates both Babin and Darden’s findings and Lerner et al.’s and Cryder et al.’s findings."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""These results offer a cohesive perspective on how both happiness and sadness relate to increased spending. Specifically, we provide support for a novel contingency that demonstrates\nwhen\nhappy people spend more. In this vein, our study replicates both Babin and Darden’s findings and Lerner et al.’s and Cryder et al.’s findings."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Explicit set-level verdict says this study replicates all three originals. Product-contingency comparisons connect the tested happiness/spending findings with the specific original results. Bibliography contains no printed target DOI.""}"
10.1007/s11002-025-09794-2,9e5e8fb1308ca417,2,,Heart strings and purse strings: Carryover effects of emotions on economic decisions,"Lerner, J. S., Small, D. A., & Loewenstein, G.",2004,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Lerner, J. S., Small, D. A., & Loewenstein, G."", ""doi"": """", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Heart strings and purse strings: Carryover effects of emotions on economic decisions"", ""year"": ""2004""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""9e5e8fb1308ca417"", ""location"": ""Section 4.2 Results (lines 225-241); References (lines 469-472)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""This value can be above zero (indicating a positive association as per Babin and Darden,\n1996\n) or below zero (indicating a negative association as per Cryder et al.,\n2008\nand Lerner et al.,\n2004\n). The results show that when products have a 4.03 (raw) utilitarian/indulgent rating or higher, the coefficient of happiness on spending is significantly\npositive\n. Additionally, when products have a 2.34 (raw) utilitarian/indulgent rating or lower, the coefficient of happiness on spending is significantly\nnegative\n.\nFig. 1\nFull size image\nFloodlight test of happiness and spending by utilitarian/indulgence rating\nThese results offer a cohesive perspective on how both happiness and sadness relate to increased spending. Specifically, we provide support for a novel contingency that demonstrates\nwhen\nhappy people spend more. In this vein, our study replicates both Babin and Darden’s findings and Lerner et al.’s and Cryder et al.’s findings."", ""reason"": ""No prior adjudication or state file exists for this work. In Section 4.2 (Results), Polman (2025) empirically retests the finding of Lerner, Small, & Loewenstein (2004) that sadness increases spending on utilitarian goods (such as a highlighter) by regressing real-life spending on dispositional happiness, product utilitarian/indulgent ratings, and their interaction (N = 307), followed by a floodlight decomposition showing a significant negative happiness-spending slope for utilitarian items (rating <= 2.34). The Results section explicitly concludes that 'our study replicates both Babin and Darden’s findings and Lerner et al.’s and Cryder et al.’s findings.' The reference list (lines 469-472) identifies Lerner, J. S., Small, D. A., & Loewenstein, G. (2004), 'Heart strings and purse strings: Carryover effects of emotions on economic decisions,' Psychological Science, 15, 337-341 (no DOI printed in source). This work is absent from the shipped dataset, whose only row (input_row_index 388) is Babin & Darden (1996, DOI 10.1016/0148-2963(95)00125-5), and it is an independent study distinct from sibling target_index 1 (Cryder et al., 2008)."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0077_9e5e8fb1308ca417"", ""target_index"": 2, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Lerner, J. S., Small, D. A., & Loewenstein, G."", ""year"": ""2004"", ""title"": ""Heart strings and purse strings: Carryover effects of emotions on economic decisions"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""These results offer a cohesive perspective on how both happiness and sadness relate to increased spending. Specifically, we provide support for a novel contingency that demonstrates\nwhen\nhappy people spend more. In this vein, our study replicates both Babin and Darden’s findings and Lerner et al.’s and Cryder et al.’s findings."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""These results offer a cohesive perspective on how both happiness and sadness relate to increased spending. Specifically, we provide support for a novel contingency that demonstrates\nwhen\nhappy people spend more. In this vein, our study replicates both Babin and Darden’s findings and Lerner et al.’s and Cryder et al.’s findings."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Explicit set-level verdict says this study replicates all three originals. Product-contingency comparisons connect the tested happiness/spending findings with the specific original results. Bibliography contains no printed target DOI.""}"
10.1080/15475441.2014.979387,fa44ae0be27e328b,1,10.1016/j.cognition.2013.02.011,The acquisition of abstract words by young infants.,Bergelson E; Swingley D,2013b,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Bergelson E; Swingley D"", ""year"": ""2013b"", ""title"": ""The acquisition of abstract words by young infants."", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.cognition.2013.02.011"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""This study confirms several patterns that have emerged in previous work ( Bergelson & Swingley, 2012 , 2013b ; Parise & Csibra, 2012 ; Tincoff & Jusczyk, 1999 , 2012 ). Namely, we find further evidence, with a new sample of infants and a new set of item pairs, that 6-9 month olds understand words."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""While the study was not designed to test the difference between noun and verb acquisition, 6–9-month-olds did perform well on one of the two verb pairs ( hug - eat ), contrary to their poor performance on a broader set of non-nouns in a prior study ( Bergelson & Swingley, 2013 ). Whether infants’ apparent success on hug-eat represents random item variation, or a first fragile glimmer of infants’ understanding of these verbs, is unclear, particularly given infants’ failure to demonstrate understanding of kiss-drink here."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly contrast success on one verb pair with prior non-noun failure while another pair fails; they also confirm the age boost from this original. The broad sentence about confirming several patterns does not give an overall verdict on the entire replication of this paper. Agreement passage: Not unexpectedly, this ability increases with age, but perhaps less expectedly the rate with which it increases is non-linear. This is the third study that finds evidence of a boost in performance around 13-14 months, independent of word type ( Bergelson & Swingley, 2012 , 2013b ).""}"
