10.1371/journal.pone.0120644,38590356c90c08ed,1,,Über das Gedächtnis,Ebbinghaus H,1885,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""corrected_value"": null, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""38590356c90c08ed"", ""location"": ""Introduction, lines 52-58 and 102-112; Method, lines 199-203"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""To help answer these questions, we consulted not only the widely published text of 1885 [\n9\n], which was translated into English in 1913 [\n20\n], but also an earlier report of 1880 [\n8\n]. This is a handwritten manuscript that he submitted for his\nHabilitation\n, which in Germany is a requirement to be considered for a full professorship. This text (the so called\nUrmanuscript\nor original manuscript) has been typeset and republished in German in 1983."", ""reason"": ""The primary source (Murre & Dros, 2015, 10.1371/journal.pone.0120644; no prior adjudication or state file exists) explicitly establishes that Ebbinghaus (1880) [8] (the Habilitation Urmanuskript, shipped as input_row_index 402 and coded as target_index 0) and Ebbinghaus (1885) [9] (Über das Gedächtnis, coded as target_index 1) are two reports of the same classic single-subject experiment on the forgetting curve conducted in 1879-1880 (lines 52-58, 68-75, 102-112). In Method (lines 199-203), the authors confirm that 'The learning and relearning times given in his 1885/1913 volume [ 20 ] are the same as in 1880 [ 8 ], but to each interval he has now added 88 s for reasons that are not made clear', and that Ebbinghaus fitted a power function in 1880 and a logarithmic function in 1885 to the same experimental dataset (lines 52-53, 757-760). Because Ebbinghaus (1885) (target_index 1) is a subsequent report of the exact same original experiment and empirical finding already represented by shipped input_row_index 402 (Ebbinghaus, 1880, target_index 0), it is covered by report equivalence and is not an omitted row."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0026_38590356c90c08ed"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""shipped_right""}","{""authors"": ""Ebbinghaus H"", ""year"": ""1885"", ""title"": ""Über das Gedächtnis"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The current study was set up to replicate the findings by Ebbinghaus [ 8 , 9 ]."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We believe that we may conclude that our attempt to replicate Ebbinghaus’ classic forgetting was successful."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Explicit overall successful verdict governs despite differences at 31 days and exploratory curve fitting.""}"
10.1037/pspp0000104,9c0e3d488066f983,1,,"The religion paradox: If religion makes people happy, why are so many dropping out?","Diener, E., Tay, L., & Myers, D. G.",2011,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Diener, E., Tay, L., & Myers, D. G."", ""doi"": """", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""The religion paradox: If religion makes people happy, why are so many dropping out?"", ""year"": ""2011""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""9c0e3d488066f983"", ""location"": ""Discussion (Study 1), p. 18"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Study 1 was a direct replication of previous cross-cultural research on the religiosity-\n\nadjustment relation. Yet, this study differed from previous research in three important ways.\n\nFirst, previous research operationalized psychological adjustment as life satisfaction\n\n(Stavrova et al., 2013), subjective well-being (Diener et al., 2011), depression (Leurent et al.,\n\n2013), or social self-esteem (Gebauer et al., 2012b). From an RASV perspective, however,\n\nglobal self-esteem is the most appropriate adjustment indicator."", ""reason"": ""Addressing the prior parent review objection: the paper explicitly states that its studies examine the replicability of prior evidence for cross-cultural differences in the religiosity-adjustment relation and describes Study 1 as a replication of prior cross-cultural research including Diener, Tay, & Myers (2011), which found in Gallup World Poll data that religious adjustment benefits (subjective well-being) were restricted to religious countries (r = .10-.20). Re-testing this cross-cultural moderation with global self-esteem (and also testing the Gallup World Poll country-level religiosity index in Note 10 and interpersonal-contact controls in Study 3) is an explicit conceptual replication. Diener et al. (2011) is completely distinct from shipped row 405 (Gebauer et al., 2013d / corrected to Gebauer, Sedikides, & Neberich, 2012b) and sibling targets 2 and 3."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0077_9c0e3d488066f983"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Diener, E., Tay, L., & Myers, D. G."", ""year"": ""2011"", ""title"": ""The religion paradox: If religion makes people happy, why are so many dropping out?"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2, 3"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Study 1 was a direct replication of previous cross-cultural research on the religiosity- adjustment relation. Yet, this study differed from previous research in three important ways. First, previous research operationalized psychological adjustment as life satisfaction (Stavrova et al., 2013), subjective well-being (Diener et al., 2011), depression (Leurent et al., 2013), or social self-esteem (Gebauer et al., 2012b). "", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Our results fully and firmly replicated and extended prior evidence for cross-cultural differences in religious adjustment benefits."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The reported moderation agrees with the prior positive moderation findings.""}"
10.1037/pspp0000104,9c0e3d488066f983,2,,Why are religious people happy? The effect of the social norm of religiosity across countries.,"Stavrova, O., Fetchenhauer, D., & Schlösser, T.",2013,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Stavrova, O., Fetchenhauer, D., & Schlösser, T."", ""doi"": """", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Why are religious people happy? The effect of the social norm of religiosity across countries."", ""year"": ""2013""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""9c0e3d488066f983"", ""location"": ""Discussion (Study 1), p. 18"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Study 1 was a direct replication of previous cross-cultural research on the religiosity-\n\nadjustment relation. Yet, this study differed from previous research in three important ways.\n\nFirst, previous research operationalized psychological adjustment as life satisfaction\n\n(Stavrova et al., 2013), subjective well-being (Diener et al., 2011), depression (Leurent et al.,\n\n2013), or social self-esteem (Gebauer et al., 2012b). From an RASV perspective, however,\n\nglobal self-esteem is the most appropriate adjustment indicator."", ""reason"": ""Addressing the prior parent review objection: the paper explicitly states that its studies examine the replicability of prior evidence for cross-cultural differences in the religiosity-adjustment relation and identifies Stavrova, Fetchenhauer, & Schlösser (2013)—which found in WVS data that religiosity relates positively to life satisfaction in religious countries but not secular countries—as one of the four prior cross-cultural studies replicated in Study 1 using global self-esteem (and further evaluated against interpersonal-contact explanations in Study 3). Stavrova et al. (2013) is an independent WVS study distinct from shipped row 405 (Gebauer et al., 2013d / corrected to Gebauer et al., 2012b) and sibling targets 1 and 3."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0077_9c0e3d488066f983"", ""target_index"": 2, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Stavrova, O., Fetchenhauer, D., & Schlösser, T."", ""year"": ""2013"", ""title"": ""Why are religious people happy? The effect of the social norm of religiosity across countries."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2, 3"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Study 1 was a direct replication of previous cross-cultural research on the religiosity- adjustment relation. Yet, this study differed from previous research in three important ways. First, previous research operationalized psychological adjustment as life satisfaction (Stavrova et al., 2013), subjective well-being (Diener et al., 2011), depression (Leurent et al., 2013), or social self-esteem (Gebauer et al., 2012b). "", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Our results fully and firmly replicated and extended prior evidence for cross-cultural differences in religious adjustment benefits."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The reported moderation agrees with the prior positive moderation findings.""}"
10.1037/pspp0000104,9c0e3d488066f983,3,,Spiritual and religious beliefs as risk factors for the onset of major depression: an international cohort study.,"Leurent, B., Nazareth, I., Bellon-Saameno, J., Geerlings, M-I., Maaroos, H., Saldivia, S., Svab, I., Torres-Gonzalez, F., Xavier, M., & King, M.",2013,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Leurent, B., Nazareth, I., Bellon-Saameno, J., Geerlings, M-I., Maaroos, H., Saldivia, S., Svab, I., Torres-Gonzalez, F., Xavier, M., & King, M."", ""doi"": """", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Spiritual and religious beliefs as risk factors for the onset of major depression: an international cohort study."", ""year"": ""2013""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""9c0e3d488066f983"", ""location"": ""Discussion (Study 1), p. 18"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Study 1 was a direct replication of previous cross-cultural research on the religiosity-\n\nadjustment relation. Yet, this study differed from previous research in three important ways.\n\nFirst, previous research operationalized psychological adjustment as life satisfaction\n\n(Stavrova et al., 2013), subjective well-being (Diener et al., 2011), depression (Leurent et al.,\n\n2013), or social self-esteem (Gebauer et al., 2012b). From an RASV perspective, however,\n\nglobal self-esteem is the most appropriate adjustment indicator."", ""reason"": ""Addressing the prior parent review objection: the paper explicitly states that its studies examine the replicability of prior cross-cultural research on the religiosity-adjustment relation to resolve prior inconsistencies, describes Leurent et al.'s (2013) international cohort finding of no sociocultural religiosity moderation when adjustment was operationalized as depression, and names Leurent et al. (2013) among the four prior cross-cultural studies directly re-examined in Study 1 using global self-esteem. Leurent et al. (2013) is an independent study distinct from shipped row 405 (Gebauer et al., 2013d / corrected to Gebauer et al., 2012b) and sibling targets 1 and 2."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0077_9c0e3d488066f983"", ""target_index"": 3, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Leurent, B., Nazareth, I., Bellon-Saameno, J., Geerlings, M-I., Maaroos, H., Saldivia, S., Svab, I., Torres-Gonzalez, F., Xavier, M., & King, M."", ""year"": ""2013"", ""title"": ""Spiritual and religious beliefs as risk factors for the onset of major depression: an international cohort study."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2, 3"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Study 1 was a direct replication of previous cross-cultural research on the religiosity- adjustment relation. Yet, this study differed from previous research in three important ways. First, previous research operationalized psychological adjustment as life satisfaction (Stavrova et al., 2013), subjective well-being (Diener et al., 2011), depression (Leurent et al., 2013), or social self-esteem (Gebauer et al., 2012b). "", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The cumulative results provide unequivocal support for RASV and for cross-cultural differences in the religiosity-adjustment relation."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The new moderation conflicts with the explicitly reported absence of moderation in this original; comparison spans introduction and discussion, so confidence is lower.""}"
10.1111/cdev.12774,c931e17eb50f6ca0,1,,Dysregulated Fear Predicts Social Wariness and Social Anxiety Symptoms during Kindergarten,"Buss KA, Davis EL, Kiel EJ, Brooker RJ, Beekman C, Early MC",2013,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": """", ""title"": ""Dysregulated Fear Predicts Social Wariness and Social Anxiety Symptoms during Kindergarten"", ""authors"": ""Buss KA, Davis EL, Kiel EJ, Brooker RJ, Beekman C, Early MC"", ""year"": ""2013"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""successful""}, ""quote"": ""The goals of the present study included (1) replication of identifying a pattern of dysregulated fear in toddlers (Buss, 2011) in a new sample; (2) replication of the prediction from toddler dysregulated fear to elevated social inhibition (Buss, 2011; Buss et al., 2013); and (3) extending our understanding of the implications of dysregulated fear for children’s development by linking this pattern of high fearfulness in low-threat contexts to physiological reactivity as a putative psychophysiological marker of dysregulated emotion processes in toddlerhood. Results were largely consistent with expectations—we were able to replicate the identification of children with dysregulated fear in this new sample, showed that this pattern of fear responding predicted inhibition across early childhood, and identified modest evidence for the idea that children who manifest a dysregulated fear pattern of behavior would also show a distinct pattern of physiological responding to laboratory challenges."", ""location"": ""discussion"", ""reason"": ""The paper explicitly names Buss et al. (2013) alongside Buss (2011) as an original target whose finding that toddler dysregulated fear predicts elevated social inhibition was successfully replicated.""}","{""authors"": ""Buss KA, Davis EL, Kiel EJ, Brooker RJ, Beekman C, Early MC"", ""year"": ""2013"", ""title"": ""Dysregulated Fear Predicts Social Wariness and Social Anxiety Symptoms during Kindergarten"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The goals of the present study included (1) replication of identifying a pattern of dysregulated fear in toddlers (Buss, 2011) in a new sample; (2) replication of the prediction from toddler dysregulated fear to elevated social inhibition (Buss, 2011; Buss et al., 2013); and (3) extending our understanding of the implications of dysregulated fear for children’s development by linking this pattern of high fearfulness in low-threat contexts to physiological reactivity as a putative psychophysiological marker of dysregulated emotion processes in toddlerhood. Results were largely consistent with expectations—we were able to replicate the identification of children with dysregulated fear in this new sample, showed that this pattern of fear responding predicted inhibition across early childhood, and identified modest evidence for the idea that children who manifest a dysregulated fear pattern of behavior would also show a distinct pattern of physiological responding to laboratory challenges."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The present study, which replicates and extends our previous work ([blinded for review]), contributes to this literature by demonstrating robustness in this pattern of fear as a marker of risk for anxiety-related problems. We demonstrated, in a second cohort of children, that a dysregulated pattern of fearful behavior - characterized by high fear in low threat situations – is associated with social inhibition across a 2-year period and is also associated with a different pattern of cardiac reactivity."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The stated replication goals explicitly name this original; the overall verdict supports dysregulated fear and its longitudinal association with inhibition. Differences in curve modeling and new RSA analyses do not reverse this verdict.""}"
10.7554/elife.73430,58f7736b9d35d428,0,10.1016/j.cell.2010.02.027,A chromatin-mediated reversible drug-tolerant state in cancer cell subpopulations,Sharma SV Lee DY Li B Quinlan MP Takahashi F Maheswaran S McDermott U Azizian N Zou L Fischbach MA Wong KK Brandstetter K Wittner B Ramaswamy S Classon M Settleman J,2010,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 0, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": ""10.1016/j.cell.2010.02.027"", ""title"": ""A chromatin-mediated reversible drug-tolerant state in cancer cell subpopulations"", ""authors"": ""Sharma SV Lee DY Li B Quinlan MP Takahashi F Maheswaran S McDermott U Azizian N Zou L Fischbach MA Wong KK Brandstetter K Wittner B Ramaswamy S Classon M Settleman J"", ""year"": ""2010"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""uninformative""}, ""quote"": ""As part of the Reproducibility Project: Cancer Biology, we published a Registered Report ( Haven et al., 2016 ) that described how we intended to replicate selected experiments from the paper ‘A chromatin-mediated reversible drug-tolerant state in cancer cell subpopulations’ ( Sharma et al., 2010 ). We attempted to independently replicate experiments that tested the phenotype of a small subpopulation of ‘drug-tolerant persister’ cells (DTPs) that are generated when PC9 cells, a protein-tyrosine kinase inhibitor (TKI)-sensitive non-small cell lung cancer cell line, is exposed to high concentrations of TKIs."", ""location"": ""Results — Partial replication: a chromatin-mediated reversible drug-tolerant state in cancer cell subpopulations"", ""reason"": ""Omitted target: this paper reports new experimental replication data (Figure 1, Materials and methods, OSF dataset) re-testing Sharma et al. (2010), with an explicit uninformative outcome summary stating that the preliminary data do not address the central claim because the remaining experiments were not continued.""}","{""authors"": ""Sharma SV Lee DY Li B Quinlan MP Takahashi F Maheswaran S McDermott U Azizian N Zou L Fischbach MA Wong KK Brandstetter K Wittner B Ramaswamy S Classon M Settleman J"", ""year"": ""2010"", ""title"": ""A chromatin-mediated reversible drug-tolerant state in cancer cell subpopulations"", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.cell.2010.02.027"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""As part of the Reproducibility Project: Cancer Biology, we published a Registered Report ( Haven et al., 2016 ) that described how we intended to replicate selected experiments from the paper ‘A chromatin-mediated reversible drug-tolerant state in cancer cell subpopulations’ ( Sharma et al., 2010 )."", ""outcome"": ""uninformative"", ""outcome_quote"": ""To summarize, this replication attempt obtained preliminary outcomes although the experiments were not continued and thus these data do not address whether DTPs maintained viability through IGF-1 receptor signaling and an altered chromatin state dependent on the histone demethylase KDM5A."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors explicitly report inability to adjudicate the central finding."", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """"}"
