10.23668/psycharchives.21850,6fb03a82818463ac,1,10.1037/edu0000618,Distributions of textbook tasks predict student learning: Data from decimal arithmetic.,"Tian, J., Braithwaite, D. W., & Siegler, R. S.",2021,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Tian, J., Braithwaite, D. W., & Siegler, R. S."", ""doi"": ""10.1037/edu0000618"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Distributions of textbook tasks predict student learning: Data from decimal arithmetic."", ""year"": ""2021""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""6fb03a82818463ac"", ""location"": ""Section 4 Discussion, lines 1779-1787; see also Section 2 Textbook Content Analyses (Study 1), lines 313-317, 386-404, 628-641, and References, lines 2248-2252"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""The analyses of decimal arithmetic problems in Swiss textbooks match Tian et al.’s findings\n\n(2021) for U.S textbooks (see also Table 2), showing a nonrandom and unbalanced\n\ndistribution, too. For instance, in SZB only 12% and in MTB only 16% of addition and\n\nsubtraction problems involve decimals with unequal decimal digits, while 32% in SZB and\n\n34% in MTB involve decimals with equal decimal digits."", ""reason"": ""In Study 1 (Section 2, 'Textbook Content Analyses (Study 1)') and Section 4 ('Discussion'), Babari, Rütsche, & Schalk (10.23668/psycharchives.21850) explicitly re-test the empirical textbook distribution findings of both Braithwaite & Siegler (2018, 10.1037/xlm0000546, fraction arithmetic in U.S. textbooks, shipped row 456, target_index 0) and Tian, Braithwaite, & Siegler (2021, Journal of Educational Psychology 113(3):516-529, 10.1037/edu0000618, decimal arithmetic in U.S. textbooks, candidate target_index 1). Following Tian et al.'s (2021) coding scheme, the authors analyze 538 decimal arithmetic problems in the Swiss textbook series SZB and 766 decimal problems in MTB, compare their distributions directly against Tian et al.'s (2021) U.S. textbook data in Table 2 and Sections 2.2 and 4, and extend the behavioral test of those decimal textbook biases to Swiss students in Study 2. Tian et al. (2021) is a distinct empirical original paper (different first author, year, journal, and mathematical domain) that is empirically re-tested and absent from the shipped rows. Both listed prior adjudication/state files (d435618b...json and 5c674783...json) are absent."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0136_6fb03a82818463ac"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Tian, J., Braithwaite, D. W., & Siegler, R. S."", ""year"": ""2021"", ""title"": ""Distributions of textbook tasks predict student learning: Data from decimal arithmetic."", ""doi"": ""10.1037/edu0000618"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Our goal was to check whether Swiss\n\nmathematics textbook series may also reveal imbalances in problem frequencies, as\n\nreported by Braithwaite and Siegler (2018) and Tian et al. (2021) for U.S. textbooks."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""These patterns largely resemble, but differ also in part from those observed in U.S.\n\ntextbooks, analyzed by Braithwaite and Siegler (2018) and Tian et al. (2021). Most notably\n\n(and to be explicitly tested in Study 2), in Swiss textbooks, children encounter +/- problems\n\ninvolving fractions with unequal denominators more frequently than those involving equal\n\ndenominators. In contrast, U.S. textbooks present these two types of in roughly equal\n\nproportions."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Study 1 confirms nonrandom decimal problem distributions but the authors also explicitly identify important distribution differences from the U.S. textbooks. The new child decimal tasks extend the finding rather than reproduce an original child experiment.""}"
10.1177/2053168018822174,6070e18087845e69,0,,,,,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,"{""corrected_value"": null, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""6070e18087845e69"", ""location"": ""Experiments, Experiment 1: Welfare spending, p. 6 (lines 455-466); Treatment effect heterogeneity, p. 9 (lines 571-587); References, pp. 13-14"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Experiment 1: Welfare spending\nOur first experiment replicates a classic question wording\nexperiment. Control subjects are asked whether we are\nspending too little, about right, or too much on “welfare.”\nTreatment subjects are asked the same question about\n“Assistance to the poor” or “Caring for the poor.” The\nGeneral Social Survey (GSS) has conducted this experiment\nevery other year since 1984; we use the 2014 GSS estimate\nas the baseline result. This experiment behaves on Lucid\nmuch as it does on MTurk and the GSS – a large increase in\nsupport for redistribution when the question is phrased as\nassistance or caring for the poor rather than as “welfare.”"", ""reason"": ""In Experiment 1 (Welfare spending), the authors empirically conduct the classic welfare versus assistance/caring for the poor question-wording experiment on Lucid (N = 3,504) and compare the treatment effect estimates against the 2014 General Social Survey (GSS) baseline result (and 2016 GSS conditional average treatment effects by race/ethnicity). However, the primary source text and reference list do not cite or identify any specific published original paper or bibliographic dataset entry for the recurring GSS welfare spending experiment (nor for what p. 9 refers to as 'the original iteration of this experiment'), leaving authors, year, title, and DOI blank in the blind candidate. The sole welfare-related citation in the section, Gilens (1996), is explicitly described as subsequent work identifying race as a moderator ('Though this is not one of the factors theorized to condition the phrasing treatment effect in the original iteration of this experiment, race has since been identified as perhaps the single most important influencing factor in position on welfare spending (Gilens, 1996)'). Shipped row 462 (Berinsky, Huber, and Lenz 2012, 10.1093/pan/mpr057) is the methodological validation template removed in prior adjudication (prior state file absent). The precise unresolved question is what specific source-backed original bibliographic identity (authors, year, title) corresponds to the GSS welfare spending experiment in Experiment 1."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0046_6070e18087845e69"", ""target_index"": 0, ""verdict"": ""undeterminable""}","{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1"", ""evidence_quote"": ""The General Social Survey (GSS) has conducted this experiment every other year since 1984; we use the 2014 GSS estimate as the baseline result."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""This experiment behaves on Lucid much as it does on MTurk and the GSS – a large increase in support for redistribution when the question is phrased as assistance or caring for the poor rather than as “welfare.”"", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors report agreement with the original experimental finding.""}"
10.1177/2053168018822174,6070e18087845e69,1,,The framing of decisions and the psychology of choice.,Tversky A and Kahneman D,1981,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Tversky A and Kahneman D"", ""doi"": """", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""The framing of decisions and the psychology of choice."", ""year"": ""1981""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""6070e18087845e69"", ""location"": ""Experiments, Experiment 2: Asian Disease problem, pp. 6-7 (lines 468-500); Table 1, p. 4 (lines 287-288); References, p. 14 (lines 1005-1006)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Experiment 2: Asian Disease problem\nOur second experiment is also a classic, this time of the\nbehavioral economics literature. Tversky and Kahneman\n(1981) show that people take the riskier option when in a\n“loss frame” rather than a “gain frame.” Subjects are asked\nto “Imagine that your country is preparing for the outbreak\nof an unusual disease, which is expected to kill 600 people.\nTwo alternative programs to combat the disease have been\nproposed.” Subjects in the control condition are told: “If\nProgram A is adopted, 200 people will be saved. If Program\nB is adopted, there is one-third probability that 600 people\nwill be saved, and two-third probability that no people will\nbe saved.” Subjects in the treatment group (the “mortality\nframe”) are told: “If Program A is adopted, 400 people will\ndie. If Program B is adopted there is one-third probability\nthat nobody will die, and two-third probability that 600 people will die.”"", ""reason"": ""In Experiment 2, the authors empirically re-test Tversky and Kahneman's (1981) Asian Disease framing experiment on a new Lucid sample, scaling treatment effect estimates by the mean and standard deviation of the original experiment and comparing the gain-versus-loss framing effect across the original undergraduate sample, MTurk, and Lucid (Figure 2). The original work is explicitly identified in the text, Table 1, and the reference list as Tversky A and Kahneman D (1981) 'The framing of decisions and the psychology of choice', Science 211(4481): 453-458 (no DOI printed). The sole shipped row (input_row_index 462: Berinsky, Huber, and Lenz 2012, 10.1093/pan/mpr057, removed in prior adjudication; prior state file absent) is a 2012 Political Analysis methodological paper on MTurk that supplies the validation template, not an alternate report of Tversky and Kahneman (1981), and none of the sibling targets (0, 2, 3, 4) duplicate this 1981 original."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0046_6070e18087845e69"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Tversky A and Kahneman D"", ""year"": ""1981"", ""title"": ""The framing of decisions and the psychology of choice."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Tversky and Kahneman (1981) show that people take the riskier option when in a “loss frame” rather than a “gain frame.”"", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Lacking a US national sample benchmark, it is unclear how to grade Lucid’s performance relative to MTurk, though we would argue that the qualitative conclusions drawn from the experiment are the same across all samples."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors report agreement with the original experimental finding.""}"
10.1177/2053168018822174,6070e18087845e69,2,,Risk orientations and policy frames.,Kam CD and Simas EN,2010,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Kam CD and Simas EN"", ""doi"": """", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Risk orientations and policy frames."", ""year"": ""2010""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""6070e18087845e69"", ""location"": ""Experiments, Experiment 3: Framing and risk, pp. 7-8 (lines 502-519); Treatment effect heterogeneity, p. 9 (lines 588-592); References, p. 14 (lines 956-957)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""This finding was replicated in\nboth MTurk and Lucid samples. Receiving the mortality\nframe increases the likelihood of selecting the probabilistic\nchoice. Risk acceptance correlates with choosing the risky\noption, but does not moderate the effect of treatment, as we\nwill discuss in greater depth."", ""reason"": ""In Experiment 3, the authors explicitly re-test Kam and Simas (2010) on a new Lucid sample, examining both the main effect of the mortality frame and whether baseline risk acceptance correlates with risky choice without moderating the mortality-frame treatment effect, and comparing standardized estimates and heterogeneity against the original study (Figures 2 and 3). The original study is identified in Table 1, the Experiment 3 heading/text, Note 11, and the reference list as Kam CD and Simas EN (2010) 'Risk orientations and policy frames', The Journal of Politics 72(2): 381-396 (no DOI printed). It is distinct from Target 1 (Tversky and Kahneman 1981, which did not test the risk-acceptance moderation hypothesis examined by Kam and Simas 2010, as Note 11 explains) and from shipped row 462 (Berinsky, Huber, and Lenz 2012, 10.1093/pan/mpr057, the methodological validation template removed in prior adjudication; prior state file absent), so report equivalence does not apply."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0046_6070e18087845e69"", ""target_index"": 2, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Kam CD and Simas EN"", ""year"": ""2010"", ""title"": ""Risk orientations and policy frames."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""3"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Our third experiment replicates Kam and Simas (2010), who show that risk acceptance correlates with choosing the risky option in an Asian Disease-type experiment, but that the treatment effect of the mortality frame does not vary appreciably with risk acceptance."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""This finding was replicated in both MTurk and Lucid samples."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors report agreement with the original experimental finding.""}"
10.1177/2053168018822174,6070e18087845e69,3,,Through a glass and darkly: Attitudes toward international trade and the curious effects of issue framing.,Hiscox MJ,2006,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Hiscox MJ"", ""doi"": """", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Through a glass and darkly: Attitudes toward international trade and the curious effects of issue framing."", ""year"": ""2006""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""6070e18087845e69"", ""location"": ""Experiments, Experiment 4: Free trade, pp. 8-9 (lines 521-537); Treatment effect heterogeneity, p. 9 (lines 593-605); References, p. 14 (lines 950-952)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Study 4 is a replication of Hiscox (2006), which measured the\neffects of positive, negative, and expert opinion frames on\nsupport for free trade. The study employed a 2 × 4 design."", ""reason"": ""In Experiment 4 (Study 4), the authors empirically replicate the 2 x 4 free-trade issue-framing and expert-opinion experiment of Hiscox (2006) on a new Lucid sample, comparing main treatment effects (Figure 2) and treatment effect heterogeneity by college education and expert-opinion condition (Figure 4) against the original sample. The original paper is explicitly identified in Table 1, Experiment 4, and the reference list as Hiscox MJ (2006) 'Through a glass and darkly: Attitudes toward international trade and the curious effects of issue framing', International Organization 60(03): 755-780 (no DOI printed). The sole shipped row (input_row_index 462: Berinsky, Huber, and Lenz 2012, 10.1093/pan/mpr057, removed in prior adjudication; prior state file absent) is a different study by different authors on MTurk validation, and no sibling target duplicates Hiscox (2006)."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0046_6070e18087845e69"", ""target_index"": 3, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Hiscox MJ"", ""year"": ""2006"", ""title"": ""Through a glass and darkly: Attitudes toward international trade and the curious effects of issue framing."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""4"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Study 4 is a replication of Hiscox (2006), which measured the effects of positive, negative, and expert opinion frames on support for free trade."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Overall, the studies yield similar experimental estimates."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors report agreement with the original experimental finding.""}"
10.1177/2053168018822174,6070e18087845e69,4,,Rumors and health care reform: Experiments in political misinformation.,Berinsky AJ,2017,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Berinsky AJ"", ""doi"": """", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Rumors and health care reform: Experiments in political misinformation."", ""year"": ""2017""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""6070e18087845e69"", ""location"": ""Experiments, Experiment 5: Healthcare rumors, p. 9 (lines 539-561); Treatment effect heterogeneity, p. 9 (lines 606-619); Discussion, p. 12 (lines 736-750); References, p. 13 (lines 875-877)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""We conclude our set of five experiments with a note of caution. We attempted to replicate Berinsky’s 2017 experiment\non belief in rumors surrounding the Affordable Care Act\n(ACA), specifically the false rumor that the ACA would\ncreate “death panels” that would make end-of-life decisions\nfor patients without their consent. In the original experiment (conducted in 2010 on a sample provided by Survey\nSampling International (SSI)), a large portion of the sample\nbelieved the rumor, and corrections delivered by\nRepublicans, Democrats, and Nonpartisan groups were all\neffective in correcting false beliefs."", ""reason"": ""In Experiment 5, the authors empirically re-test Berinsky's (2017) experiment on correcting ACA 'death panel' misinformation using a new Lucid sample, comparing baseline rumor belief, average treatment effects (Figure 2), and partisan conditional average treatment effects (Figure 5) directly against the original 2010 SSI sample reported in Berinsky (2017). The reference list identifies this target as Berinsky AJ (2017) 'Rumors and health care reform: Experiments in political misinformation', British Journal of Political Science 47(2): 241-262 (no DOI printed). Although Adam J. Berinsky is also first author of shipped row 462 (Berinsky, Huber, and Lenz 2012, 10.1093/pan/mpr057, 'Evaluating Online Labor Markets for Experimental Research: Amazon.com's Mechanical Turk', Political Analysis 20(3): 351-368, removed in prior adjudication; prior state file absent), the two works are substantively distinct studies—indeed, p. 6 (line 447) explicitly notes that the Berinsky (2017) experiment 'has not been previously replicated on an MTurk sample'—so report equivalence does not apply."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0046_6070e18087845e69"", ""target_index"": 4, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Berinsky AJ"", ""year"": ""2017"", ""title"": ""Rumors and health care reform: Experiments in political misinformation."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""5"", ""evidence_quote"": ""We attempted to replicate Berinsky’s 2017 experiment on belief in rumors surrounding the Affordable Care Act (ACA), specifically the false rumor that the ACA would create “death panels” that would make end-of-life decisions for patients without their consent."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Treatment effect heterogeneity for the healthcare rumors is also low for both the original Berinsky (2017) sample and for the Lucid sample (Figure 5). Treatment effects for all possible treatment possibilities are similar for both Democrats and Republicans for both samples. It is important to note that while we do not replicate the original ATEs for this study in the previous section, here we see that the CATEs are statistically differentiable for only Democrats receiving the Democratic correction to the healthcare rumor."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Direct comparison against identified experimental source; Berinsky average effects differ while heterogeneity agrees.""}"
10.1111/j.1474-9726.2010.00627.x,22bd1fb37b70c337,1,,Association of the FOXO3A locus with extreme longevity in a southern Italian centenarian study,"Anselmi CV, Malovini A, Roncarati R, Novelli V, Villa F, Condorelli G, Bellazzi R, Puca AA",2009,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": """", ""title"": ""Association of the FOXO3A locus with extreme longevity in a southern Italian centenarian study"", ""authors"": ""Anselmi CV, Malovini A, Roncarati R, Novelli V, Villa F, Condorelli G, Bellazzi R, Puca AA"", ""year"": ""2009""}, ""quote"": ""The first study published on the association of SNPs in FOXO3A (Willcox et al. 2008) was conducted on an all male population and, moreover, five of six SNPs reported by Anselmi et al. (2009) were specific for males, whereas only one was specific for females, indicating a gender effect. Moreover, adding further support to a male-specific association in the case-control study we observe no associations in females even though the sample size is larger for females than for males (about 2.5-fold)."", ""location"": ""Discussion, line 26"", ""reason"": ""Omitted target: the paper directly re-tests the tagging SNPs, haplotypes, and sex-specific associations reported by Anselmi et al. (2009) and compares its case-control results against them.""}","{""authors"": ""Anselmi CV, Malovini A, Roncarati R, Novelli V, Villa F, Condorelli G, Bellazzi R, Puca AA"", ""year"": ""2009"", ""title"": ""Association of the FOXO3A locus with extreme longevity in a southern Italian centenarian study"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""First, we conducted case-control analyses and found [as seen in previous studies (Anselmi et al."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The first study published on the association of SNPs in FOXO3A (Willcox et al. 2008) was conducted on an all male population and, moreover, five of six SNPs reported by Anselmi et al."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Agreement on the association is accompanied by an explicit contrast: no female association and/or no stronger centenarian association.""}"
10.1111/j.1474-9726.2010.00627.x,22bd1fb37b70c337,2,,Association of FOXO3A variation with human longevity confirmed in German centenarians,"Flachsbart F, Caliebe A, Kleindorp R, Blanche H, von Eller-Eberstein H, Nikolaus S, Schreiber S, Nebel A",2009,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 2, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": """", ""title"": ""Association of FOXO3A variation with human longevity confirmed in German centenarians"", ""authors"": ""Flachsbart F, Caliebe A, Kleindorp R, Blanche H, von Eller-Eberstein H, Nikolaus S, Schreiber S, Nebel A"", ""year"": ""2009""}, ""quote"": ""Considering previous reporting (Flachsbart et al. 2009) of a most significant effect in centenarians, we analyzed the centenarians separately. We were, however, not able to replicate this finding; that is, we did not find additional SNPs to be associated with longevity or an increased effect of the SNPs in the centenarians (compared to the controls) as when comparing the entire group of oldest old to the controls."", ""location"": ""Discussion, line 28"", ""reason"": ""Omitted target: the paper directly re-tests the SNP associations and centenarian-specific effect reported by Flachsbart et al. (2009).""}","{""authors"": ""Flachsbart F, Caliebe A, Kleindorp R, Blanche H, von Eller-Eberstein H, Nikolaus S, Schreiber S, Nebel A"", ""year"": ""2009"", ""title"": ""Association of FOXO3A variation with human longevity confirmed in German centenarians"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""First, we conducted case-control analyses and found [as seen in previous studies (Anselmi et al."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Considering previous reporting (Flachsbart et al. 2009) of a most significant effect in centenarians, we analyzed the centenarians separately."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Agreement on the association is accompanied by an explicit contrast: no female association and/or no stronger centenarian association.""}"
10.1111/j.1474-9726.2010.00627.x,22bd1fb37b70c337,3,,Genetic association of FOXO1A and FOXO3A with longevity trait in Han Chinese populations,"Li Y, Wang WJ, Cao H, Lu J, Wu C, Hu FY, Guo J, Zhao L, Yang F, Zhang YX, Li W, Zheng GY, Cui H, Chen X, Zhu Z, He H, Dong B, Mo X, Zeng Y, Tian XL",2009,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 3, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": """", ""title"": ""Genetic association of FOXO1A and FOXO3A with longevity trait in Han Chinese populations"", ""authors"": ""Li Y, Wang WJ, Cao H, Lu J, Wu C, Hu FY, Guo J, Zhao L, Yang F, Zhang YX, Li W, Zheng GY, Cui H, Chen X, Zhu Z, He H, Dong B, Mo X, Zeng Y, Tian XL"", ""year"": ""2009""}, ""quote"": ""Flachsbart et al. (2009) and Li et al. (2009) reported significant findings in the populations composed of both genders. However, as seen in Table 1, 2, and 3, the differences in allele, genotype and haplotype frequencies in our cohorts were found to be most pronounced for males."", ""location"": ""Discussion, line 26"", ""reason"": ""Omitted target: the paper directly re-tests the SNP, haplotype, and combined-gender associations reported by Li et al. (2009) and compares its findings against them.""}","{""authors"": ""Li Y, Wang WJ, Cao H, Lu J, Wu C, Hu FY, Guo J, Zhao L, Yang F, Zhang YX, Li W, Zheng GY, Cui H, Chen X, Zhu Z, He H, Dong B, Mo X, Zeng Y, Tian XL"", ""year"": ""2009"", ""title"": ""Genetic association of FOXO1A and FOXO3A with longevity trait in Han Chinese populations"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""First, we conducted case-control analyses and found [as seen in previous studies (Anselmi et al."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Flachsbart et al. (2009) and Li et al."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Agreement on the association is accompanied by an explicit contrast: no female association and/or no stronger centenarian association.""}"
10.1111/j.1474-9726.2010.00627.x,22bd1fb37b70c337,4,,Association of common genetic variation in the insulin ⁄ IGF1 signaling pathway with human longevity,"Pawlikowska L, Hu D, Huntsman S, Sung A, Chu C, Chen J, Joyner AH, Schork NJ, Hsueh WC, Reiner AP, Psaty BM, Atzmon G, Barzilai N, Cummings SR, Browner WS, Kwok PY, Ziv E",2009,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 4, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": """", ""title"": ""Association of common genetic variation in the insulin ⁄ IGF1 signaling pathway with human longevity"", ""authors"": ""Pawlikowska L, Hu D, Huntsman S, Sung A, Chu C, Chen J, Joyner AH, Schork NJ, Hsueh WC, Reiner AP, Psaty BM, Atzmon G, Barzilai N, Cummings SR, Browner WS, Kwok PY, Ziv E"", ""year"": ""2009""}, ""quote"": ""Four of them (rs13217795, rs2764264, rs479744, and rs9400239) have previously been reported to be associated (after correction for multiple testing) in at least one study (Flachsbart et al. 2009;Willcox et al. 2008), whereas three SNPs (rs13220810, 7762395, rs9486902) have been shown to be nominally significant (Flachsbart et al. 2009;Pawlikowska et al. 2009)."", ""location"": ""Discussion, line 26"", ""reason"": ""Omitted target: the paper directly re-tests the tagging SNP associations reported by Pawlikowska et al. (2009) and compares its allele, genotype, and sex-stratified results against them.""}","{""authors"": ""Pawlikowska L, Hu D, Huntsman S, Sung A, Chu C, Chen J, Joyner AH, Schork NJ, Hsueh WC, Reiner AP, Psaty BM, Atzmon G, Barzilai N, Cummings SR, Browner WS, Kwok PY, Ziv E"", ""year"": ""2009"", ""title"": ""Association of common genetic variation in the insulin ⁄ IGF1 signaling pathway with human longevity"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""First, we conducted case-control analyses and found [as seen in previous studies (Anselmi et al."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""No nominally significant effects (either by PCCA or PCCG) were found for the females.\nTo correct for multiple testing, while taking into account the LD between the individual SNPs, permutation analysis was performed using the Plink software."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The original association is described as female-specific; the present study finds no female associations.""}"
10.1080/14719037.2017.1282000,f47e46981f1f5369,0,,,Van Ryzin,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Van Ryzin"", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""description"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2, 3"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Van Ryzin experimentally manipulated expectations and performance and found a direct effect of performance, but no disconfirmation. We performed: an exact replication; a conceptual replication with extreme manipulations; a conceptual replication that reversed the order of a performance and expectations manipulation."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Study 1 and 2 reproduced original findings. In contrast, study 3 indicates that expectation cues are retrospectively used to anchor prior experiences of performance."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Two replications agree, while the third is explicitly contrasted. Original bibliographic entry unavailable in abstract.""}"
10.1177/1948550618775410,05781ebeec482e07,1,10.1037/aca0000069,Different stories: How levels of familiarity with literary and genre fiction relate to mentalizing.,"Kidd, D., & Castano, E.",2017a,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Kidd, D., & Castano, E."", ""doi"": ""10.1037/aca0000069"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Different stories: How levels of familiarity with literary and genre fiction relate to mentalizing."", ""year"": ""2017""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""05781ebeec482e07"", ""location"": ""Results, Additional analyses: Replication and extension of Kidd and Castano (2017a) (lines 1035-1046); Table 10 (lines 884-931); Conclusions (lines 1105-1108); References (lines 1228-1231)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Replication and extension of Kidd and Castano (2017a). The\npresent data afford the opportunity to replicate and extend correlational findings concerning the relations of familiarity with\nliterary and genre authors included on the ART and performance on the RMET (Kidd & Castano, 2017a). Literary and\ngenre fiction familiarity scores were calculated as described\nin Kidd and Castano (2017a) and square-root transformed.\nFollowing Kidd and Castano (2017a), guessing scores on\nthe ART were included as a covariate in the models regressing\nthe ToM measures on literary and genre familiarity scores (see\nTable 10). In the model testing effects on the MJT, baseline\njudgment scores were also included as a covariate. For both\n\nToM measures, familiarity with literary authors positively predicted performance, but familiarity with genre authors did not."", ""reason"": ""No prior adjudication or state file exists for this work. Full-text review of 05781ebeec482e07.txt (Kidd & Castano, 2018, 10.1177/1948550618775410) establishes all three required facts for target_index 1: (1) Actual empirical retesting of a specific published finding: In addition to experimentally retesting Experiment 5 of Kidd and Castano (2013) (target_index 0), the authors include a dedicated Results subsection titled 'Replication and extension of Kidd and Castano (2017a)' and Table 10 ('Literary and Genre Author Familiarity as Predictors of RMET and MJT Performance'), computing square-root-transformed literary and genre author familiarity scores from the Author Recognition Test (ART) across all three new experiments (Experiments 1, 2, and 3; N = 305, 318, and 346) and regressing Reading the Mind in the Eyes Test (RMET) and Moral Judgment Task (MJT) performance on literary and genre familiarity controlling for ART guessing scores, concluding both in Results and in Conclusions ('replicated in each of the three present studies') that familiarity with literary authors positively predicted Theory of Mind (ToM) performance whereas familiarity with genre authors did not. (2) Source-backed original identity: Explicitly cited as Kidd and Castano (2017a) and listed in the References (lines 1228-1231) as 'Kidd, D., & Castano, E. (2017a). Different stories: How levels of familiarity with literary and genre fiction relate to mentalizing. Psychology of Aesthetics, Creativity, and the Arts, 11, 474–486. doi:10.1037/aca0000069' (also verified in bibliography_metadata.json). (3) Absence and non-equivalence against all shipped rows: The sole shipped row for this work is input_row_index 483 (Kidd & Castano, 2013, 'Reading Literary Fiction Improves Theory of Mind', Science, 10.1126/science.1239918), which matches sibling target_index 0. Report equivalence does not apply because Kidd and Castano (2013) reports short-term experimental manipulations of reading assigned literary vs. popular fiction texts, whereas Kidd and Castano (2017) is a separate later article reporting correlational studies of lifetime familiarity with literary vs. genre fiction authors on the ART."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0002_05781ebeec482e07"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Kidd, D., & Castano, E."", ""year"": ""2017a"", ""title"": ""Different stories: How levels of familiarity with literary and genre fiction relate to mentalizing."", ""doi"": ""10.1037/aca0000069"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2, 3"", ""evidence_quote"": ""More broadly, these experimental effects align well with the documented positive relation between lifetime experience with literary fiction (but not popular genre fiction) and ToM reported by Kidd and Castano (2017a) and replicated in each of the three present studies."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""More broadly, these experimental effects align well with the documented positive relation between lifetime experience with literary fiction (but not popular genre fiction) and ToM reported by Kidd and Castano (2017a) and replicated in each of the three present studies."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors explicitly characterize the aggregate outcome for this target.""}"
10.1177/0956797618761661,089bc93ae014e8a3,1,,The nature of adolescent competencies predicted by preschool delay of gratification.,"Mischel W., Shoda Y., Peake P. K.",1988,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Mischel W., Shoda Y., Peake P. K."", ""year"": ""1988"", ""title"": ""The nature of adolescent competencies predicted by preschool delay of gratification."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We attempted to extend the famous findings of Mischel and Shoda (\nMischel et al., 1988\n;\nMischel et al., 1989\n;\nShoda et al., 1990\n) by examining associations between early delay of gratification and adolescent outcomes in a more diverse sample of children and with more sophisticated statistical models."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We attempted to extend the famous findings of Mischel and Shoda (\nMischel et al., 1988\n;\nMischel et al., 1989\n;\nShoda et al., 1990\n) by examining associations between early delay of gratification and adolescent outcomes in a more diverse sample of children and with more sophisticated statistical models. As with the earlier studies, we found statistically significant, although smaller, bivariate associations between early delay ability and later achievement. But we also found that these associations were highly sensitive to the inclusion of controls. Moreover, we failed to find even bivariate associations between delay of gratification at age 54 months and a host of behavioral outcomes at age 15, which was remarkable given the stability in self-control measures found in other studies (e.g.,\nMoffitt et al., 2011\n)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors compare the longitudinal findings as a set: academic associations agree, behavioral associations do not. Reduced magnitude alone is not the basis for mixed.""}"
10.1037/pspa0000121,0ebf1107d4b56633,1,10.1177/1745691616674458,"Registered replication report: Strack, Martin, & Stepper (1988).","Wagenmakers, E.-J., Beek, T., Dijkhoff, L., Gronau, Q. F., Acosta, A., Adams, R. B., Jr.,... Zwaan, R. A.",2016,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": ""10.1177/1745691616674458"", ""title"": ""Registered replication report: Strack, Martin, & Stepper (1988)."", ""authors"": ""Wagenmakers, E.-J., Beek, T., Dijkhoff, L., Gronau, Q. F., Acosta, A., Adams, R. B., Jr.,... Zwaan, R. A."", ""year"": ""2016"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""successful""}, ""quote"": ""However, in the present work we sought\nto compare the outcomes of the two specific procedures that were\nused in past studies, one by Strack et al. (1988) and the other by\nWagenmakers et al. (2016), and therefore, we repeated the same\nprocedures that were used in those studies."", ""location"": {""start_line"": 433, ""end_line"": 437, ""form_feed_page"": 1}, ""reason"": ""Omitted target: the paper explicitly repeats the camera-present procedure of Wagenmakers et al. (2016) to re-test its reported null finding and confirms the absence of the facial-feedback effect when a camera is present."", ""quote_provenance"": {""kind"": ""new_independent_literal_primary_quotation"", ""inspection_result"": ""evidence_followup/integration/strict_literal_quote_inspection.result.json"", ""issue_index"": 161, ""source_path"": ""/home/lukas/.t3/worktrees/fred-data/audit-flora-blind-2026-10/audit/flora_blind_2026-10/texts/0ebf1107d4b56633.txt"", ""source_sha256"": ""f685841263a68b8c86da2012d015af6704f2e26fc97314a23af0ee9fcd15cfaf"", ""raw_start_offset"": 32011, ""raw_end_offset"": 32288, ""scientific_verdict_unchanged"": true, ""reason"": ""Parent verified exact raw slice and semantic identity with the earlier accepted passage under its recorded display-normalisation policy. Independent primary-context inspector supported this passage. Original provider wording and whole prior decision retained in history; raw provider reply unchanged.""}}","{""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""However, in the present work we sought to compare the outcomes of the two specific procedures that were used in past studies, one by Strack et al. (1988) and the other by Wagenmakers et al. (2016), and therefore, we repeated the same procedures that were used in those studies."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Our results suggest that the original findings of a facial feedback effect (Strack et al., 1988) and the null effect in the replication studies (Wagenmakers et al., 2016) are not contradictory: The facial-feedback effect was present when the camera was absent and participants were not concerned with performance monitoring; The effect diminished when the participants’ performance was recorded and participants were warned that their performance was being monitored."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""authors"": ""Wagenmakers, E.-J., Beek, T., Dijkhoff, L., Gronau, Q. F., Acosta, A., Adams, R. B., Jr.,... Zwaan, R. A."", ""year"": ""2016"", ""title"": ""Registered replication report: Strack, Martin, & Stepper (1988)."", ""doi"": ""10.1177/1745691616674458"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The camera condition repeats and confirms the null finding in the published replication project; this is an explicit target, not only a source of materials.""}"
10.1073/pnas.2103313118,643e1bb7a06e2a12,7,,,Fernbach; Kan; Lynch,2015,full_text,,error,obvious,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Fernbach, P. M.; Kan, C.; Lynch, J. G., Jr."", ""year"": ""2015"", ""title"": ""Squeezed: Coping with Constraint through Efficiency and Prioritization"", ""doi"": """", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""643e1bb7a06e2a12"", ""location"": ""PRIMARY PROJECT REPORT: Fernbach (line 564); see also Method (main text, line 15), PRIMARY PROJECT TABLE (lines 976-980), and PRIMARY PROJECT SPREADSHEET (lines 1058-1059)"", ""method"": ""obvious"", ""quote"": ""Study 2 of Fernbach, Kan, and Lynch 2015 contains two primary predictions and analyses. This study manipulated the level of financial constraint and asked participants to provide their strategy to address the constraint. Responses were coded as efficiency plans (such as finding sales or deals) or priority plans (roughly involving trade-offs or sacrifices). The authors predicted increased constraint would shift the planning mix toward prioritization and would increase the speed of generating priority plans relative to efficiency plans."", ""reason"": ""Parent independently verifies the exact named Fernbach/Kan/Lynch2015Study2financialconstraint planning-mix experiment in the current primary project report, TableAandprojectspreadsheet. Official Crossref/publisher-deposited original metadata supplies the previously omitted title Squeezed:CopingWithConstraintThroughEfficiencyAndPrioritization, same three authors,2015printyear, and exactly the efficiency/prioritization finding;2014online/2015printare the same article. All17shipped originals are different empirical papers. Missing bibliographic title in the acquired replication report is not evidence that the named actual retested original is unidentified once this primary authoritative metadata has been checked. Keep blindcitation fields/rawexpertreply unchanged; verified parent citation is separate and DOI remainsblank because currenttextdoesnotprintit. This is identity-only: older mixed outcome is not automatically preserved, because the amended timing analyses are not reconciled in an overall original-specific verdict; unknown outcome is retained rather than forcing an unrequested analysis judgment."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0049_643e1bb7a06e2a12"", ""target_index"": 7, ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""parent_primary_identity_receipt"": ""evidence_followup/integration/fernbach_original_0019.metadata_verification.json""}","{""authors"": ""Fernbach; Kan; Lynch"", ""year"": ""2015"", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""2"", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Study 2 of Fernbach, Kan, and Lynch 2015 contains two primary predictions and analyses."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In the replication, there was no interaction of constraint and plan type on planning mix, F(1,222) = 0.29, p = .752. In testing for response times, there was an interaction of constraint and plan time on log-transformed response time, F(2, 299) = 3.04, p = .049."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The project report compares failure of the original planning-mix finding and a significant response-time interaction. An added model matching the original fails too; no overall verdict overrides the reported partly agreeing comparisons. Full title and author initials unavailable in acquired text.""}"
