 false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Original central interaction is null in primary spreadsheet (r=-0.03, 95% CI [-0.11133135,0.04324818]); replication interaction is also null. Agreement is successful even without significance. Lower confidence because the narrative does not explicitly compare its null with the original null and project date labels disagree; full citation in write-up says 2018, summary says 2019.""}"
10.5539/ibr.v5n4p71,8c158b1f567dad84,0,,,"Hofstede, G.",1980,full_text,,correct,obvious,"{""field"": ""missing_row"", ""target_index"": 0, ""method"": ""obvious"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""This study replicates Hofstede’s study in the same way; using a matched sample on as many\ncharacteristics as possible (age and education). A total of 795 graduate students have participated in this study of\nwhich 375 were males and 420 were females.\n"", ""location"": ""Independent parent primary-source check; exhausted_candidate_0056.parent.json offsets"", ""reason"": ""Parent independently inspected the Jordan new795participant matched-sample survey, VSM94 dimensions, IBM Arab-culture benchmark and the actual shipped520unique Hofstede Culture's Consequences identity. This is a retest of the cultural-dimension finding already represented by that row, not a separately identified empirical original. The inline1980 citation versus shipped1999 is retained as bibliographic uncertainty; no unverified later-printing or edition history is asserted and no extra book is manufactured from the year difference. This missing-pair disposition does not adjudicate the exact edition/year of the shipped original.""}","{""authors"": ""Hofstede, G."", ""year"": ""1980"", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""This study replicates Hofstede’s study with data obtained from students studying for their master degrees in different universities in Jordan."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In conclusion we argue that Hofstede’s (1980, 1991, 2005) assumption that people who grow up in the same place will share similar views about life does not apply to his findings about Arab cultures and thus his generalization made to Jordanian culture is not validated."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The overall conclusion rejects the generalization to Jordan, which decides the verdict despite agreement on some dimensions.""}"
10.1080/09515089.2014.893288,cbde872a2413d44a,0,,Gender and Philosophical Intuition,"Buckwalter, Wesley, and Stich, Stephen",2011,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Buckwalter, Wesley, and Stich, Stephen"", ""year"": ""2011"", ""title"": ""Gender and Philosophical Intuition"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In specific we attempted replication of Section 3.2 and 3.8 in Buckwalter and Stich (2011)."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We attempted to replicate three of the classes of questions that Buckwalter and Stich review in their paper and for which they report significant differences. We failed to replicate the results."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract explicitly states overall failure of the replication.""}"
10.1111/lang.12371,f7c59e409b876924,0,,,"You, Dörnyei, and Csizér",2016,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""You, Dörnyei, and Csizér"", ""year"": ""2016"", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We report a preregistered conceptual replication and extension of You, Dörnyei, and Csizér (2016), who found a central motivational role for vision."", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Abstract reports an alternative model but does not settle the overall replication outcome or all comparisons with the original.""}"
10.1017/xps.2020.18,166a0786310a6eeb,0,,,Walsh and Schulzke,,abstract,,correct,argon,"{""corrected_value"": null, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""166a0786310a6eeb"", ""location"": ""Abstract"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Existing research finds that weapons that eliminate the possibility of military casualties, such as drones, increase popular support for engaging in conflict. We argue that this effect may be overstated because the choice of weapons technology is endogenous to conflict. Leaders may select to use drones in conflicts where the risk of harm to ground forces is especially high. To address this, we replicate and extend the research design of Walsh and Schulzke across three survey experiments."", ""reason"": ""No prior adjudication or state file exists for this work. The blind target ('Walsh and Schulzke', with empty year, title, and DOI because the available source text consists solely of the article header and abstract without a reference list) refers to the exact original study already represented in shipped row 538: Walsh and Schulzke (2018), 'Drones and Support for the Use of Force' (DOI: 10.3998/mpub.9946611). The abstract explicitly states the original finding ('weapons that eliminate the possibility of military casualties, such as drones, increase popular support for engaging in conflict'), states that the authors 'replicate and extend the research design of Walsh and Schulzke across three survey experiments', and reports that support does not differ between drone strikes and ground troops. Because this original study is already present in shipped row 538 and went unmatched only due to missing bibliographic fields in the abstract-only source text, no target is missing."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0006_166a0786310a6eeb"", ""target_index"": 0, ""verdict"": ""shipped_right""}","{""authors"": ""Walsh and Schulzke"", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2, 3"", ""evidence_quote"": ""To address this, we replicate and extend the research design of Walsh and Schulzke across three survey experiments."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We find that support for military action does not differ across treatments in which subjects are told that the attack involves drone strikes or ground troops."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract states that the prior finding was greater support with drones and reports no corresponding support difference in the replication experiments.""}"
10.1109/esem.2015.7321191,b271f6834dc1f14f,0,,,Apel et al.,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Apel et al."", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In this study, aiming at increasing the existing body of evidence and assessing results for systems developed under an alternative version control paradigm, we replicate an experiment conducted by Apel et al. to compare the unstructured and semistructured approach with respect to the occurrence of conflicts reported by both approaches."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Similar to the original study, we observed that semistructured merge reduces the number of conflicts in 55% of the scenarios of the new sample. However, the observed average conflict reduction of 62% in these scenarios is far superior than what has been observed before."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Explicit agreement with original conflict reduction finding; larger reduction does not imply mixed.""}"
10.3389/fneur.2025.1709389,eef5ae27bbc775a1,1,10.1212/WNL.88.16_supplement.P1.376,Continuous daily assessment of multiple sclerosis disability using remote step count monitoring.,"Block VJ, Lizee A, Crabtree-Hartman E, Bevan C, Graves J, Bove R.",2016,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Block VJ, Lizee A, Crabtree-Hartman E, Bevan C, Graves J, Bove R."", ""year"": ""2016"", ""title"": ""Continuous daily assessment of multiple sclerosis disability using remote step count monitoring."", ""doi"": ""10.1212/WNL.88.16_supplement.P1.376"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We obtained the original activity data from the CA ( 11 ,  12 ) and VA ( 13 ) cohorts, harmonized overlapping variables collected in both studies, and conducted secondary data analyses to replicate analyses in the CA cohort using the VA dataset ( 13 )."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Mirroring the approach used in the CA cohort ( 11 ,  12 ) study of 97 participants with MS using wrist-worn Fitbit activity monitors, we replicated key findings in the VA cohort ( 13 ) of 61 participants with MS with less severe disability, assessed using the hip-worn ActiGraph sensors."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Overall verdict explicitly states key findings of the CA studies (11, 12) were replicated in the independent VA cohort; smaller correlations and reliability are not framed as replication failure.""}"
10.1016/j.learninstruc.2015.08.002,f2f8f188a2c40775,0,,,Karpicke and Blunt,2011,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Karpicke and Blunt"", ""year"": ""2011"", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Karpicke and Blunt (2011) showed in college students that retrieval practice produced more learning from educational texts than concept mapping on a 1-week delayed test."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""While the results essentially replicate Karpicke and Blunt's finding which shows that retrieval practice leads to better overall performance than concept mapping, this effect was less pronounced for people with experience using this technique than it was for trained participants."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Explicit overall replication verdict. Attenuation for experienced participants does not turn support into mixed.""}"
10.1093/pnasnexus/pgaf327,4557265215dceef5,1,10.1038/s41562-021-01133-5,Reconsidering evidence of moral contagion in online social networks,"Burton JW, Cruz N, Hahn U",2021,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Burton JW, Cruz N, Hahn U"", ""doi"": ""10.1038/s41562-021-01133-5"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Reconsidering evidence of moral contagion in online social networks"", ""year"": ""2021""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""4557265215dceef5"", ""location"": ""Introduction; Results (A pre-registered replication of moral contagion); Discussion; References"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""In our pre-registered replication, we also found that the moral contagion model outperformed Burton et al. ( 24 )'s nonsense “XYZ contagion” model as determined by comparing AIC/BIC model fit statistics, see SI Appendix, Section S1 . Furthermore, models with our pre-registered covariates consistently outperformed models without them, see SI Appendix, Section S1 . Together, these findings directly challenge two key claims made by Burton et al. ( 24 ): moral contagion does not replicate well out of the original Brady et al. ( 1 ) sample, and the moral contagion model performs “no better” than a nonsense “XYZ contagion” model."", ""reason"": ""No prior adjudication or state files exist for this work. In addition to replicating Brady et al. (2017; shipped row 591, 10.1073/pnas.1618923114), the authors explicitly retest the specific published empirical finding of Burton, Cruz, & Hahn (2021) ('Reconsidering evidence of moral contagion in online social networks', Nature Human Behaviour, 5, 1629–1635, DOI: 10.1038/s41562-021-01133-5; verified in Crossref metadata and Reference 24) that moral contagion models perform no better than a nonsense 'XYZ contagion' model, fitting and comparing AIC/BIC model fit statistics of the moral contagion and XYZ contagion models across five new pre-registered Twitter datasets (N = 849,266). Shipped row 591 is Brady et al. (2017), the earlier phenomenon paper by a different author team, not an alternate report of Burton et al. (2021)."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0032_4557265215dceef5"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Burton JW, Cruz N, Hahn U"", ""year"": ""2021"", ""title"": ""Reconsidering evidence of moral contagion in online social networks"", ""doi"": ""10.1038/s41562-021-01133-5"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We also tested Burton et al. ( 24 )'s key claim that moral contagion models perform no better than the nonsense “XYZ contagion” model."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Our findings also directly challenge previous claims made by Burton et al. ( 24 ) that moral contagion may not replicate well out of the original Brady et al. ( 1 ) sample, and that the moral contagion model performs “no better” than a nonsense “XYZ contagion” model."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors explicitly assess this published finding in new samples; verdict follows their stated overall comparison.""}"
10.3390/su9071286,935c2f360e27f419,1,,Eco-Friendliness in the Brand Experience of High-Tech Products.,"Saari, U.",2016,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": """", ""title"": ""Eco-Friendliness in the Brand Experience of High-Tech Products."", ""authors"": ""Saari, U."", ""year"": ""2016"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""successful""}, ""quote"": ""In this study we replicate the extended BXS [19] cross-nationally to measure differences in the ways consumers experience the eco-friendliness of global smartphone brands in a cross-national context."", ""location"": ""Research Method and Data, line 49 (and Reference 19, line 273)"", ""reason"": ""The paper explicitly re-tests the eco-friendliness BXS extension developed in Saari (2016) [19], which had previously only been confirmed in one Western country, and confirms its construct validity and reliability across both Finland and India.""}","{""authors"": ""Saari, U."", ""year"": ""2016"", ""title"": ""Eco-Friendliness in the Brand Experience of High-Tech Products."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In this study we replicate the extended BXS [19] cross-nationally to measure differences in the ways consumers experience the eco-friendliness of global smartphone brands in a cross-national context. The survey items were selected from the previously developed and extended BXS [18,19,107]. We assess the four items measuring eco-friendliness as well as the other four brand experience dimensions from the BXS by conducting structural equation modeling (SEM) and confirmatory factor analysis (CFA) following the same steps that Brakus et al. [18] undertook."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The findings of our study indicate that the eco-friendliness dimension can be generalized in two countries with very different demographics and economic situations."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The earlier eco-friendliness dimension is explicitly retested and confirmed in both countries; changes to the other dimensions do not negate this target finding.""}"
10.1002/ijop.12519,ea72ea7a5091d5aa,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Previous research has found that negative-valence appeals relating to the self (Don't be a cheater) are more effective at reducing unethical behaviour than appeals relating to behaviour (Don't cheat). We aimed to replicate these findings and examine whether positive-valence appeals relating to the self (Be an honest person) and to behaviour (Answer honestly) could have a similar effect."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""A meta-analysis of the five experiments revealed a small effect of the self-appeals relative to the behaviour-appeals."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract states the original self-appeal advantage and reports the same advantage in the aggregate of the five new experiments. Smaller effects do not change success. Citation identity cannot be established from the abstract.""}"
10.3386/w20657,5125e2ec82209943,1,,Revisiting the Impacts of Teachers.,"Rothstein, Jesse.",2014,full_text,competing_supported_matches,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Rothstein, Jesse."", ""year"": ""2014"", ""title"": ""Revisiting the Impacts of Teachers."", ""doi"": """", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""statistically successful but flawed""}, ""quote"": ""However, in the same paper, Rothstein argued that the CFR methodology was\n\ninvalid, given the relationship between changes in the value-added of teachers in a grade and\n\nchanges in students’ prior-year test scores. We also find such a relationship, but offer a different\n\ninterpretation. Rather than invalidating the CFR research design, we show that it reflects a\n\nmechanical relationship, resulting from the fact that prior test scores and the value-added\n\nestimates were sometimes estimated with the same data.\n\n\n       In addition to replicating the CFR and Rothstein analyses, we extended their work in\n\nthree ways."", ""location"": {""start_line"": 160, ""end_line"": 175, ""form_feed_page"": 4}, ""reason"": ""The authors explicitly state that they replicate Rothstein (2014) and re-test his lagged-score placebo finding in Table 5, reproducing the statistically significant relationship (.268) while showing it is mechanically confounded by using overlapping data to estimate the dependent and independent variables."", ""quote_provenance"": {""kind"": ""new_independent_literal_primary_quotation"", ""inspection_result"": ""evidence_followup/integration/strict_literal_quote_inspection.result.json"", ""issue_index"": 162, ""source_path"": ""/home/lukas/.t3/worktrees/fred-data/audit-flora-blind-2026-10/audit/flora_blind_2026-10/texts/5125e2ec82209943.txt"", ""source_sha256"": ""4de9c966f8a8779342f882bdda3c88b6cce114d552de845dd34caed68411eea2"", ""raw_start_offset"": 7319, ""raw_end_offset"": 7939, ""scientific_verdict_unchanged"": true, ""reason"": ""Parent verified exact raw slice and semantic identity with the earlier accepted passage under its recorded display-normalisation policy. Independent primary-context inspector supported this passage. Original provider wording and whole prior decision retained in history; raw provider reply unchanged.""}}","{""authors"": ""Rothstein, Jesse."", ""year"": ""2014"", ""title"": ""Revisiting the Impacts of Teachers."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In addition to replicating the CFR and Rothstein analyses, we extended their work in three ways."", ""outcome"": ""statistically successful but flawed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In a recent paper, Rothstein (2014) reports a statistically significant relationship between change in teacher value-added and changes in baseline achievement as prima facie evidence of bias in the CFR method. We find similar results, but offer a different interpretation. We find that the predicted change has a coefficient of .268 when lagged scores are the dependent variable. However, rather than invalidating the CFR methodology, the lagged score test merely demonstrates the hazards of using the same data to estimate the dependent and 19 independent variables."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Statistical placebo relationship is replicated but shown to be mechanically confounded, a central methodological critique.""}"
10.3386/w20657,5125e2ec82209943,2,,"Match Quality, Worker Productivity, and Worker Mobility: Direct Evidence From Teachers.","Jackson, Kirabo.",Forthcoming,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 2, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""In addition to replicating the CFR and Rothstein analyses, we extended their work in three ways. First, we tested whether the predictive validity of value-added estimates differed for evidence drawn from the same school or from a different school."", ""location"": ""introduction"", ""reason"": ""The authors explicitly identify CFR and Rothstein as the replicated studies and frame the school-context analysis (Table 6) as an extension of the CFR forecast-bias test, citing Jackson (forthcoming) as background motivation rather than re-testing Jackson's variance-decomposition study.""}","{""authors"": ""Jackson, Kirabo."", ""year"": ""Forthcoming"", ""title"": ""Match Quality, Worker Productivity, and Worker Mobility: Direct Evidence From Teachers."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Using data from North Carolina, Jackson (forthcoming) estimates that teacher-school match effects account for roughly one-third of the variance in teacher effects. To test the possibility that teacher effects vary by context, we first divide the data available for each teacher’s value-added into value-added estimates using observations only from the same school and those from all available schools."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Consistent with Jackson’s findings regarding teacher-school matches, the point estimate on value-added estimate of the same school, 1.054, is larger than the coefficient on the difference between that from all schools and similar schools, .817. However, we could not reject the hypothesis that both coefficients were equal to one (p value=.163)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors describe the direction as consistent with Jackson. Equality cannot be rejected; this tentative outcome follows their comparison, not statistical significance.""}"
10.1038/sj.ejhg.5200687,6cbb858bffa236db,1,,"Two stage genome-wide search in inflammatory bowel disease provides evidence for susceptibility loci on chromosomes 3, 7 and 12","Satsangi J, Parkes M, Louis E et al",1996,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,"{""corrected_value"": null, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""6cbb858bffa236db"", ""location"": ""Abstract (lines 66-70) and References (lines 200-206, 555-571)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""We have conducted both a replication study on another linkage region, chromosome 6p (\nIBD3\n), and extension studies on two other regions, chromosomes 3p and 7q. Microsatellite markers across each region were genotyped in 284 IBD ASPs from 234 families. A nonparametric peak multipoint LOD score of 3.0 was observed near D6S291, replicating the previous linkage to chromosome 6p (\nIBD3\n). Nominal evidence of linkage was observed at both the 3p and 7q regions."", ""reason"": ""The supplied source text contains only the abstract and reference list, with no article body (Introduction, Methods, Results, or Discussion). While the abstract states that the authors conducted 'extension studies on two other regions, chromosomes 3p and 7q' by genotyping microsatellite markers in 284 IBD ASPs and observing '[n]ominal evidence of linkage ... at both the 3p and 7q regions', it provides no in-text citations or sample-overlap details. Because the full text is unavailable, the evidence does not settle (1) whether the 3p and 7q analyses empirically re-test a published finding in an independent sample versus extending the Oxford group's own prior family cohort and marker map (senior Oxford author Derek Jewell is also an author of Satsangi et al. 1996), or (2) which of the multiple chromosome 3 and 7 linkage references in the bibliography (Satsangi et al. 1996, Nat Genet 14: 199-202; Rioux et al. 1998, Gastroenterology 115: 1062-1065; Vermeire et al. 2000, Inflamm Bowel Dis 6: 165-170) is cited for those regions. No prior adjudication or prior state file existed for this work."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0052_6cbb858bffa236db"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""undeterminable""}","{""authors"": ""Satsangi J, Parkes M, Louis E et al"", ""year"": ""1996"", ""title"": ""Two stage genome-wide search in inflammatory bowel disease provides evidence for susceptibility loci on chromosomes 3, 7 and 12"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""description"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We have conducted both a replication study on another linkage region, chromosome 6p (\nIBD3\n), and extension studies on two other regions, chromosomes 3p and 7q."", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false}"
10.1177/10422587251322409,f4bca80f3a34ca1b,0,,,,2003,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": ""2003"", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We conducted a three-step replication of Davidsson and Honig’s study on the roles of human and social capital in venture creation processes."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We largely validate D&H’s findings, highlight the importance of modeling initial entrepreneurial processes, and emphasize the underappreciated complexities and value of replication studies."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Overall authors verdict largely validates the findings. Original citation title and reference-list authors are unavailable in abstract; do not reconstruct from external knowledge. Also reports exact reanalysis and changed specification, whose computational and robustness results cannot separately be resolved from this abstract.""}"
10.1097/aud.0000000000000393,c45668f74c2e855b,1,,Lexical effects on spoken word recognition by pediatric cochlear implant users,"Kirk, KI; Pisoni, DB; Osberger, MJ.",1995,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Kirk, KI; Pisoni, DB; Osberger, MJ."", ""year"": ""1995"", ""title"": ""Lexical effects on spoken word recognition by pediatric cochlear implant users"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""These findings also replicate the earlier results reported by Kirk et al. (1995) for spoken words in isolation with children and Bell and Wilson (2001) for spoken words in isolation and sentences with adults."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""These findings also replicate the earlier results reported by Kirk et al. (1995) for spoken words in isolation with children and Bell and Wilson (2001) for spoken words in isolation and sentences with adults."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly state that their own findings replicate this original result.""}"
