10.1097/00005650-199204000-00001,535f4428e6ed49dd,1,,"Relations between physicians’ behaviors and analogue patients’ satisfaction, recall and impressions","Roter, D. L., Hall, J. A., Katz, N. R.",1987,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""request_id"": ""scope_535f4428e6ed49dd_1"", ""file_key"": ""535f4428e6ed49dd"", ""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""In Roter’s study, as well as in ours, the\nquality of technical-medical care (‘‘profi-\nciency”) was better explained by RIAS task-\nclusters than by RIAS socio-emotional clus-\nters. Also, in Roter’s study, as well as in ours,\n“giving information” and, especially, “giv-\ning medical information” proved to be im-\nportant in the assessed quality of care."", ""location"": ""Discussion, p. 293 (also Abstract, p. 283; Introduction, p. 284; Methods, pp. 285-287; References 7-8, p. 296)"", ""reason"": ""Addressing the unresolved objection using the replicating text and both supplied primary abstracts (3695653.txt and 3695654.txt): Hall, Roter, and Katz (1987, \""Task versus socio-emotional behaviors in physicians\"", Med Care 1987;25:399-412; Reference 7, represented in shipped row input_row_index 649, DOI 10.1097/00005650-198705000-00004) and Roter, Hall, and Katz (1987, \""Relations between physicians’ behaviors and analogue patients’ satisfaction, recall and impressions\"", Med Care 1987;25:437; Reference 8, candidate target_index 1) are two companion articles from the same analogue-patient study of 43 primary care physicians interacting with two standardized simulated-patient cases, scored with RIAS, speech error counts, and filtered-audio vocal affect ratings. As the primary abstracts establish, Reference 7 (3695653.txt, shipped row 649) scored the encounter transcripts for \""physician proficiency\"" and analyzed the internal associations among physicians' task-oriented, verbal socioemotional, and vocal-affect behaviors and their relation to physician proficiency (technical-medical quality). Reference 8 (3695654.txt) instead added ratings from 258 role-playing analogue patients to test associations between physicians' behaviors and analogue patients' satisfaction, recall of information, and global impressions. In Bensing and Dronkers (1992), \""only general practitioner (GP) behavior is studied. GP’s behavior is related to panel-assessed quality of care on three separate dimensions (technical-medical, psychosocial, and the management of the physician-patient relationship)\"" (Abstract, p. 283). Bensing and Dronkers collect no patient satisfaction, patient recall, or patient impression data and do not retest the analogue-patient satisfaction/recall/impression findings unique to Reference 8. Instead, they treat References 7 and 8 jointly as a single study (\""Roter’s study\"", \""Hall and Roter’s study\"", \""a study into the relative contribution of affective and instrumental behavior to the quality of care ('medical proficiency').7,8\"", p. 284), empirically retest the internal correlations among RIAS task, verbal socioemotional, and nonverbal affect measures (Tables 2-3) and their associations with panel-assessed quality of care/\""proficiency\"" (Tables 4-5; Discussion, p. 293), and quote Reference 8 for the concluding call at the end of the two-paper sequence to replicate the study in a natural setting using video (p. 293). Because the underlying study and the specific empirical findings actually retested by Bensing and Dronkers are already represented by shipped row input_row_index 649 (Reference 7), Reference 8 is not a genuinely missing original target.""}","{""authors"": ""Roter, D. L., Hall, J. A., Katz, N. R."", ""year"": ""1987"", ""title"": ""Relations between physicians’ behaviors and analogue patients’ satisfaction, recall and impressions"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In the last of her two papers about the\nrelative relevance of instrumental or task-re-\nlated behavior on one hand, and socio-emo-\ntional or affective behavior on the other,\nRoter called on other researchers “‘to repli-\ncate her findings through different method-\nologies and in the natural setting.’”* This call\nwas based on two possible weaknesses of\nher study: 1) the use of simulated patients\ninstead of real patients, which raises ques-\ntions about the generalization of the results,\nand 2) the use of audiotapes instead of video-\ntapes, which limited the possibility to regis-\nter nonverbal affect to vocal-tone, only. Our\nstudy can be seen as such a replication: it\ntook place in the natural setting (real general\npractitioners with real patients who had hy-\npertension) and used partly different meth-\nodologies (video instead of audio, which\nmade it possible to register visual as well vo-\ncal cues). In addition, some refinements\nhave been made in RIAS to get a more de-\ntailed picture of GP’s socio-emotional behav-\nior."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The results presented partly corroborate\nthose from the previous studies in which\nRoter’s Interaction Analysis System was\nused, but they also shed some new light on\nthe controversies found in the literature\nabout the relative relevance of instrumental\nversus affective behavior."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Discussion explicitly describes partial corroboration of the earlier RIAS studies, then identifies both shared associations and important differences, including failure to corroborate the overall primacy of task-related behavior.""}"
10.1192/bjp.161.2.223,00bd205abdb1ee24,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,"{""target_index"": 0, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""obvious"", ""verdict"": ""undeterminable"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""Forty-two schizophrenic patients and their close relatives took part in an Italian replication study of expressed emotion (EE). The patients were selected from the psychiatric ward of a general hospital in Milan and were subsequently followed up for nine months. All patients attended a community service clinic as out-patients, and all but one were prescribed neuroleptics for the duration of the study. Relatives were assigned to the high-EE group if they scored 4 or 5 on the emotional overinvolvement (EOI) scale, or showed hostility, or made six or more critical comments. On this basis, 18 (42%) families were rated as low EE and 24 (57%) as high EE. At follow-up, the admission rate for the 9-month period was significantly higher for the high-EE group (P<0.05). Furthermore, significantly fewer patients were readmitted from families showing high warmth (P<0.05). The presence of high warmth appeared to be associated with a lower admission rate, even in high-EE families."", ""location"": ""Complete available primary abstract; independent original-identity validation where specified"", ""reason"": ""Actual primary evidence-access/identity limit: available complete abstract describes an Italian expressed-emotion replication and nine-month readmission comparisons but names no original author/year/reference. Multiple empirical EE findings predate this work; the supplied Vaughn-Leff title alone cannot uniquely identify the closest target. Source recovery ended partial, not verified full text. Two Argon replies described this actual ambiguity; bundle remains unvalidated. Parent independently checked the whole abstract and preserves the candidate unresolved, with no asserted missing original. NEW parent primary-source disposition; no rejected provider quote was repaired, no partial model reply was promoted and no attempt budget reset.""}","{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Forty-two schizophrenic patients and their close relatives took part in an Italian replication study of expressed emotion (EE)."", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": """", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract identifies a replication but neither identifies the specific earlier paper nor states its finding or an overall replication verdict.""}"
10.1353/hub.2006.0033,6d0a7118936c5d54,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,"{""corrected_value"": null, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""6d0a7118936c5d54"", ""location"": ""Abstract, line 5"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Our previous studies showed a statistically significant association between UBL5 sequence variation and several obesity- and diabetes-related quantitative physiological measures in Asian Indian and Micronesian cohorts. Here we undertake a replication study in a Mexican American cohort where the original linkage signal was first detected."", ""reason"": ""Prior adjudication and state files were checked and do not exist (never-adjudicated work). The supplied primary source is an abstract only, without in-text citations or a reference list. The abstract states that 'Our previous studies' showed significant associations between UBL5 sequence variation and obesity- and diabetes-related traits in Asian Indian and Micronesian cohorts and undertakes a replication study in a Mexican American cohort, concluding that the data are consistent with 'our earlier published studies.' Because no specific citations, years, titles, or DOIs are given in the abstract, the primary source evidence cannot establish the bibliographic identity of the target study or studies, nor whether shipped row 656 (Jowett et al., 2004, 10.2337/diabetes.53.9.2467) fully accounts for the retested prior studies or if an additional original report is missing."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0053_6d0a7118936c5d54"", ""target_index"": 0, ""verdict"": ""undeterminable""}","{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Our previous studies showed a statistically significant association between UBL5 sequence variation and several obesity- and diabetes-related quantitative physiological measures in Asian Indian and Micronesian cohorts. Here we undertake 