10.1017/s0033291706008257,6ec9c383d4fddd12,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,"{""corrected_value"": null, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""6ec9c383d4fddd12"", ""location"": ""Abstract (line 4)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""The present report is the second large-scale validation study of the PDSQ in psychiatric out-patients. Because some of the sizes of the diagnostic groups in the initial report were modest, it is important to determine whether the recommended cut-off scores to screen for psychiatric disorders remained the same in a replication study."", ""reason"": ""The supplied primary source contains only the title, author line (M. Zimmerman; I. Chelminski), and abstract, with no full text or reference list. The abstract explicitly describes an empirical replication study testing whether the diagnostic performance (receiver operating characteristic curve analysis) and recommended subscale cut-off scores of the Psychiatric Diagnostic Screening Questionnaire (PDSQ) from 'the initial validation study' / 'the initial report' hold in a new sample of 670 psychiatric out-patients, but gives no in-text citation or bibliographic details for that initial report. Consequently, the blind coder recorded an unnamed target placeholder (target_index 0). Shipped row 658 (Zimmerman & Mattia, 2001, 'A Self-Report Scale to Help Make Psychiatric Diagnoses', Archives of General Psychiatry 58(8): 787, DOI 10.1001/archpsyc.58.8.787) is by the PDSQ's developer Mark Zimmerman and likely represents the initial PDSQ validation report, but without the full text or reference list the supplied primary evidence does not settle whether shipped row 658 is the exact initial validation report re-tested here rather than a related PDSQ scale-development paper. No prior adjudication or prior state file existed for this work."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0054_6ec9c383d4fddd12"", ""target_index"": 0, ""verdict"": ""undeterminable""}","{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The present report is the second large-scale validation study of the PDSQ in psychiatric out-patients. Because some of the sizes of the diagnostic groups in the initial report were modest, it is important to determine whether the recommended cut-off scores to screen for psychiatric disorders remained the same in a replication study."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Based on receiver operating curve analysis, the PDSQ performed as well in the replication sample as in the initial validation study."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract explicitly compares overall diagnostic performance with the initial validation and says it performed as well. A count of unchanged cutoff scores does not by itself overturn that overall comparison. The abstract gives no citation identifying the initial validation paper; bibliographic fields are left empty.""}"
10.2174/1874402800902010006,498d97fc3bf87bf8,0,,A pilot study of functional magnetic resonance imaging brain correlates of deception in healthy young men.,"Kozel, F. A.; Revell, L. J.; Lorberbaum, J. P.; Shastri, A.; Elhai, J. D.; Horner, M. D.; Smith, A.; Nahas, Z.; Bohning, D. E.; George, M. S.",2004,full_text,competing_supported_matches,correct,argon,"{""corrected_value"": null, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""498d97fc3bf87bf8"", ""location"": ""Introduction (lines 47-51); see also Materials and Methodology (lines 86-89), Results (lines 225-234), Figure 1 (lines 296-308), and References [3-5] (lines 440-452)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""After successfully replicating the group results of fMRI\ndetection of deception in a laboratory paradigm involving\nsubjects giving deceptive and truthful answers [3, 4], we\ndeveloped a method to identify deception at the individual\nlevel [5]."", ""reason"": ""Under the Codebook Step 2 replication-chain rule ('when the finding has already been re-tested by others, the target is the study closest to this paper in the chain — the one it actually re-tests — not the chain's original source') and consistent with the prior Argon adjudication (27dd47e58906b908bd5d7e819d1bcc262bbffaed7af4d2c61baf63107bc2bbfd.json; prior state file 5a996c5b...json is absent), Reference [3] (Kozel et al., 2004, J. Neuropsychiatr. Clin. Neurosci., 16(3), 295-305; blind target_index 0) is the original 2004 group-level pilot study at the start of a four-study replication chain ([3] -> [4] -> [5] -> current 2009 study). The study closest in the chain whose ring/watch mock-crime protocol, individual ROI detection model, and Lie-minus-True group activation map (compared directly against the Kozel et al. 2005 Model Building Group in Figure 1) are actually re-tested by this paper is Reference [5] (Kozel et al., 2005, Biol. Psychiatry, 58(8), 605-613, DOI 10.1016/j.biopsych.2005.07.040), which is already represented by retained shipped row input_row_index '660' (blind target_index 2). Note also that Luna's triage omission_quote failed mechanical validation because it spliced out parenthetical statistical thresholds from lines 195-198."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0035_498d97fc3bf87bf8"", ""target_index"": 0, ""verdict"": ""shipped_right""}","{""authors"": ""Kozel, F. A.; Revell, L. J.; Lorberbaum, J. P.; Shastri, A.; Elhai, J. D.; Horner, M. D.; Smith, A.; Nahas, Z.; Bohning, D. E.; George, M. S."", ""year"": ""2004"", ""title"": ""A pilot study of functional magnetic resonance imaging brain correlates of deception in healthy young men."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The Lie-minus-True group map revealed significant\nactivation in similar regions from the prior cohorts using this\nparadigm (see Figure and Table). Specifically, the regions of\nright orbitofrontal, right inferior frontal, right middle frontal,\nand right cingulate gyrus have replicated now in five\nindependent groups from four studies across several\nparadigms [3-5]."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""This method for detecting deception was successfully\nreplicated on a different scanner at a different location for\nboth the individual and group analyses."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The explicit overall verdict states successful replication for both group and individual analyses. It covers the group-level findings attributed to references 3–5, outweighing the specific left temporal contrast.""}"
10.2174/1874402800902010006,498d97fc3bf87bf8,1,,A replication study of the neural correlates of deception.,"Kozel, F. A.; Padgett, T. M.; George, M. S.",2004,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""corrected_value"": null, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""498d97fc3bf87bf8"", ""location"": ""Introduction (lines 47-51); see also Materials and Methodology (lines 86-89), Results (lines 225-234), Figure 1 (lines 296-308), and References [3-5] (lines 440-452)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""After successfully replicating the group results of fMRI\ndetection of deception in a laboratory paradigm involving\nsubjects giving deceptive and truthful answers [3, 4], we\ndeveloped a method to identify deception at the individual\nlevel [5]."", ""reason"": ""Under the Codebook Step 2 replication-chain and earlier-replication rules ('An earlier replication attempt that this paper does not itself re-test is NOT a target' and 'when the finding has already been re-tested by others, the target is the study closest to this paper in the chain — the one it actually re-tests — not the chain's original source') and consistent with the prior Argon adjudication (27dd47e58906b908bd5d7e819d1bcc262bbffaed7af4d2c61baf63107bc2bbfd.json; prior state file 5a996c5b...json is absent), Reference [4] (Kozel, Padgett, & George, 2004, Behav. Neurosci., 118(4), 852-856; blind target_index 1) is an earlier group-level replication of Reference [3] in the same replication chain culminating in Reference [5] (Kozel et al., 2005, Biol. Psychiatry, 58(8), 605-613, DOI 10.1016/j.biopsych.2005.07.040). The study closest to the 2009 paper in the chain—whose exact protocol and both individual and group Lie-minus-True analyses this paper directly re-tests—is Reference [5], which is already represented by retained shipped row input_row_index '660' (blind target_index 2). Note also that Luna's triage omission_quote failed mechanical validation because it spliced out text from lines 195-198."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0035_498d97fc3bf87bf8"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""shipped_right""}","{""authors"": ""Kozel, F. A.; Padgett, T. M.; George, M. S."", ""year"": ""2004"", ""title"": ""A replication study of the neural correlates of deception."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The Lie-minus-True group map revealed significant\nactivation in similar regions from the prior cohorts using this\nparadigm (see Figure and Table). Specifically, the regions of\nright orbitofrontal, right inferior frontal, right middle frontal,\nand right cingulate gyrus have replicated now in five\nindependent groups from four studies across several\nparadigms [3-5]."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""This method for detecting deception was successfully\nreplicated on a different scanner at a different location for\nboth the individual and group analyses."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The explicit overall verdict states successful replication for both group and individual analyses. It covers the group-level findings attributed to references 3–5, outweighing the specific left temporal contrast.""}"
10.1111/j.1469-1809.2009.00555.x,78b62459579bc203,0,10.1038/ng.125,Identification of ten loci associated with height highlights new biological pathways in human growth,Lettre G; Jackson AU; Gieger C; Schumacher FR; Berndt SI; Sanna S; Eyheramendy S; Voight BF; Butler JL; Guiducci C; Illig T; Hackett R; Heid IM; Jacobs KB; Lyssenko V; Uda M; Boehnke M; Chanock SJ; Groop LC; Hu FB; Isomaa B; Kraft P; Peltonen L; Salomaa V; Schlessinger D; Hunter DJ; Hayes RB; Abecasis GR; Wichmann HE; Mohlke KL; Hirschhorn JN,2008,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Lettre G; Jackson AU; Gieger C; Schumacher FR; Berndt SI; Sanna S; Eyheramendy S; Voight BF; Butler JL; Guiducci C; Illig T; Hackett R; Heid IM; Jacobs KB; Lyssenko V; Uda M; Boehnke M; Chanock SJ; Groop LC; Hu FB; Isomaa B; Kraft P; Peltonen L; Salomaa V; Schlessinger D; Hunter DJ; Hayes RB; Abecasis GR; Wichmann HE; Mohlke KL; Hirschhorn JN"", ""doi"": ""10.1038/ng.125"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Identification of ten loci associated with height highlights new biological pathways in human growth"", ""year"": ""2008""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""78b62459579bc203"", ""location"": ""Introduction, paragraph 2 (line 60); Results, 'Replication of HMGA2 in Caucasians' (line 139); Discussion, paragraph 1 (line 204); References (line 244)."", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Recent GWAS reports have revealed a novel variant rs1042725 in HMGA2 for human adult height in the European populations.( Lettre et al. , 2008 , Weedon et al. , 2008 , Weedon et al. , 2007 ) Replication of GWAS findings can be quite difficult, especially for the quantitative trait. In this study, we successfully replicated this finding both in the unrelated and family-based US Caucasian populations, which demonstrated the validity of the initial finding."", ""reason"": ""No prior adjudication or state files exist for this work. In 78b62459579bc203 (Yang et al., 2010), the authors explicitly frame their study as an independent replication of the HMGA2 rs1042725 association with adult height reported across three European GWAS—Weedon et al. (2007), Lettre et al. (2008), and Weedon et al. (2008)—noting in the Introduction that 'these studies are performed by the same groups, and the samples are mainly from northern Europe. Replications in multiple additional populations by independent groups are needed to evaluate the credibility and generality of this finding.' In both Results and Discussion, the authors directly compare their empirical replication results (direction of allele effect and statistical significance in unrelated and family-based US Caucasian cohorts) against all three GWAS citations together. Under the codebook rules (including replication chains, where subsequent primary GWAS reports in the chain that are directly retested are valid targets), Lettre et al. (2008) (Nat Genet 40:584-591, DOI 10.1038/ng.125) is a distinct primary empirical study from shipped row 662 (Weedon et al., 2007, Nat Genet 39:1245-1250, DOI 10.1038/ng2121) and is genuinely missing from the shipped rows."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0059_78b62459579bc203"", ""target_index"": 0, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Lettre G; Jackson AU; Gieger C; Schumacher FR; Berndt SI; Sanna S; Eyheramendy S; Voight BF; Butler JL; Guiducci C; Illig T; Hackett R; Heid IM; Jacobs KB; Lyssenko V; Uda M; Boehnke M; Chanock SJ; Groop LC; Hu FB; Isomaa B; Kraft P; Peltonen L; Salomaa V; Schlessinger D; Hunter DJ; Hayes RB; Abecasis GR; Wichmann HE; Mohlke KL; Hirschhorn JN"", ""year"": ""2008"", ""title"": ""Identification of ten loci associated with height highlights new biological pathways in human growth"", ""doi"": ""10.1038/ng.125"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Recent GWAS reports have revealed a novel variant rs1042725 in HMGA2 for human adult height in the European populations.( Lettre et al. , 2008 , Weedon et al. , 2008 , Weedon et al. , 2007 ) Replication of GWAS findings can be quite difficult, especially for the quantitative trait. In this study, we successfully replicated this finding both in the unrelated and family-based US Caucasian populations, which demonstrated the validity of the initial finding."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In summary, using data from ~11,000 subjects, we have confirmed the recently found association between HMGA2 and human adult height even across ethnic boundaries, in both Caucasian and Chinese populations, which highlights the importance of this gene involved in normal growth."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The paper explicitly groups these three GWAS as the finding being replicated and concludes confirmation across populations.""}"
10.1111/j.1469-1809.2009.00555.x,78b62459579bc203,1,10.1038/ng.121,Genome-wide association analysis identifies 20 loci that influence adult height,Weedon MN; Lango H; Lindgren CM; Wallace C; Evans DM; Mangino M; Freathy RM; Perry JR; Stevens S; Hall AS; Samani NJ; Shields B; Prokopenko I; Farrall M; Dominiczak A; Johnson T; Bergmann S; Beckmann JS; Vollenweider P; Waterworth DM; Mooser V; Palmer CN; Morris AD; Ouwehand WH; Zhao JH; Li S; Loos RJ; Barroso I; Deloukas P; Sandhu MS; Wheeler E; Soranzo N; Inouye M; Wareham NJ; Caulfield M; Munroe PB; Hattersley AT; Mccarthy MI; Frayling TM,2008,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Weedon MN; Lango H; Lindgren CM; Wallace C; Evans DM; Mangino M; Freathy RM; Perry JR; Stevens S; Hall AS; Samani NJ; Shields B; Prokopenko I; Farrall M; Dominiczak A; Johnson T; Bergmann S; Beckmann JS; Vollenweider P; Waterworth DM; Mooser V; Palmer CN; Morris AD; Ouwehand WH; Zhao JH; Li S; Loos RJ; Barroso I; Deloukas P; Sandhu MS; Wheeler E; Soranzo N; Inouye M; Wareham NJ; Caulfield M; Munroe PB; Hattersley AT; Mccarthy MI; Frayling TM"", ""doi"": ""10.1038/ng.121"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Genome-wide association analysis identifies 20 loci that influence adult height"", ""year"": ""2008""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""78b62459579bc203"", ""location"": ""Introduction, paragraph 2 (line 60); Results, 'Replication of HMGA2 in Caucasians' (line 139); Discussion, paragraph 1 (line 204); References (line 286)."", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Significant association of rs1042725 with height was successfully replicated both in the unrelated ( P = 1.58×10 −3 ) and family-based Caucasian samples ( P = 3.0×10 −7 ), even after adjusting for multiple testing (significance threshold: P < 2.94×10 −3 ). The allele effect was in the same direction as in the previous studies.( Weedon et al. , 2007 , Lettre et al. , 2008 , Weedon et al. , 2008 )"", ""reason"": ""No prior adjudication or state files exist for this work. In 78b62459579bc203 (Yang et al., 2010), the authors explicitly retest the HMGA2 rs1042725 adult-height association reported in three European GWAS—Weedon et al. (2007), Lettre et al. (2008), and Weedon et al. (2008)—and directly evaluate their new US Caucasian and Chinese empirical findings against all three studies in the Results and Discussion. Weedon et al. (2008) ('Genome-wide association analysis identifies 20 loci that influence adult height', Nat Genet 40:575-583, DOI 10.1038/ng.121) is a separate primary GWAS paper with distinct discovery cohorts and scope from shipped row 662 (Weedon et al., 2007, 'A common variant of HMGA2 is associated with adult and childhood height in the general population', Nat Genet 39:1245-1250, DOI 10.1038/ng2121), not a version-of-record variant of the 2007 article. It is therefore a genuinely missing replication–original pair."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0059_78b62459579bc203"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Weedon MN; Lango H; Lindgren CM; Wallace C; Evans DM; Mangino M; Freathy RM; Perry JR; Stevens S; Hall AS; Samani NJ; Shields B; Prokopenko I; Farrall M; Dominiczak A; Johnson T; Bergmann S; Beckmann JS; Vollenweider P; Waterworth DM; Mooser V; Palmer CN; Morris AD; Ouwehand WH; Zhao JH; Li S; Loos RJ; Barroso I; Deloukas P; Sandhu MS; Wheeler E; Soranzo N; Inouye M; Wareham NJ; Caulfield M; Munroe PB; Hattersley AT; Mccarthy MI; Frayling TM"", ""year"": ""2008"", ""title"": ""Genome-wide association analysis identifies 20 loci that influence adult height"", ""doi"": ""10.1038/ng.121"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Recent GWAS reports have revealed a novel variant rs1042725 in HMGA2 for human adult height in the European populations.( Lettre et al. , 2008 , Weedon et al. , 2008 , Weedon et al. , 2007 ) Replication of GWAS findings can be quite difficult, especially for the quantitative trait. In this study, we successfully replicated this finding both in the unrelated and family-based US Caucasian populations, which demonstrated the validity of the initial finding."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In summary, using data from ~11,000 subjects, we have confirmed the recently found association between HMGA2 and human adult height even across ethnic boundaries, in both Caucasian and Chinese populations, which highlights the importance of this gene involved in normal growth."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The paper explicitly groups these three GWAS as the finding being replicated and concludes confirmation across populations.""}"
10.1037/a0019128,28219495b9e5b5f0,1,,"Now you see it, now you don’t: On turning semantic interference into facilitation in a Stroop-like task.","Finkbeiner, M., & Caramazza, A.",2006,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Finkbeiner, M., & Caramazza, A."", ""doi"": """", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Now you see it, now you don’t: On turning semantic interference into facilitation in a Stroop-like task."", ""year"": ""2006""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""28219495b9e5b5f0"", ""location"": ""Experiment 2, Results and discussion (lines 555-562); see also Experiment 2 introduction (lines 425-429), Experiment 2 discussion (lines 579-582), and References (lines 893-894)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""We\nintroduced a control condition, in which distractors and picture were semantically related. If\ndistractors are not processed under masked conditions, no effect should be found with\nsemantically related distractors either. However, a semantic facilitation effect was present. As\nstimuli were presented under the same masking and timing parameters in both conditions, it\nseems unlikely that the same subjects could only process the distractors when they were\nsemantically related to the target. Also, the same semantic facilitation effect was found by\nFinkbeiner & Caramazza (2006) who used the same presentation parameters as we did."", ""reason"": ""No prior adjudication or state file exists for this work. In Experiment 2 (N = 40 Dutch speakers), Dhooge and Hartsuiker empirically retest the finding of Finkbeiner and Caramazza (2006) that picture-word interference with semantically related vs. unrelated distractors produces semantic interference when distractors are visible but reverses into semantic facilitation when distractors are masked using the same presentation timing parameters. Experiment 2 tests the relatedness by visibility interaction (F1(1,39) = 26.62, p < .001; F2(1,19) = 17.57, p < .001), confirms both visible semantic interference and masked semantic facilitation, explicitly compares the finding with Finkbeiner and Caramazza (2006), and states that 'by replicating the semantic facilitation effect, this experiment also provides further evidence for a non-competitive model of lexical selection.' The reference list (lines 893-894) identifies the primary study as 'Finkbeiner, M., & Caramazza, A. (2006). Now you see it, now you don’t: On turning semantic interference into facilitation in a Stroop-like task. Cortex, 42, 790–796.' (distinct from the 2006b reply commentary on lines 895-896), with no DOI printed in the source text. The sole shipped row (input_row_index 663, 10.1037/0096-3445.132.2.228, Miozzo & Caramazza, 2003, Journal of Experimental Psychology: General, 132, 228-252) represents the unmasked distractor-frequency original retested in Experiment 1 (sibling target_index 0). Because Finkbeiner and Caramazza (2006) is an independent study in Cortex testing a different empirical phenomenon (masked reversal of semantic interference into facilitation), report equivalence does not apply and the target is genuinely absent from the shipped rows. Luna's invalid table-formatted duplicate_quote was discarded and replaced with a verified contiguous primary-source quote preserving exact line breaks."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0017_28219495b9e5b5f0"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Finkbeiner, M., & Caramazza, A."", ""year"": ""2006"", ""title"": ""Now you see it, now you don’t: On turning semantic interference into facilitation in a Stroop-like task."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""To ensure that distractors were processed under masked conditions, we used the same time parameters as Finkbeiner and Caramazza (2006), and also included control conditions in which picture and distractor were either semantically related or unrelated. If distractors are processed, semantic facilitation should be found in the masked condition and semantic interference in the visible condition."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Also, the same semantic facilitation effect was found by Finkbeiner & Caramazza (2006) who used the same presentation parameters as we did."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": """"}"
10.1111/j.1469-8986.2010.01022.x,3a565148942156d6,1,,Retest reliability of medial frontal negativities during performance monitoring.,"Segalowitz, S. J., Santesso, D. L., Murphy, T. I., Homan, D., Chantziantoniou, D. K., & Khan, S.",2010,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Segalowitz, S. J., Santesso, D. L., Murphy, T. I., Homan, D., Chantziantoniou, D. K., & Khan, S."", ""doi"": """", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Retest reliability of medial frontal negativities during performance monitoring."", ""year"": ""2010""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""3a565148942156d6"", ""location"": ""Discussion, p. 1170 (lines 234-237); see also Abstract (lines 62-64), Introduction (lines 72-73, 90-92), Results (lines 215-220), and References (lines 319-322)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Current results replicate previous ﬁndings of moderate to\nmeans.                                                                    good temporal stability for grand mean ERN and Pe component\n                                                                          amplitudes over time (Olvet & Hajcak, 2009a; Segalowitz et al.,\n                                                                          2010)."", ""reason"": ""Larson et al. (2010) collect two-session flanker-task ERP data (N = 20 across a 2-week retest interval) to re-test the test-retest reliability (temporal stability) of grand mean ERN and Pe amplitudes, finding moderate to good temporal stability via intraclass and zero-order correlations (ERN ICC = .66, r = .49; CRN ICC = .75, r = .72; Pe ICC = .48, r = .32; ROI ERN ICC = .77, Pe ICC = .79) and explicitly concluding in the Discussion that their results replicate the temporal stability findings of both Olvet & Hajcak (2009a) and Segalowitz et al. (2010). The reference list (lines 319-322) identifies Segalowitz, S. J., Santesso, D. L., Murphy, T. I., Homan, D., Chantziantoniou, D. K., & Khan, S. (2010), 'Retest reliability of medial frontal negativities during performance monitoring', Psychophysiology (in press), with no DOI printed in the text. The sole shipped row (input_row_index 664) is Olvet & Hajcak (2009a) in Brain Research (DOI 10.1016/j.brainres.2009.05.079), which is a separate primary study by different authors in a different journal; no prior adjudication/state files existed at the supplied paths."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0133_3a565148942156d6"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Segalowitz, S. J., Santesso, D. L., Murphy, T. I., Homan, D., Chantziantoniou, D. K., & Khan, S."", ""year"": ""2010"", ""title"": ""Retest reliability of medial frontal negativities during performance monitoring."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Current results replicate previous ﬁndings of moderate to\nmeans.                                                                    good temporal stability for grand mean ERN and Pe component\n                                                                          amplitudes over time (Olvet & Hajcak, 2009a; Segalowitz et al.,\n                                                                          2010)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In a replication of\nprevious results, intraclass and zero-order correlations revealed moderate to good temporal stability for participants’\n(N 5 20) grand mean ERN and Pe component amplitudes."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The discussion explicitly identifies this paper within the set of findings replicated for temporal stability of grand mean amplitudes.""}"
10.4103/1463-1741.70502,29ee86af191172e9,0,,Habituation and dishabituation to speech and office noise.,"Banbury S, Berry DC",1997,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Banbury S, Berry DC"", ""doi"": """", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Habituation and dishabituation to speech and office noise."", ""year"": ""1997""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""29ee86af191172e9"", ""location"": ""Experiment 1, Discussion (lines 756-767); see also Experiment 1 introduction (lines 392-440, 495-528), Results (lines 674-702), and References (lines 1557-1559)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""The results from this study support\nthe findings from Banbury and Berry[14] showing that office\n\nnoise can disrupt performance on working memory tasks (i.e.\n\nmental arithmetic) but that this disruption can be habituated\n\nto after a period of time in noise. This study has gone on\n\nto show that participants fully habituated to the office noise\n\nfollowing 10 minutes intervening exposure."", ""reason"": ""No prior adjudication or state file exists for this work. In Experiment 1 (N = 36 undergraduates), Smith, Waters, and Jones empirically retest the laboratory finding of Banbury and Berry (1997, ref. 14) that background office noise with speech initially disrupts cognitive task performance and that prior exposure to office noise produces habituation that removes this disruption (testing 5-minute habituation blocks up to 20 minutes). Experiment 1 finds a significant noise condition by habituation block interaction (F(8,132) = 4, p < 0.05) with initial impairment in office noise eliminated after 10 minutes of prior exposure, and the Discussion explicitly compares these results to Banbury and Berry [14]. Reference 14 in the bibliography (lines 1557-1559) provides the exact identity: 'Banbury S, Berry DC. Habituation and dishabituation to speech and office noise. J Exp Psychol Appl 1997;3:181-95.' with no DOI printed in the source text. The only shipped row for this work is input_row_index 665 (10.1038/365611a0, Rauscher, Shaw, & Ky, 1993, 'Music and spatial task performance', Nature, 365, 611), which is the Mozart effect original tested in Experiment 2 (sibling target_index 1), and sibling target_index 2 is Thompson et al. (2001). Because Banbury and Berry (1997) is an independent study by different authors on office-noise habituation, report equivalence does not apply and it is genuinely absent from all shipped rows."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0018_29ee86af191172e9"", ""target_index"": 0, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""authors"": ""Banbury S, Berry DC"", ""year"": ""1997"", ""title"": ""Habituation and dishabituation to speech and office noise."", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1"", ""evidence_quote"": ""The first experiment reported in this article was designed specifically to extend the evidence provided by Banbury and Berry[14] addressing the issue of habituation to office noise, in an attempt to identify how long the filter takes to attenuate to the irrelevant sound stimuli."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The results from this study support the findings from Banbury and Berry[14] showing that office noise can disrupt performance on working memory tasks (i.e. mental arithmetic) but that this disruption can be habituated to after a period of time in noise."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly support the original habituation finding, now establishing its time course with a different working-memory task. The shorter time needed does not overturn that verdict.""}"
10.4103/1463-1741.70502,29ee86af191172e9,2,,"Arousal, mood, and the Mozart effect.","Thompson WF, Scbellenberg EG, Husain G",2001,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Thompson WF, Scbellenberg EG, Husain G"", ""doi"": """", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Arousal, mood, and the Mozart effect."", ""year"": ""2001""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""29ee86af191172e9"", ""location"": ""Experiment 2, Discussion (lines 1373-1380); see also Experiment 2 introduction (lines 1117-1168), Results (lines 1339-1360), and References (lines 1663-1665)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""There was evidence to suggest that the Mozart\n\neffect is not due to positive mood arousal as suggested by\n\nChabris[38] and Thompson et al.[36] Indeed, the mood ratings\n\nshowed no evidence that listening to Mozart increased\nalertness or hedonic tone."", ""reason"": ""No prior adjudication or state file exists for this work. In Experiment 2 (N = 24 undergraduates in a within-subjects design), Smith, Waters, and Jones empirically retest the published finding of Thompson, Schellenberg, and Husain (2001, ref. 36) that listening to Mozart's Sonata K448 increases positive arousal and mood ratings and that the Mozart effect on spatial-temporal performance is explained by positive arousal and mood. Experiment 2 directly measures participants' mood ratings (alertness, hedonic tone, and anxiety) before and after listening to Mozart K448, a positive mood induction, and silence alongside spatial-temporal performance on the RMPFB test, finding no effect of Mozart on alertness or hedonic tone and superior spatial performance after Mozart relative to both silence and mood induction, and explicitly concludes against Thompson et al. [36] (whereas Chabris [38] is explicitly described on lines 966-969 as a meta-analysis and excluded under the codebook). Reference 36 (lines 1663-1665) provides the literal source citation: 'Thompson WF, Scbellenberg EG, Husain G. Arousal, mood, and the Mozart effect. Psychol Sci 2001;12:248-51.' (with the literal transcription 'Scbellenberg EG' in the reference list and no DOI printed). The sole shipped row (input_row_index 665, 10.1038/365611a0, Rauscher, Shaw, & Ky, 1993, Nature, 365, 611) represents the original Mozart spatial-performance study (sibling target_index 1), while sibling target_index 0 is Banbury and Berry (1997). Thompson et al. (2001) is an independent empirical article in Psychological Science by different authors, so report equivalence does not apply and it is genuinely absent from the shipped rows."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0018_29ee86af191172e9"", ""target_index"": 2, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""authors"": ""Thompson WF, Scbellenberg EG, Husain G"", ""year"": ""2001"", ""title"": ""Arousal, mood, and the Mozart effect."", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""This study attempted to resolve the inconsistency found in the studies by Rauscher et al,[35,39] and the studies of Chabris[38] and Thompson et al.[36] This involved the following approaches."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""There was evidence to suggest that the Mozart effect is not due to positive mood arousal as suggested by Chabris[38] and Thompson et al.[36] Indeed, the mood ratings showed no evidence that listening to Mozart increased alertness or hedonic tone."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Experiment 2 retests the empirical mood-and-arousal explanation: the paper states Thompson's finding of higher positive mood after Mozart and directly measures this in the fresh sample. Its authors explicitly reject that explanation. The nonmusical positive-mood induction did not improve mood, a limitation discussed by the authors without an uninformative verdict. Chabris is a meta-analysis and excluded. The surname is copied from the supplied reference transcription and may contain OCR error.""}"
