10.1353/jhe.2015.0006,6b768bc6727abad5,0,,,,2004,abstract,,correct,argon,"{""corrected_value"": null, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""6b768bc6727abad5"", ""location"": ""Abstract, line 4"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""This study replicates and extends a 2004 content analysis of three major higher education journals. The original study examined the methodological characteristics of all published research in these journals from 1996 to 2000, recommending that higher education programs adjust their graduate training to better match the heavily quantitative and statistically sophisticated journal content."", ""reason"": ""Prior adjudication and state files were checked and do not exist (never-adjudicated work). The supplied source text is an abstract only, which states that the paper replicates and extends a single 2004 content analysis of three major higher education journals examining the methodological characteristics of published research from 1996 to 2000 and its implications for graduate training. Because the blind coder only had the abstract, the blind target recorded only year '2004' with blank authors and title. This single retested 2004 study is already represented by shipped row 706 (Hutchinson & Lovell, 2004, 'A Review of Methodological Characteristics of Research Published in Key Journals in Higher Education: Implications for Graduate Research Training', 10.1023/b:rihe.0000027392.94172.d2), so no additional original target is missing."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0052_6b768bc6727abad5"", ""target_index"": 0, ""verdict"": ""shipped_right""}","{""authors"": """", ""year"": ""2004"", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""This study replicates and extends a 2004 content analysis of three major higher education journals."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Our findings reveal a field that continues to be dominated by quantitative methods and which is increasingly using more advanced statistical techniques."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Abstract states the original found heavily quantitative and statistically sophisticated content, and the new sample continues quantitative dominance with increasingly advanced methods. This is an implicit agreeing comparison. Original bibliographic entry is absent from the abstract.""}"
10.1080/00913367.2015.1089426,bd919fa8b9034efe,0,,Tests of Graphic Visuals and Cigarette Package Warning Combinations: Implications for the Framework Convention on Tobacco Control,"Kees, Jeremy, Scot Burton, J. Craig Andrews, and John Kozup",2006,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 0, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""The authors use an advertising context to replicate and extend ﬁndings of Kees and colleagues’ (2010) study of the direct and indirect effects of graphic pictorial cigarette warnings."", ""location"": ""Abstract, p. 33"", ""reason"": ""The paper explicitly defines its replication target as Kees et al. (2010) and benchmarks its stimuli and hypotheses against that later study by the same author team; Kees et al. (2006) is an earlier study in the same chain cited for background and measures and mentioned alongside the 2010 study in the Study 1 discussion, not a separate target.""}","{""authors"": ""Kees, Jeremy, Scot Burton, J. Craig Andrews, and John Kozup"", ""year"": ""2006"", ""title"": ""Tests of Graphic Visuals and Cigarette Package Warning Combinations: Implications for the Framework Convention on Tobacco Control"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1a, 1b"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Despite extension to the use of updated, currently required text warnings, ad context, and (controversial) pictorial stimuli that were selected by the FDA, cessation-related outcomes replicate ﬁndings and conclusions of Kees and colleagues (2006, 2010)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Despite extension to the use of updated, currently required text warnings, ad context, and (controversial) pictorial stimuli that were selected by the FDA, cessation-related outcomes replicate ﬁndings and conclusions of Kees and colleagues (2006, 2010)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true}"
10.1177/0734282915580885,cf5667fa368c5800,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The present study reports on a replication of the technical adequacy of the Student Subjective Wellbeing Questionnaire (SSWQ), which is a 16-item self-report instrument for assessing youth’s academic efficacy, educational purpose, joy of learning, and school connectedness, with a sample of adolescents in Grades 6 to 7 ( N = 438)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Overall, this study provides a strong, positive replication of the technical adequacy of the SSWQ, suggesting that it is a psychometrically sound measure for school psychological research and, potentially, practice."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract gives an explicit overall positive replication verdict. Its unnamed original SSWQ validation is not bibliographically identifiable from the abstract alone, which does not reduce confidence in the stated outcome.""}"
10.1371/journal.pone.0121564,af4a8c309c18e227,1,,Attention please: No affective priming effects in a valent/neutral-categorisation task.,"Werner B, Rothermund K",2013,full_text,competing_supported_matches,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Werner B, Rothermund K"", ""year"": ""2013"", ""title"": ""Attention please: No affective priming effects in a valent/neutral-categorisation task."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We therefore decided to run a new experiment in which the essential ingredients of the experiments run by Werner and Rothermund [\n7\n] were combined with the typical priming parameters used by Spruyt and colleagues."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Still, the mere fact that we did obtain this effect under strict automaticity conditions is logically sufficient to reject the general assertion that the evaluative priming effect fails to replicate in the valent/non-valent categorization task [\n7\n,\n22\n]."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The paper directly adjudicates the conflicting published results using a new sample and combined procedural elements.""}"
10.1080/1357650x.2015.1016530,5f5efa4665888309,1,10.3389/fpsyg.2014.00134,Differences between left- and right-handers in approach/avoidance motivation: influence of consistency of handedness measures.,"Hardie, S. M. & Wright, L.",2014,full_text,,error,obvious,"{""field"": ""missing_row"", ""target_index"": 1, ""method"": ""obvious"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Hardie, S. M. & Wright, L."", ""year"": ""2014"", ""title"": ""Differences between left- and right-handers in approach/avoidance motivation: influence of consistency of handedness measures."", ""doi"": ""10.3389/fpsyg.2014.00134"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined""}, ""quote"": ""Our results replicate those of Wright et al. (2009) and Hardie and Wright (2014) that left-handers have higher BIS scores than right-handers while, as expected, there is no handedness difference for any BAS sub-scale. "", ""location"": ""Independent parent primary-source context; exhausted_candidate_0029.parent.json raw offsets"", ""reason"": ""Parent independently inspected prediction, new N86 sample, own Discussion and exact Hardie/Wright2014reference. Current authors explicitly replicate its handedness-direction finding and separately test/contrast its N689handedness-consistency/strength finding using comparable classifications. Shipped Wright/Hardie/Wilson2009N110is a different empirical study and lacks that later consistency finding; this is not a year/DOI-only difference or a duplicate report. New identity-only outcome remains unknown.""}","{""authors"": ""Hardie, S. M. & Wright, L."", ""year"": ""2014"", ""title"": ""Differences between left- and right-handers in approach/avoidance motivation: influence of consistency of handedness measures."", ""doi"": ""10.3389/fpsyg.2014.00134"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Our results replicate those of Wright et al. (2009) and Hardie and Wright (2014) that left-handers have higher BIS scores than right-handers while, as expected, there is no handedness difference for any BAS sub-scale."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Hardie and Wright (2014), using the type of classification scheme advocated by Prichard et al. (2013) and a number of different cut-off values to define consistent versus inconsistent handedness, found small but statistically significant effects of consistency or strength of handedness for their sample (N=689) as a whole (consistently handed individuals had higher BIS scores than those of inconsistent handedness). However, when they looked separately at right-handers and left- handers an effect was apparent among the 202 left-handers but not among 481 right-handers. Our own failure to find an effect of strength of handedness may, therefore, be a consequence of the relatively low number of left-handers (36) in our sample."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Direction finding confirmed; strength finding differs for Hardie and Wright. Wright et al. has an explicit overall replication verdict."", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """"}"
