10.1037/xge0000992,4d3349e34a8ca04b,1,10.1016/j.jesp.2004.09.001,Expectations and emotions of Olympic athletes.,"McGraw, A. P., Mellers, B. A., & Tetlock, P. E.",2005,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""McGraw, A. P., Mellers, B. A., & Tetlock, P. E."", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.jesp.2004.09.001"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Expectations and emotions of Olympic athletes."", ""year"": ""2005""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""4d3349e34a8ca04b"", ""location"": ""Introduction (Replication Study), lines 204-224; Results (Category-Based and Expectation-Based Counterfactual Results), lines 378-385; General Discussion, lines 1192-1196; References, lines 1284-1285"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""If medalists were using expectations formed prior to an Olympic\nevent (McGraw et al., 2005), we would expect that medal expectations would be correlated with the presence of smiles and they\nwere, Wald \""2(2) ! 4.316, p ! .014. Individuals performing better\nthan expectations were more likely to exhibit a smile (MBetter !\n2.660, SE ! 0.205) than people performing worse than expected\n(MWorse ! 0.899, SE ! 0.386), t(389) ! 3.863, p # .001. These\nresults suggests that both ex post and ex ante counterfactuals\ninfluenced medalists’ emotions."", ""reason"": ""Prior adjudication (b7f518d6f27c1b3209c4093ab5badc3771df5c04207bb4e66f6312dadcc3efb8.json) linked the sole shipped row (input_row_index 782: Medvec, Madey, & Gilovich, 1995, 10.1037/0022-3514.69.4.603) to blind target_index 0; the prior state file does not exist. The primary text explicitly frames its study as 'a conceptual replication of the first studies in Medvec et al. (1995) and McGraw et al. (2005)' (lines 204-205) using automated facial expression encoding of 413 Olympic medal stand photographs and Sports Illustrated medal predictions. In the Results section (lines 378-385), the authors empirically re-test McGraw et al.'s (2005) expectation-based (ex ante) counterfactual finding—that athletes performing better than prior expectations display more positive emotion (smiles) than those performing worse than expected—reporting a significant effect of medal expectations on smiles (Wald chi-square(2) = 4.316, p = .014; M_Better = 2.660 vs. M_Worse = 0.899, t(389) = 3.863, p < .001), and concluding in the General Discussion (lines 1192-1196) that their findings were consistent with both Medvec et al. (1995) and McGraw et al. (2005). Reference identity is verified in the reference list (lines 1284-1285) and Crossref metadata (10.1016/j.jesp.2004.09.001). Because McGraw, Mellers, & Tetlock (2005) is an independent study by different authors testing ex ante expectations in the 2000 Summer Olympics rather than an alternate report of Medvec, Madey, & Gilovich (1995, 1992 Summer Olympics ex post category comparisons), it is genuinely absent from all shipped rows."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0037_4d3349e34a8ca04b"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""McGraw, A. P., Mellers, B. A., & Tetlock, P. E."", ""year"": ""2005"", ""title"": ""Expectations and emotions of Olympic athletes."", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.jesp.2004.09.001"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In sum, this research demonstrated consistent findings with both Medvec et al. (1995) and McGraw et al. (2005) finding support for both ex post and ex ante counterfactual thinking among Olympic athletes."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In sum, this research demonstrated consistent findings with both Medvec et al. (1995) and McGraw et al. (2005) finding support for both ex post and ex ante counterfactual thinking among Olympic athletes."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The concluding verdict explicitly states consistent findings with both named targets.""}"
10.1186/s12886-022-02359-1,d025a0502d173534,1,10.1136/bjophthalmol-2018-312218,Analysis of multiple genetic loci reveals MPDZ-NF1B rs1324183 as a putative genetic marker for keratoconus.,"Wang YM, Ma L, Lu SY, Chan TCY, Yam JCS, Tang SM, et al.",2018,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Wang YM, Ma L, Lu SY, Chan TCY, Yam JCS, Tang SM, et al."", ""year"": ""2018"", ""title"": ""Analysis of multiple genetic loci reveals MPDZ-NF1B rs1324183 as a putative genetic marker for keratoconus."", ""doi"": ""10.1136/bjophthalmol-2018-312218"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In recently years, Wang YM and Hao XD et al. both analyzed the association of SNP locus with keratoconus in Chinese population, and revealed that the rs1324183 (\nMPDZ-NF1B\n), which located between the\nMPDZ\nand\nNF1B\ngenes, is a putative genetic marker for monitoring the progression of keratoconus and should be further investigated in other different Chinese populations [\n23\n,\n24\n]. Thence, in this study, we collected a total of 114 KC patients who live in northwestern of China for the first time and further confirmed the association between the rs1324183 (\nMPDZ-NF1B\n) and KC, providing a clear basis for early clinical diagnosis in Chinese population."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Notably, many studies have reported that the SNP rs1324183 (\nMPDZ-NFIB\n) is a genetic marker highly correlated with KC cases [\n23\n,\n24\n,\n30\n,\n31\n,\n33\n], and in our population, we have also further confirmed that the genotype AA of rs1324183 confers a higher risk for KC (Table\n2\n)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors explicitly confirm the shared SNP–KC association and risk genotype in their independent Chinese sample.""}"
10.1016/j.ynirp.2022.100149,8d5eccbe2686d4da,0,10.1038/s41380-020-0754-0,Brain aging in major depressive disorder: results from the ENIGMA major depressive disorder working group.,"Han L.K.M., Dinga R., Hahn T., Ching C.R.K., Eyler L.T., Aftanas L., Aghajani M., Aleman A., Baune B.T., Berger K., Brak I., Filho G.B., Carballedo A., Connolly C.G., Couvy-Duchesne B., Cullen K.R., Dannlowski U., Davey C.G., Dima D., Duran F.L.S., Enneking V., Filimonova E., Frenzel S., Frodl T., Fu C.H.Y., Godlewska B.R., Gotlib I.H., Grabe H.J., Groenewold N.A., Grotegerd D., Gruber O., Hall G.B., Harrison B.J., Hatton S.N., Hermesdorf M., Hickie I.B., Ho T.C., Hosten N., Jansen A., Kähler C., Kircher T., Klimes-Dougan B., Krämer B., Krug A., Lagopoulos J., Leenings R., MacMaster F.P., MacQueen G., McIntosh A., McLellan Q., McMahon K.L., Medland S.E., Mueller B.A., Mwangi B., Osipov E., Portella M.J., Pozzi E., Reneman L., Repple J., Rosa P.G.P., Sacchet M.D., Sämann P.G., Schnell K., Schrantee A., Simulionyte E., Soares J.C., Sommer J., Stein D.J., Steinsträter O., Strike L.T., Thomopoulos S.I., van Tol M.-J., Veer I.M., Vermeiren R.R.J.M., Walter H., van der Wee N.J.A., van der Werff S.J.A., Whalley H., Winter N.R., Wittfeld K., Wright M.J., Wu M.-J., Völzke H., Yang T.T., Zannias V., de Zubicaray G.I., Zunta-Soares G.B., Abé C., Alda M., Andreassen O.A., Bøen E., Bonnin C.M., Canales-Rodriguez E.J., Cannon D., Caseras X., Chaim-Avancini T.M., Elvsåshagen T., Favre P., Foley S.F., Fullerton J.M., Goikolea J.M., Haarman B.C.M., Hajek T., Henry C., Houenou J., Howells F.M., Ingvar M., Kuplicki R., Lafer B., Landén M., Machado-Vieira R., Malt U.F., McDonald C., Mitchell P.B., Nabulsi L., Otaduy M.C.G., Overs B.J., Polosan M., Pomarol-Clotet E., Radua J., Rive M.M., Roberts G., Ruhe H.G., Salvador R., Sarró S., Satterthwaite T.D., Savitz J., Schene A.H., Schofield P.R., Serpa M.H., Sim K., Soeiro-de-Souza M.G., Sutherland A.N., Temmingh H.S., Timmons G.M., Uhlmann A., Vieta E., Wolf D.H., Zanetti M.V., Jahanshad N., Thompson P.M., Veltman D.J., Penninx B.W.J.H., Marquand A.F., Cole J.H., Schmaal L.",2021,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,"{""field"": ""missing_row"", ""target_index"": 0, ""method"": ""obvious"", ""verdict"": ""undeterminable"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""Our previous ENIGMA MDD consortium study, the largest study of brain age in depression to date (N = 2126 controls and N = 2675 patients), showed a +1.08 year higher brain-PAD in depression (Cohen's d = 0.14), but with no evidence that this effect was driven by specific (clinical) characteristics such as age, age of onset, recurrence status, remission status, or antidepressant use (\nHan et al., 2021a\n). The subsequent addition of new cohorts to the ENIGMA MDD consortium since our previous brain age study provides us with an unique opportunity to perform an independent replication study in new data to validate our developed algorithm, as well as determine whether depression is consistently associated with older appearing brains (\nWrigglesworth et al., 2021\n)."", ""location"": ""Independent parent primary-source check; exhausted_candidate_0056.parent.json offsets"", ""reason"": ""Parent independently read the current new13cohort/N1517 brain-age analysis and exact Han2021a journal citation. The shipped799Schmaal2018conference abstract has the same ENIGMAbrain-aging title/core team; fresh official Crossref metadata confirms the conference record, but its publisher body is403unavailable and offers no sample/result proof. The available evidence does not settle preliminary/full-report equivalence. Different DOI/year/author order alone cannot certify either another original or identity. This missing-pair remains unverified; the existing final original-specific scope_primary_799_0006 retains its single remaining attempt and dependent outcome question. No duplicate scope, original substitution or outcome transfer is made.""}","{""authors"": ""Han L.K.M., Dinga R., Hahn T., Ching C.R.K., Eyler L.T., Aftanas L., Aghajani M., Aleman A., Baune B.T., Berger K., Brak I., Filho G.B., Carballedo A., Connolly C.G., Couvy-Duchesne B., Cullen K.R., Dannlowski U., Davey C.G., Dima D., Duran F.L.S., Enneking V., Filimonova E., Frenzel S., Frodl T., Fu C.H.Y., Godlewska B.R., Gotlib I.H., Grabe H.J., Groenewold N.A., Grotegerd D., Gruber O., Hall G.B., Harrison B.J., Hatton S.N., Hermesdorf M., Hickie I.B., Ho T.C., Hosten N., Jansen A., Kähler C., Kircher T., Klimes-Dougan B., Krämer B., Krug A., Lagopoulos J., Leenings R., MacMaster F.P., MacQueen G., McIntosh A., McLellan Q., McMahon K.L., Medland S.E., Mueller B.A., Mwangi B., Osipov E., Portella M.J., Pozzi E., Reneman L., Repple J., Rosa P.G.P., Sacchet M.D., Sämann P.G., Schnell K., Schrantee A., Simulionyte E., Soares J.C., Sommer J., Stein D.J., Steinsträter O., Strike L.T., Thomopoulos S.I., van Tol M.-J., Veer I.M., Vermeiren R.R.J.M., Walter H., van der Wee N.J.A., van der Werff S.J.A., Whalley H., Winter N.R., Wittfeld K., Wright M.J., Wu M.-J., Völzke H., Yang T.T., Zannias V., de Zubicaray G.I., Zunta-Soares G.B., Abé C., Alda M., Andreassen O.A., Bøen E., Bonnin C.M., Canales-Rodriguez E.J., Cannon D., Caseras X., Chaim-Avancini T.M., Elvsåshagen T., Favre P., Foley S.F., Fullerton J.M., Goikolea J.M., Haarman B.C.M., Hajek T., Henry C., Houenou J., Howells F.M., Ingvar M., Kuplicki R., Lafer B., Landén M., Machado-Vieira R., Malt U.F., McDonald C., Mitchell P.B., Nabulsi L., Otaduy M.C.G., Overs B.J., Polosan M., Pomarol-Clotet E., Radua J., Rive M.M., Roberts G., Ruhe H.G., Salvador R., Sarró S., Satterthwaite T.D., Savitz J., Schene A.H., Schofield P.R., Serpa M.H., Sim K., Soeiro-de-Souza M.G., Sutherland A.N., Temmingh H.S., Timmons G.M., Uhlmann A., Vieta E., Wolf D.H., Zanetti M.V., Jahanshad N., Thompson P.M., Veltman D.J., Penninx B.W.J.H., Marquand A.F., Cole J.H., Schmaal L."", ""year"": ""2021"", ""title"": ""Brain aging in major depressive disorder: results from the ENIGMA major depressive disorder working group."", ""doi"": ""10.1038/s41380-020-0754-0"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Our previous ENIGMA MDD consortium study, the largest study of brain age in depression to date (N = 2126 controls and N = 2675 patients), showed a +1.08 year higher brain-PAD in depression (Cohen's d = 0.14), but with no evidence that this effect was driven by specific (clinical) characteristics such as age, age of onset, recurrence status, remission status, or antidepressant use (\nHan et al., 2021a\n). The subsequent addition of new cohorts to the ENIGMA MDD consortium since our previous brain age study provides us with an unique opportunity to perform an independent replication study in new data to validate our developed algorithm, as well as determine whether depression is consistently associated with older appearing brains (\nWrigglesworth et al., 2021\n)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""This replication study using data from 13 cohorts around the world confirmed our previous findings that persons with major depressive disorder show advanced brain aging compared to controls by approximately +1 year."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Overall conclusion explicitly confirms the original brain-age gap using new independent cohorts. Cohort heterogeneity does not override that verdict.""}"
10.1002/jaba.908,7d4203391941be65,1,10.1901/jaba.1995.28-317,Training residential supervisors to provide feedback for maintaining staff teaching skills with people who have severe disabilities.,"Parsons, M. B., & Reid, D. H.",1995,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Parsons, M. B., & Reid, D. H."", ""year"": ""1995"", ""title"": ""Training residential supervisors to provide feedback for maintaining staff teaching skills with people who have severe disabilities."", ""doi"": ""10.1901/jaba.1995.28-317"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""The results of Study 2 extend ﬁndings of\nprevious studies (Parsons & Reid, 1995;\nShuler & Carroll, 2019), by demonstrating that\ntherapists’\nguided-compliance\nintegrity\nincreased after receiving performance feedback\nfrom one of the supervisors trained in Study\n1. All three therapists in Study 2 reached our\nmastery criterion following three to six feedback sessions (M = four sessions)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The results of Study 2 extend ﬁndings of\nprevious studies (Parsons & Reid, 1995;\nShuler & Carroll, 2019), by demonstrating that\ntherapists’\nguided-compliance\nintegrity\nincreased after receiving performance feedback\nfrom one of the supervisors trained in Study\n1. All three therapists in Study 2 reached our\nmastery criterion following three to six feedback sessions (M = four sessions)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true}"
10.1073/pnas.2202700119,67cac39d6ecd7223,1,,Experimental investigation of the subjective value of information in trading.,"Rafaeli S., Raban D.",2003,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""authors"": ""Rafaeli S., Raban D."", ""year"": ""2003"", ""title"": ""Experimental investigation of the subjective value of information in trading."", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""The current research is closest to a set of studies in information science conducted by Rafaeli and Raban (27, 28), who replicated the endowment effect for information in a game mimicking sales decisions. They gave participants the opportunity to buy and sell information, and observed greater willingness-to-accept prices than willingness-to-pay amounts. We extend their work in several critical ways."", ""outcome_quote"": ""However, we predicted that participants endowed with the three-fact bundle would be less likely than nonendowed participants to choose the four-fact bundle, because doing so would feel to them like a loss—rather than foregone gain—of three facts. Indeed, participants in the endowed condition were more likely to prefer learning just three facts (68%) than those in the nonendowed condition (46%) (χ2(1, n = 146) = 7.03, P = 0.008, Φ = 0.219) (Fig. 2)."", ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The paper explicitly extends the cited information-endowment experiments, addressing their monetary and instrumental confounds. Study 2 finds the predicted ownership preference in noninstrumental information, agreeing with their endowment finding. Target relation is conceptual and somewhat less explicit than the Asian Disease target.""}"
10.1073/pnas.2202700119,67cac39d6ecd7223,2,,The effect of source nature and status on the subjective value of information.,"Raban D. R., Rafaeli S.",2006,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""authors"": ""Raban D. R., Rafaeli S."", ""year"": ""2006"", ""title"": ""The effect of source nature and status on the subjective value of information."", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""The current research is closest to a set of studies in information science conducted by Rafaeli and Raban (27, 28), who replicated the endowment effect for information in a game mimicking sales decisions. They gave participants the opportunity to buy and sell information, and observed greater willingness-to-accept prices than willingness-to-pay amounts. We extend their work in several critical ways."", ""outcome_quote"": ""However, we predicted that participants endowed with the three-fact bundle would be less likely than nonendowed participants to choose the four-fact bundle, because doing so would feel to them like a loss—rather than foregone gain—of three facts. Indeed, participants in the endowed condition were more likely to prefer learning just three facts (68%) than those in the nonendowed condition (46%) (χ2(1, n = 146) = 7.03, P = 0.008, Φ = 0.219) (Fig. 2)."", ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The paper explicitly extends the cited information-endowment experiments, addressing their monetary and instrumental confounds. Study 2 finds the predicted ownership preference in noninstrumental information, agreeing with their endowment finding. Target relation is conceptual and somewhat less explicit than the Asian Disease target.""}"
10.1002/jaba.921,fadd7348ab03f217,1,10.1002/jaba.863,Machine learning to analyze single-case graphs: A comparison to visual inspection.,"Lanovaz, M. J., & Hranchuk, K.",2021,full_text,,error,obvious,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""obvious"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Lanovaz, M. J., & Hranchuk, K."", ""year"": ""2021"", ""title"": ""Machine learning to analyze single-case graphs: A comparison to visual inspection."", ""doi"": ""10.1002/jaba.863"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined""}, ""quote"": ""A secondary purpose was to replicate and extend Lanovaz and Hranchuk (2021) by examining correspondence between visual inspection, the conservative dual-criteria method, and the machine-learning algorithms on a novel dataset. We extended both studies by using clinical data and adding a 10-point scale with definitional criteria to visual-inspection ratings."", ""location"": ""Introduction"", ""reason"": ""The authors explicitly define a secondary purpose as replicating Lanovaz and Hranchuk (2021) on new clinical data, distinct from the primary Wolfe et al. (2018) comparison. The reference identifies DOI 10.1002/jaba.863; the sole shipped pair is Wolfe (10.1002/jaba.453). Parent reviewed the cited results/purpose and reference identity against all supplied shipped originals. This decision confirms the missing paper-level pair only; its overall outcome has not been adjudicated.""}","{""authors"": ""Lanovaz, M. J., & Hranchuk, K."", ""year"": ""2021"", ""title"": ""Machine learning to analyze single-case graphs: A comparison to visual inspection."", ""doi"": ""10.1002/jaba.863"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""A secondary purpose was to replicate and extend Lanovaz and Hranchuk (2021) by examining correspondence between visual inspection, the conservative dual-criteria method, and the machine-learning algorithms on a novel dataset."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In contrast, raters performed better on the clinical graphs than previously reported by Lanovaz and Hranchuk (2021) on simulated ones."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The discussion explicitly contrasts improved rater performance and the phase-length relation with this target; the predicted classifier agreement advantage was absent. No overall author verdict overrides these differences.""}"
10.1002/acp.4037,7086ab0209b0caf9,0,10.1002/acp.3301,Why education predicts decreased belief in conspiracy theories,"van Prooijen, J.-W.",2017,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""van Prooijen, J.-W."", ""year"": ""2017"", ""title"": ""Why education predicts decreased belief in conspiracy theories"", ""doi"": ""10.1002/acp.3301"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""The main aim of the study was to replicate and extend van Prooijen's findings on how education and its outcomes (cognitive complexity, subjective social standing, self‐esteem, a feeling of control and powerlessness, cognitive reflection, epistemic curiosity and scientific reasoning) predict conspiracy beliefs."", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract identifies the target and reports the present mediators and negative education association, but does not state which original mediator findings were confirmed or contradicted or supply an overall replication verdict. A shared education association alone cannot settle the replication of the multiple targeted pathways.""}"
10.1016/j.psychres.2024.115855,d39b63aa6fc0aca2,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Recently, we found hypomethylation of OXT in patients with Major Depressive Disorder (MDD) compared to healthy controls (HC). Since the replicability of findings is a key point of criticism in (epi‑)genetic research, we aimed to confirm our previous findings in a pre-registered study (data was stored in a database prior to pre-registration) within a new sample of n = 85 patients with MDD and n = 85 HC."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In accordance with our previous study, we found hypomethylation of OXT in patients with MDD compared to HC."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract explicitly confirms the original patient-control hypomethylation finding. The earlier paper is not identified by citation in the abstract.""}"
10.1098/rsos.250290,db1ca79d149b328f,1,10.1126/science.1253932,Avoiding overhead aversion in charity,"Gneezy U , Keenan EA , Gneezy A",2014,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Gneezy U , Keenan EA , Gneezy A"", ""year"": ""2014"", ""title"": ""Avoiding overhead aversion in charity"", ""doi"": ""10.1126/science.1253932"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Hypothesis 7, which found that participants were biased towards a charity where overhead is paid for by another donor even when cost-effectiveness is kept constant, acts as a conceptual replication of Gneezy et al . [ 16 ]."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Hypothesis 7, which found that participants were biased towards a charity where overhead is paid for by another donor even when cost-effectiveness is kept constant, acts as a conceptual replication of Gneezy et al . [ 16 ]. In contrast to the between-subject design that they used in their study, our extension shows that this effect occurs in a within-subject context as well."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors explicitly confirm the replicated finding; the overall verdict governs the Baron and Szymanska target.""}"
10.1371/journal.pone.0321397,4c6e097247f8884e,0,,Development of the Assessment of Physiotherapy Practice (APP): a standardised and valid approach to assessment of clinical competence in physiotherapy.,"Dalton M, Keating J, Davidson M.",2009,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""corrected_value"": null, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""4c6e097247f8884e"", ""location"": ""Introduction, lines 102-110; Methods (Factorial validity evidence), lines 194-196; References 3-4, lines 364-368"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""The original APP development work provided initial validity (e.g., dimensionality, differential item functioning, discriminant) and reliability (e.g., inter-rater) evidence [\n3\n–\n5\n]. The APP was designed to provide a unidimensional assessment of entry-level competency, whereby 20 items are aggregated into a single factor or total score representing overall minimal clinical competence to practice [\n3\n,\n4\n]."", ""reason"": ""Prior adjudication and state files at the supplied context paths do not exist (never-adjudicated work). In this multi-site archival replication study (N = 1,865 students across 8,979 clinical placements), the authors empirically re-test both the original 1-factor representation and the 2-factor representation (shipped row input_row_index 831, Reubenson, Ng, & Gucciardi, 2020, 10.1016/j.jphys.2020.03.009, Reference 7 / blind target_index 2) of the Assessment of Physiotherapy Practice (APP). However, blind target_index 0 (Reference 3: Dalton, Keating, & Davidson, 2009, Australian Learning and Teaching Council Final report PP6-28) and blind target_index 1 (Reference 4: Dalton, Davidson, & Keating, 2011, Journal of Physiotherapy 57(4):239-46) are duplicate citations of the same original 1-factor APP development and dimensionality work. The Introduction refers to References 3-5 in the singular as 'The original APP development work provided initial validity (e.g., dimensionality, differential item functioning, discriminant) and reliability (e.g., inter-rater) evidence [3–5]' and cites [3, 4] together for the unidimensional design. Methods (lines 194-196) identifies Reference 3 as 'the original protocol [3]', whereas Reference 4 is the peer-reviewed journal publication reporting the primary empirical cross-sectional Rasch dimensionality/validity study (and Discussion lines 300-306 cites [4, 5, 7], omitting [3], when naming the empirical validation evidence whose limitations this study addresses). Under the codebook rule to prefer the published empirical article over a protocol or preliminary/institutional report and the review instructions to retain only one canonical missing pair when two unmatched targets cite the same original work, target_index 1 (Dalton et al., 2011) is retained as the canonical missing original and target_index 0 (Dalton et al., 2009) is excluded as its duplicate. Note: Luna's omission_quote failed mechanical validation because it stripped literal newlines inside the bracketed citation."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0036_4c6e097247f8884e"", ""target_index"": 0, ""verdict"": ""shipped_right""}","{""authors"": ""Dalton M, Keating J, Davidson M."", ""year"": ""2009"", ""title"": ""Development of the Assessment of Physiotherapy Practice (APP): a standardised and valid approach to assessment of clinical competence in physiotherapy."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Our multi-site replication study supported the psychometric integrity of the 1-factor [\n3\n–\n5\n] and 2-factor [\n7\n] representations of entry-level physiotherapy performance utilising the APP."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Our multi-site replication study supported the psychometric integrity of the 1-factor [\n3\n–\n5\n] and 2-factor [\n7\n] representations of entry-level physiotherapy performance utilising the APP."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors explicitly support both structural representations in a different, broad multi-site cohort. Relative superiority of two factors does not negate their stated support for the one-factor representation.""}"
10.1371/journal.pone.0321397,4c6e097247f8884e,1,,The Assessment of Physiotherapy Practice (APP) is a valid measure of professional competence of physiotherapy students: a cross-sectional study with Rasch analysis.,"Dalton M, Davidson M, Keating J.",2011,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Dalton M, Davidson M, Keating J."", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""The Assessment of Physiotherapy Practice (APP) is a valid measure of professional competence of physiotherapy students: a cross-sectional study with Rasch analysis."", ""year"": ""2011""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""4c6e097247f8884e"", ""location"": ""Results (Measurement invariance evidence), lines 260-264; Discussion, lines 274-280; Reference 4, lines 367-368"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""We chose to examine and present the findings of the 1-factor model because it is the original operationalisation of APP for student performance [\n3\n–\n5\n] and commonly implemented among clinical education programs in ANZ. Overall, model-data fit statistics supported scalar invariance for both the 1-factor and 2-factor solutions across time and contextual factors."", ""reason"": ""Prior adjudication and state files at the supplied context paths do not exist (never-adjudicated work). Using a new multi-site archival dataset (8,979 clinical placement assessments from 1,865 physiotherapy students across 19 ANZ universities), the paper explicitly re-tests the factorial validity (confirmatory factor analysis) and longitudinal/contextual measurement invariance of the original 1-factor structural representation of the APP alongside the competing 2-factor representation. Results (lines 258-264, Table 4) present the measurement invariance tests for the 1-factor CFA model and find scalar invariance across time and contextual factors, and Discussion (lines 274-280) and Conclusion (lines 318-319) explicitly conclude that their multi-site replication study supported the psychometric integrity of both the 1-factor [3–5] and 2-factor [7] representations while finding relative superiority for the 2-factor model. Reference 4 (Dalton, Davidson, & Keating, 2011, J Physiother. 2011;57(4):239–46) is the canonical peer-reviewed journal publication reporting the original cross-sectional Rasch dimensionality/validity study establishing the 1-factor APP structure (whereas sibling target_index 0, Reference 3, is the 2009 ALTC institutional grant final report / original protocol for that same development project, and Reference 5 is the 2012 inter-rater reliability study not re-tested here). The only shipped row (input_row_index 831: Reubenson, Ng, & Gucciardi, 2020, 10.1016/j.jphys.2020.03.009) is Reference 7 (blind target_index 2), a different study by different authors that proposed the competing 2-factor model, so report equivalence with shipped row 831 does not apply. No DOI is printed for Reference 4 in the source text or Crossref metadata file, so doi is omitted. Note: Luna's omission_quote failed validation only because it omitted literal newlines inside '[\\n3\\n–\\n5\\n]'."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0036_4c6e097247f8884e"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Dalton M, Davidson M, Keating J."", ""year"": ""2011"", ""title"": ""The Assessment of Physiotherapy Practice (APP) is a valid measure of professional competence of physiotherapy students: a cross-sectional study with Rasch analysis."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Our multi-site replication study supported the psychometric integrity of the 1-factor [\n3\n–\n5\n] and 2-factor [\n7\n] representations of entry-level physiotherapy performance utilising the APP."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Our multi-site replication study supported the psychometric integrity of the 1-factor [\n3\n–\n5\n] and 2-factor [\n7\n] representations of entry-level physiotherapy performance utilising the APP."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors explicitly support both structural representations in a different, broad multi-site cohort. Relative superiority of two factors does not negate their stated support for the one-factor representation.""}"
10.1017/s0261444825100992,c93427e18d97d673,1,10.1177/1362168820938814,Commitment to the profession of ELT and an organization: A profile of expat faculty in South Korea.,"Moodie, I.",2023,full_text,,error,obvious,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""obvious"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Moodie, I."", ""year"": ""2023"", ""title"": ""Commitment to the profession of ELT and an organization: A profile of expat faculty in South Korea."", ""doi"": ""10.1177/1362168820938814"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined""}, ""quote"": ""Similarly, based on a prior\n               study (Moodie, 2023), Hypothesis 6 expected higher affective and normative commitment among\n               NESTs with formal teacher qualifications. These results did not replicate, indicating that teacher\n               qualifications do not necessarily translate into stronger commitment profiles among expatriate\n               NESTs."", ""location"": {""start_line"": 815, ""end_line"": 819, ""form_feed_page"": 16}, ""reason"": ""Hypothesis 6 directly retests Moodie (2023)'s teacher-qualification commitment finding and the discussion states that these results did not replicate. The reference identifies DOI 10.1177/1362168820938814. The sole shipped pair is McInerney et al. (2015), a different paper. Parent reviewed the cited results/purpose and reference identity against all supplied shipped originals. This decision confirms the missing paper-level pair only; its overall outcome has not been adjudicated."", ""quote_provenance"": {""kind"": ""new_independent_literal_primary_quotation"", ""inspection_result"": ""evidence_followup/integration/strict_literal_quote_inspection.result.json"", ""issue_index"": 165, ""source_path"": ""/home/lukas/.t3/worktrees/fred-data/audit-flora-blind-2026-10/audit/flora_blind_2026-10/texts/c93427e18d97d673.txt"", ""source_sha256"": ""b0549a16b7dd10000e6e785e7e8704bd8946b2869e534a14424b216774d34f1e"", ""raw_start_offset"": 70763, ""raw_end_offset"": 71143, ""scientific_verdict_unchanged"": true, ""reason"": ""Parent verified exact raw slice and semantic identity with the earlier accepted passage under its recorded display-normalisation policy. Independent primary-context inspector supported this passage. Original provider wording and whole prior decision retained in history; raw provider reply unchanged.""}}","{""authors"": ""Moodie, I."", ""year"": ""2023"", ""title"": ""Commitment to the profession of ELT and an organization: A profile of expat faculty in South Korea."", ""doi"": ""10.1177/1362168820938814"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Similarly, based on a prior study (Moodie, 2023), Hypothesis 6 expected higher affective and normative commitment among NESTs with formal teacher qualifications. These results did not replicate, indicating that teacher qualifications do not necessarily translate into stronger commitment profiles among expatriate NESTs."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Similarly, based on a prior study (Moodie, 2023), Hypothesis 6 expected higher affective and normative commitment among NESTs with formal teacher qualifications. These results did not replicate, indicating that teacher qualifications do not necessarily translate into stronger commitment profiles among expatriate NESTs."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The extension explicitly re-tests the published qualification–commitment result and reports it did not replicate.""}"
10.1177/0022022110381114,3531eb735322f77d,1,,"Self-awareness and other-awareness II: Mirror self-recognition, social contingency awareness, and synchronic imitation.","Asendorpf, J. B., Warkentin, V., & Baudonnière, P.- M.",1996,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""request_id"": ""scope_3531eb735322f77d_126"", ""file_key"": ""3531eb735322f77d"", ""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""The video recordings of the test phase were coded for the presence or absence of self-oriented\nbehavior and analyzed using a nonparametric, one-tailed binomial test with a threshold level set\nat .60 based on findings indicating that more than 60% of children older than 20 months will\nself-orient within this paradigm (see Amsterdam, 1972; Bertenthal & Fisher, 1978, Lewis &\nBrooks-Gunn, 1979)."", ""location"": ""Method (Dependent Measure, Analysis, and Reliability), p. 4 (also Introduction, p. 2)"", ""reason"": ""Independent inspection of both Broesch et al. (2010) and the full text of Asendorpf, Warkentin, & Baudonnière (1996; Developmental Psychology, 32(2), 313–321, DOI 10.1037/0012-1649.32.2.313) positively establishes that Asendorpf et al. (1996) is not an empirically retested target finding. Regardless of citation location, Asendorpf et al. (1996) did not investigate the 18-to-24-month developmental emergence of mirror self-recognition (MSR) or pass rates beyond 20 months: they tested a single cross-sectional age slice of 18.2- to 18.9-month-old Munich infants (M = 18.6 months; plus reanalyzed 19-month-olds from Asendorpf & Baudonnière, 1993), chosen on the basis of Lewis & Brooks-Gunn’s (1979) prior 18-month median benchmark so that roughly half the sample would be recognizers (45% immediate recognizers in the classic linguistic rouge test), in order to test (1) whether MSR is associated with sustained synchronic imitation and social contingency awareness and (2) whether modeling doll-cleaning with a tissue and verbally prompting children to clean their face reduces ambiguous non-touching cases at 18.6 months (from 26% to 11%, increasing total recognizers to 61%, chi-square(2, N = 98) = 4.38, p = .11). Broesch et al. (2010) do not test synchronic imitation, contingency awareness, or doll-cleaning/tissue prompting, and instead test 18- to 72-month-olds (Experiment 1, M = 41.62 months) and 36- to 55-month-olds (Experiment 2, M = 44.2 months) on an unprompted nonlinguistic sticker mark test against the >60% (>20 months) and >70%–88% (24+ months) age-emergence benchmarks established by Amsterdam (1972; shipped row input_row_index 843) and the longitudinal/age-series studies (targets 3, 4, 5, and 6).""}","{""authors"": ""Asendorpf, J. B., Warkentin, V., & Baudonnière, P.- M."", ""year"": ""1996"", ""title"": ""Self-awareness and other-awareness II: Mirror self-recognition, social contingency awareness, and synchronic imitation."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Numerous developmental studies of predominantly urban Western middle-class children suggest that children reach this milestone by 18 to 24 months of age. By 18 months, approximately 50% pass and by 24 months a significant majority of children (more than 70%) are reported to self- refer by touching or removing a mark from their own body (face or other body region) while exploring their mirror image (Amsterdam, 1972; Asendorpf, Warkentin, & Baudonnière, 1996; Bard, Todd, Bernier, Love, & Leavens, 2006; Bertenthal & Fisher, 1978; Lewis & Brooks-Gunn, 1979; Lewis & Ramsay, 2004; Nielsen, Dissanayake, & Kashima, 2003; Schulman & Kaplowitz, 1976). Here we report cross-cultural observations on the emergence of mirror self-recognition (MSR), using a simplified mark test, raising questions regarding the meaning and universal validity of this test as an ontogenetic benchmark of self-concept. The basic rationale behind the mark test is that when a child is marked surreptitiously and attempts to touch the mark on the body, as opposed to doing nothing or trying to reach for it on the mirror surface, it indicates that the specular image stands for the child’s own body. Self-oriented gestures are taken as the behavioral index of self-recognition, indicating that the embodied self is the referent of what is seen in the mirror (Rochat, 2003). In a different account, Nielsen, Suddendorf, and Slaughter (2006) claim that the mark test is passed by individuals who have a rapidly updatable image of themselves that they recognize in the mirror. Regardless of the rela- tive validity of these accounts, the general consensus is that passing the mark test indicates that children recognize their image and that those who fail the test do not. From a comparative perspective, chimpanzees, orangutans, dolphins, and Asian elephants are among the few nonhuman species reported to pass the mark test (Gallup, 1982; Plotnik & de Waal, 2006; Povinelli, 1995; Reiss & Marino, 1998). Nonhuman individuals passing the test typically have extensive prior experience with reflective surfaces. In contrast, research with humans suggests that children’s relative familiarity with mirrors, which greatly varies across contexts, does not correlate with the age at which the mark test is passed (Priel & deSchonen, 1986). Priel and deShonen (1986) tested Bedouin nomadic children with no previous mirror experience and compared them to same-age Israeli children familiar with mirrors. They found no significant difference in the developmental onset of MSR between mirror familiar and unfa- miliar children. However, more recent cross-cultural studies point to significant cultural variations in the onset of MSR. Keller and collaborators compared 18- to 20-month-olds from urban Greece, Costa Rica, Germany, as well as from a rural community in Cameroon, and they report a greater proportion of German, Greek, and Costa Rican children passing the test (more than 50%), compared to Cameroonian children (less than 4%) (Keller, Kartner, Borke, Yovsi, & Kleis, 2005; Keller et al., 2004). These authors correlate such variations to variations in par- enting strategies that exist across these cultures, fostering more or less autonomy in the young child. If such variability is validated with other cultures, testing children older than 18 to 20 months would allow us to determine whether this variability pertains only to a developmental delay, pos- sibly resulting from variations in parenting styles (see Keller et al., 2005; Keller et al., 2004) or whether such differences persist throughout development. If the latter is the case, this would sug- gest that there may be culture-specific responses to the passing of the mirror mark test (i.e., greater occurrence of potential false negatives). To probe this alternative explanation, we per- formed two experiments testing (a) whether cross-cultural variability in the passing of the mirror mark test could be verified across more cultures and (b) whether such variability would persist beyond the age at which children typically are reported to pass the mirror mark test. In two experiments, we tested children 18 to 72 months old living in seven distinct cultural groups. Because of the exploratory nature of this work, aside from probing these questions, no specific hypotheses were made."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The second experiment demonstrates that a significant amount of variability exists across cultural contexts in children’s behavioral response to this version of the MSR test that measures spontaneous self-recognition (see also Lewis & Ramsey, 2004). Consistent with past research, 77% to 88% of Western children (Canada and United States) pass this version of the MSR test by spontaneously displaying self-oriented behaviors. In contrast, only half of the children from Saint Lucia, Grenada, or Peru and none of the Fijian children of the same age showed any sign of such behaviors."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Western samples agree with the cited developmental benchmark; non-Western samples explicitly contrast with it. No overall replication verdict supersedes these comparisons.""}"
10.1177/0022022110381114,3531eb735322f77d,2,,Self-awareness in human and chimpanzee infants: What is measured and what is meant by the mark and mirror test?,"Bard, K. A., Todd, B. K., Bernier, C., Love, J., & Leavens, D. A.",2006,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,"{""request_id"": ""scope_3531eb735322f77d_126"", ""file_key"": ""3531eb735322f77d"", ""target_index"": 2, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""undeterminable"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""In 2006, Bard et al. compared spontaneous and\nprompted self-orienting behaviors of children in the mark test and reported that maternal prompt-\ning increased self-referential verbal labeling. Although no statistical tests were applied (n = 4),\nthese findings do suggest that verbal prompts may be needed to encourage self-referential behav-\nior and labeling (Bard et al., 2006)."", ""location"": ""General Discussion, p. 9 (also Introduction, p. 2)"", ""reason"": ""In the Introduction (p. 2), Broesch et al. (2010) cite Bard, Todd, Bernier, Love, & Leavens (2006; Infancy, 9(2), 191–219, DOI 10.1207/s15327078in0902_6) among eight studies attributed with the 18-to-24-month Western MSR pass-rate finding (~50% at 18 months and >70% at 24 months), whereas in the General Discussion (p. 9) they describe Bard et al. (2006) in terms of an n = 4 comparison of spontaneous versus maternally prompted self-orienting and verbal self-labeling. The supplied original source record for Bard et al. (2006) contains only the primary author abstract (level: primary_author_abstract), which confirms that the article reports a primary study with human infants comparing developmental and comparative mark-test procedures and maternal scaffolding in addition to the prompting subanalysis, but does not provide the full-text methods, infant age range, or unprompted mark-directed pass rates by age. Without the full text of Bard et al. (2006), the available evidence cannot settle the precise unresolved question of whether Bard et al.’s primary human-infant study empirically established the 18-to-24-month spontaneous mark-directed pass-rate benchmark retested by Broesch et al. (2010) or only tested procedural/scaffolding variations.""}","{""authors"": ""Bard, K. A., Todd, B. K., Bernier, C., Love, J., & Leavens, D. A."", ""year"": ""2006"", ""title"": ""Self-awareness in human and chimpanzee infants: What is measured and what is meant by the mark and mirror test?"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Numerous developmental studies of predominantly urban Western middle-class children suggest that children reach this milestone by 18 to 24 months of age. By 18 months, approximately 50% pass and by 24 months a significant majority of children (more than 70%) are reported to self- refer by touching or removing a mark from their own body (face or other body region) while exploring their mirror image (Amsterdam, 1972; Asendorpf, Warkentin, & Baudonnière, 1996; Bard, Todd, Bernier, Love, & Leavens, 2006; Bertenthal & Fisher, 1978; Lewis & Brooks-Gunn, 1979; Lewis & Ramsay, 2004; Nielsen, Dissanayake, & Kashima, 2003; Schulman & Kaplowitz, 1976). Here we report cross-cultural observations on the emergence of mirror self-recognition (MSR), using a simplified mark test, raising questions regarding the meaning and universal validity of this test as an ontogenetic benchmark of self-concept. The basic rationale behind the mark test is that when a child is marked surreptitiously and attempts to touch the mark on the body, as opposed to doing nothing or trying to reach for it on the mirror surface, it indicates that the specular image stands for the child’s own body. Self-oriented gestures are taken as the behavioral index of self-recognition, indicating that the embodied self is the referent of what is seen in the mirror (Rochat, 2003). In a different account, Nielsen, Suddendorf, and Slaughter (2006) claim that the mark test is passed by individuals who have a rapidly updatable image of themselves that they recognize in the mirror. Regardless of the rela- tive validity of these accounts, the general consensus is that passing the mark test indicates that children recognize their image and that those who fail the test do not. From a comparative perspective, chimpanzees, orangutans, dolphins, and Asian elephants are among the few nonhuman species reported to pass the mark test (Gallup, 1982; Plotnik & de Waal, 2006; Povinelli, 1995; Reiss & Marino, 1998). Nonhuman individuals passing the test typically have extensive prior experience with reflective surfaces. In contrast, research with humans suggests that children’s relative familiarity with mirrors, which greatly varies across contexts, does not correlate with the age at which the mark test is passed (Priel & deSchonen, 1986). Priel and deShonen (1986) tested Bedouin nomadic children with no previous mirror experience and compared them to same-age Israeli children familiar with mirrors. They found no significant difference in the developmental onset of MSR between mirror familiar and unfa- miliar children. However, more recent cross-cultural studies point to significant cultural variations in the onset of MSR. Keller and collaborators compared 18- to 20-month-olds from urban Greece, Costa Rica, Germany, as well as from a rural community in Cameroon, and they report a greater proportion of German, Greek, and Costa Rican children passing the test (more than 50%), compared to Cameroonian children (less than 4%) (Keller, Kartner, Borke, Yovsi, & Kleis, 2005; Keller et al., 2004). These authors correlate such variations to variations in par- enting strategies that exist across these cultures, fostering more or less autonomy in the young child. If such variability is validated with other cultures, testing children older than 18 to 20 months would allow us to determine whether this variability pertains only to a developmental delay, pos- sibly resulting from variations in parenting styles (see Keller et al., 2005; Keller et al., 2004) or whether such differences persist throughout development. If the latter is the case, this would sug- gest that there may be culture-specific responses to the passing of the mirror mark test (i.e., greater occurrence of potential false negatives). To probe this alternative explanation, we per- formed two experiments testing (a) whether cross-cultural variability in the passing of the mirror mark test could be verified across more cultures and (b) whether such variability would persist beyond the age at which children typically are reported to pass the mirror mark test. In two experiments, we tested children 18 to 72 months old living in seven distinct cultural groups. Because of the exploratory nature of this work, aside from probing these questions, no specific hypotheses were made."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The second experiment demonstrates that a significant amount of variability exists across cultural contexts in children’s behavioral response to this version of the MSR test that measures spontaneous self-recognition (see also Lewis & Ramsey, 2004). Consistent with past research, 77% to 88% of Western children (Canada and United States) pass this version of the MSR test by spontaneously displaying self-oriented behaviors. In contrast, only half of the children from Saint Lucia, Grenada, or Peru and none of the Fijian children of the same age showed any sign of such behaviors."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Western samples agree with the cited developmental benchmark; non-Western samples explicitly contrast with it. No overall replication verdict supersedes these comparisons.""}"
10.1177/0022022110381114,3531eb735322f77d,3,,Development of self-recognition in the infant.,"Bertenthal, B., & Fisher, K.",1978,full_text,,error,obvious,"{""target_index"": 3, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""obvious"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Bertenthal, B., & Fisher, K."", ""year"": ""1978"", ""title"": ""Development of self-recognition in the infant."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""mixed""}, ""quote"": ""The first experiment shows that only 2 out of 82 18- to 72-month-old Kenyan children\nmanifested self-oriented behaviors toward the mark, most of them freezing while staring at their\nspecular image. This result is in sharp contrast with the multiple studies reporting that by 20 months\nof age, 60% to 85% of Western middle-class children pass the mark test (Amsterdam, 1972;\nBertenthal & Fisher, 1978; Lewis & Brooks-Gunn, 1979). The question is how to explain this"", ""location"": ""Parent-selected exact primary passage, character offsets33353:33818; Discussion"", ""reason"": ""Bertenthal and Fisher (1978) is attributed not only in the introduction's 18-to-24-month Western benchmark set that the paper attempts to replicate across cultures, but also in the Method and General Discussion sections as a direct source of the >60% (60% to 85%) pass-rate benchmark tested via the binomial analysis and contrasted with the cross-cultural results. Rejecting it in favor of Amsterdam (1972) confuses Amsterdam's role as the original paradigm source with the empirical benchmark findings directly re-tested here. Parent independently read the actual primary Results/Discussion contrast and original-specific references. This new exact quotation replaces older whitespace-normalized evidence; raw model replies and prior decision remain preserved. This identity recheck preserves the previously independently verified outcome; no new outcome is inferred from the quoted identity passage.""}","{""authors"": ""Bertenthal, B., & Fisher, K."", ""year"": ""1978"", ""title"": ""Development of self-recognition in the infant."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Numerous developmental studies of predominantly urban Western middle-class children suggest that children reach this milestone by 18 to 24 months of age. By 18 months, approximately 50% pass and by 24 months a significant majority of children (more than 70%) are reported to self- refer by touching or removing a mark from their own body (face or other body region) while exploring their mirror image (Amsterdam, 1972; Asendorpf, Warkentin, & Baudonnière, 1996; Bard, Todd, Bernier, Love, & Leavens, 2006; Bertenthal & Fisher, 1978; Lewis & Brooks-Gunn, 1979; Lewis & Ramsay, 2004; Nielsen, Dissanayake, & Kashima, 2003; Schulman & Kaplowitz, 1976). Here we report cross-cultural observations on the emergence of mirror self-recognition (MSR), using a simplified mark test, raising questions regarding the meaning and universal validity of this test as an ontogenetic benchmark of self-concept. The basic rationale behind the mark test is that when a child is marked surreptitiously and attempts to touch the mark on the body, as opposed to doing nothing or trying to reach for it on the mirror surface, it indicates that the specular image stands for the child’s own body. Self-oriented gestures are taken as the behavioral index of self-recognition, indicating that the embodied self is the referent of what is seen in the mirror (Rochat, 2003). In a different account, Nielsen, Suddendorf, and Slaughter (2006) claim that the mark test is passed by individuals who have a rapidly updatable image of themselves that they recognize in the mirror. Regardless of the rela- tive validity of these accounts, the general consensus is that passing the mark test indicates that children recognize their image and that those who fail the test do not. From a comparative perspective, chimpanzees, orangutans, dolphins, and Asian elephants are among the few nonhuman species reported to pass the mark test (Gallup, 1982; Plotnik & de Waal, 2006; Povinelli, 1995; Reiss & Marino, 1998). Nonhuman individuals passing the test typically have extensive prior experience with reflective surfaces. In contrast, research with humans suggests that children’s relative familiarity with mirrors, which greatly varies across contexts, does not correlate with the age at which the mark test is passed (Priel & deSchonen, 1986). Priel and deShonen (1986) tested Bedouin nomadic children with no previous mirror experience and compared them to same-age Israeli children familiar with mirrors. They found no significant difference in the developmental onset of MSR between mirror familiar and unfa- miliar children. However, more recent cross-cultural studies point to significant cultural variations in the onset of MSR. Keller and collaborators compared 18- to 20-month-olds from urban Greece, Costa Rica, Germany, as well as from a rural community in Cameroon, and they report a greater proportion of German, Greek, and Costa Rican children passing the test (more than 50%), compared to Cameroonian children (less than 4%) (Keller, Kartner, Borke, Yovsi, & Kleis, 2005; Keller et al., 2004). These authors correlate such variations to variations in par- enting strategies that exist across these cultures, fostering more or less autonomy in the young child. If such variability is validated with other cultures, testing children older than 18 to 20 months would allow us to determine whether this variability pertains only to a developmental delay, pos- sibly resulting from variations in parenting styles (see Keller et al., 2005; Keller et al., 2004) or whether such differences persist throughout development. If the latter is the case, this would sug- gest that there may be culture-specific responses to the passing of the mirror mark test (i.e., greater occurrence of potential false negatives). To probe this alternative explanation, we per- formed two experiments testing (a) whether cross-cultural variability in the passing of the mirror mark test could be verified across more cultures and (b) whether such variability would persist beyond the age at which children typically are reported to pass the mirror mark test. In two experiments, we tested children 18 to 72 months old living in seven distinct cultural groups. Because of the exploratory nature of this work, aside from probing these questions, no specific hypotheses were made."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The second experiment demonstrates that a significant amount of variability exists across cultural contexts in children’s behavioral response to this version of the MSR test that measures spontaneous self-recognition (see also Lewis & Ramsey, 2004). Consistent with past research, 77% to 88% of Western children (Canada and United States) pass this version of the MSR test by spontaneously displaying self-oriented behaviors. In contrast, only half of the children from Saint Lucia, Grenada, or Peru and none of the Fijian children of the same age showed any sign of such behaviors."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Western samples agree with the cited developmental benchmark; non-Western samples explicitly contrast with it. No overall replication verdict supersedes these comparisons.""}"
10.1177/0022022110381114,3531eb735322f77d,4,,Social cognition and the acquisition of self.,"Lewis, M., & Brooks-Gunn, J.",1979,full_text,,error,obvious,"{""target_index"": 4, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""obvious"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Lewis, M., & Brooks-Gunn, J."", ""year"": ""1979"", ""title"": ""Social cognition and the acquisition of self."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""mixed""}, ""quote"": ""These results are in sharp contrast with what is reported with Western children. For\nexample, Lewis and Ramsay (2004), using a comparable procedure, report that over 80% of\nchildren by 21 months and 100% of children by 24 months of age pass the test by touching or\nremoving the mark (see also Lewis & Brooks-Gunn, 1979)."", ""location"": ""Parent-selected exact primary Results passage, character offsets19280:19601"", ""reason"": ""Lewis and Brooks-Gunn (1979) is directly cited for the 18-to-24-month Western empirical benchmark in the introduction, for the .60 binomial test threshold in the Method section, and as an explicit comparison in both the Experiment 1 results and General Discussion. Its reported developmental finding is re-tested on the same footing as Amsterdam (1972), which is distinguished only as the source of the original linguistic mark-test procedure. Parent independently read the actual primary Results/Discussion contrast and original-specific references. This new exact quotation replaces older whitespace-normalized evidence; raw model replies and prior decision remain preserved. This identity recheck preserves the previously independently verified outcome; no new outcome is inferred from the quoted identity passage.""}","{""authors"": ""Lewis, M., & Brooks-Gunn, J."", ""year"": ""1979"", ""title"": ""Social cognition and the acquisition o