c responses to the passing of the mirror mark test (i.e., greater occurrence of potential false negatives). To probe this alternative explanation, we per- formed two experiments testing (a) whether cross-cultural variability in the passing of the mirror mark test could be verified across more cultures and (b) whether such variability would persist beyond the age at which children typically are reported to pass the mirror mark test. In two experiments, we tested children 18 to 72 months old living in seven distinct cultural groups. Because of the exploratory nature of this work, aside from probing these questions, no specific hypotheses were made."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The second experiment demonstrates that a significant amount of variability exists across cultural contexts in children’s behavioral response to this version of the MSR test that measures spontaneous self-recognition (see also Lewis & Ramsey, 2004). Consistent with past research, 77% to 88% of Western children (Canada and United States) pass this version of the MSR test by spontaneously displaying self-oriented behaviors. In contrast, only half of the children from Saint Lucia, Grenada, or Peru and none of the Fijian children of the same age showed any sign of such behaviors."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Western samples agree with the cited developmental benchmark; non-Western samples explicitly contrast with it. No overall replication verdict supersedes these comparisons.""}"
10.1177/0022022110381114,3531eb735322f77d,6,,A longitudinal investigation of self-other discrimination and the emergence of mirror self-recognition.,"Nielsen, M., Dissanayake, C., & Kashima, Y.",2003,full_text,,error,obvious,"{""target_index"": 6, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""obvious"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Nielsen, M., Dissanayake, C., & Kashima, Y."", ""year"": ""2003"", ""title"": ""A longitudinal investigation of self-other discrimination and the emergence of mirror self-recognition."", ""doi"": ""10.1016/S0163-6383(03)00018-3"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined""}, ""quote"": ""Numerous developmental studies of predominantly urban Western middle-class children suggest\nthat children reach this milestone by 18 to 24 months of age. By 18 months, approximately 50%\npass and by 24 months a significant majority of children (more than 70%) are reported to self-\nrefer by touching or removing a mark from their own body (face or other body region) while\nexploring their mirror image (Amsterdam, 1972; Asendorpf, Warkentin, & Baudonnière, 1996;\nBard, Todd, Bernier, Love, & Leavens, 2006; Bertenthal & Fisher, 1978; Lewis & Brooks-Gunn,\n1979; Lewis & Ramsay, 2004; Nielsen, Dissanayake, & Kashima, 2003; Schulman & Kaplowitz,\n1976). Here we report cross-cultural observations on the emergence of mirror self-recognition\n(MSR), using a simplified mark test, raising questions regarding the meaning and universal\nvalidity of this test as an ontogenetic benchmark of self-concept."", ""location"": ""Independent raw primary benchmark/replication-purpose context; original identity evidence in strict_literal_parent_applied_0007.json"", ""reason"": ""The current paper explicitly attributes the18–24month Western mark-test benchmark to Nielsen/Dissanayake/Kashima2003 and tests that specific benchmark in newly observed cultural samples. NEW complete original author-hosted paper independently confirms all three authors, exact longitudinal self-other/MSR title,98infants observed9–24months, sticker mark testing and mean/median/modal onset18months. This is substantive original benchmark data, not merely a theoretical self-concept citation. It is absent from all shipped counterparts. Current Methods names a DIFFERENT two-author Nielsen/Dissanayake2003report; no claim that this is a citation to the three-author original is retained. New original evidence corroborates the benchmark independently, answering that earlier source objection without another unchanged model call. Identity is confirmed; original-specific cross-cultural outcome remains undetermined rather than carrying forward an unsupported mixed label."", ""quote_provenance"": {""kind"": ""new_independent_literal_primary_quotation"", ""issue_index"": 166, ""source_sha256"": ""77f7119e8b349e831610e237949a2f349bacf3e6dbc033527b56e9ce0b73fbc2"", ""raw_start_offset"": 5387, ""raw_end_offset"": 6281, ""quote_sha256"": ""7aff4d0663510c8433d520dfe0cde08a3aecd4c25799eff2d700f4dbdb6be161"", ""scientific_identity_confirmed"": true, ""unsupported_legacy_outcome_withheld"": true}}","{""authors"": ""Nielsen, M., Dissanayake, C., & Kashima, Y."", ""year"": ""2003"", ""title"": ""A longitudinal investigation of self-other discrimination and the emergence of mirror self-recognition."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Numerous developmental studies of predominantly urban Western middle-class children suggest that children reach this milestone by 18 to 24 months of age. By 18 months, approximately 50% pass and by 24 months a significant majority of children (more than 70%) are reported to self- refer by touching or removing a mark from their own body (face or other body region) while explor