e second experiment demonstrates that a significant amount of variability exists across cultural contexts in children’s behavioral response to this version of the MSR test that measures spontaneous self-recognition (see also Lewis & Ramsey, 2004). Consistent with past research, 77% to 88% of Western children (Canada and United States) pass this version of the MSR test by spontaneously displaying self-oriented behaviors. In contrast, only half of the children from Saint Lucia, Grenada, or Peru and none of the Fijian children of the same age showed any sign of such behaviors."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Western samples agree with the cited developmental benchmark; non-Western samples explicitly contrast with it. No overall replication verdict supersedes these comparisons.""}"
10.1177/0022022110381114,3531eb735322f77d,7,,Mirror-image response during the first two years of life.,"Schulman, A. H., & Kaplowitz, C.",1976,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,"{""request_id"": ""scope_3531eb735322f77d_7"", ""file_key"": ""3531eb735322f77d"", ""target_index"": 7, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""undeterminable"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""Numerous developmental studies of predominantly urban Western middle-class children suggest\nthat children reach this milestone by 18 to 24 months of age. By 18 months, approximately 50%\npass and by 24 months a significant majority of children (more than 70%) are reported to self-\nrefer by touching or removing a mark from their own body (face or other body region) while\nexploring their mirror image (Amsterdam, 1972; Asendorpf, Warkentin, & Baudonnière, 1996;\nBard, Todd, Bernier, Love, & Leavens, 2006; Bertenthal & Fisher, 1978; Lewis & Brooks-Gunn,\n1979; Lewis & Ramsay, 2004; Nielsen, Dissanayake, & Kashima, 2003; Schulman & Kaplowitz,\n1976). Here we report cross-cultural observations on the emergence of mirror self-recognition\n(MSR), using a simplified mark test, raising questions regarding the meaning and universal\nvalidity of this test as an ontogenetic benchmark of self-concept."", ""location"": ""Introduction, p. 2; cf. Method (Dependent Measure, Analysis, and Reliability, p. 4), Experiment 1 Results and Discussion (p. 5), General Discussion (pp. 8-9), and References (p. 12)"", ""reason"": ""Addressing the unresolved scope objection: citation location or frequency alone cannot establish background status, nor can inclusion in an introductory citation cluster automatically establish a retested target. On p. 2, Broesch et al. (2010) cite Schulman and Kaplowitz (1976, \""Mirror-image response during the first two years of life\"", Developmental Psychobiology, 10, 133-142) solely inside an eight-study omnibus parenthetical supporting the summary statement that by 18 months ~50% of Western children pass and by 24 months >70% self-refer by touching or removing a mark on their body. Primary sources supplied for sibling targets in that same eight-study cluster (asendorpf1996.txt for target_index 1, bard2006.txt for target_index 2, and nielsen2003.txt for target_index 6) demonstrate that this cluster pools heterogeneous works—for example, Asendorpf et al. (1996) tested synchronic imitation only at 18 months and Bard et al. (2006) tested procedural/scaffolding variations—rather than eight uniform benchmark reports. Conversely, for the retained benchmark studies, Broesch et al. (2010) explicitly state the specific empirical findings tested by their 18-to-72-month cross-cultural mark-test experiments: Amsterdam (1972; shipped input_row_index 843), Bertenthal and Fisher (1978; target_index 3), and Lewis and Brooks-Gunn (1979; target_index 4) are explicitly identified as reporting that >60% (60% to 85%) of Western middle-class children older than 20 months pass the mark test, which directly defines the .60 probability threshold tested in the one-tailed binomial analyses of Experiments 1 and 2 (Method, p. 4; General Discussion, p. 8), and Lewis and Ramsay (2004; target_index 5) is explicitly identified as reporting >80% passing by 21 months and 100% by 24 months using a comparable spontaneous mark-test procedure (Results, p. 5; General Discussion, p. 9). Unlike those retained studies, Broesch et al. (2010) never state an individual empirical finding from Schulman and Kaplowitz (1976), and unlike targets 1, 2, and 6, no primary text or abstract of Schulman and Kaplowitz (1976) is present in the supplied evidence. The precise unresolved question is whether Schulman and Kaplowitz (1976) itself reported the specific 18-to-24-month mark-directed self-recognition pass-rate finding retested in Experiments 1 and 2 or was cited only for general developmental background on infant mirror-image responses.""}","{""authors"": ""Schulman, A. H., & Kaplowitz, C."", ""year"": ""1976"", ""title"": ""Mirror-image response during the first two years of life."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Numerous developmental studies of predominantly urban Western middle-class children suggest that children reach this milestone by 18 to 24 months of age. By 18 months, approximately 50% pass and by 24 months a significant majority of children (more than 70%) are reported to self- refer by touching or removing a mark from their own body (face or other body region) while exploring their mirror image (Amsterdam, 1972; Asendorpf, Warkentin, & Baudonnière, 1996; Bard, Todd, Bernier, Love, & Leavens, 2006; Bertenthal & Fisher, 1978; Lewis & Brooks-Gunn, 1979; Lewis & Ramsay, 2004; Nielsen, Dissanayake, & Kashima, 2003; Schulman & Kaplowitz, 1976). Here we report cross-cultural observations on the emergence of mirror self-recognition (MSR), using a simplified mark test, raising questions regarding the meaning and universal validity of this test as an ontogenetic benchmark of self-concept. The basic rationale behind the mark test is that when a child is marked surreptitiously and attempts to touch the mark on the body, as opposed to doing nothing or trying to reach for it on the mirror surface, it indicates that the specular image stands for the child’s own body. Self-oriented gestures are taken as the behavioral index of self-recognition, indicating that the embodied self is the referent of what is seen in the mirror (Rochat, 2003). In a different account, Nielsen, Suddendorf, and Slaughter (2006) claim that the mark test is passed by individuals who have a rapidly updatable image of themselves that they recognize in the mirror. Regardless of the rela- tive validity of these accounts, the general consensus is that passing the mark test indicates that children recognize their image and that those who fail the test do not. From a comparative perspective, chimpanzees, orangutans, dolphins, and Asian elephants are among the few nonhuman species reported to pass the mark test (Gallup, 1982; Plotnik & de Waal, 2006; Povinelli, 1995; Reiss & Marino, 1998). Nonhuman individuals passing the test typically have extensive prior experience with reflective surfaces. In contrast, research with humans suggests that children’s relative familiarity with mirrors, which greatly varies across contexts, does not correlate with the age at which the mark test is passed (Priel & deSchonen, 1986). Priel and deShonen (1986) tested Bedouin nomadic children with no previous mirror experience and compared them to same-age Israeli children familiar with mirrors. They found no significant difference in the developmental onset of MSR between mirror familiar and unfa- miliar children. However, more recent cross-cultural studies point to significant cultural variations in the onset of MSR. Keller and collaborators compared 18- to 20-month-olds from urban Greece, Costa Rica, Germany, as well as from a rural community in Cameroon, and they report a greater proportion of German, Greek, and Costa Rican children passing the test (more than 50%), compared to Cameroonian children (less than 4%) (Keller, Kartner, Borke, Yovsi, & Kleis, 2005; Keller et al., 2004). These authors correlate such variations to variations in par- enting strategies that exist across these cultures, fostering more or less autonomy in the young child. If such variability is validated with other cultures, testing children older than 18 to 20 months would allow us to determine whether this variability pertains only to a developmental delay, pos- sibly resulting from variations in parenting styles (see Keller et al., 2005; Keller et al., 2004) or whether such differences persist throughout development. If the latter is the case, this would sug- gest that there may be culture-specific responses to the passing of the mirror mark test (i.e., greater occurrence of potential false negatives). To probe this alternative explanation, we per- formed two experiments testing (a) whether cross-cultural variability in the passing of the mirror mark test could be verified across more cultures and (b) whether such variability would persist beyond the age at which children typically are reported to pass the mirror mark test. In two experiments, we tested children 18 to 72 months old living in seven distinct cultural groups. Because of the exploratory nature of this work, aside from probing these questions, no specific hypotheses were made."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The second experiment demonstrates that a significant amount of variability exists across cultural contexts in children’s behavioral response to this version of the MSR test that measures spontaneous self-recognition (see also Lewis & Ramsey, 2004). Consistent with past research, 77% to 88% of Western children (Canada and United States) pass this version of the MSR test by spontaneously displaying self-oriented behaviors. In contrast, only half of the children from Saint Lucia, Grenada, or Peru and none of the Fijian children of the same age showed any sign of such behaviors."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Western samples agree with the cited developmental benchmark; non-Western samples explicitly contrast with it. No overall replication verdict supersedes these comparisons.""}"
10.1177/0022022110381114,3531eb735322f77d,8,,Parenting styles and the development of the categorical self: A longitudinal study on mirror self-recognition in Cameroonian Nso and German families.,"Keller, H., Kartner, J., Borke, J., Yovsi, R., & Kleis, A.",2005,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 8, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""However, more recent cross-cultural studies point to significant cultural variations in the onset of MSR. Keller and collaborators compared 18- to 20-month-olds from urban Greece, Costa Rica, Germany, as well as from a rural community in Cameroon, and they report a greater proportion of German, Greek, and Costa Rican children passing the test (more than 50%), compared to Cameroonian children (less than 4%) (Keller, Kartner, Borke, Yovsi, & Kleis, 2005; Keller et al., 2004)."", ""location"": ""introduction, p. 2"", ""reason"": ""Keller et al. (2005) is cited as prior cross-cultural context and for its contrasting parenting-style developmental-delay explanation, whereas the authors explicitly frame their study as replicating the classic Western 18- to 24-month mark-test benchmark (Amsterdam, 1972) across cultures and older ages.""}","{""authors"": ""Keller, H., Kartner, J., Borke, J., Yovsi, R., & Kleis, A."", ""year"": ""2005"", ""title"": ""Parenting styles and the development of the categorical self: A longitudinal study on mirror self-recognition in Cameroonian Nso and German families."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Numerous developmental studies of predominantly urban Western middle-class children suggest that children reach this milestone by 18 to 24 months of age. By 18 months, approximately 50% pass and by 24 months a significant majority of children (more than 70%) are reported to self- refer by touching or removing a mark from their own body (face or other body region) while exploring their mirror image (Amsterdam, 1972; Asendorpf, Warkentin, & Baudonnière, 1996; Bard, Todd, Bernier, Love, & Leavens, 2006; Bertenthal & Fisher, 1978; Lewis & Brooks-Gunn, 1979; Lewis & Ramsay, 2004; Nielsen, Dissanayake, & Kashima, 2003; Schulman & Kaplowitz, 1976). Here we report cross-cultural observations on the emergence of mirror self-recognition (MSR), using a simplified mark test, raising questions regarding the meaning and universal validity of this test as an ontogenetic benchmark of self-concept. The basic rationale behind the mark test is that when a child is marked surreptitiously and attempts to touch the mark on the body, as opposed to doing nothing or trying to reach for it on the mirror surface, it indicates that the specular image stands for the child’s own body. Self-oriented gestures are taken as the behavioral index of self-recognition, indicating that the embodied self is the referent of what is seen in the mirror (Rochat, 2003). In a different account, Nielsen, Suddendorf, and Slaughter (2006) claim that the mark test is passed by individuals who have a rapidly updatable image of themselves that they recognize in the mirror. Regardless of the rela- tive validity of these accounts, the general consensus is that passing the mark test indicates that children recognize their image and that those who fail the test do not. From a comparative perspective, chimpanzees, orangutans, dolphins, and Asian elephants are among the few nonhuman species reported to pass the mark test (Gallup, 1982; Plotnik & de Waal, 2006; Povinelli, 1995; Reiss & Marino, 1998). Nonhuman individuals passing the test typically have extensive prior experience with reflective surfaces. In contrast, research with humans suggests that children’s relative familiarity with mirrors, which greatly varies across contexts, does not correlate with the age at which the mark test is passed (Priel & deSchonen, 1986). Priel and deShonen (1986) tested Bedouin nomadic children with no previous mirror experience and compared them to same-age Israeli children familiar with mirrors. They found no significant difference in the developmental onset of MSR between mirror familiar and unfa- miliar children. However, more recent cross-cultural studies point to significant cultural variations in the onset of MSR. Keller and collaborators compared 18- to 20-month-olds from urban Greece, Costa Rica, Germany, as well as from a rural community in Cameroon, and they report a greater proportion of German, Greek, and Costa Rican children passing the test (more than 50%), compared to Cameroonian children (less than 4%) (Keller, Kartner, Borke, Yovsi, & Kleis, 2005; Keller et al., 2004). These authors correlate such variations to variations in par- enting strategies that exist across these cultures, fostering more or less autonomy in the young child. If such variability is validated with other cultures, testing children older than 18 to 20 months would allow us to determine whether this variability pertains only to a developmental delay, pos- sibly resulting from variations in parenting styles (see Keller et al., 2005; Keller et al., 2004) or whether such differences persist throughout development. If the latter is the case, this would sug- gest that there may be culture-specific responses to the passing of the mirror mark test (i.e., greater occurrence of potential false negatives). To probe this alternative explanation, we per- formed two experiments testing (a) whether cross-cultural variability in the passing of the mirror mark test could be verified across more cultures and (b) whether such variability would persist beyond the age at which children typically are reported to pass the mirror mark test. In two experiments, we tested children 18 to 72 months old living in seven distinct cultural groups. Because of the exploratory nature of this work, aside from probing these questions, no specific hypotheses were made."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The second experiment demonstrates that a significant amount of variability exists across cultural contexts in children’s behavioral response to this version of the MSR test that measures spontaneous self-recognition (see also Lewis & Ramsey, 2004). Consistent with past research, 77% to 88% of Western children (Canada and United States) pass this version of the MSR test by spontaneously displaying self-oriented behaviors. In contrast, only half of the children from Saint Lucia, Grenada, or Peru and none of the Fijian children of the same age showed any sign of such behaviors."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Cross-cultural variability is confirmed.""}"
10.1177/0022022110381114,3531eb735322f77d,9,,Developmental consequences of early parenting experiences: Self-recognition and self-regulation in three cultural communities.,"Keller, H., Yovsi, R., Borke, J., Kartner, J., Henning, J., & Zaira, P.",2004,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 9, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""However, more recent cross-cultural studies point to significant cultural variations in the onset of MSR. Keller and collaborators compared 18- to 20-month-olds from urban Greece, Costa Rica, Germany, as well as from a rural community in Cameroon, and they report a greater proportion of German, Greek, and Costa Rican children passing the test (more than 50%), compared to Cameroonian children (less than 4%) (Keller, Kartner, Borke, Yovsi, & Kleis, 2005; Keller et al., 2004)."", ""location"": ""introduction, p. 2"", ""reason"": ""Keller et al. (2004) is cited as motivating cross-cultural background and for its parenting-style explanation of MSR onset, not as the specific target finding re-tested by this study's mark-test experiments.""}","{""authors"": ""Keller, H., Yovsi, R., Borke, J., Kartner, J., Henning, J., & Zaira, P."", ""year"": ""2004"", ""title"": ""Developmental consequences of early parenting experiences: Self-recognition and self-regulation in three cultural communities."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Numerous developmental studies of predominantly urban Western middle-class children suggest that children reach this milestone by 18 to 24 months of age. By 18 months, approximately 50% pass and by 24 months a significant majority of children (more than 70%) are reported to self- refer by touching or removing a mark from their own body (face or other body region) while exploring their mirror image (Amsterdam, 1972; Asendorpf, Warkentin, & Baudonnière, 1996; Bard, Todd, Bernier, Love, & Leavens, 2006; Bertenthal & Fisher, 1978; Lewis & Brooks-Gunn, 1979; Lewis & Ramsay, 2004; Nielsen, Dissanayake, & Kashima, 2003; Schulman & Kaplowitz, 1976). Here we report cross-cultural observations on the emergence of mirror self-recognition (MSR), using a simplified mark test, raising questions regarding the meaning and universal validity of this test as an ontogenetic benchmark of self-concept. The basic rationale behind the mark test is that when a child is marked surreptitiously and attempts to touch the mark on the body, as opposed to doing nothing or trying to reach for it on the mirror surface, it indicates that the specular image stands for the child’s own body. Self-oriented gestures are taken as the behavioral index of self-recognition, indicating that the embodied self is the referent of what is seen in the mirror (Rochat, 2003). In a different account, Nielsen, Suddendorf, and Slaughter (2006) claim that the mark test is passed by individuals who have a rapidly updatable image of themselves that they recognize in the mirror. Regardless of the rela- tive validity of these accounts, the general consensus is that passing the mark test indicates that children recognize their image and that those who fail the test do not. From a comparative perspective, chimpanzees, orangutans, dolphins, and Asian elephants are among the few nonhuman species reported to pass the mark test (Gallup, 1982; Plotnik & de Waal, 2006; Povinelli, 1995; Reiss & Marino, 1998). Nonhuman individuals passing the test typically have extensive prior experience with reflective surfaces. In contrast, research with humans suggests that children’s relative familiarity with mirrors, which greatly varies across contexts, does not correlate with the age at which the mark test is passed (Priel & deSchonen, 1986). Priel and deShonen (1986) tested Bedouin nomadic children with no previous mirror experience and compared them to same-age Israeli children familiar with mirrors. They found no significant difference in the developmental onset of MSR between mirror familiar and unfa- miliar children. However, more recent cross-cultural studies point to significant cultural variations in the onset of MSR. Keller and collaborators compared 18- to 20-month-olds from urban Greece, Costa Rica, Germany, as well as from a rural community in Cameroon, and they report a greater proportion of German, Greek, and Costa Rican children passing the test (more than 50%), compared to Cameroonian children (less than 4%) (Keller, Kartner, Borke, Yovsi, & Kleis, 2005; Keller et al., 2004). These authors correlate such variations to variations in par- enting strategies that exist across these cultures, fostering more or less autonomy in the young child. If such variability is validated with other cultures, testing children older than 18 to 20 months would allow us to determine whether this variability pertains only to a developmental delay, pos- sibly resulting from variations in parenting styles (see Keller et al., 2005; Keller et al., 2004) or whether such differences persist throughout development. If the latter is the case, this would sug- gest that there may be culture-specific responses to the passing of the mirror mark test (i.e., greater occurrence of potential false negatives). To probe this alternative explanation, we per- formed two experiments testing (a) whether cross-cultural variability in the passing of the mirror mark test could be verified across more cultures and (b) whether such variability would persist beyond the age at which children typically are reported to pass the mirror mark test. In two experiments, we tested children 18 to 72 months old living in seven distinct cultural groups. Because of the exploratory nature of this work, aside from probing these questions, no specific hypotheses were made."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The second experiment demonstrates that a significant amount of variability exists across cultural contexts in children’s behavioral response to this version of the MSR test that measures spontaneous self-recognition (see also Lewis & Ramsey, 2004). Consistent with past research, 77% to 88% of Western children (Canada and United States) pass this version of the MSR test by spontaneously displaying self-oriented behaviors. In contrast, only half of the children from Saint Lucia, Grenada, or Peru and none of the Fijian children of the same age showed any sign of such behaviors."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Cross-cultural variability is confirmed.""}"
10.1523/eneuro.0332-25.2025,5c0e372f71ca86f5,1,10.1126/science.7256273,Phenomenological space-time: toward an experiential relativity.,DeLong AJ,1981,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""DeLong AJ"", ""doi"": ""10.1126/science.7256273"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Phenomenological space-time: toward an experiential relativity."", ""year"": ""1981""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""5c0e372f71ca86f5"", ""location"": ""Discussion, section 'Resting in a VR environment does not replicate', line 2 (see also Results, line 2; References, lines 26-27)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Second, the size of the VR did not significantly impact the retrospective time estimation as would have been expected through other studies ( DeLong, 1981 ; Riemer et al., 2014 ). Two differences can explain this incongruence: our study tested a different time scale and used a retrospective, not a prospective paradigm. Additionally, temporal distortions previously reported to be affected by the scale of the environment were realized in nonstatic environments."", ""reason"": ""In Experiment 3 (N = 57), the authors experimentally manipulated virtual room size (small room, N = 29 vs. large room, N = 28) to empirically test whether environmental scale affects duration estimation (Wilcoxon rank-sum test, W = 419.5, p = 0.835) and explicitly contrasted their null effect with the prior published environmental-size finding of DeLong (1981). The Reference list confirms the exact identity: DeLong AJ (1981), 'Phenomenological space-time: toward an experiential relativity.', Science 213:681–683, DOI 10.1126/science.7256273. The only shipped row (input_row_index 844) is Azizi, Polti, and van Wassenhove (2023; 10.1523/jneurosci.0816-23.2023), the principal MEG study on spontaneous alpha burst time, and sibling target 2 is Riemer, Hölzl, and Kleinböhl (2014; 10.1007/s00221-014-3848-6); all three are completely distinct studies from different decades and author groups, so report equivalence does not apply. Prior adjudication and state files were checked and are legitimately absent."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0042_5c0e372f71ca86f5"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""DeLong AJ"", ""year"": ""1981"", ""title"": ""Phenomenological space-time: toward an experiential relativity."", ""doi"": ""10.1126/science.7256273"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""3"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Second, the size of the VR did not significantly impact the retrospective time estimation as would have been expected through other studies ( DeLong, 1981 ; Riemer et al., 2014 )."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Second, the size of the VR did not significantly impact the retrospective time estimation as would have been expected through other studies ( DeLong, 1981 ; Riemer et al., 2014 ). Two differences can explain this incongruence: our study tested a different time scale and used a retrospective, not a prospective paradigm."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Experiment 3 explicitly tested whether environmental size affects time estimation and contrasts its null effect with this cited earlier result.""}"
10.1523/eneuro.0332-25.2025,5c0e372f71ca86f5,2,10.1007/s00221-014-3848-6,Interrelations between the perception of time and space in large-scale environments.,"Riemer M, Hölzl R, Kleinböhl D",2014,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Riemer M, Hölzl R, Kleinböhl D"", ""doi"": ""10.1007/s00221-014-3848-6"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Interrelations between the perception of time and space in large-scale environments."", ""year"": ""2014""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""5c0e372f71ca86f5"", ""location"": ""Discussion, section 'Resting in a VR environment does not replicate', line 2 (see also Results, line 2; References, lines 67-68)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Second, the size of the VR did not significantly impact the retrospective time estimation as would have been expected through other studies ( DeLong, 1981 ; Riemer et al., 2014 ). Two differences can explain this incongruence: our study tested a different time scale and used a retrospective, not a prospective paradigm. Additionally, temporal distortions previously reported to be affected by the scale of the environment were realized in nonstatic environments."", ""reason"": ""In Experiment 3 (N = 57), the authors empirically tested whether virtual environment size (small vs. large virtual room) impacts duration estimation (W = 419.5, p = 0.835) and explicitly compared their null result against the expected environmental-scale effect from Riemer et al. (2014). The Reference list establishes the exact identity: Riemer M, Hölzl R, Kleinböhl D (2014), 'Interrelations between the perception of time and space in large-scale environments.', Exp Brain Res 232:1317–1325, DOI 10.1007/s00221-014-3848-6. This work is entirely distinct from shipped row 844 (Azizi et al., 2023; 10.1523/jneurosci.0816-23.2023) and from sibling target 1 (DeLong, 1981; 10.1126/science.7256273), with no report equivalence. Prior adjudication and state files were checked and are legitimately absent."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0042_5c0e372f71ca86f5"", ""target_index"": 2, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Riemer M, Hölzl R, Kleinböhl D"", ""year"": ""2014"", ""title"": ""Interrelations between the perception of time and space in large-scale environments."", ""doi"": ""10.1007/s00221-014-3848-6"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""3"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Second, the size of the VR did not significantly impact the retrospective time estimation as would have been expected through other studies ( DeLong, 1981 ; Riemer et al., 2014 )."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Second, the size of the VR did not significantly impact the retrospective time estimation as would have been expected through other studies ( DeLong, 1981 ; Riemer et al., 2014 ). Two differences can explain this incongruence: our study tested a different time scale and used a retrospective, not a prospective paradigm."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The same explicit contrast identifies this independent earlier paper as a target of the environmental-size test.""}"
10.1111/lang.12403,a58225dce84de1c5,0,10.1016/j.neuropsychologia.2015.10.020,Tracking lexical consolidation with ERPs: Lexical and semantic-priming effects on N400 and LPC responses to newly-learned words.,"Bakker, I., Takashima, A., vanHell, J. G., Janzen, G., & McQueen, J. M.",2015,full_text,same_author_year_siblings,unverified,unresolved,"{""corrected_value"": null, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""a58225dce84de1c5"", ""location"": ""Introduction (The Current Study), lines 216-228; see also lines 168-183, Discussion lines 777-812, and References lines 995-998"", ""method"": ""obvious"", ""quote"": ""Bakker et al. (2015) findings may thus be\nunique for multilingual participants with extensive experience learning for-\neign languages, and may not generalize to all learners, including learners with\nlittle foreign language learning experience. This question was addressed in\nthe present study by testing learners who have little experience with learning\nforeign languages and who are functionally monolingual.\n     Taken together, the current study sought to conceptually replicate and extend\nthe findings of Bakker et al. (2015), and investigate novel vocabulary learn-\ning in monolingual English speakers with minimal foreign language learning\nexperience. In addition, we examined whether an extended period of offline\nconsolidation would further enhance semantic integration by testing learners\nimmediately after learning and one day after learning, as well as one week after\nlearning."", ""reason"": ""Parent retains the existing one-final-attempt scope_primary_845_0007. Actual current ERP reference differs from shipped theta/beta reference, but the earlier report-equivalence objection has not been settled; same cohort/companion finding versus wrong empirical original cannot be certified solely from title, DOI or noncitation. The new omission opinion is therefore withheld, and an unapproved replacement original must not be integrated or exported again as an extra pair. All prior and temporarily applied parent opinions remain in history; original/outcome and dependent omission remain unverified pending the existing final scope, no budget reset."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0083_a58225dce84de1c5"", ""target_index"": 0, ""verdict"": ""undeterminable""}","{""authors"": ""Bakker, I., Takashima, A., vanHell, J. G., Janzen, G., & McQueen, J. M."", ""year"": ""2015"", ""title"": ""Tracking lexical consolidation with ERPs: Lexical and semantic-priming effects on N400 and LPC responses to newly-learned words."", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.neuropsychologia.2015.10.020"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Taken together, the current study sought to conceptually replicate and extend the findings of Bakker et al. (2015), and investigate novel vocabulary learning in monolingual English speakers with minimal foreign language learning experience."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Both types of learners showed a LPC semantic priming effect (indexing controlled semantic retrieval), but no N400 effect (indexing at least partial automatic semantic retrieval), after a 24-hour consolidation period. The main difference is that only the experienced learners (Bakker et al., 2015) also showed a frontal LPC priming effect shortly after learning (for words trained and tested on Day 2)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly compare agreement after 24 hours with absence of the original immediate LPC effect. No overall replication verdict overrides these comparisons.""}"
10.1371/journal.pone.0229161,b74519d198cc668b,1,10.1177/0361684315580125,From sex objects to human beings: Masking sexual body parts and humanization as moderators to women’s objectification,"Bernard, P., Gervais, S. J., Allen, J., Delmée, A., & Klein, O.",2015,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Bernard, P., Gervais, S. J., Allen, J., Delmée, A., & Klein, O."", ""year"": ""2015"", ""title"": ""From sex objects to human beings: Masking sexual body parts and humanization as moderators to women’s objectification"", ""doi"": ""10.1177/0361684315580125"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2a"", ""evidence_quote"": ""We conducted three studies: a direct replication of Bernard and colleagues’ study [\n4\n], using their original materials, but with counterbalanced stimuli as in [\n5\n] (Study 1); a conceptual replication–to our knowledge the first one–with different sexualized materials to investigate whether the same pattern of results would emerge with different stimuli (Study 2a; indeed, so far most of the evidence in favor of the IBRT has emerged from research using the set of stimuli developed by Bernard and colleagues [\n4\n]); an investigation of the inversion effect with non-sexualized stimuli (Study 2b).\nWe expected (H1) stronger inversion effects for male than female targets and (H1b) that the participant’s gender would not moderate this difference between male and female targets. The confirmation of this hypothesis in Study 1 would be a direct replication of [\n5\n], and its confirmation in Study 2a would be a conceptual replication, enhancing its external validity beyond the specific stimuli. Study 2b tested a boundary condition, namely, whether differences in inversion effects between male and female targets would emerge also for non-sexualized stimuli."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Study 1 replicated the original results, showing a stronger inversion effect for male photos. However, no difference between male and female stimuli emerged in either Study 2a or 2b."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Direct replication agrees, conceptual replication fails; authors explicitly contrast these results. The validity critique does not remove the explicit close/conceptual mixed pattern.""}"
10.3389/fnhum.2022.937515,1b5f207ca572ce22,1,10.1113/jphysiol.2009.175885,Modulation of interhemispheric inhibition by volitional motor activity: an ipsilateral silent period study.,"Giovannelli F., Borgheresi A., Balestrieri F., Zaccara G., Viggiano M. P., Cincotta M., et al.",2009,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Giovannelli F., Borgheresi A., Balestrieri F., Zaccara G., Viggiano M. P., Cincotta M., et al."", ""doi"": ""10.1113/jphysiol.2009.175885"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Modulation of interhemispheric inhibition by volitional motor activity: an ipsilateral silent period study."", ""year"": ""2009""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""1b5f207ca572ce22"", ""location"": ""Discussion / Results / References, line 1"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""The iSP paradigm was found to be inadequate for the aim, as the iSP was not modulated by hand contraction. In fact, during the iSP paradigm we manipulated the activity of the contralateral hand, expecting to induce a stronger interhemispheric inhibition in the Contracted compared to the Relaxed condition (Giovannelli et al., 2009 ), which we did not observe. The null result on iSP modulation was surprising, considering the consistent effects reported previously throughout several manipulations of contralateral hand activity (Giovannelli et al., 2009 )."", ""reason"": ""In the source paper (Zazio et al., 2022), the authors manipulate contralateral hand activity (Contracted vs. Relaxed) during the ipsilateral silent period (iSP) paradigm specifically to reproduce the interhemispheric inhibition modulation reported by Giovannelli et al. (2009, 10.1113/jphysiol.2009.175885), test Contracted vs. Relaxed in the Results (t(19) = -1.67, p = 0.94; Bayesian t-test BF01 = 5.95), and explicitly state in the Discussion (line 1) that they expected 'to induce a stronger interhemispheric inhibition in the Contracted compared to the Relaxed condition (Giovannelli et al., 2009 ), which we did not observe.' The only shipped row for this work (input_row_index 855) is Bortoletto et al. (2021, 10.1016/j.brs.2021.02.002), which concerns early latency TMS-EEG P25 connectivity rather than iSP modulation by contralateral hand contraction. Thus, Giovannelli et al. (2009) is a genuinely absent replication-original pair."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0008_1b5f207ca572ce22"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Giovannelli F., Borgheresi A., Balestrieri F., Zaccara G., Viggiano M. P., Cincotta M., et al."", ""year"": ""2009"", ""title"": ""Modulation of interhemispheric inhibition by volitional motor activity: an ipsilateral silent period study."", ""doi"": ""10.1113/jphysiol.2009.175885"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In fact, during the iSP paradigm we manipulated the activity of the contralateral hand, expecting to induce a stronger interhemispheric inhibition in the Contracted compared to the Relaxed condition (Giovannelli et al., 2009 ), which we did not observe."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In fact, during the iSP paradigm we manipulated the activity of the contralateral hand, expecting to induce a stronger interhemispheric inhibition in the Contracted compared to the Relaxed condition (Giovannelli et al., 2009 ), which we did not observe. The null result on iSP modulation was surprising, considering the consistent effects reported previously throughout several manipulations of contralateral hand activity (Giovannelli et al., 2009 )."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The predicted published contraction effect was directly tested but not observed; the authors do not give a verdict that the attempt as a whole was incapable of adjudication.""}"
10.3102/0162373720948884,6f1653adad3488ee,1,,"Selection into Identification in Fixed Effects Models, with Application to Head Start","Miller, D. L., Shenhav, N. A., & Grosz, M. Z.",2019,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Miller, D. L., Shenhav, N. A., & Grosz, M. Z."", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""reproduction"", ""title"": ""Selection into Identification in Fixed Effects Models, with Application to Head Start"", ""year"": ""2019""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""6f1653adad3488ee"", ""location"": ""Results, Selection into Identification"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""We also performed the MSG (2019) reweighting-on-observables \n\nprocedure for the Deming cohort sample. Similar to MSG (2019), we found reweighting \n\nattenuated the FFE estimates of Head Start for high school graduation, idleness, learning \n\ndisability and poor health, but find little change on other young adult outcomes or ASI. These \n\nresults closely replicate MSG (2019) findings and can be found in Table S16 of the online \n\nappendix."", ""reason"": ""No prior adjudication or prior state file exists for this work. In addition to replicating and extending Deming (2009) (shipped row input_row_index 871), Pages, Lukes, Bailey, & Duncan (2020) [6f1653adad3488ee.txt] explicitly perform an empirical reproduction ('Replication of MSG (2019)') of Miller, Shenhav, & Grosz (2019) ('Selection into Identification in Fixed Effects Models, with Application to Head Start', NBER Working Paper Series, 26174; cited as MSG 2019). Specifically, in the Introduction, in Results under 'Robustness Checks -> Selection into Identification', and in Table S16 ('Table S16. Comparison of Head Start Impacts for Deming’s and Combined cohorts Unweighted/Weighted'), the authors re-run the MSG (2019) reweighting-on-observables procedure on the original Deming CNLSY cohort sample (N = 1,251; 581 switchers) and directly compare their reproduced weighted family fixed effects (FFE) estimates ('Replicated Weighted Deming', footnote b: 'Replication of MSG (2019)') side-by-side against MSG (2019)'s reported weighted estimates ('Weighted Deming', footnote a: 'Deming’s cohort weighted estimates, from MSG (2019)'), confirming that reweighting similarly attenuates the FFE estimates for high school graduation, idleness, learning disability, and poor health. Miller, Shenhav, & Grosz (2019) is an independent work by different authors published 10 years after Deming (2009) and is absent from shipped_rows (which contains only input_row_index 871, Deming 2009)."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0054_6f1653adad3488ee"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""outcome_computation"": ""computationally reproducible"", ""outcome_computational_quote"": ""These results closely replicate MSG (2019) findings and can be found in Table S16 of the online appendix."", ""outcome_robustness"": ""not checked"", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": ""We also performed the MSG (2019) reweighting-on-observables procedure for the Deming cohort sample. Similar to MSG (2019), we found reweighting attenuated the FFE estimates of Head Start for high school graduation, idleness, learning disability and poor health, but find little change on other young adult outcomes or ASI."", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""authors"": ""Miller, D. L., Shenhav, N. A., & Grosz, M. Z."", ""year"": ""2019"", ""title"": ""Selection into Identification in Fixed Effects Models, with Application to Head Start"", ""evidence_quote"": ""We also performed the MSG (2019) reweighting-on-observables procedure for the Deming cohort sample."", ""outcome"": """", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Table S16 directly compares MSG weighted estimates with their reproduced values. Reweighting is MSG finding being reproduced; no distinct alternative specifications testing MSG result are reported. New combined cohorts are independent extensions, not original-data robustness.""}"
10.1007/s00125-010-2002-7,47e3e03c3643c827,1,,Common genetic variation near melatonin receptor MTNR1B contributes to raised plasma glucose and increased risk of type 2 diabetes among Indian Asians and European Caucasians,"Chambers JC, Zhang W, Zabaneh D et al",2009,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Chambers JC, Zhang W, Zabaneh D et al"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Common genetic variation near melatonin receptor MTNR1B contributes to raised plasma glucose and increased risk of type 2 diabetes among Indian Asians and European Caucasians"", ""year"": ""2009""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""47e3e03c3643c827"", ""location"": ""Methods, Statistical analyses (lines 9-10); Results (lines 16-17); References ref. 5 (line 102); ESM Table 2 (line 133)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""The MAGIC SNPs were supplemented by 13 additional SNPs from previously published GWASs (ESM Table 2) for a total of 29 SNPs that we attempted to replicate in our African-American cohort.\nOur replication effort occurred in two stages. In the first stage, we attempted to replicate 20 of the 29 exact published SNPs (i.e. direct replication) available in the HUFS dataset. For this stage, SNPs were considered replicated if the same HUFS SNP had a p value <0.05. For the second stage, we performed a local replication analysis based on a 500 kb linkage disequilibrium (LD) block containing a query SNP determined by the SNPs most distant from the query SNP with r 2 ≥0.3. We used the HapMap CEU LD data (http://hapmap.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/ downloads/ld_data/2008-06/00README.TXT) for all SNPs except for rs2166706 where the Gujarati Indians in Houston, Texas, USA (GIH) reference dataset (http:// hapmap.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/downloads/ld_data/2008-09_ phaseIII/00README.txt) was used to match the original reported GWAS population [5]."", ""reason"": ""The source paper empirically re-tests the fasting plasma glucose (FPG) association of SNP rs2166706 near MTNR1B (11q21) reported by Chambers et al. (2009) (ref. [5], Diabetes 58:2703-2708; ESM Table 2 line 133, Excel serial date 40028.0 = 2009-08-04) in 927 unrelated non-diabetic African-American participants from the Howard University Family Study (HUFS). Specifically, rs2166706 was one of the 13 additional SNPs from prior GWASs included in the 29-SNP replication pool, was directly tested in Stage 1 (failing to reach p < 0.05, as Results line 16 reports that SNPs from remaining studies that did not directly replicate are not shown), and was specifically queried in Stage 2 local LD-based replication using the HapMap GIH reference panel to match the original Indian Asian discovery population of ref. [5]. This original study is distinct from the sole shipped row (873, Dupuis et al. 2010, Nat Genet 42:105-116, doi:10.1038/ng.520, which tested MAGIC meta-analysis SNP rs10830963 at MTNR1B) and from sibling target 2 (Bouatia-Naji et al. 2008, Nat Genet, which reported rs1387153 at MTNR1B in European cohorts) as well as targets 3-6. Prior adjudication covered only row 873 (target 0), and no prior state file existed."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0034_47e3e03c3643c827"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Chambers JC, Zhang W, Zabaneh D et al"", ""year"": ""2009"", ""title"": ""Common genetic variation near melatonin receptor MTNR1B contributes to raised plasma glucose and increased risk of type 2 diabetes among Indian Asians and European Caucasians"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The MAGIC SNPs were supplemented by 13 additional SNPs from previously published GWASs (ESM Table 2) for a total of 29 SNPs that we attempted to replicate in our African-American cohort.\nChambers\t40028.0\tDiabetes\tCommon genetic variation near melatonin receptor MTNR1B contributes to raised plasma glucose and increased risk of type-2 diabetes amongst Indian Asians and European whites\tFasting plasma glucose"", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""SNPs from the remaining studies that did not directly replicate are not shown."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Mapped the study to its SNPs using primary ESM Table 2. Outcomes aggregate direct and local tests. For targets without individual narrative, the residual nonreplication statement supports failure with lower confidence; the local replication list refers to different discovery SNPs. Citation metadata limited to what this article and ESM print.""}"
10.1007/s00125-010-2002-7,47e3e03c3643c827,2,,A variant near MTNR1B is associated with increased fasting plasma glucose levels and type 2 diabetes risk,Bouatia-Naji,2008,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Bouatia-Naji"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""A variant near MTNR1B is associated with increased fasting plasma glucose levels and type 2 diabetes risk"", ""year"": ""2008""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""47e3e03c3643c827"", ""location"": ""Methods, Statistical analyses (lines 9-10); Results (lines 16-17); ESM Table 2 (line 134)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""39820.0\t19060909.0\tBouatia-Naji\t39789.0\tNat Genet\tA variant near MTNR1B is associated with increased fasting plasma glucose levels and type 2 diabetes risk\tFasting plasma glucose\t2,151 individuals\t6,332 cases, 9,132 controls\t11q21\tMTNR1B\trs1387153-T\trs1387153\t0.29\t2e-36\t\t0.07\t[0.05-0.08] mmol/l increase\tIllumina [308,846]\tN"", ""reason"": ""The source paper empirically re-tests the fasting plasma glucose (FPG) association of SNP rs1387153 near MTNR1B (11q21) reported by Bouatia-Naji et al. (ESM Table 2 line 134, PubMed ID 19060909, Nat Genet, Excel serial date 39789.0 = 2008-12-07, giving year 2008) in 927 African-American participants from the HUFS cohort. In Methods (lines 9-10), the authors state that the 16 MAGIC SNPs were supplemented by 13 additional SNPs from previously published GWASs listed in ESM Table 2 for a total of 29 SNPs attempted for replication via Stage 1 direct replication (rs1387153 had no 'c' exclusion footnote and did not achieve p < 0.05; Results line 16) and Stage 2 500 kb LD-block local replication. This work is an independent GWAS discovery paper distinct from the sole shipped row (873, Dupuis et al. 2010, Nat Genet 42:105-116, doi:10.1038/ng.520, the later MAGIC meta-analysis testing rs10830963 at MTNR1B) and from sibling target 1 (Chambers et al. 2009, Diabetes, testing rs2166706 in Indian Asians and Europeans) as well as targets 3-6. Author and year fields reflect the exact metadata printed in ESM Table 2. Prior adjudication covered only row 873 (target 0), and no prior state file existed."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0034_47e3e03c3643c827"", ""target_index"": 2, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Bouatia-Naji"", ""year"": ""2008"", ""title"": ""A variant near MTNR1B is associated with increased fasting plasma glucose levels and type 2 diabetes risk"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The MAGIC SNPs were supplemented by 13 additional SNPs from previously published GWASs (ESM Table 2) for a total of 29 SNPs that we attempted to replicate in our African-American cohort.\nBouatia-Naji\t39789.0\tNat Genet\tA variant near MTNR1B is associated with increased fasting plasma glucose levels and type 2 diabetes risk\tFasting plasma glucose"", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""SNPs from the remaining studies that did not directly replicate are not shown."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Mapped the study to its SNPs using primary ESM Table 2. Outcomes aggregate direct and local tests. For targets without individual narrative, the residual nonreplication statement supports failure with lower confidence; the local replication list refers to different discovery SNPs. Citation metadata limited to what this article and ESM print.""}"
10.1007/s00125-010-2002-7,47e3e03c3643c827,3,,Variations in the G6PC2/ABCB11 genomic region are associated with fasting glucose levels,Chen,2008,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Chen"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Variations in the G6PC2/ABCB11 genomic region are associated with fasting glucose levels"", ""year"": ""2008""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""47e3e03c3643c827"", ""location"": ""Methods, Statistical analyses (lines 9-10); Results (lines 16-17); ESM Table 2 (line 135)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""39777.0\t18521185.0\tChen\t39603.0\tJ Clin Invest\tVariations in the G6PC2/ABCB11 genomic region are associated with fasting glucose levels\tFasting plasma glucose\t5,088 nondiabetic individuals\t18,436 nondiabetic individuals\t2q24.3\tG6PC2,ABCB11\trs563694-C\trs563694\t0.34\t4e-07"", ""reason"": ""The source paper empirically re-tests the fasting plasma glucose (FPG) association of SNP rs563694 in the G6PC2/ABCB11 region (2q24.3) reported by Chen et al. (ESM Table 2 line 135, PubMed ID 18521185, J Clin Invest, Excel serial date 39603.0 = 2008-06-04, giving year 2008) in 927 African-American participants from the HUFS cohort. As stated in Methods (lines 9-10), rs563694 was one of the 13 additional GWAS SNPs in ESM Table 2 tested in Stage 1 direct replication (available in HUFS without a 'c' exclusion flag, not reaching p < 0.05; Results line 16) and Stage 2 local LD-window replication. This study is an independent original GWAS distinct from the sole shipped row (873, Dupuis et al. 2010, Nat Genet 42:105-116, doi:10.1038/ng.520, which reported a different G6PC2 SNP, rs560887, that was unavailable for direct testing in HUFS) and from sibling targets 1, 2, 4, 5, and 6. Author and year fields match the primary ESM Table 2 record. Prior adjudication covered only row 873 (target 0), and no prior state file existed."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0034_47e3e03c3643c827"", ""target_index"": 3, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Chen"", ""year"": ""2008"", ""title"": ""Variations in the G6PC2/ABCB11 genomic region are associated with fasting glucose levels"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The MAGIC SNPs were supplemented by 13 additional SNPs from previously published GWASs (ESM Table 2) for a total of 29 SNPs that we attempted to replicate in our African-American cohort.\nChen\t39603.0\tJ Clin Invest\tVariations in the G6PC2/ABCB11 genomic region are associated with fasting glucose levels\tFasting plasma glucose"", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""SNPs from the remaining studies that did not directly replicate are not shown."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Mapped the study to its SNPs using primary ESM Table 2. Outcomes aggregate direct and local tests. For targets without individual narrative, the residual nonreplication statement supports failure with lower confidence; the local replication list refers to different discovery SNPs. Citation metadata limited to what this article and ESM print.""}"
10.1007/s00125-010-2002-7,47e3e03c3643c827,4,,Genome-wide association with diabetes-related traits in the Framingham Heart Study,Meigs,2007,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Meigs"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Genome-wide association with diabetes-related traits in the Framingham Heart Study"", ""year"": ""2007""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""47e3e03c3643c827"", ""location"": ""Methods, Statistical analyses (lines 9-10); Results (lines 16-17); Discussion (line 21); Table 1 (line 47); Table 2 (lines 93-95); ESM Table 2 (lines 136-137)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""39777.0\t17903298.0\tMeigs\t39344.0\tBMC Med Genet\tGenome-wide association with diabetes-related traits in the Framingham Heart Study\tFasting plasma glucose\t1,087 individuals (Framingham)\tNR\t8p11.21\tZMAT4\trs2722425-?\trs2722425\tNR\t9e-06\t(FPG)\tNR\tNR\tAffymetrix[70,897]\tN"", ""reason"": ""The source paper empirically re-tests two fasting plasma glucose (FPG) SNP associations reported by Meigs et al. (ESM Table 2 lines 136-137, PubMed ID 17903298, BMC Med Genet, Excel serial date 39344.0 = 2007-09-19, giving year 2007): rs2722425 in ZMAT4 (8p11.21) and rs180730 in PRDM5 (4q27). In the HUFS African-American cohort, rs2722425 was directly replicated in Stage 1 (β = 0.01326, p = 0.024; Results line 16, Table 1 line 47) and locally replicated via three LD proxies in Stage 2 (including rs1823643, corrected p = 0.007; Table 2 lines 93-95), whereas rs180730 did not replicate (Results line 16). Discussion (line 21) explicitly contrasts ZMAT4 with the shipped MAGIC meta-analysis (row 873, Dupuis et al. 2010), noting that ZMAT4 was 'identified in previous GWASs but not replicated in the MAGIC meta-analysis'. Meigs et al. (2007) in BMC Med Genet is also a distinct paper reporting different SNPs and analyses from sibling target 5 (Florez et al. 2007, Diabetes, PubMed ID 17848626, which integrated multi-cohort replication datasets and reported 7 completely different SNPs including rs625643) as well as targets 1-3 and 6. Prior adjudication covered only row 873 (target 0), and no prior state file existed."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0034_47e3e03c3643c827"", ""target_index"": 4, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Meigs"", ""year"": ""2007"", ""title"": ""Genome-wide association with diabetes-related traits in the Framingham Heart Study"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The MAGIC SNPs were supplemented by 13 additional SNPs from previously published GWASs (ESM Table 2) for a total of 29 SNPs that we attempted to replicate in our African-American cohort.\nMeigs\t39344.0\tBMC Med Genet\tGenome-wide association with diabetes-related traits in the Framingham Heart Study\tFasting plasma glucose"", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We also replicated SNPs from other GWASs for FPG: rs2722425 within ZMAT4 (p value=0.024) as well as rs625643 (p value= 0.048), which is located in a functionally unknown region on chromosome 1 (Table 1). SNPs from the remaining studies that did not directly replicate are not shown."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Mapped the study to its SNPs using primary ESM Table 2. Outcomes aggregate direct and local tests. For targets without individual narrative, the residual nonreplication statement supports failure with lower confidence; the local replication list refers to different discovery SNPs. Citation metadata limited to what this article and ESM print.""}"
10.1007/s00125-010-2002-7,47e3e03c3643c827,5,,A 100K genome-wide association scan for diabetes and related traits in the Framingham Heart Study: replication and integration with other genome-wide datasets,Florez,2007,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Florez"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""A 100K genome-wide association scan for diabetes and related traits in the Framingham Heart Study: replication and integration with other genome-wide datasets"", ""year"": ""2007""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""47e3e03c3643c827"", ""location"": ""Methods, Statistical analyses (lines 9-10); Results (lines 16-17); Discussion (line 22); Table 1 (line 46); Table 2 (line 82); ESM Table 2 (lines 138-144)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""39777.0\t17848626.0\tFlorez\t39335.0\tDiabetes\tA 100K genome-wide association scan for diabetes and related traits in the Framingham Heart Study: replication and integration with other genome-wide datasets\tType 2 diabetes and 6 quantitative traits\t1,087 family members\t1,465 unrelated individuals; 2,175 cases and 2,412 controls\t1p32.3\tAK024684\tNR\trs625643\tNR\t0.004\tNS\tNR\tNR\tAffymetrix [66,543]\tN"", ""reason"": ""The source paper empirically re-tests the candidate SNP associations reported by Florez et al. (ESM Table 2 lines 138-144, PubMed ID 17848626, Diabetes, Excel serial date 39335.0 = 2007-09-10, giving year 2007), which contributed 7 of the 13 non-MAGIC SNPs in the 29-SNP replication pool (rs625643 in AK024684 on 1p32.3, rs952635 in PDE4B, rs7531174 in SLC44A3, rs1489100 in AJ012503, rs2806739 on 13q21.1, rs2241119 in TSHR, and rs2009833 in ATP8B4). Five of these SNPs were directly tested in Stage 1 (while PDE4B and ATP8B4 were unavailable for direct comparison; Results line 16), with rs625643 directly replicating (β = 0.01141, p = 0.048 in text / 0.05 in Table 1 line 46) and locally replicating via LD proxy rs671431 in Stage 2 (corrected p = 0.04; Table 2 line 82, Discussion line 22), while the remaining tested SNPs did not replicate. Florez et al. (2007) is completely distinct from the sole shipped row (873, Dupuis et al. 2010, Nat Genet 42:105-116, doi:10.1038/ng.520) and is a separate publication reporting a disjoint set of multi-cohort integrated SNPs from sibling target 4 (Meigs et al. 2007, BMC Med Genet, PubMed ID 17903298, which reported rs2722425 in ZMAT4 and rs180730 in PRDM5) as well as targets 1-3 and 6. Prior adjudication covered only row 873 (target 0), and no prior state file existed."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0034_47e3e03c3643c827"", ""target_index"": 5, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Florez"", ""year"": ""2007"", ""title"": ""A 100K genome-wide association scan for diabetes and related traits in the Framingham Heart Study: replication and integration with other genome-wide datasets"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The MAGIC SNPs were supplemented by 13 additional SNPs from previously published GWASs (ESM Table 2) for a total of 29 SNPs that we attempted to replicate in our African-American cohort.\nFlorez\t39335.0\tDiabetes\tA 100K genome-wide association scan for diabetes and related traits in the Framingham Heart Study: replication and integration with other genome-wide datasets\tType 2 diabetes and 6 quantitative traits"", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We also replicated SNPs from other GWASs for FPG: rs2722425 within ZMAT4 (p value=0.024) as well as rs625643 (p value= 0.048), which is located in a functionally unknown region on chromosome 1 (Table 1). SNPs from the remaining studies that did not directly replicate are not shown."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outc