10.1109/iwsc55060.2022.00015,a0f5200ab76450d1,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,"{""corrected_value"": null, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""a0f5200ab76450d1"", ""location"": ""Abstract (line 4)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""While it has been shown to support natural language code search and code documentation generation tasks, its effectiveness for code clone detection is not explored in depth. In this paper, we aim to replicate and evaluate the performance of CodeBERT for code clone detection on multiple datasets with varying functionalities to understand (1) whether CodeBERT can generalize to unseen code, (2) how fine-tuning can effect CodeBERT’s performance on unseen code, and (3) how CodeBERT performs for detecting various code clone types."", ""reason"": ""No prior adjudication or state file exists for this work. The supplied primary text contains only the title, authors, and abstract (4 lines total, without main text or bibliography). While the abstract states that the authors 'aim to replicate and evaluate the performance of CodeBERT for code clone detection on multiple datasets,' it provides no bibliographic citation or specific original empirical claim being retested, and explicitly notes that while CodeBERT was shown to support code search and documentation generation, 'its effectiveness for code clone detection is not explored in depth.' Consequently, the unresolved question is which specific published work and finding this study empirically replicates—specifically, whether target_index 0 refers to the already-shipped CodeBERT paper in input_row_index 878 (Feng et al., 2020, 'CodeBERT: A Pre-Trained Model for Programming and Natural Languages,' DOI 10.18653/v1/2020.findings-emnlp.139) or a distinct prior study applying CodeBERT to code clone detection."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0080_a0f5200ab76450d1"", ""target_index"": 0, ""verdict"": ""undeterminable""}","{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In this paper, we aim to replicate and evaluate the performance of CodeBERT for code clone detection on multiple datasets with varying functionalities to understand (1) whether CodeBERT can generalize to unseen code, (2) how fine-tuning can effect CodeBERT’s performance on unseen code, and (3) how CodeBERT performs for detecting various code clone types."", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": """", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Abstract does not identify the target paper or state its original finding; reported performance cannot establish agreement.""}"
10.1037/a0025169,da9c8f0984382fe0,1,10.1016/j.jrp.2005.05.003,The causal effects of extraversion on positive affect and neuroticism on negative affect: Manipulating state extraversion and state neuroticism in an experimental approach.,"McNiel, J. M., & Fleeson, W.",2006,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""McNiel, J. M., & Fleeson, W."", ""year"": ""2006"", ""title"": ""The causal effects of extraversion on positive affect and neuroticism on negative affect: Manipulating state extraversion and state neuroticism in an experimental approach"", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.jrp.2005.05.003"", ""authors_o"": ""McNiel; Fleeson"", ""year_o"": ""2006"", ""title_o"": ""The causal effects of extraversion on positive affect and neuroticism on negative affect: Manipulating state extraversion and state neuroticism in an experimental approach"", ""doi_o"": ""10.1016/j.jrp.2005.05.003"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Although possible, two studies have assessed negative affect under similar circumstances and have failed to find the state by trait interaction that would suggest a mood cost for acting counterdispositionally (McNiel & Fleeson, 2006; McNiel et al., 2010). On the other hand, one of these samples was small (n⫽45) and both employed a within-subject design that could promote demand or carryover effects. In fact, the studies did find significant order effects and some inconsistent trait and acting condition effects on negative affect. So although there was not a significant interaction suggesting an affective cost, the full mood consequences of acting more or less extraverted require some clarification, particularly with regard to negative affect. Therefore, our studies include between-subjects manipulations, larger sample sizes, and measures of both positive and negative affect to better assess any possible distress that might accompany counterdispositional behavior."", ""outcome_quote"": ""Results replicated and extended past findings suggesting that acting extraverted produces hedonic benefits regardless of disposition. Positive affect increased and negative affect did not, even for participants acting out of character."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true}, ""quote"": ""Although possible, two studies have assessed negative affect\n\nunder similar circumstances and have failed to find the state by\n\ntrait interaction that would suggest a mood cost for acting coun-\n\nterdispositionally (McNiel & Fleeson, 2006; McNiel et al., 2010).\n\nOn the other hand, one of these samples was small (n⫽45) and\n\nboth employed a within-subject design that could promote demand\nor carryover effects. In fact, the studies did find significant order\n\neffects and some inconsistent trait and acting condition effects on\n\nnegative affect. So although there was not a significant interaction\n\nsuggesting an affective cost, the full mood consequences of acting\n\nmore or less extraverted require some clarification, particularly\n\nwith regard to negative affect. Therefore, our studies include\n\nbetween-subjects manipulations, larger sample sizes, and measures\nof both positive and negative affect to better assess any possible\n\ndistress that might accompany counterdispositional behavior."", ""location"": {""start_line"": 341, ""end_line"": 367, ""form_feed_page"": 1}, ""reason"": ""The paper directly re-tests McNiel and Fleeson's (2006) empirical finding that manipulating state extraversion/introversion produces no state-by-trait interaction on negative affect (and increases positive affect regardless of disposition), explicitly critiquing the small sample size and within-subject design of the two prior negative-affect studies and conducting a stronger between-subjects test across Studies 1 and 2 that confirms the absence of a counterdispositional negative-affect cost (successful replication)."", ""quote_provenance"": {""kind"": ""new_independent_literal_primary_quotation"", ""inspection_result"": ""evidence_followup/integration/strict_literal_quote_inspection.result.json"", ""issue_index"": 167, ""source_path"": ""/home/lukas/.t3/worktrees/fred-data/audit-flora-blind-2026-10/audit/flora_blind_2026-10/texts/da9c8f0984382fe0.txt"", ""source_sha256"": ""867fd5956f03830be17943c70473284d4a334a1d3b1bf5031d60ab6c9f13e17d"", ""raw_start_offset"": 12250, ""raw_end_offset"": 13241, ""scientific_verdict_unchanged"": true, ""reason"": ""Parent verified exact raw slice and semantic identity with the earlier accepted passage under its recorded display-normalisation policy. Independent primary-context inspector supported this passage. Original provider wording and whole prior decision retained in history; raw provider reply unchanged.""}}","{""authors"": ""McNiel, J. M., & Fleeson, W."", ""year"": ""2006"", ""title"": ""The causal effects of extraversion on positive affect and neuroticism on negative affect: Manipulating state extraversion and state neuroticism in an experimental approach."", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.jrp.2005.05.003"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Although possible, two studies have assessed negative affect under similar circumstances and have failed to find the state by trait interaction that would suggest a mood cost for acting counterdispositionally (McNiel & Fleeson, 2006; McNiel et al., 2010). Therefore, our studies include between-subjects manipulations, larger sample sizes, and measures of both positive and negative affect to better assess any possible distress that might accompany counterdispositional behavior."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""This study tested whether the pattern of relationships among extraversion, mood, behavior, and cognition found in Study 1 would generalize to a new interview situation. Very similar to previous research, participants reported more positive affect when acting extraverted, and this did not depend on their dispositional levels of extraversion. We again tested whether acting counterdispositionally might cause negative affect (along with positive affect in the case of introverts) and again failed to find any evidence for this idea."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The reported positive-affect benefit and/or absence of dispositional negative-affect cost agrees with this original’s findings. Cognitive depletion is a novel extension, not a failed replication of these mood findings.""}"
10.1037/a0025169,da9c8f0984382fe0,2,10.1002/per.738,The effect of state extraversion on four types of affect.,"McNiel, J. M., Lowman, J. C., & Fleeson, W.",2010,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 2, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""McNiel, J. M., Lowman, J. C., & Fleeson, W."", ""year"": ""2010"", ""title"": ""The effect of state extraversion on four types of affect"", ""doi"": ""10.1002/per.738"", ""authors_o"": ""McNiel; Lowman; Fleeson"", ""year_o"": ""2010"", ""title_o"": ""The effect of state extraversion on four types of affect"", ""doi_o"": ""10.1002/per.738"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Although possible, two studies have assessed negative affect under similar circumstances and have failed to find the state by trait interaction that would suggest a mood cost for acting counterdispositionally (McNiel & Fleeson, 2006; McNiel et al., 2010). On the other hand, one of these samples was small (n⫽45) and both employed a within-subject design that could promote demand or carryover effects. In fact, the studies did find significant order effects and some inconsistent trait and acting condition effects on negative affect. So although there was not a significant interaction suggesting an affective cost, the full mood consequences of acting more or less extraverted require some clarification, particularly with regard to negative affect. Therefore, our studies include between-subjects manipulations, larger sample sizes, and measures of both positive and negative affect to better assess any possible distress that might accompany counterdispositional behavior."", ""outcome_quote"": ""Results replicated and extended past findings suggesting that acting extraverted produces hedonic benefits regardless of disposition. Positive affect increased and negative affect did not, even for participants acting out of character."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true}, ""quote"": ""Although possible, two studies have assessed negative affect\n\nunder similar circumstances and have failed to find the state by\n\ntrait interaction that would suggest a mood cost for acting coun-\n\nterdispositionally (McNiel & Fleeson, 2006; McNiel et al., 2010).\n\nOn the other hand, one of these samples was small (n⫽45) and\n\nboth employed a within-subject design that could promote demand\nor carryover effects. In fact, the studies did find significant order\n\neffects and some inconsistent trait and acting condition effects on\n\nnegative affect. So although there was not a significant interaction\n\nsuggesting an affective cost, the full mood consequences of acting\n\nmore or less extraverted require some clarification, particularly\n\nwith regard to negative affect. Therefore, our studies include\n\nbetween-subjects manipulations, larger sample sizes, and measures\nof both positive and negative affect to better assess any possible\n\ndistress that might accompany counterdispositional behavior."", ""location"": {""start_line"": 341, ""end_line"": 367, ""form_feed_page"": 1}, ""reason"": ""The paper directly re-tests McNiel, Lowman, and Fleeson's (2010) empirical finding that manipulating state extraversion/introversion produces no state-by-trait interaction on negative affect (and increases positive affect regardless of disposition), explicitly critiquing the within-subject design and order effects in the prior negative-affect studies and conducting a stronger between-subjects test across Studies 1 and 2 that confirms the absence of a counterdispositional negative-affect cost (successful replication)."", ""quote_provenance"": {""kind"": ""new_independent_literal_primary_quotation"", ""inspection_result"": ""evidence_followup/integration/strict_literal_quote_inspection.result.json"", ""issue_index"": 168, ""source_path"": ""/home/lukas/.t3/worktrees/fred-data/audit-flora-blind-2026-10/audit/flora_blind_2026-10/texts/da9c8f0984382fe0.txt"", ""source_sha256"": ""867fd5956f03830be17943c70473284d4a334a1d3b1bf5031d60ab6c9f13e17d"", ""raw_start_offset"": 12250, ""raw_end_offset"": 13241, ""scientific_verdict_unchanged"": true, ""reason"": ""Parent verified exact raw slice and semantic identity with the earlier accepted passage under its recorded display-normalisation policy. Independent primary-context inspector supported this passage. Original provider wording and whole prior decision retained in history; raw provider reply unchanged.""}}","{""authors"": ""McNiel, J. M., Lowman, J. C., & Fleeson, W."", ""year"": ""2010"", ""title"": ""The effect of state extraversion on four types of affect."", ""doi"": ""10.1002/per.738"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Although possible, two studies have assessed negative affect under similar circumstances and have failed to find the state by trait interaction that would suggest a mood cost for acting counterdispositionally (McNiel & Fleeson, 2006; McNiel et al., 2010). Therefore, our studies include between-subjects manipulations, larger sample sizes, and measures of both positive and negative affect to better assess any possible distress that might accompany counterdispositional behavior."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""This study tested whether the pattern of relationships among extraversion, mood, behavior, and cognition found in Study 1 would generalize to a new interview situation. Very similar to previous research, participants reported more positive affect when acting extraverted, and this did not depend on their dispositional levels of extraversion. We again tested whether acting counterdispositionally might cause negative affect (along with positive affect in the case of introverts) and again failed to find any evidence for this idea."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The reported positive-affect benefit and/or absence of dispositional negative-affect cost agrees with this original’s findings. Cognitive depletion is a novel extension, not a failed replication of these mood findings.""}"
10.1038/sj.gene.6364398,c547481cb569f28e,1,,Functional annotation of a novel NFKB1 promoter polymorphism that increases risk for ulcerative colitis.,"Karban AS, Okazaki T, Panhuysen CI, Gallegos T, Potter JJ, Bailey-Wilson JE et al.",2004,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": """", ""title"": ""Functional annotation of a novel NFKB1 promoter polymorphism that increases risk for ulcerative colitis."", ""authors"": ""Karban AS, Okazaki T, Panhuysen CI, Gallegos T, Potter JJ, Bailey-Wilson JE et al."", ""year"": ""2004"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""failed""}, ""quote"": ""In terms of the putative NFKB1 94delATTG risk allele, Karban et\nal.25 first reported an association with risk of developing UC. We therefore tested this specific hypothesis in our\nentire set of samples but did not find significant evidence of association (OR = 0.99, 95% CI: 0.81-1.2;\nSupplementary Table 2)."", ""location"": ""Primary original-specific empirical retest, raw chars[11898,12206); Methods/Results/Discussion/References and ALLshipped counterparts checked"", ""reason"": ""This is an omitted target: the paper explicitly re-tests the ulcerative colitis association of NFKB1 -94delATTG reported by Karban et al. (2004) in its new sample cohorts and finds no significant evidence of association.""}","{""authors"": ""Karban AS, Okazaki T, Panhuysen CI, Gallegos T, Potter JJ, Bailey-Wilson JE et al."", ""year"": ""2004"", ""title"": ""Functional annotation of a novel NFKB1 promoter polymorphism that increases risk for ulcerative colitis."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We first set out to confirm the published associations of , susceptibility to CD or UC with NFKB1 and TLR4 alleles. Specifically, we attempted to replicate the putative association of the -94delATTG allele of NFKB1 (NFKB1 94delATTG) with UC and the 299Gly allele of TLR4 (TLR4299G1y) with CD and UC.24,25"", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In terms of the putative NFKB1 94delATTG risk allele, Karban et al.25 first reported an association with risk of developing UC. We therefore tested this specific hypothesis in our entire set of samples but did not find significant evidence of association (OR = 0.99, 95% CI: 0.81-1.2; Supplementary Table 2)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The paper’s own samples did not support the original UC association; the pooled literature finding is not the outcome of the new-sample replication.""}"
10.1017/s003329170500543x,bfd9f32714e4aa69,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Overall findings of our first direct interview family study of obsessive–compulsive disorder (OCD) indicated that OCD is familial. In this replication study, we carefully examined the role of informant data in ascertaining OCD in relatives."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""When relative diagnoses were derived without the benefit of proband informant reports, no evidence of familial OCD transmission was found. When diagnoses were made including information from the proband about the relative, evidence of familial OCD was found, but only when the diagnostic threshold was lowered to include cases with probable OCD or OCD symptoms."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Original familial association is stated. Authors report no familial evidence under one diagnostic procedure and evidence under another with a lowered threshold, and explicitly describe their results as inconsistent. Less robust support does not itself cause the mixed verdict; the contrasted presence and absence of the finding does.""}"
10.1371/journal.pone.0138922,1b9af749879bd4dc,1,,Cognitive style and religiosity: The role of conflict detection.,"Pennycook G, Cheyne JA, Barr N, Koehler DJ, Fugelsang JA.",2013,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Pennycook G, Cheyne JA, Barr N, Koehler DJ, Fugelsang JA."", ""doi"": """", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Cognitive style and religiosity: The role of conflict detection."", ""year"": ""2013""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""1b9af749879bd4dc"", ""location"": ""Results / Discussion / References (ref 11), line 1 and line 12"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Indeed, when participants completed the analytic thinking task first we found significant negative correlations between NFC and intrinsic religiosity, r (205) = -.18, p = .011, and NFC and belief in supernatural agents, r (205) = -.17, p = .017, respectively, and a non-significant negative correlation between NFC and intuitive religious beliefs, r (205) = -.12, p = .080. These patterns mirror findings reported by Pennycook et al. (Study 1) [ 11 ]. When participants completed the religious beliefs measures first, however, NFC was no longer correlated with any of the religious beliefs measures: intrinsic religiosity r (205) = .06, p = .394; intuitive religious beliefs r (205) = .06, p = .384; belief in supernatural agents r (205) = .03, p = .647."", ""reason"": ""In the source paper (Finley et al., 2015), the authors test the correlations between Need for Cognition (NFC) and religious belief measures (intrinsic religiosity, intuitive religious beliefs, and belief in supernatural agents) and explicitly evaluate whether these patterns reproduce the correlations reported by Pennycook et al. (2013, Study 1, reference [11]), stating that when participants completed the analytic thinking task first, 'These patterns mirror findings reported by Pennycook et al. (Study 1) [ 11 ].' The only shipped row for this work (input_row_index 888) is Gervais & Norenzayan (2012, 10.1126/science.1215647), a different paper and experimental effect. Therefore, Pennycook et al. (2013) is a genuinely absent replication-original pair."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0008_1b9af749879bd4dc"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Pennycook G, Cheyne JA, Barr N, Koehler DJ, Fugelsang JA."", ""year"": ""2013"", ""title"": ""Cognitive style and religiosity: The role of conflict detection."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""1"", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Furthermore, in light of prior evidence linking the need for cognition (NFC) and religiosity [ 11 ], we also included a measure of need for cognition (NFC) at the end of the study to allow for exploratory analyses probing the relationship between NFC and religious beliefs; these analyses yielded additional support for the measurement order hypothesis."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""These patterns mirror findings reported by Pennycook et al. (Study 1) [ 11 ]. When participants completed the religious beliefs measures first, however, NFC was no longer correlated with any of the religious beliefs measures: intrinsic religiosity r (205) = .06, p = .394; intuitive religious beliefs r (205) = .06, p = .384; belief in supernatural agents r (205) = .03, p = .647."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Explicit exploratory re-test of NFC/religiosity relationship agrees under analytic-first order and fails under reversed order. The reference prints 2013 (despite volume issue metadata suggesting a later issue).""}"
10.1080/17470210903377380,55cf730a319892f9,1,10.1037/0278-7393.30.3.723,The time course of recovery for grammatical class information during lexical processing for syntactic construction,"Pechmann, T.; Garrett, M. F.; Zerbst, D.",2004,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Pechmann, T.; Garrett, M. F.; Zerbst, D."", ""doi"": ""10.1037/0278-7393.30.3.723"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""The time course of recovery for grammatical class information during lexical processing for syntactic construction"", ""year"": ""2004""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""55cf730a319892f9"", ""location"": ""Introduction; Experiment 1 Discussion; Experiment 2 Method, Results, and Discussion; General Discussion; Table 2; References (line 1)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Experiment 2 revealed that the word class effect could not be separated from the imageability effect: Naming latencies were sensitive to distractor imageability (see Lupker, 1979 ), but were not sensitive to distractor word class when imageability was controlled. These results conflict with the results of Pechmann and colleagues (2004 ; Pechmann & Zerbst, 2002 )."", ""reason"": ""No prior adjudication or state file exists for this work. While Experiment 1 directly replicates Pechmann and Zerbst (2002, Experiment 4; DOI 10.1037/0278-7393.28.1.233, represented by shipped row 899) in German, Pechmann, Garrett, and Zerbst (2004, DOI 10.1037/0278-7393.30.3.723) is a separate published study that tested the picture-word interference grammatical class effect in English (Experiments 2 and 3) to rule out German grammatical gender confounds. In Experiment 2 of the present paper, the authors test native English speakers in sentence-frame and bare-noun picture naming while orthogonally manipulating distractor word class and imageability to retest whether the word class effect reported by Pechmann et al. (2004) and Pechmann and Zerbst (2002) holds when imageability is controlled, and explicitly conclude in the General Discussion that Experiment 2 conflicts with Pechmann et al. (2004; Pechmann & Zerbst, 2002). Because Pechmann, Garrett, and Zerbst (2004) is a distinct empirical article whose English PWI word-class finding is empirically retested and contrasted in Experiment 2 and is absent from shipped row 899, target_index 1 is a genuinely missing original target."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0040_55cf730a319892f9"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Pechmann, T.; Garrett, M. F.; Zerbst, D."", ""year"": ""2004"", ""title"": ""The time course of recovery for grammatical class information during lexical processing for syntactic construction"", ""doi"": ""10.1037/0278-7393.30.3.723"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""If the word class effect observed here and by Pechmann and colleagues (2004 ; Pechmann & Zerbst, 2002 ) does not reflect a lexical effect, it would remove such evidence as support for models that assume word class constraints on lexical selection (e.g., Dell et al., 2008 ). In Experiment 2, we tried to establish whether a word class effect could be obtained when the variable imageability was controlled."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Experiment 2 revealed that the word class effect could not be separated from the imageability effect: Naming latencies were sensitive to distractor imageability (see Lupker, 1979 ), but were not sensitive to distractor word class when imageability was controlled. These results conflict with the results of Pechmann and colleagues (2004 ; Pechmann & Zerbst, 2002 )."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly compare the tested word class effects to this original; outcome follows the reported agreement and disagreement.""}"
10.1016/j.jalz.2011.05.2412,22ff3d21c62fefc0,1,,Genome-wide association study identifies variants at CLU and PICALM associated with Alzheimer’s disease.,"Harold D, Abraham R, Hollingworth P, Sims R, Gerrish A, Hamshere ML, et al.",2009,full_text,competing_supported_matches,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""Complement receptor 1 gene polymorphism rs3818361 was recently shown to increase the risk of Alzheimer’s disease (AD). We performed an independent replication study of this genetic variant in 2470 individuals from Spain."", ""location"": ""Abstract, p. e124"", ""reason"": ""Harold et al. (2009, reference [2]) did not achieve genome-wide significance for CR1 and is cited only as concurrent background context and included in the pooled meta-analysis, whereas the new-sample replication specifically tests and compares its results against the CR1 rs3818361 finding reported by Lambert et al. [1].""}","{""authors"": ""Harold D, Abraham R, Hollingworth P, Sims R, Gerrish A, Hamshere ML, et al."", ""year"": ""2009"", ""title"": ""Genome-wide association study identifies variants at CLU and PICALM associated with Alzheimer’s disease."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Furthermore, a consistent signal within CR1 gene was observed in a concurrent independent GWA study published by other European researchers, although no GWAS significance was achieved in that particular study [2]. Because the follow-up and replication of GWAS findings provide further validation of proposed signals, we decided to evaluate a CR1 marker with the most consistent association to AD (rs3818361) as a genetic modifying factor for AD risk in a sample of the Spanish population."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Overall, the CR1 rs3818361 marker was not significantly associated to AD in this sample of the Spanish population."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""fulltext"", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The paper describes this original as a consistent CR1 signal without genome-wide significance; its own sample likewise shows a nonsignificant same-direction trend. Implicit agreement; less certain than the explicit comparison to Lambert. Original data included in meta-analysis do not alone constitute a separate reproduction.""}"
10.3389/fpsyg.2014.00875,10507719f2a93503,1,,Studies of similarity,"Tversky, A., Gati, I.",1978,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""corrected_value"": null, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""10507719f2a93503"", ""location"": ""Introduction (chunk 8, view_line_1based 10); Study 2a and 2b: Diagnosticity and Grouping (chunk 20, view_line_1based 23); Study 2b Discussion (chunk 30, view_line_1based 32); References (chunk 37, view_line_1based 39)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""In Study 2 we attempted to replicate Experiment 4 in Tversky and Gati ( 1978 ) which was reported in Tversky ( 1977 ) as the second study providing support for the diagnosticity effect."", ""reason"": ""Addressing the prior adjudication and parent_objection explicitly: the source text establishes that Study 2 in Tversky (1977) and Experiment 4 in Tversky and Gati (1978) are two published reports of the exact same original country-similarity diagnosticity experiment (20 sets of 4 countries, N = 33, with diagnosticity effects of 11.8% on choices for b and 11.6% on choices for c), where Tversky (1977) reported the study in summary form and Tversky and Gati (1978) described the same experiment in greater procedural and statistical detail ('Study 2 on the diagnosticity principle, (described in more detail as Experiment 4 in Tversky and Gati, 1978 ) is a conceptual replication using semantic stimuli'). The codebook's 'closest study in the chain' rule applies to replication chains where a finding was subsequently re-tested by others, not to summary versus full companion reports of the same original experiment by the original authors. Under the candidate review report-equivalence rules—which require confirming the absence of the original work/finding across all shipped rows under alternate citations or preliminary/full reports—this underlying original experiment and finding are already represented by shipped row 919 (Tversky, 1977, 10.1037/0033-295x.84.4.327), so Tversky and Gati (1978) is not an independent omitted original. Note also that Luna's identity_quote failed mechanical validation by omitting the space before the closing parenthesis in 'Tversky and Gati, 1978 )'."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0004_10507719f2a93503"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""shipped_right""}","{""authors"": ""Tversky, A., Gati, I."", ""year"": ""1978"", ""title"": ""Studies of similarity"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""Experiment 4"", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""2a, 2b"", ""evidence_quote"": ""In Study 2 we attempted to replicate Experiment 4 in Tversky and Gati ( 1978 ) which was reported in Tversky ( 1977 ) as the second study providing support for the diagnosticity effect."", ""outcome"": ""statistically successful but flawed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Thus, as the meta-analysis indicates, without eliminating possible substitution effects we replicate the original findings by Tversky ( 1977 ) and Tversky and Gati ( 1978 ). However, eliminating substitution effects strongly reduces the observed differences between conditions with averages of 2.89% (1a), 17.5% (1b), −1.21% (2a), and 1.24% (2b). Overall the size of differences between conditions, when substitution effects were eliminated, clustered around zero (see Figure 6 )."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors replicate the original choice pattern but identify substitution as a central confound; the effect disappears overall once that confound is eliminated. This is the paper’s main message, with the explicit joint verdict naming both originals.""}"
10.1017/ssh.2017.27,0245b18b007f60d7,1,10.1126/science.1251936,Inequality in the Long Run,"Piketty, Thomas, and Emmanuel Saez",2014,full_text,competing_supported_matches; conflicting_dois; same_author_year_siblings,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""I replicate the figure from Science here as Figure 4. It reproduced the data in Figure 10.6 from Piketty’s book except it drops the point for 1810, but it now inexplicably adds a point for 1890 that isn't in the book’s version. The point plotted appears to be a simple linear interpolation between 1870 and 1910."", ""location"": ""results"", ""reason"": ""Duplicate / secondary review article rather than an omitted target. Sutch explicitly describes Piketty and Saez (2014) in Science as 'a coauthored review with Emmanuel Saez which presents his data for the top ten percent' that 'reproduced the data in Figure 10.6 from Piketty’s book,' and Sutch's empirical critique in that section analyzes Piketty's book spreadsheet (TS10.1DetailsUS) and Technical Appendix (Piketty 2014b). Per the codebook rules on ruling out another work by the same authors and a review versus the primary study, it is not a separate original target.""}","{""authors"": ""Piketty, Thomas, and Emmanuel Saez"", ""year"": ""2014"", ""title"": ""Inequality in the Long Run"", ""doi"": ""10.1126/science.1251936"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""At least in the issue of Science for May 23, 2014 he has a coauthored review with Emmanuel Saez which presents his data for the top ten percent but does not present the companion series for the top one percent [Piketty and Saez 2014: Figure 2, 839]. I replicate the figure from Science here as Figure 4. It reproduced the data in Figure 10.6 from Piketty’s book except it drops the point for 1810, but it now inexplicably adds a point for 1890 that isn't in the book’s version."", ""outcome"": """", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""fulltext"", ""outcome_computation"": ""computational issues"", ""outcome_computational_quote"": ""For 1980 Piketty reported that he averaged the 1983 figure from Wolff [1994] with the 1989 Federal Reserve figure from Kennickell [2009]. Had he done that, the number would be 67.7, but he reported 67.2 (which is the 1989 figure unaveraged)."", ""outcome_robustness"": ""robustness challenges"", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": ""For 1870 Piketty reports Soltow’s number taken from Lindert, 71 percent, but without applying the 1.2 multiplier that he used on the top one-percent wealth share, which consistency suggests he should have done. Had he done so, his 1870 figure would be 85.2 percent of the wealth in the hands of the top 10 percent. But that figure implies there was a fall in the wealth share between 1870 and 1910."", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Reconstruction yields substantive discrepancies in reported shares; alternative constructions of the same source estimates qualify the historical trend.""}"
10.1371/journal.pone.0193787,ff87921f978478fb,0,,Implicit and explicit behavioral tendencies in male and female depression.,"Seidel E-M, Habel U, Finkelmeyer A, Schneider F, Gur RC, Derntl B.",2010,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Seidel E-M, Habel U, Finkelmeyer A, Schneider F, Gur RC, Derntl B."", ""year"": ""2010"", ""title"": ""Implicit and explicit behavioral tendencies in male and female depression."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Although we do not have previous genetic research to base our hypotheses on, based on clinical experiences, information processing theories in depression and the research by Seidel and colleagues [ 42 ], we expected avoidance of negative (sad, angry) facial expressions in remitted depressed S/L g -carriers who have experienced childhood trauma, as this represents the most vulnerable subgroup."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In line with Seidel and colleagues [ 42 ], we expected automatic avoidance of angry faces as well. We did not find this, however."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The specific re-tested Seidel finding is automatic avoidance of angry faces, explicitly not found. New sad-face/genetic interaction results are not a successful replication of Seidel’s angry-face effect.""}"
10.3389/fmicb.2018.01598,93b6eb882efbcfbb,1,10.1016/j.jpedsurg.2014.01.060,Intestinal dysbiosis and bacterial enteroinvasion in a murine model of Hirschsprung’s disease.,"Pierre, J. F., Barlow-Anacker, A. J., Erickson, C. S., Heneghan, A. F., Leverson, G. E., Dowd, S. E.",2014,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Pierre, J. F., Barlow-Anacker, A. J., Erickson, C. S., Heneghan, A. F., Leverson, G. E., Dowd, S. E."", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.jpedsurg.2014.01.060"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Intestinal dysbiosis and bacterial enteroinvasion in a murine model of Hirschsprung’s disease."", ""year"": ""2014""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""93b6eb882efbcfbb"", ""location"": ""Introduction, lines 188-194; Discussion, lines 537-539, 562-564, 586-589; References, lines 1732-1754"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Previous work using murine models of HSCR and HAEC revealed intestinal dysbiosis associated with aganglionosis, indicating a potential role for intestinal microbiomes in the promotion of HAEC (\nWard et al., 2012\n;\nPierre et al., 2014\n). We examined the reproducibility of these findings and also investigated the contribution of inter-facility effects to microbiome variability in C57BL/6J mice, reproducing the findings of\nRausch et al. (2016)\non a smaller scale."", ""reason"": ""No prior adjudication or state file exists for this work. In the Introduction, the authors explicitly state that they examined the reproducibility of the intestinal dysbiosis findings in murine models of HSCR and HAEC reported by both Ward et al. (2012) and Pierre et al. (2014). In the Discussion, they directly compare their empirical genotype-based (KO vs. WT) taxonomic differences in Ednrb mice against the findings of Pierre et al. (2014) across multiple taxa, including enrichment of Enterobacteriaceae/Proteobacteria in Laramie KO mice, higher abundance of Bacteroides in KO mice, and lower abundance of Lactobacillus in diseased versus healthy mice. Reference lines 1732-1754 identify Pierre et al. (2014) (10.1016/j.jpedsurg.2014.01.060, J. Pediatr. Surg. 49, 1242-1251), which is an independent original study from a different research group rather than an alternate report of shipped row 938 (Ward; Pieretti; Dowd; Cox; Goldstein, 2012, 10.1111/j.1365-2982.2012.01937.x) or of sibling target_index 2 (Rausch et al., 2016)."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0073_93b6eb882efbcfbb"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Pierre, J. F., Barlow-Anacker, A. J., Erickson, C. S., Heneghan, A. F., Leverson, G. E., Dowd, S. E."", ""year"": ""2014"", ""title"": ""Intestinal dysbiosis and bacterial enteroinvasion in a murine model of Hirschsprung’s disease."", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.jpedsurg.2014.01.060"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Previous work using murine models of HSCR and HAEC revealed intestinal dysbiosis associated with aganglionosis, indicating a potential role for intestinal microbiomes in the promotion of HAEC (\nWard et al., 2012\n;\nPierre et al., 2014\n). We examined the reproducibility of these findings and also investigated the contribution of inter-facility effects to microbiome variability in C57BL/6J mice, reproducing the findings of\nRausch et al. (2016)\non a smaller scale."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Instead, Laramie Ednrb mice showed an enrichment of family Enterobacteriaceae from phylum Proteobacteria. Observations for these taxa are consistent with previously published work in another HSCR/HAEC mouse model (\nPierre et al., 2014\n) and shifts in Bacteroidetes (\nFrank et al., 2007\n;\nWalker et al., 2011\n) and Proteobacteria (\nFrank et al., 2007\n) have been reported in other IBD related to HAEC."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Introduction explicitly includes this paper among the dysbiosis findings re-tested; discussion compares concordant taxa.""}"
10.3389/fmicb.2018.01598,93b6eb882efbcfbb,2,10.1016/j.ijmm.2016.03.004,Analysis of factors contributing to variation in the c57bl/6j fecal microbiota across german animal facilities.,"Rausch, P., Basic, M., Batra, A., Bischoff, S. C., Blaut, M., Clavel, T.",2016,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Rausch, P., Basic, M., Batra, A., Bischoff, S. C., Blaut, M., Clavel, T."", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.ijmm.2016.03.004"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Analysis of factors contributing to variation in the c57bl/6j fecal microbiota across german animal facilities."", ""year"": ""2016""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""93b6eb882efbcfbb"", ""location"": ""Introduction, lines 188-194; Results, lines 445-479; Discussion, lines 493-495; References, lines 1784-1806"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Previous work using murine models of HSCR and HAEC revealed intestinal dysbiosis associated with aganglionosis, indicating a potential role for intestinal microbiomes in the promotion of HAEC (\nWard et al., 2012\n;\nPierre et al., 2014\n). We examined the reproducibility of these findings and also investigated the contribution of inter-facility effects to microbiome variability in C57BL/6J mice, reproducing the findings of\nRausch et al. (2016)\non a smaller scale."", ""reason"": ""No prior adjudication or state file exists for this work. Beyond testing the Ednrb disease model, the authors conducted a dedicated empirical sub-study of fecal microbiomes in normal C57BL/6J mice across two facilities (n = 10 mice per facility in Boston and Laramie) explicitly 'reproducing the findings of Rausch et al. (2016) on a smaller scale,' testing inter-facility PERMANOVA UniFrac differences, core microbiome conservation, and alpha diversity (Results lines 445-479) and concluding in the Discussion that 'Our findings in C57BL/6J mice are consistent with a previous report ( Rausch et al., 2016 ).' Reference lines 1784-1806 identify Rausch et al. (2016) (10.1016/j.ijmm.2016.03.004, Int. J. Med. Microbiol. 306, 343-355), which is completely distinct from shipped row 938 (Ward et al., 2012, 10.1111/j.1365-2982.2012.01937.x) and from sibling target_index 1 (Pierre et al., 2014, 10.1016/j.jpedsurg.2014.01.060)."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0073_93b6eb882efbcfbb"", ""target_index"": 2, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Rausch, P., Basic, M., Batra, A., Bischoff, S. C., Blaut, M., Clavel, T."", ""year"": ""2016"", ""title"": ""Analysis of factors contributing to variation in the c57bl/6j fecal microbiota across german animal facilities."", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.ijmm.2016.03.004"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Previous work using murine models of HSCR and HAEC revealed intestinal dysbiosis associated with aganglionosis, indicating a potential role for intestinal microbiomes in the promotion of HAEC (\nWard et al., 2012\n;\nPierre et al., 2014\n). We examined the reproducibility of these findings and also investigated the contribution of inter-facility effects to microbiome variability in C57BL/6J mice, reproducing the findings of\nRausch et al. (2016)\non a smaller scale."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The same facility-specific effect was also seen in fecal samples from C57BL/6J mice. These effects were independent of sex or cage influences. Our findings in C57BL/6J mice are consistent with a previous report (\nRausch et al., 2016\n)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Specific facility-effect replication explicitly stated and confirmed.""}"
