10.51910/ijhdr.v11i40.598,d7591f8fdbd060c8,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Three series including five independent blinded and randomised experiments each were carried out in the same way as in the original study. Only time and conductor of experiments were modified."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""No specific effects of agitated dilutions of gibberellic acid were found in the first two replication series (p=0.263 and p=0.062). In the third serie with gibbous Lemna gibba L. we observed a significant effect (p=0.009) of the homeopathic treatment, however growth was increased in contrast to decreasing in the former study."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The first two series show no original effect and the third shows the opposite direction. The abstract identifies no bibliographic entry for the former study; no target citation is guessed.""}"
10.1016/j.jml.2022.104352,23e78360aaf0d0af,1,10.1121/1.408177,Training Japanese listeners to identify English /r/ and /l/. II: The role of phonetic environment and talker variability in learning new perceptual categories,"Lively, S. E., Logan, J. S., & Pisoni, D. B.",1993,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": ""10.1121/1.408177"", ""title"": ""Training Japanese listeners to identify English /r/ and /l/. II: The role of phonetic environment and talker variability in learning new perceptual categories"", ""authors"": ""Lively, S. E., Logan, J. S., & Pisoni, D. B."", ""year"": ""1993"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""failed""}, ""quote"": ""The goal of the current paper is to replicate the key experiments from Logan et al. (1991) and Lively et al. (1993) and test the hypothesis that using high variability input (specifically, input with multiple talkers) leads to more generalisation in phonetic learning than when low variability input is used."", ""location"": ""Introduction (line 6)"", ""reason"": ""The paper explicitly re-tests both Logan et al. (1991) and Lively et al. (1993) (specifically Experiment 2 of Lively et al., 1993, providing the single-talker low-variability comparison and the familiar-versus-novel post-test contrast). As settled for the paired Logan et al. (1991) row, the replication outcome for Lively et al. (1993) is 'failed' rather than 'uninformative': the study was high-powered (N = 166, 99% power for the original effect size), found no evidence of the claimed multi-talker generalization advantage across both primary measures, and concluded that any true effect is much smaller than reported in the seminal studies, with the ambiguous Bayes factors reflecting cautious Bayesian interpretation rather than a methodological failure preventing adjudication.""}","{""authors"": ""Lively, S. E., Logan, J. S., & Pisoni, D. B."", ""year"": ""1993"", ""title"": ""Training Japanese listeners to identify English /r/ and /l/. II: The role of phonetic environment and talker variability in learning new perceptual categories"", ""doi"": ""10.1121/1.408177"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""2"", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The goal of the current paper is to replicate the key experiments from Logan et al. (1991) and Lively et al. (1993) and test the hypothesis that using high variability input (specifically, input with multiple talkers) leads to more generalisation in phonetic learning than when low variability input is used."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The current study attempted to replicate the findings of seminal work by Logan and colleagues (1991) and Lively and colleagues (1993), which found that phonetic training with multiple talkers led to greater generalization to novel talkers than single talker training. Across two different measures, our study found no evidence of such effect in a sample of 166 participants. Bayes Factor analyses found that in each case the evidence for a variability benefit was ambiguous."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Central multiple-talker advantage was not supported; ambiguous Bayes factors do not establish the null. Positive general phonetic learning is ancillary to the central contrast.""}"
10.1037/tmb0000086,1c66ad66043de6d2,1,10.1177/09567976211024535,How accurate are accuracy-nudge interventions? A preregistered direct replication of Pennycook et al. (2020).,"Roozenbeek, J., Freeman, A. L. J., & van der Linden, S.",2021,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Roozenbeek, J., Freeman, A. L. J., & van der Linden, S."", ""doi"": ""10.1177/09567976211024535"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""How accurate are accuracy-nudge interventions? A preregistered direct replication of Pennycook et al. (2020)."", ""year"": ""2021""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""1c66ad66043de6d2"", ""location"": ""Discussion (lines 909-920)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Our hypotheses attempted to replicate Roozenbeek et al.’s (2021)\nﬁndings. Roozenbeek et al. tested whether prompting people to\nthink about accuracy reduced their willingness to share misinfor-\nmation and/or increased their willingness to share true information\nrelated to COVID-19 generally. They found the accuracy nudge\ndecreased intentions to share misinformation but did not signiﬁ-\ncantly increase intentions to share true information. Our ﬁndings\nare in partial agreement with Roozenbeek and colleagues. The\naccuracy nudge did not decrease willingness to share false general\nCOVID-19 information in Kyrgyzstan or the United States. In India,\nthe accuracy nudge did decrease willingness to share general\nCOVID-19 misinformation."", ""reason"": ""No prior adjudication or state file exists for this work, and an intact primary-source passage is quoted without line-break hyphen repair. The full text explicitly sets out to test whether the findings of Pennycook et al. (2020) or Roozenbeek et al. (2021) replicate across three samples (Kyrgyzstan in Study 1; India and the United States in Study 2), adopts Roozenbeek et al.'s (2021) frequentist and Bayesian one-tailed t-test analytic framework, and directly compares its empirical results against Roozenbeek et al.'s finding that the accuracy nudge decreases willingness to share false COVID-19 headlines without increasing willingness to share true headlines (Table 3, Results, and Discussion). The reference list (lines 1220-1223) and Crossref metadata confirm the identity of Roozenbeek, Freeman, & van der Linden (2021), Psychological Science, 32(7), 1169-1178, DOI 10.1177/09567976211024535. Shipped row 975 represents only Pennycook et al. (2020) (DOI 10.1177/0956797620939054, sibling target_index 0); Roozenbeek et al. (2021) is an independent replication-chain target by different authors reporting a distinct empirical pattern and is absent from all shipped rows."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0009_1c66ad66043de6d2"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Roozenbeek, J., Freeman, A. L. J., & van der Linden, S."", ""year"": ""2021"", ""title"": ""How accurate are accuracy-nudge interventions? A preregistered direct replication of Pennycook et al. (2020)."", ""doi"": ""10.1177/09567976211024535"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""The\npresent study aims to replicate Pennycook et al. and test whether\nﬁndings from Pennycook et al. or Roozenbeek et al. are replicated in\na third U.S. sample."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Our ﬁndings\nare in partial agreement with Roozenbeek and colleagues. The\naccuracy nudge did not decrease willingness to share false general\nCOVID-19 information in Kyrgyzstan or the United States. In India,\nthe accuracy nudge did decrease willingness to share general\nCOVID-19 misinformation. However, in full agreement with Roo-\nzenbeek and colleagues, we found strong evidence suggesting\nthat the accuracy nudge does not signiﬁcantly increase the spread\nof true information in any of the three countries."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Aggregated across the two replication studies and three country samples; authors explicitly describe partial or mixed replication. Vaccine-specific measures extend the original claim.""}"
10.1027/1618-3169/a000554,bb552c10b784b963,0,10.1016/s0022-5371(80)90403-x,Auditory encoding in visual short-term recall: Effects of noise intensity and spatial location.,"Colle, H. A.",1980,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 0, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": ""10.1016/s0022-5371(80)90403-x"", ""title"": ""Auditory encoding in visual short-term recall: Effects of noise intensity and spatial location."", ""authors"": ""Colle, H. A."", ""year"": ""1980"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined""}, ""quote"": ""In all seven experiments conducted so far, the changing-state effect on serial recall was independent of intensity (Colle, 1980; Ellermeier & Hellbrück, 1998; Schlittmeier et al., 2008; Tremblay & Jones, 1999), and this was also the case in the present study."", ""location"": ""discussion"", ""reason"": ""Omitted target: the paper frames its conceptual replication around the set of seven prior experiments across four papers (including Colle, 1980) and directly compares both its replicated changing-state result and its unexpected overall intensity effect against Colle (1980). Omitted-target identity retained; individual outcome held pending final source-scope review.""}","{""authors"": ""Colle, H. A."", ""year"": ""1980"", ""title"": ""Auditory encoding in visual short-term recall: Effects of noise intensity and spatial location."", ""doi"": ""10.1016/s0022-5371(80)90403-x"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""1, 2, 3"", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In all seven experiments conducted so far, the changing-state effect on serial recall was independent of intensity (Colle, 1980; Ellermeier & Hellbrück, 1998; Schlittmeier et al., 2008; Tremblay & Jones, 1999), and this was also the case in the present study."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We replicated the classic finding that the increased disruption by changing-state relative to steady-state sequences is independent of intensity. Contrary to previous studies, we found an unexpected main effect of intensity. Steady-state and changing-state sequences presented at 75 dB(A) were more disruptive than presented at 45 dB(A), suggesting that intensity plays a more important role than previously assumed in the disruption of working memory performance."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors explicitly confirm intensity-independent changing-state disruption but contrast their overall intensity effect with this original’s reported null intensity effect. Both comparisons concern the same target."", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """"}"
10.1027/1618-3169/a000554,bb552c10b784b963,1,10.1037/0096-1523.24.5.1406,"Is level irrelevant in „irrelevant speech“? Effects of loudness, signal-to-noise ratio, and binaural unmasking.","Ellermeier, W., & Hellbrück, J.",1998,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": ""10.1037/0096-1523.24.5.1406"", ""title"": ""Is level irrelevant in „irrelevant speech“? Effects of loudness, signal-to-noise ratio, and binaural unmasking."", ""authors"": ""Ellermeier, W., & Hellbrück, J."", ""year"": ""1998"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined""}, ""quote"": ""In all seven experiments conducted so far, the changing-state effect on serial recall was independent of intensity (Colle, 1980; Ellermeier & Hellbrück, 1998; Schlittmeier et al., 2008; Tremblay & Jones, 1999), and this was also the case in the present study."", ""location"": ""discussion"", ""reason"": ""Omitted target: the paper explicitly includes Ellermeier & Hellbrück (1998) among the four original studies whose intensity findings are conceptually re-tested and directly compared with the present results. Omitted-target identity retained; individual outcome held pending final source-scope review.""}","{""authors"": ""Ellermeier, W., & Hellbrück, J."", ""year"": ""1998"", ""title"": ""Is level irrelevant in „irrelevant speech“? Effects of loudness, signal-to-noise ratio, and binaural unmasking."", ""doi"": ""10.1037/0096-1523.24.5.1406"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""1"", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In all seven experiments conducted so far, the changing-state effect on serial recall was independent of intensity (Colle, 1980; Ellermeier & Hellbrück, 1998; Schlittmeier et al., 2008; Tremblay & Jones, 1999), and this was also the case in the present study."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We replicated the classic finding that the increased disruption by changing-state relative to steady-state sequences is independent of intensity. Contrary to previous studies, we found an unexpected main effect of intensity. Steady-state and changing-state sequences presented at 75 dB(A) were more disruptive than presented at 45 dB(A), suggesting that intensity plays a more important role than previously assumed in the disruption of working memory performance."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors explicitly confirm intensity-independent changing-state disruption but contrast their overall intensity effect with this original’s reported null intensity effect. Both comparisons concern the same target."", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """"}"
