10.1098/rsos.221091,393e7ac9c52214e5,0,10.7554/eLife.53900,"Distinct neural contributions to metacognition for detecting, but not discriminating visual stimuli.","Mazor M, Friston KJ, Fleming SM.",2020,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Mazor M, Friston KJ, Fleming SM."", ""doi"": ""10.7554/eLife.53900"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Distinct neural contributions to metacognition for detecting, but not discriminating visual stimuli."", ""year"": ""2020""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""393e7ac9c52214e5"", ""location"": ""Introduction / Section 1.1 Hypotheses (lines 9-19); Results Section 3.2.6 Hypothesis 6 (line 45); Discussion (line 49); References [6] and [15] (lines 61, 70)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""In our original study [6], a cluster in the rTPJ showed a negative linear effect of confidence, which was significantly more negative in detection ‘no’ compared with ‘yes’ responses. In the current study, activation in this region again showed a negative linear, as well as a positive quadratic effect of confidence. However, an interaction between confidence and detection response was not significant (t34 = 0.72, p = 0.47)."", ""reason"": ""This paper is an empirical pre-registered fMRI replication of the authors' original 2020 eLife fMRI study [6] (Mazor M, Friston KJ, Fleming SM, 2020, 'Distinct neural contributions to metacognition for detecting, but not discriminating visual stimuli', eLife 9, e53900, DOI 10.7554/eLife.53900), explicitly referred to throughout as 'our original study [6]' and retesting its Hypothesis 6 alongside five hypotheses in the rTPJ, frontopolar cortex, and vmPFC. The sole shipped row (input_row_index 979, DOI 10.1093/nc/niab025) is a separate 2021 behavioural Registered Report by Mazor, Moran, & Fleming in Neuroscience of Consciousness ('Metacognitive asymmetries in visual perception', cited separately as reference [15]), which is not an alternate report of the 2020 eLife fMRI study [6]. Thus, the replicated 2020 eLife study is a genuine missing row."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0027_393e7ac9c52214e5"", ""target_index"": 0, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Mazor M, Friston KJ, Fleming SM."", ""year"": ""2020"", ""title"": ""Distinct neural contributions to metacognition for detecting, but not discriminating visual stimuli."", ""doi"": ""10.7554/eLife.53900"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In a previous study we identified distinct neural contributions to confidence in perceptual detection that were not observed in a performance-matched discrimination task. In this pre-registered follow-up study, we set out to replicate this finding and to further characterize the computational origins of a quadratic modulation of confidence."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We successfully replicated the quadratic modulation of subjective confidence in prefrontal, parietal and temporal cortices. However, in contrast with our original report, this quadratic effect was similar in detection and discrimination responses, but stronger in the novel asymmetric-discrimination condition."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Quadratic confidence modulation replicated, but authors explicitly contrast absence of detection specificity; rTPJ response asymmetry also failed. Success statement concerns one finding, not an overall successful verdict.""}"
10.1111/acer.15532,a679446e24e8a56a,1,10.1007/s00213-018-4922-8,"Alcohol use disorder symptoms are associated with greater relative value ascribed to alcohol, but not greater discounting of costs imposed on alcohol.","Hogarth, L. & Hardy, L.",2018,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Hogarth, L. & Hardy, L."", ""year"": ""2018"", ""title"": ""Alcohol use disorder symptoms are associated with greater relative value ascribed to alcohol, but not greater discounting of costs imposed on alcohol."", ""doi"": ""10.1007/s00213-018-4922-8"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Our behavioral findings partially replicate previous results, showing that choice preference for alcohol was affected by the relative value of the alternative reward (Hogarth & Hardy,  2018 ; Karlsson et al.,  2024 )."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Our behavioral findings partially replicate previous results, showing that choice preference for alcohol was affected by the relative value of the alternative reward (Hogarth & Hardy,  2018 ; Karlsson et al.,  2024 )."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly describe their behavioral findings as partially replicating the set of results from both cited papers. Relative-cost sensitivity replicated, while the relation between alcohol use severity and alcohol choice did not. The overall partial-replication verdict applies to both named originals.""}"
10.1121/10.0042222,3b3b69b425327e86,1,10.1016/j.cognition.2022.105320,Do individual differences in lexical reliance reflect states or traits?,"Giovannone, N., and Theodore, R. M.",2023,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Giovannone, N., and Theodore, R. M."", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.cognition.2022.105320"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Do individual differences in lexical reliance reflect states or traits?"", ""year"": ""2023""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""3b3b69b425327e86"", ""location"": ""Section VI (Discussion), lines 3143-3163; References, lines 4105-4106"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Indeed, Giovannone & Theodore (2023) found in both sessions of their Ganong task that\n\n662\n\nindividuals who showed greater lexical effect used continuum VOT less during category\n\n663\n\nidentification. Results from Task 2 (Ganong Task) of the current experiment found the same\n\n664\n\npattern. However, in Task 1, we instead found a positive relationship (consistent with the original\n\n665\n\nstudy). Thus, the findings are again mixed on whether there is a general pattern of trading-off high\n\n666\n\nlevel information and acoustic information across individuals."", ""reason"": ""The primary source (Zhai, Clayards, & Sonderegger, 10.1121/10.0042222) explicitly formulates Research Question 2 (Section II, lines 1074-1078) to empirically re-test the negative individual-level trade-off between lexical reliance and acoustic continuum use reported by Giovannone & Theodore (2023), and tests this specific published finding with new participant data (N = 89) in Task 1 and Task 2 (Section V.B, lines 2436-2450; Section V.B.2, lines 2879-2915; Section VI, lines 3143-3163). The reference list (lines 4105-4106) establishes the exact bibliographic identity: Giovannone, N., and Theodore, R. M. (2023), 'Do individual differences in lexical reliance reflect states or traits?,' Cognition, 232, 105320 (doi:10.1016/j.cognition.2022.105320). The only shipped row for this work is input_row_index 993 (Lai, Wade, & Tamminga, 2022, Linguistics Vanguard, 10.1515/lingvan-2020-0040, which corresponds to target_index 0 and was confirmed in the prior adjudication file 7e51318dbb3bf89a9a482b8875991f4bb42320518fb2d8f6199855688fa555c1.json; no prior state file exists). Giovannone & Theodore (2023) is a completely independent original work by different authors in a different journal and is genuinely omitted from the shipped dataset."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0027_3b3b69b425327e86"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Giovannone, N., and Theodore, R. M."", ""year"": ""2023"", ""title"": ""Do individual differences in lexical reliance reflect states or traits?"", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.cognition.2022.105320"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Indeed, Giovannone & Theodore (2023) found in both sessions of their Ganong task that individuals who showed greater lexical effect used continuum VOT less during category identification. Results from Task 2 (Ganong Task) of the current experiment found the same pattern. However, in Task 1, we instead found a positive relationship (consistent with the original study). Thus, the findings are again mixed on whether there is a general pattern of trading-off high level information and acoustic information across individuals."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Indeed, Giovannone & Theodore (2023) found in both sessions of their Ganong task that individuals who showed greater lexical effect used continuum VOT less during category identification. Results from Task 2 (Ganong Task) of the current experiment found the same pattern. However, in Task 1, we instead found a positive relationship (consistent with the original study). Thus, the findings are again mixed on whether there is a general pattern of trading-off high level information and acoustic information across individuals."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The paper explicitly reports an agreeing result and a differing result across the tested relationships/tasks.""}"
10.1037/ebs0000298,d3aed2ed281ad7c4,0,,,Arnocky et al.,2017,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Arnocky et al."", ""year"": ""2017"", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""This study sought to directly replicate a previous investigation that demonstrated a positive association between altruism and indices of mating success (Arnocky et al., 2017)."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Overall, the findings are inconsistent with those of the original study and provide evidence that altruism does not predict mating success in humans."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract states an overall verdict of inconsistency with the original study and no association across the tested mating-success measures.""}"
10.1080/713755986,fed9b2985183e3e8,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""3"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Using the naming task, no affective priming was found in the present studies irrespective of prime-set size and target-set size (Experiment 1), irrespective of stimulus-onset asynchrony (Experiment 2), and even when a nearly exact replication of previous work that demonstrated the effect was conducted (Experiment 3)."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Using the naming task, no affective priming was found in the present studies irrespective of prime-set size and target-set size (Experiment 1), irrespective of stimulus-onset asynchrony (Experiment 2), and even when a nearly exact replication of previous work that demonstrated the effect was conducted (Experiment 3)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true}"
10.3389/fnins.2017.00664,69c54c69a95cf6e3,1,10.1016/j.brs.2011.01.003,Neglect-like effects induced by tDCS modulation of posterior parietal cortices in healthy subjects,"Giglia G., Mattaliano P., Puma A., Rizzo S., Fierro B., Brighina F.",2011,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Giglia G., Mattaliano P., Puma A., Rizzo S., Fierro B., Brighina F."", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.brs.2011.01.003"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Neglect-like effects induced by tDCS modulation of posterior parietal cortices in healthy subjects"", ""year"": ""2011""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""69c54c69a95cf6e3"", ""location"": ""Discussion - Inter-individual variability, lines 774-782; Results, lines 503-515; References, lines 1668-1693"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""In contrast to both Benwell et al. (\n2015\n) and Giglia et al. (\n2011\n) we did not succeed here in replicating the overall rightward shift of spatial attention bias with a bi-parietal left P5 anode/right P6 cathode montage. Although there was indeed a small\nmean\nrightward shift relative to the sham protocol in response to both stimulation intensities, and for both performance groups (Figure\n6\n), these shifts failed to reach significance. The Bayesian analysis of this overall rightward shift, comparing the two studies, indicates that the replication data is unfortunately insensitive to allow us to distinguish between the presence or absence of an effect (BF = 1.58), although eliciting an overall stimulation effect was not the main aim of this study."", ""reason"": ""Prior adjudication and state files were checked and do not exist (never-adjudicated work). The primary source (Learmonth et al., 2017) explicitly selects the bi-parietal left P5 anode / right P6 cathode (LA/RC) montage because it previously induced a significant overall rightward shift of visuospatial attention in both Benwell et al. (2015) and Giglia et al. (2011) (Introduction, lines 250-255), empirically tests this overall rightward shift relative to sham in the new data (Results, lines 503-515 and 748-756; t(28) = 1.31, p = 0.2, BF = 1.58), and concludes in the Discussion (lines 774-782) that in contrast to both Benwell et al. (2015) and Giglia et al. (2011), it did not succeed in replicating the overall rightward shift of spatial attention bias. Reference 33 (lines 1668-1693) provides full bibliographic identity and DOI (10.1016/j.brs.2011.01.003) for Giglia et al. (2011), which is an independent 2011 study by a distinct research group not represented by the sole shipped row 1020 (Benwell et al., 2015, 10.1016/j.cortex.2015.05.007, which matches sibling blind target 0)."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0051_69c54c69a95cf6e3"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Giglia G., Mattaliano P., Puma A., Rizzo S., Fierro B., Brighina F."", ""year"": ""2011"", ""title"": ""Neglect-like effects induced by tDCS modulation of posterior parietal cortices in healthy subjects"", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.brs.2011.01.003"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In contrast to both Benwell et al. (\n2015\n) and Giglia et al. (\n2011\n) we did not succeed here in replicating the overall rightward shift of spatial attention bias with a bi-parietal left P5 anode/right P6 cathode montage. Although there was indeed a small\nmean\nrightward shift relative to the sham protocol in response to both stimulation intensities, and for both performance groups (Figure\n6\n), these shifts failed to reach significance. The Bayesian analysis of this overall rightward shift, comparing the two studies, indicates that the replication data is unfortunately insensitive to allow us to distinguish between the presence or absence of an effect (BF = 1.58), although eliciting an overall stimulation effect was not the main aim of this study."", ""outcome"": ""uninformative"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In contrast to both Benwell et al. (\n2015\n) and Giglia et al. (\n2011\n) we did not succeed here in replicating the overall rightward shift of spatial attention bias with a bi-parietal left P5 anode/right P6 cathode montage. Although there was indeed a small\nmean\nrightward shift relative to the sham protocol in response to both stimulation intensities, and for both performance groups (Figure\n6\n), these shifts failed to reach significance. The Bayesian analysis of this overall rightward shift, comparing the two studies, indicates that the replication data is unfortunately insensitive to allow us to distinguish between the presence or absence of an effect (BF = 1.58), although eliciting an overall stimulation effect was not the main aim of this study."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true}"
10.1016/j.infbeh.2019.101340,d7dd7a918f384d33,1,10.3389/fpsyg.2012.00370,Actions seen through babies’ eyes: A dissociation between looking time and predictive gaze.,"Daum, M. M., Attig, M., Gunawan, R., Prinz, W., & Gredebäck, G.",2012,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Daum, M. M., Attig, M., Gunawan, R., Prinz, W., & Gredebäck, G."", ""year"": ""2012"", ""title"": ""Actions seen through babies’ eyes: A dissociation between looking time and predictive gaze."", ""doi"": ""10.3389/fpsyg.2012.00370"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""In the path-paradigm, we expected 11-month-olds and 32-month-olds to anticipate the old path, thus the novel goal, as the action is performed by a non-human agent (Daum et al., 2012)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Considering the path-paradigm with the animated agent, 11-month-olds looked longer towards the familiarized path leading to the novel goal. Hence results are similar to Study 1 and Daum et al. (2012)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""New samples re-test the path-versus-goal result in animated-agent tasks. The paper explicitly describes agreement with Daum for infant and toddler path anticipations; adults also anticipate the goal in the animated path paradigm.""}"
10.1016/j.infbeh.2019.101340,d7dd7a918f384d33,2,10.1016/j.yhbeh.2017.03.006,Oxytocin promotes attention to social cues regardless of group membership.,"Pfundmair, M., Zwarg, C., Paulus, M., & Rimpel, A.",2017,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Pfundmair, M., Zwarg, C., Paulus, M., & Rimpel, A."", ""year"": ""2017"", ""title"": ""Oxytocin promotes attention to social cues regardless of group membership."", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.yhbeh.2017.03.006"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""We hypothesized to find anticipations towards the familiarized goal within the adult sample in both paradigms (Daum et al., 2012; Pfundmair et al., 2017)."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Pfundmair et al. (2017) found that adults anticipated the goal of a grasping hand as well as of a grasping claw, which is in contrast to our findings."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Study 2 explicitly predicts adult goal anticipations from this prior finding and contrasts its observed adult hand-path anticipation with that result. The original oxytocin effect itself is not re-tested; target is the adult action-goal anticipation finding.""}"
10.1177/1948550616652209,d715987328d983ae,1,,Look me in the eye: Manipulated eye gaze affects dominance mindsets,"Tang, D., & Schmeichel, B. J.",2015,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Tang, D., & Schmeichel, B. J."", ""year"": ""2015"", ""title"": ""Look me in the eye: Manipulated eye gaze affects dominance mindsets"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We expected to replicate Carney et al.’s (2010) results when\nparticipants looked directly ahead, such that high-power (vs.\nlow power) posers report feeling more powerful and take the\ngambling risk more often; we also expected these participants\nto demonstrate dominance in the ultimatum game (consistent\nwith Tang & Schmeichel, 2015)."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In sum, holding a direct gaze increased the likelihood of\nrejecting a low offer on the hypothetical ultimatum game,\nwhich is consistent with the results of Tang and Schmeichel\n(2015). However, we did not find any effects of the pose or\ngaze manipulations on the amount of money participants\noffered to the opponent or the minimum amount of money par\u0002ticipants were willing to accept from the opponent."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true}"
10.1080/23743603.2016.1248081,0e4a00331066c9e6,1,10.1016/j.obhdp.2011.11.009,Power and overconﬁdent decision-making.,"Fast, N. J., Sivanathan, N., Mayer, N. D., & Galinsky, A. D.",2012,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Fast, N. J., Sivanathan, N., Mayer, N. D., & Galinsky, A. D."", ""year"": ""2012"", ""title"": ""Power and overconﬁdent decision-making."", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.obhdp.2011.11.009"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Building from these ﬁndings, Fast and colleagues (2012)\nreported ﬁve experiments that used multiple instantiations of power – including manip-\nulations via episodic recall tasks, role assignment, and extant work related power –\nwhere they found high power to be associated with overconﬁdent decision-making.\nBased on these ﬁndings, we propose that exaggerated expressions of conﬁdence will\nemerge as a function of the degree to which high-power poses facilitate a high-power\nmind-set."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""High-power (M = 0.14, SD = 0.53) posers did not have higher post-manipulation levels of\noverconﬁdence than low-power posers (M = −0.01, SD = 0.51), F(1,104) = 0.26, p = 0.61,\nd = 0.099, r = 0.05."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The extension directly tests the published power-to-overconfidence relationship using embodied power. The introduction states the original positive finding and derives this study’s prediction from it; the new-sample comparison fails to show it. This is a conceptual retest, even though called an extension.""}"
10.1080/23743603.2016.1248081,0e4a00331066c9e6,2,10.1016/j.psyneuen.2015.02.023,Testosterone and cortisol jointly modulate risk-taking.,"Mehta, P. H., Welker, K. M., Zilioli, S., & Carré, J. M.",2015,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Mehta, P. H., Welker, K. M., Zilioli, S., & Carré, J. M."", ""year"": ""2015"", ""title"": ""Testosterone and cortisol jointly modulate risk-taking."", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.psyneuen.2015.02.023"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Because Carney et al.\n(2010) found high-power poses to both increase testosterone and decrease cortisol, the\ninteraction between these eﬀects may predict risk-taking, as has been previously shown\n(Mehta et al., 2015). Furthermore, although Ranehill et al. (2015) failed to replicate\nCarney et al.’s (2010) eﬀects of power posing on hormonal changes, other manipulations\nrelated to power, such as winning a competition, have been shown to jointly impact\ntestosterone and cortisol (e.g. see Hamilton, Carré, Mehta, Olmstead, & Whitaker, 2015).\nThus, in the present research, we measured both testosterone and cortisol at two time\npoints, as per Carney et al.’s (2010) original design and tested for both main and\ninteractive eﬀects."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The interaction\nbetween post-manipulation testosterone and cortisol was also unrelated to gambling\ndecisions, z = 0.02, p = 0.90, φ = 0.037."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The paper explicitly invokes the previously shown testosterone-by-cortisol prediction of risk-taking, then measures both hormones to test their interactive effects in its own sample. The corresponding interaction is unrelated to gambling decisions, contrary to that published prediction.""}"
10.1177/23982128241255798,b035fe7f6b702c00,1,10.1523/JNEUROSCI.0349-18.2018,Single-trial phase entrainment of theta oscillations in sensory regions predicts human associative memory performance,"Wang, D.; Clouter, A.; Chen, Q.; et al.",2018,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Wang, D.; Clouter, A.; Chen, Q.; et al."", ""year"": ""2018"", ""title"": ""Single-trial phase entrainment of theta oscillations in sensory regions predicts human associative memory performance"", ""doi"": ""10.1523/JNEUROSCI.0349-18.2018"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Despite the theoretical and practical importance of TIME, there has not yet been a replication of the combined behavioural results of Clouter et al. (2017) and Wang et al. (2018) outside the original research group. Of particular importance for interpretation of memory enhancement effects is a replication in which all relevant control conditions (asynchronous theta, no-flicker, and synchronous non-theta) are combined in a single experiment. This is the aim of the present experiment."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Together with the lack of evidence for any advantage of synchronous theta against asynchronous theta or synchronous delta, we conclude that we have failed to replicate the TIME reported by Clouter et al. (2017) and Wang et al. (2018) ."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""An explicit overall non-replication verdict names both targets. Incidental synchrony-discrimination confirmation is outside the central memory effect.""}"
10.1177/1948550616646426,6b77954dd4b14dae,1,,Deflecting the trajectory and changing the narrative: How self-affirmation affects academic performance and motivation under identity threat.,"Sherman, D. K., Hartson, K. A., Binning, K. R., Purdie-Vaughns, V., Garcia, J., Taborsky-Barba, S., . . . Cohen, G. L.",2013,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Sherman, D. K., Hartson, K. A., Binning, K. R., Purdie-Vaughns, V., Garcia, J., Taborsky-Barba, S., . . . Cohen, G. L."", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Deflecting the trajectory and changing the narrative: How self-affirmation affects academic performance and motivation under identity threat."", ""year"": ""2013""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""6b77954dd4b14dae"", ""location"": ""Results and Discussion, lines 260-270 and 292-300; References, lines 491-500"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""We also explored whether affirmations worked best for the                             school (not by classroom). Testing the simple model of the\nlowest performing students, as has been found in prior                                   intervention, race, and their interaction on end of semester\nresearch (Sherman et al., 2013). To pursue this possibility,                             grades, nesting students within classrooms provided signifi-\nwe included the students’ previous term GPA as a covariate                               cantly better fit to the data, likelihood ratio test w2 (1) ¼\nand interaction term with experimental condition and ethnic                              20.26, p < .001. In addition, nesting students within classrooms\nstereotype status. This controls for prior differences in                                within schools provided significantly better fit to the data as\nachievement and allows an even more powerful test of the                                 well, likelihood ratio test w2(1) ¼ 3.85, p < .05. Therefore,\neffect. In this model, previous term GPA significantly pre-                              we proceeded with the multilevel modeling nesting students\ndicted later term GPA (b ¼ .952, 95% confidence interval                                 in both classrooms and schools.\n[CI] [0.879, 1.024]), no other main effect or interaction                                   The nested model for race, experimental condition, and their\napproached significance (all ps > .266)."", ""reason"": ""Prior adjudication and state files were checked and do not exist (never-adjudicated work). In addition to replicating Cohen et al. (2006) (shipped row 1059, sibling blind target 0), Protzko and Aronson (2016) empirically retest specific findings attributed to Sherman et al. (2013) in their new sample: in Results and Discussion (lines 260-270), they test whether self-affirmations worked best for the lowest-performing students as found in Sherman et al. (2013) by entering previous-term GPA and its interaction with experimental condition and ethnic stereotype status (all interaction and condition ps > .266), and in lines 292-300 they also retest combining Hispanic students into the negatively stereotyped group as focused on by Sherman et al. (2013). The full bibliographic citation for Sherman et al. (2013) is provided in the References (lines 491-500, Journal of Personality and Social Psychology, 104, 591-618; no DOI is printed in the source), and it is a distinct empirical study absent from the sole shipped row 1059 (Cohen et al., 2006, 10.1126/science.1128317)."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0052_6b77954dd4b14dae"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Sherman, D. K., Hartson, K. A., Binning, K. R., Purdie-Vaughns, V., Garcia, J., Taborsky-Barba, S., . . . Cohen, G. L."", ""year"": ""2013"", ""title"": ""Deflecting the trajectory and changing the narrative: How self-affirmation affects academic performance and motivation under identity threat."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We also explored whether affirmations worked best for the lowest performing students, as has been found in prior research (Sherman et al., 2013)."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We also explored whether affirmations worked best for the lowest performing students, as has been found in prior research (Sherman et al., 2013). To pursue this possibility, we included the students’ previous term GPA as a covariate and interaction term with experimental condition and ethnic stereotype status. This controls for prior differences in achievement and allows an even more powerful test of the effect. In this model, previous term GPA significantly predicted later term GPA (b ¼ .952, 95% confidence interval [CI] [0.879, 1.024]), no other main effect or interaction approached significance (all ps > .266)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The reported test does not support the corresponding original finding.""}"
10.51224/cik.v1i2.39,be770b85c49bd7e8,1,10.1016/j.humov.2016.08.009,Expecting to teach enhances motor learning and information processing during practice,"Daou, M., Lohse, K. R., & Miller, M. W.",2016,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Daou, M., Lohse, K. R., & Miller, M. W."", ""year"": ""2016"", ""title"": ""Expecting to teach enhances motor learning and information processing during practice"", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.humov.2016.08.009"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The primary purpose of this research was to replicate the findings of Daou and colleagues (Daou, Lohse, et al., 2016; Daou, Buchanan, et al., 2016)"", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The present study fails to replicate the benefits reported in the original experiments."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Explicit overall failure verdict applies to the two named original experiments. Motivation/practice checks and uncertain declarative knowledge estimates do not override this overall verdict.""}"
10.1177/1948550616639649,7009c476dabe7640,1,,"The retrospective gambler’s fallacy: Unlikely events, constructing the past, and multiple universes.","Oppenheimer, D. M., & Monin, B.",2009,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Oppenheimer, D. M., & Monin, B."", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""The retrospective gambler’s fallacy: Unlikely events, constructing the past, and multiple universes."", ""year"": ""2009""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""7009c476dabe7640"", ""location"": ""General Discussion (lines 1097-1103)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""To allay such concerns, we used a posi-\n\ntive control, the retrospective gambler’s fallacy (Oppenheimer\n\n& Monin, 2009). In all cases, we observed the expected pattern\n\nof results."", ""reason"": ""Across Experiments 1a, 1b, and 2, the authors administer the retrospective gambler's fallacy task (Oppenheimer & Monin, 2009) to new samples as a positive-control replication alongside their replication of Eskine (2013), report the sample sizes, means, SDs, exact difference-in-means, 95% CIs, and standardized effect sizes d in Table 2 and Figure 2, and conclude in the General Discussion that in all cases they observed the expected pattern of results. Only Eskine (2012/2013, row 1078) is in the shipped rows; Oppenheimer & Monin (2009) is a genuine omitted replication target."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0054_7009c476dabe7640"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Oppenheimer, D. M., & Monin, B."", ""year"": ""2009"", ""title"": ""The retrospective gambler’s fallacy: Unlikely events, constructing the past, and multiple universes."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1a, 1b, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""To help indicate the overall quality of our replication attempt, we included as a positive control an additional experiment with a well-defined effect size. Specifically, we used the retrospective gambler’s task (Oppenheimer & Monin, 2009)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""To allay such concerns, we used a positive control, the retrospective gambler’s fallacy (Oppenheimer & Monin, 2009). In all cases, we observed the expected pattern of results."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The positive-control experiment re-tests the specifically attributed published finding in new samples, and the authors state that its expected pattern occurred in all cases. Klein et al. supplied materials and prior evidence; the paper names Oppenheimer and Monin as the finding tested.""}"
10.1098/rsos.191114,43fa795352067a92,1,10.1177/1088868309352321,Two decades of terror management theory: a meta-analysis of mortality salience research,"Burke BL, Martens A, Faucher EH",2010,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Burke BL, Martens A, Faucher EH"", ""doi"": ""10.1177/1088868309352321"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""reproduction"", ""title"": ""Two decades of terror management theory: a meta-analysis of mortality salience research"", ""year"": ""2010""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""43fa795352067a92"", ""location"": ""Introduction, lines 12-16; Discussion, lines 38 and 40; Authors' contributions, line 51; References, lines 65-69"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Overall, the pattern of results reported in the previous section are inconsistent with the original results of Goldenberg et al. [9]. This does not necessarily mean that the original effect was a false positive, but it does suggest that (i) the effect may be substantially smaller than originally reported or (ii) that the effect is extremely sensitive to contextual factors and perhaps absent in some populations. Consistent with the former interpretation, our own reanalysis of Burke et al. ([3]; see also [11]) provides compelling evidence that the effect sizes of previous research on mortality salience may have been overestimated."", ""reason"": ""In addition to conducting a new-sample laboratory replication of Goldenberg et al. (2001) Study 2 (shipped input_row_index 1093, doi:10.1037/0096-3445.130.3.427), the authors perform and report their own original-data reanalysis of the 277 mortality-salience effect sizes from the meta-analysis of Burke, Martens, and Faucher (2010) (doi:10.1177/1088868309352321) in Figure 1 and lines 13-16, converting correlations to Fisher's z scores, testing funnel-plot asymmetry with Egger's regression (z = 2.06, p = 0.039), and fitting a Vevea and Hedges selection model (chi2(1) = 47.87, p < 0.001; bias-corrected r = 0.22 vs Burke et al.'s r = 0.35). Shipped row 1093 covers only the Goldenberg et al. (2001) experiment, so Burke et al. (2010) is a genuinely absent reproduction target."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0032_43fa795352067a92"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Burke BL, Martens A, Faucher EH"", ""year"": ""2010"", ""title"": ""Two decades of terror management theory: a meta-analysis of mortality salience research"", ""doi"": ""10.1177/1088868309352321"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Our own inspection of the data reported by Burke et al. [3] converges to the conclusion that the robustness of these effect sizes may have been overestimated."", ""outcome"": """", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": ""not checked"", ""outcome_computational_quote"": ""Instead of analysing raw correlation coefficients, which are constrained to adopt values from −1 to +1 and do not follow a normal distribution, we converted all effect sizes to Fisher's z scores."", ""outcome_robustness"": ""robustness challenges"", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": ""Consistent with the former interpretation, our own reanalysis of Burke et al. ([3]; see also [11]) provides compelling evidence that the effect sizes of previous research on mortality salience may have been overestimated."", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Same meta-analytic data reanalysed using bias-sensitive alternatives; bias-corrected effect 37% smaller and original absence-of-bias conclusion challenged. Original-number reproduction was outside these alternative analyses.""}"
10.3390/jintelligence7010006,cfc2ba332d77bd60,1,,Psychological Examining in the United States Army.,Yerkes R.M.,1921,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Yerkes R.M."", ""year"": ""1921"", ""title"": ""Psychological Examining in the United States Army."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""To further test the viability of the one-factor model, we performed confirmatory factor analysis on two correlation matrices of Army Beta data for World War I draftees ([15], pp. 390, 634)."", ""outcome"": """", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_computation"": ""not checked"", ""outcome_computational_quote"": ""To further test the viability of the one-factor model, we performed confirmatory factor analysis on two correlation matrices of Army Beta data for World War I draftees ([15], pp. 390, 634)."", ""outcome_robustness"": ""robust"", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": ""Thus, even in two samples of World War I draftees—groups that would be much more likely to have a separate factor for recognition of written numbers than a modern sample of college students—a one-factor model fit the data just as well as a two-factor model. Again, parsimony dictates that researchers should favor a one-factor model of intelligence from the historic Army Beta data."", ""outcome_reasoning"": ""A secondary reproduction reanalyses two original correlation matrices using alternative factor models to test the substantive validity finding. Recovering original reported coefficients was outside this analysis; the original general-ability interpretation survives the tested alternative. Both matrices belong to one original volume.""}"
10.2307/146341,b7595c5566c460f7,0,,,Holzer,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Holzer"", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Examining self-reported reservation wages from the 1979-80 waves of the National Longitudinal Survey of Youth (NLSY), Holzer concludes that 26 to 42 percent of the race difference in the length of jobless spells is due to the higher wages sought by young Black men. This replication uses NLSY reservation wage data from 1979 through 1986."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Thus, evidence from the NLSY does not support the claim that reservation wage differences explain the race employment gap."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true}"
10.1002/bem.10105,54ae4433391a94ab,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,"{""corrected_value"": null, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""54ae4433391a94ab"", ""location"": ""Abstract (line 5); new primary identities/access receipts in original_identity_0008.result.json"", ""method"": ""obvious"", ""quote"": ""Our study was a replication and extension with methodological improvements to a previous study on effects of the electromagnetic field (EMF) emitted by a 902 MHz mobile phone on human cognitive functioning. Improvements on the previous study included multicentre testing and a double blind design. A total of 64 subjects (32 men and 32 women) in two independent laboratories performed a battery of 9 cognitive tasks twice: while the EMF was on and while it was off. Reaction times (RTs) and accuracy were recorded. The order of exposure and tasks was counterbalanced across subjects and gender. There were no statistically significant differences in performance between genders or laboratories. Although the RTs and the accuracy of answers were very similar to those of our previous study, our previous results were not replicated."", ""reason"": ""New deposited current references explicitly include the shipped Koivisto2000response-time battery, another Koivisto2000n-back report and Krause2000EEG report. Original primary abstracts distinguish twelve reaction-time tasks/simpleRT-vigilance-arithmetic, n-back load0–3, and EEG/ERD endpoints. Current abstract only says nine cognitive tasks and ourpreviousstudy; its exact task composition and citation assignment remain unavailable. The related Haarala2004n-back report also has64subjects/twolaboratories, but equal N/sites cannot prove dataset/report equivalence or establish the2003nine-task endpoints. Current full body was not recovered: the archived allegedPDF is HTML without PDFmagic, and fresh full/epdf/browser routes yield only the abstract. Primary evidence therefore cannot uniquely map the actual retest or certify whether shipped1100is exhaustive. This is a documented target-scope/access limit after actual new attempts, not a claim that no original exists, an inferred new missing paper, or a blanket exclusion."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0039_54ae4433391a94ab"", ""target_index"": 0, ""verdict"": ""undeterminable""}","{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Our study was a replication and extension with methodological improvements to a previous study on effects of the electromagnetic field (EMF) emitted by a 902 MHz mobile phone on human cognitive functioning."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Although the RTs and the accuracy of answers were very similar to those of our previous study, our previous results were not replicated."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Explicit overall failure verdict. The abstract does not identify the previous study bibliographically; no guess is made among related prior works.""}"
10.1038/sj.ejhg.5201317,46d9e2d0b98ee6de,1,,Genome-wide linkage analysis and evidence of gene-by-gene interactions in a sample of 362 multiplex Parkinson disease families.,"Pankratz N, Nichols WC, Uniacke SK et al",2003,full_text,competing_supported_matches; same_author_year_siblings,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Pankratz N, Nichols WC, Uniacke SK et al"", ""year"": ""2003"", ""title"": ""Genome-wide linkage analysis and evidence of gene-by-gene interactions in a sample of 362 multiplex Parkinson disease families."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The studies by Pankratz et al showed a significant linkage of\n  In the 15 families, that included at least one affected               PD to 2q36 – 37 in a North American population. The\nindividual with an age of onset p50 years, a marker in                  sample of Pankratz et al17,19 was primarily Caucasian\nintron 7 of the parkin gene (D6S305) was genotyped in                   (94%), although Hispanics (5%) also participated. Interest-\norder to identify families more likely to have a mutation               ingly, the Hispanic families in the sample provided a\nin this known PD-susceptibility gene. Linkage analysis was              substantial portion of the linkage evidence.19 We could not\nrepeated excluding those families showing possible linkage              find a significant linkage to this region in our European\nto D6S305.                                                              families."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Recently, significant linkage has been\nreported to a 39.5 cM region on the long arm of chromosome 2 (2q36-37; PARK11) in North American\nParkinson families under an autosomal dominant model of inheritance. We have performed a replication\nstudy to confirm linkage to this region in a European population. Linkage analysis in 153 individuals from\n45 European families with a strong family history of PD did not show any significant LOD score in this\nregion. Therefore, PARK11 does not seem to play a major role for familial PD in the European population.\n"", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The European-family replication did not confirm significant PARK11 linkage reported in the Pankratz studies.""}"
10.1111/j.1742-9536.2011.00001.x,6fdb3d74d29e1b1b,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,"{""corrected_value"": null, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""6fdb3d74d29e1b1b"", ""location"": ""Abstract"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""We tested the claim that people who frequently play action video games (video game players (VGPs)) have a greater capacity of attention than those who rarely play video games (non‐video game players (NVGPs)). We first attempted to replicate previous findings by comparing VGPs and NVGPs on a flanker compatibility task (Experiment 1). In contrast with previous studies, VGPs did not demonstrate greater intrusion from distractors, calling into question the reliability of previous findings."", ""reason"": ""No prior adjudication or prior state file exists for this work. The supplied primary source text (6fdb3d74d29e1b1b.txt) contains only the title and abstract, without full text or a reference list. While the abstract explicitly states that Experiment 1 'attempted to replicate previous findings by comparing VGPs and NVGPs on a flanker compatibility task' and contrasts the results with 'previous studies', it includes no citations. Therefore, the available source text cannot resolve whether 'previous findings' / 'previous studies' refers exclusively to shipped row input_row_index 1105 (Green & Bavelier, 2003) or also includes another distinct original paper not present in shipped_rows."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0054_6fdb3d74d29e1b1b"", ""target_index"": 0, ""verdict"": ""undeterminable""}","{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1"", ""evidence_quote"": ""We first attempted to replicate previous findings by comparing VGPs and NVGPs on a flanker compatibility task (Experiment 1)."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In contrast with previous studies, VGPs did not demonstrate greater intrusion from distractors, calling into question the reliability of previous findings."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Abstract explicitly contrasts the intended finding with its absence. Original citation identity is unavailable in the saved abstract.""}"
10.1128/jvi.00693-11,c8ced3fa8a29c729,1,,"Detection of an infectious retrovirus, XMRV, in blood cells of patients with chronic fatigue syndrome.","Lombardi V. C., et al.",2009,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Lombardi V. C., et al."", ""year"": ""2009"", ""title"": ""Detection of an infectious retrovirus, XMRV, in blood cells of patients with chronic fatigue syndrome."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Furthermore, we also analyzed the WPI samples using tests from the two studies that found XMRV or XMRV-like viruses in CFS patients, viz. a PCR assay for gag sequences, both in single-round ( 12 ) and nested formats ( 11 ), and a test for viral growth in cultured cells ( 12 ). Neither of these tests revealed any evidence of XMRV."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Our findings do not support an association between CFS and MLV-related viruses, including XMRV, and the off-label use of antiretrovirals for the treatment of CFS does not seem justified at present."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Overall verdict rejects the association reported by both positive original studies; new samples and repeated assays find no specific viral association. Initial positives attributed to contamination are not a successful replication verdict.""}"
10.3389/fpsyg.2012.00504,ed3b243f07ac64de,1,10.1007/s10071-009-0225-0,Horses (Equus caballus) select the greater of two quantities in small numerical contrasts,"Uller C., Lewis J.",2009,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": ""10.1007/s10071-009-0225-0"", ""title"": ""Horses (Equus caballus) select the greater of two quantities in small numerical contrasts"", ""authors"": ""Uller C., Lewis J."", ""year"": ""2009"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""failed""}, ""quote"": ""Second, we wished to expand on the previous study by Uller and Lewis (\n2009\n) on quantity discriminations in horses; thereby we purposefully did not replicate that study in detail, but instead applied a different methodology closely resembling the monkey-study by Schmitt and Fischer. By doing so, we hoped not only to investigate if horses could also discriminate quantities beyond those tested by Uller and Lewis, but also to investigate the robustness of horses’ performances when a different methodological approach is used (Proops et al.,\n2009a\n)."", ""location"": ""introduction"", ""reason"": ""This is an omitted target: the paper explicitly conducts a conceptual replication of Uller and Lewis (2009) to test the robustness of horses' quantity discrimination across methodologies and concludes that its findings are in contrast to that study.""}","{""authors"": ""Uller C., Lewis J."", ""year"": ""2009"", ""title"": ""Horses (Equus caballus) select the greater of two quantities in small numerical contrasts"", ""doi"": ""10.1007/s10071-009-0225-0"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Second, we wished to expand on the previous study by Uller and Lewis (\n2009\n) on quantity discriminations in horses; thereby we purposefully did not replicate that study in detail, but instead applied a different methodology closely resembling the monkey-study by Schmitt and Fischer. By doing so, we hoped not only to investigate if horses could also discriminate quantities beyond those tested by Uller and Lewis, but also to investigate the robustness of horses’ performances when a different methodological approach is used (Proops et al.,\n2009a\n)."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In contrast to our expectations and to a previous report (Uller and Lewis,\n2009\n) we did not find evidence that horses will discriminate between two different quantities."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly contrast their lack of discrimination with this original.""}"
