10.3390/rs14030597,58537b5a1a67b22d,1,10.1016/j.rse.2011.01.021,Differences in Landsat-Based Trend Analyses in Drylands due to the Choice of Vegetation Estimate.,"Sonnenschein, R.; Kuemmerle, T.; Udelhoven, T.; Stellmes, M.; Hostert, P.",2011,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""both_wrong"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": ""10.1016/j.rse.2011.01.021"", ""title"": ""Differences in Landsat-Based Trend Analyses in Drylands due to the Choice of Vegetation Estimate."", ""authors"": ""Sonnenschein, R.; Kuemmerle, T.; Udelhoven, T.; Stellmes, M.; Hostert, P."", ""year"": ""2011"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined""}, ""quote"": ""Although not numerically comparable (due to several reasons discussed in Section 1.1), these numbers resemble the findings in Hostert et al. [42], who found that over 40% of the mountain area of central Crete (Psiloritis) showed a decreasing trend of vegetation cover. Spatial patterns are also very similar to the maps published by Sonnenschein et al. [49], both indicating that general reproducibility with the past papers is given from a thematic perspective with a focus on overgrazing and that similar conclusions would have been drawn as before."", ""location"": ""Results and Discussion, Comparison of Long-Term Vegetation Change"", ""reason"": ""The paper explicitly re-tests Sonnenschein et al. (2011) [49] alongside Hostert et al. (2003) [42] across an expanded island-wide Crete sample using 2,816 Landsat images, so shipped erred by omitting this target, while blind erred by coding its outcome as mixed instead of failed. Parent retains this explicit omitted target but withholds its outcome for the unresolved failed-versus-methodological-flaw scope.""}","{""authors"": ""Sonnenschein, R.; Kuemmerle, T.; Udelhoven, T.; Stellmes, M.; Hostert, P."", ""year"": ""2011"", ""title"": ""Differences in Landsat-Based Trend Analyses in Drylands due to the Choice of Vegetation Estimate."", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.rse.2011.01.021"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The VD 1.0 processing was performed in FORCE, too, although some more user interactions were necessary to reproduce a workflow comparable to [42,49]."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""However, the results from VD 2.0 are fundamentally different to those from VD 1.0 (Figure 5b). Severe cover loss is limited to small areas and the magnitude of cover change is smaller compared to VD 1.0. Overall, net cover change is positive with 18% of areas showing a decreasing trend of vegetation cover, while half (50%) of all semi-natural areas exhibit an increase (Table 4)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly say the restricted-data VD 1.0 reconstruction agrees with both references, but the full-data VD 2.0 test reverses the net trend. Close agreement alongside a changed-data replication that disagrees is mixed under the codebook.""}"
10.1371/journal.pone.0281140,1b32e1b335ae9787,1,,Parents’ beliefs in misinformation about vaccines are strengthened by pro-vaccine campaigns.,"Pluviano S, Watt C, Ragazzini G, Della Sala SD.",2019,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Pluviano S, Watt C, Ragazzini G, Della Sala SD."", ""doi"": """", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Parents’ beliefs in misinformation about vaccines are strengthened by pro-vaccine campaigns."", ""year"": ""2019""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""1b32e1b335ae9787"", ""location"": ""Study 1 Results (Parent analyses), lines 544-551; General Discussion, line 693; References (ref 28), lines 1020-1021"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Next, analysis focused on participants with children, to allow comparison with Pluviano et al. (2019), who found a familiarity backfire effect in a parent sample. To this end, full-factorial repeated measures ANCOVAs were conducted with the within-subjects factor time (T1, T2), the between-subjects factors condition (control, myths only, myths vs. facts, visual, fear appeal) and parent status (yes, no), and VAX-ID as a covariate. (Note, this analysis was deemed appropriate despite unequal sample sizes, as ANOVA is relatively robust to sample size differences as long as variances are not also unequal. Nevertheless, analyses were repeated with equal sample sizes using a random subsample of non-parents; results were comparable.) There were no significant main effects or interactions involving parent status across all three dependent variables, all\nF\n(1/4, 360) ≤ 2.15,\np\n≥ .074, η\np\n2\n≤ .023; this indicates that the effect of the experimental manipulations did not differ as a function of parent status."", ""reason"": ""The source paper (Ecker et al., 2023) is a replication and extension of Pluviano et al. (2017, shipped row 1198, 10.1371/journal.pone.0181640) that also explicitly tests whether the familiarity backfire effect reported specifically in a parent sample by Pluviano, Watt, Ragazzini, & Della Sala (2019, reference 28) occurs among participants with children. In Study 1 Results (lines 544-555), the authors run full-factorial repeated-measures ANCOVAs including parent status specifically 'to allow comparison with Pluviano et al. (2019), who found a familiarity backfire effect in a parent sample,' and conclude in the Discussion (line 693) that 'Our results did not replicate the familiarity backfire effects reported in Pluviano et al. (2017, 2019).' Because only Pluviano et al. (2017) is in shipped_rows (row 1198), Pluviano et al. (2019) is a distinct, genuinely absent replication-original pair."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0007_1b32e1b335ae9787"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Pluviano S, Watt C, Ragazzini G, Della Sala SD."", ""year"": ""2019"", ""title"": ""Parents’ beliefs in misinformation about vaccines are strengthened by pro-vaccine campaigns."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Next, analysis focused on participants with children, to allow comparison with Pluviano et al. (2019), who found a familiarity backfire effect in a parent sample."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We found that no intervention was associated with greater misinformation belief or vaccine hesitancy than control at any timepoint. This was true across all analyses and subgroups, including in parents (at odds with [\n34\n]) and in those participants higher in anti-vaccination sentiment (broadly in line with [\n54\n])."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly contrast absence of backfire with the target finding.""}"
10.1186/s12888-023-04903-9,b3185f4a2cdbe718,1,10.1007/s10803-017-3422-8,The Glasgow Sensory Questionnaire: validation of a French language version and refinement of sensory profiles of people with high Autism-Spectrum Quotient,"Sapey-Triomphe L-A, Moulin A, Sonié S, Schmitz C",2018,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": ""10.1007/s10803-017-3422-8"", ""quote"": ""Regarding the second aim, it was expected that the German student sample would show similar sensory processing differences as the French sample depending on whether participants scored higher or lower on the AQ [ 51 ]. More precisely, a stronger correlation between hyper- and hyposensitivity, higher hypersensitivity scores in relation to hyposensitivity scores, and a more homogeneous pattern of sensory processing differences in all seven sensory modalities for individuals with higher compared to individuals with lower AQ scores were expected."", ""location"": ""introduction"", ""reason"": ""This study explicitly re-tests the sensory profile findings reported by Sapey-Triomphe et al. (2018) [51] in addition to Robertson and Simmons (2013) [48], making Sapey-Triomphe et al. (2018) an omitted replication target.""}","{""authors"": ""Sapey-Triomphe L-A, Moulin A, Sonié S, Schmitz C"", ""year"": ""2018"", ""title"": ""The Glasgow Sensory Questionnaire: validation of a French language version and refinement of sensory profiles of people with high Autism-Spectrum Quotient"", ""doi"": ""10.1007/s10803-017-3422-8"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The two main goals of this study were: (a) the validation of the German version of the GSQ according to the Universalist model of cross-cultural adaptation [ 29 ] and (b) the replication of sensory processing differences of individuals with higher or lower AQ scores as reported in the original English version by Robertson and Simmons [ 48 ] and in the French version of Sapey-Triomphe et al. [ 51 ]."", ""outcome"": ""uninformative"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Contrary to expectations, only 35 out of 297 students had AQ scores higher than 26 in our sample. Therefore, the present sample seems not suitable for drawing meaningful conclusions about the sensory processing differences of individuals with higher AQ scores."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors also call the replication unsuccessful, but explicitly judge their sample unsuitable for meaningful conclusions because too few participants had high AQ. The codebook special verdict for a non-adjudicating attempt takes precedence. Both named targets are included in the replication objective.""}"
10.1007/s11002-024-09739-1,aac86ddf4e1dacb0,1,10.1093/jcr/ucac017,Bundle Selection and Variety Seeking: The Importance of Combinatorics.,"O’Donnell, M., Critcher, C. R., & Nelson, L. D.",2023,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""O’Donnell, M., Critcher, C. R., & Nelson, L. D."", ""year"": ""2023"", ""title"": ""Bundle Selection and Variety Seeking: The Importance of Combinatorics."", ""doi"": ""10.1093/jcr/ucac017"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""Supplemental Study E"", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1a, 1b, 2a, 2b"", ""evidence_quote"": ""To clarify the direction of effect, we conducted four studies in two product categories and found evidence of a stronger preference for variety in bundled offering conditions (compared to single offering conditions), replicating O’Donnell et al.’s (2023) findings."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""To clarify the direction of effect, we conducted four studies in two product categories and found evidence of a stronger preference for variety in bundled offering conditions (compared to single offering conditions), replicating O’Donnell et al.’s (2023) findings."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The paper explicitly compares its own results with this target and states the direction of agreement or contradiction.""}"
