10.1038/s41598-025-12592-5,7d504644e1b73d22,1,10.1002/eat.22374,DNA methylation in individuals with anorexia nervosa and in matched normal-eater controls: A genome-wide study.,"Booij, L. et al.",2015,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Booij, L. et al."", ""doi"": ""10.1002/eat.22374"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""DNA methylation in individuals with anorexia nervosa and in matched normal-eater controls: A genome-wide study."", ""year"": ""2015""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""7d504644e1b73d22"", ""location"": ""Discussion, lines 494–498 (see also Results, lines 292–307, and Discussion, lines 387–403)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""In sum, we could not replicate findings of our previous EWAS approach\n21\nand studies by others\n20\n."", ""reason"": ""No prior adjudication or state files exist for this work. In an independent sample of 189 female patients with anorexia nervosa and 67 healthy-lean controls, Rajcsanyi et al. (2025) empirically test DNA methylation across 15 CpG sites at the NR1H3 locus using two-sided robust linear regression (Results, lines 292–307; Methods, line 542) and explicitly evaluate both the hypomethylation finding of their own prior EWAS (Kesselmeier et al. 2018/2016, reference 21, target_index 0) and the opposite hypermethylation finding reported by Booij et al. (2015, reference 20, target_index 1), concluding in the Discussion that 'we were unable to replicate methylation differences at NR1H3 , regardless of the methylation direction previously reported 20 , 21' (lines 397–403) and 'could not replicate findings of our previous EWAS approach 21 and studies by others 20' (lines 494–498). Shipped row 1217 (Kesselmeier et al. 2016/2018, DOI 10.1080/15622975.2016.1190033) is a separate study by a different research group reporting opposite methylation direction at NR1H3, so Booij et al. (2015) (DOI 10.1002/eat.22374) is a genuinely absent original target."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0061_7d504644e1b73d22"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Booij, L. et al."", ""year"": ""2015"", ""title"": ""DNA methylation in individuals with anorexia nervosa and in matched normal-eater controls: A genome-wide study."", ""doi"": ""10.1002/eat.22374"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In sum, we could not replicate findings of our previous EWAS approach\n21\nand studies by others\n20\n."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In sum, we could not replicate findings of our previous EWAS approach\n21\nand studies by others\n20\n."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true}"
10.1080/23273798.2026.2618581,d2f57b9fdad23ed5,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Here, we aim to replicate this lenient listener effect for second-language speakers, and to determine whether this effect extends to speakers with a language disorder."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The results replicate the lenient listener effect for second-language speakers and extend it to language disordered speakers."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Abstract explicitly states replication success; original citation cannot be identified from abstract alone.""}"
10.1007/s00426-026-02299-1,9b8d376ac7e0109c,1,,Attention shifts and the simon effect: A failure to replicate Stoffer (1991).,"Weeks, D. J., Chua, R., & Hamblin, K.",1996,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""The present experiment aimed to directly replicate Stoffer’s ( 1991 ) study, which extended the earlier work of Umilta & Liotti ( 1987 ). Stoffer ( 1991 ) investigated both global and local Simon effects under simultaneous and sequential target presentation, using two distinct reference objects as external precues: a large box and two small boxes. Consistent with Umilta & Liotti’s findings ( 1987 ), Stoffer ( 1991 ) observed only a local Simon effect for sequential target presentation when two small boxes were employed as reference objects, whereas no (local or global) Simon occurred for the large box as reference object. Weeks et al. ( 1996 ) previously failed to replicate these findings."", ""location"": ""Discussion"", ""reason"": ""Weeks et al. (1996) is an earlier replication attempt of Stoffer (1991) cited as background context and compared in the discussion; the present study's design, sample-size calculation, and procedural parameters directly re-test Stoffer (1991) rather than Weeks et al. (1996).""}","{""authors"": ""Weeks, D. J., Chua, R., & Hamblin, K."", ""year"": ""1996"", ""title"": ""Attention shifts and the simon effect: A failure to replicate Stoffer (1991)."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Instead, Weeks et al. ( 1996 ) showed that target presentation (simultaneous or sequential) alone influenced the local Simon effect in a similar vein in both precue types (as shown by the three-way interaction between target presentation mode, relative stimulus position and response). Our current study replicated this finding of the dependency of the local Simon effect on target presentation."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In sum, Stoffer ( 1991 ) observed a reliable modulation of the local Simon effect under the sequential target presentation only for the two small boxes as precue (i.e., a four-way interaction between precue type, target presentation mode, relative stimulus position, and response), describing both, target presentation and precue type as mutually influencing factors for the local Simon effect. In contrast, the replications by Weeks et al. ( 1996 ) and our current study found no evidence for the reported mutual dependency. Instead, Weeks et al. ( 1996 ) showed that target presentation (simultaneous or sequential) alone influenced the local Simon effect in a similar vein in both precue types (as shown by the three-way interaction between target presentation mode, relative stimulus position and response). Our current study replicated this finding of the dependency of the local Simon effect on target presentation."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors explicitly replicate Weeks’s local effect dependency but contrast their concurrent global and local effects with Weeks’s absence of that concurrence."", ""outcome_additional_quote"": ""Substantial global and local Simon effects — reflecting the activation of two distinct spatial reference frames — occurred concurrently across both simultaneous and sequential target presentation. This contrasts with earlier reports by Stoffer ( 1991 ) and Weeks et al. ( 1996 ), where concurrent global and local effects were absent.""}"
10.1111/lang.12451,2193a7ee7fb9cefc,1,10.1017/S0272263197001010,"Accent, intelligibility, and comprehensibility: Evidence from four L1s.","Derwing, T. M., & Munro, M. J.",1997,abstract,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Derwing, T. M., & Munro, M. J."", ""doi"": ""10.1017/S0272263197001010"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Accent, intelligibility, and comprehensibility: Evidence from four L1s."", ""year"": ""1997""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""2193a7ee7fb9cefc"", ""location"": ""Abstract (line 3) and Reference entries (line 7)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Abstract This study investigated the relationship among intelligibility, comprehensibility, and accentedness in the speech of second language learners of Spanish of varying proficiency in instructed contexts. It conceptually replicated studies by Munro and Derwing (1995a) and Derwing and Munro (1997), who found partial independence among the three speech dimensions but also evidence that proficiency may mediate the relationship between linguistic features of stimuli (e.g., phonemic and grammatical error rates) and speech dimensions."", ""reason"": ""No prior adjudication or state file exists for this work. The abstract explicitly states that the study conceptually replicated both Munro and Derwing (1995a) and Derwing and Munro (1997), specifically retesting their findings of partial independence among intelligibility, comprehensibility, and accentedness and proficiency mediation of the relationship between linguistic error rates and speech dimensions using new speech data from 42 L2 Spanish learners rated by 80 native Spanish listeners. The supplied publisher reference list provides the full bibliographic identity for Derwing and Munro (1997) in Studies in Second Language Acquisition (19, 1-16, DOI 10.1017/S0272263197001010), confirmed in Crossref metadata. Shipped row 1267 (Munro & Derwing, 1995, Language Learning, 45, 73-97, DOI 10.1111/j.1467-1770.1995.tb00963.x) matches sibling target_index 0; Derwing and Munro (1997) is a separate subsequent empirical study across four L1s rather than an alternate report of the 1995 paper, and is genuinely absent from the shipped rows."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0010_2193a7ee7fb9cefc"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Derwing, T. M., & Munro, M. J."", ""year"": ""1997"", ""title"": ""Accent, intelligibility, and comprehensibility: Evidence from four L1s."", ""doi"": ""10.1017/S0272263197001010"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""It conceptually replicated studies by Munro and Derwing (1995a) and Derwing and Munro (1997), who found partial independence among the three speech dimensions but also evidence that proficiency may mediate the relationship between linguistic features of stimuli (e.g., phonemic and grammatical error rates) and speech dimensions."", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": """", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract identifies the replicated studies but reports no comparison of the present findings to their results.""}"
