10.1037/a0031751,5deb52da79c2ece6,1,10.1007/s11211-006-0012-x,The development of system justification in the developing world.,"Henry, P. J., & Saul, A.",2006,full_text,,error,obvious,"{""field"": ""missing_row"", ""target_index"": 1, ""method"": ""obvious"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Henry, P. J., & Saul, A."", ""year"": ""2006"", ""title"": ""The development of system justification in the developing world."", ""doi"": ""10.1007/s11211-006-0012-x"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined""}, ""quote"": ""most basic test of the hypothesis, and one that closely replicates the\n                                                                                                                    research by Jost et al. (2003) and Henry and Saul (2006), tests\n                                      Legi\u0001macy\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                                                    the main effect of social status on legitimacy. "", ""location"": ""Independent parent primary-source context; exhausted_candidate_0029.parent.json raw offsets"", ""reason"": ""Parent independently read PresentStudy and the actual new international WorldValuesSurvey analyses and references. The paper expressly says its main social-status/legitimacy test closely replicates Henry/Saul2006as well as Jost2003, then separately motivates the international branch by Henry/Saul and analyses N96662in65countries, beyond the US-focused shipped Jost paper. The specific reported effect is actually tested in new data, not merely mentioned as theory or a measurement source. The current two-column extraction includes a figure-label intrusion; the full contiguous raw passage is retained unchanged, never joined or repaired. Printed reference establishes the exact Henry/Saul2006paper and DOI. No outcome inferred.""}","{""authors"": ""Henry, P. J., & Saul, A."", ""year"": ""2006"", ""title"": ""The development of system justification in the developing world."", ""doi"": ""10.1007/s11211-006-0012-x"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The most basic test of the hypothesis, and one that closely replicates the research by Jost et al. (2003) and Henry and Saul (2006), tests the main effect of social status on legitimacy."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In sum, the status–legitimacy effect is not robust and is unlikely to be the result of dissonance."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Explicit close replication of both cited papers; overall verdict rejects the status–legitimacy effect. A single agreeing estimate does not override the overall verdict.""}"
10.1016/j.xjidi.2026.100490,a586b619ee0fe9a6,0,10.1038/ncomms3870,Two new susceptibility loci 1q24.2 and 11p11.2 confer risk to severe acne,"He L., Wu W.J., Yang J.K., Cheng H., Zuo X.B., Lai W., et al.",2014,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""He L., Wu W.J., Yang J.K., Cheng H., Zuo X.B., Lai W., et al."", ""year"": ""2014"", ""title"": ""Two new susceptibility loci 1q24.2 and 11p11.2 confer risk to severe acne"", ""doi"": ""10.1038/ncomms3870"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""A systematic literature search identified 7 independent GWAS on acne ( Figure 1 ). From these studies, 88 previously reported acne-associated variants were extracted for replication analysis ( Supplementary Table S1 ). All variants had shown suggestive evidence of association in previous reports, defined by a significance threshold of P < 1 × 10 −5 . Among them, 5 variants (rs80293268, rs1256580, rs2901000, rs629725, and rs34560261) had been reported in more than 1 previous acne GWAS.\nHe L, Wu W-J, Yang J-K, Cheng H, Zuo X-B, Lai W, et al. Two new susceptibility loci 1q24.2 and 11p11.2 confer risk to severe acne. Nat Commun 2014; 5(1): 2870."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Of the 88 previously reported acne-associated variants, 71 were represented in the SMCGES dataset by either the reported variant itself or a proxy variant in strong linkage disequilibrium (r 2 ≥ 0.8). After accounting for duplicate proxy mapping, these corresponded to 68 unique variants that were tested in the replication analysis ( Supplementary Table S2 ). Additive logistic regression identified 2 variants that exceeded the conservative study-wide significance threshold on the basis of the 88 literature-derived acne-associated variants after Bonferroni correction ( P < 5.68 × 10 −4 ) ( Table 2 ). These were rs1159268 at 1q41 and rs738409 at 22q13.31. Both variants showed effects in the same direction as previously reported. No additional variants met the corrected significance threshold."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Supplementary Table S2 maps tested variants to this reference; none meets the stated corrected replication threshold.""}"
