10.1287/orsc.2016.1065,a065b4f4a5188b65,1,,"Entrepreneurial spawning: Public corporations and the genesis of new ventures, 1986 to 1999.","Gompers P, Lerner J, Scharfstein DS",2005,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Gompers P, Lerner J, Scharfstein DS"", ""doi"": """", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Entrepreneurial spawning: Public corporations and the genesis of new ventures, 1986 to 1999."", ""year"": ""2005""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""a065b4f4a5188b65"", ""location"": ""Results (page 8, lines 460-465); References (page 17, lines 1092-1096)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""We begin by replicating prior results regarding firm size      results), or (3) an entirely hands-off approach in which\nand the likelihood of becoming an entrepreneur (e.g.,          CEM automatically determines how the matching should\nElfenbein et al. 2010, Gompers et al. 2005, Kacperczyk         occur. Finally, the results are stronger when we exclude\n2012, Sørensen 2007). Figure 1 shows that, consistent          non-ASR spin-offs from the dependent variable (there\nwith prior findings, workers at smaller firms are more         are 202 ASR spin-offs).\nlikely to become entrepreneurs."", ""reason"": ""No prior adjudication or state file exists for this work. In the Results section, Kacperczyk & Marx (2016) use a newly collected dataset of 20,670 job transitions (18,994 matched observations) in the automatic speech recognition (ASR) industry to empirically retest the negative relationship between prior employer size and entrepreneurial spawning ('the small-firm effect'), explicitly stating that they 'begin by replicating prior results regarding firm size and the likelihood of becoming an entrepreneur (e.g., Elfenbein et al. 2010, Gompers et al. 2005, Kacperczyk 2012, Sørensen 2007)' in Figure 1 and Table 2 (columns 1 and 2). The reference list (lines 1092-1096) identifies Gompers P, Lerner J, Scharfstein DS (2005), 'Entrepreneurial spawning: Public corporations and the genesis of new ventures, 1986 to 1999,' Journal of Finance 60(2):577-614 (no DOI printed in source). The only shipped row (input_row_index 1314) is Elfenbein, Hamilton, & Zenger (2010, Management Science 56(4):659-681, DOI 10.1287/mnsc.1090.1130), and sibling targets 2 (Kacperczyk 2012) and 3 (Sørensen 2007) are separate studies on different industries/populations; report equivalence does not apply."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0080_a065b4f4a5188b65"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Gompers P, Lerner J, Scharfstein DS"", ""year"": ""2005"", ""title"": ""Entrepreneurial spawning: Public corporations and the genesis of new ventures, 1986 to 1999."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We begin by replicating prior results regarding firm size and the likelihood of becoming an entrepreneur (e.g., Elfenbein et al. 2010, Gompers et al. 2005, Kacperczyk 2012, Sørensen 2007)."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Overall, we are able to replicate the commonly found “small-firm effect,” but when we restrict our analyses to observe workers in dissolved firms alone, we no longer find the negative effect."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors confirm the cited small-firm result in the ordinary sample and explicitly report its disappearance and reversal among defunct firms. Their aggregate statement distinguishes these outcomes.""}"
