10.1016/j.jvb.2013.06.007,e92a7cc95ddcbf0a,0,,Rule breaking in adolescence and entrepreneurial status: An empirical investigation.,"Zhang, Z. & Arvey, R.D.",2009,abstract,same_author_year_siblings,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Zhang, Z. & Arvey, R.D."", ""year"": ""2009"", ""title"": ""Rule breaking in adolescence and entrepreneurial status: An empirical investigation."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Drawing from Zhang and Arvey's retrospective study [Zhang, Z. & Arvey, R.D. (2009). Rule breaking in adolescence and entrepreneurial status: An empirical investigation. Journal of Business Venturing, 24(5), 436–447], which found a link between entrepreneurship status of male adults and their recalled early antisocial rule-breaking behavior in adolescence, the present study utilized prospective longitudinal data from a Swedish cohort study to clarify the connection between antisocial rule-breaking, crime, and entrepreneurship by applying a developmental perspective."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Drawing from Zhang and Arvey's retrospective study [Zhang, Z. & Arvey, R.D. (2009). Rule breaking in adolescence and entrepreneurial status: An empirical investigation. Journal of Business Venturing, 24(5), 436–447], which found a link between entrepreneurship status of male adults and their recalled early antisocial rule-breaking behavior in adolescence, the present study utilized prospective longitudinal data from a Swedish cohort study to clarify the connection between antisocial rule-breaking, crime, and entrepreneurship by applying a developmental perspective. Regression results, which controlled for early socioeconomic background and intellectual competencies, indeed identified early antisocial rule-breaking behavior in adolescence as a valid positive predictor of a subsequent entrepreneurial career in adulthood in men (but not in women)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Original male-adult finding and corresponding new longitudinal male-sample result are explicitly stated. Women and registered crime are extensions, not failures of the original male-sample finding.""}"
10.23668/psycharchives.22555,fff5229f57531090,1,10.1016/j.leaqua.2020.101384,"Careless responding in questionnaire measures: Detection, impact, and remedies.","Goldammer, P., Annen, H., Stöckli, P. L., & Klaus, J.",2020,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""Research Question 2 (RQ2) concerned how accurately established careless-responding indicators distinguish truthful from careless responding. The preregistered indicators were attention-check accuracy, fake-adjective accuracy, longstring, and page time. H2a: We predicted that all indicators would distinguish T1 and T2 from OC (H2a). H2b: For the covertly careless condition, we predicted substantially weaker discrimination than for overtly careless responding, with the possibility that some indicators might fail to distinguish truthful from covertly careless responding altogether."", ""location"": ""The Current Study, p. 8"", ""reason"": ""Inspecting the substantive relationship rather than section labels confirms that the paper does not re-test a specific empirical finding from Goldammer et al. (2020). In the Introduction (pp. 3–6), Goldammer et al. (2020) is cited alongside Curran (2016), Huang et al. (2012), and Meade and Craig (2012) as background on careless-responding indicators and the criterion problem, noting that repetitive-responding indicators like longstring (Johnson, 2005) do not necessarily distinguish careful from careless responders equally well. Rather than re-testing Goldammer et al.'s finding of weak longstring discrimination under induced carelessness, RQ2 tests a new adherence-monitored crossover design (truthful vs. overtly and covertly careless instructions) across attention checks, fake-adjective nonwords, longstring, page time, retest correspondence, and raw response variability, and H2a explicitly predicts the opposite of Goldammer et al.'s longstring result—namely, that all preregistered indicators (including longstring) would distinguish truthful from overtly careless responding. Only after H2a failed for longstring in the Results (p. 26) do the authors note post hoc in the Discussion (pp. 31–32) that their results were broadly consistent with Goldammer et al. (2020), while also contrasting their retest-correspondence measure with Goldammer et al.'s conventional personal-reliability indices and noting that their short item sequence provided only a limited test of longstring.""}","{""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""authors"": ""Goldammer, P., Annen, H., Stöckli, P. L., & Klaus, J."", ""year"": ""2020"", ""title"": ""Careless responding in questionnaire measures: Detection, impact, and remedies."", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.leaqua.2020.101384"", ""evidence_quote"": ""However, indicators targeting restricted or repetitive responding, such as longstring (Johnson, 2005), do not necessarily distinguish careful responders from careless responders equally well (Goldammer et al., 2020). A central challenge in evaluating such indicators is establishing a valid criterion for whether participants actually responded carelessly. In the present study, we addressed this problem by experimentally instructing participants to respond either truthfully or carelessly and separately monitoring their adherence to these instructions in real time."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Longstring showed comparatively weak discrimination in the present study, particularly for overtly careless responding. This finding is consistent with Goldammer et al. (2020), who likewise reported weak performance of longstring under experimentally induced carelessness."", ""outcome_reasoning"": ""New experimentally induced careless responses test the published indicator discrimination finding, explicitly agreeing on weak longstring performance. General preregistered predictions about all indicators are not imported as contradictions of this specific original finding.""}"
10.1038/s41598-023-48874-z,84057613eb74c2bd,1,,Motivated encoding selectively promotes memory for future inconsequential semantically-related events.,"Oyarzún, J. P., Packard, P. A., de Diego-Balaguer, R. & Fuentemilla, L.",2016,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Oyarzún, J. P., Packard, P. A., de Diego-Balaguer, R. & Fuentemilla, L."", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Motivated encoding selectively promotes memory for future inconsequential semantically-related events."", ""year"": ""2016""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""84057613eb74c2bd"", ""location"": ""Experiment 2 Introduction"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Experiment 2a replicates the design of Experiment 1 from the RREM study\n6\nto test retrospective memory enhancement 24-hours after encoding. In Experiment 2b, we incorporated a post-conditioning phase to examine prospective memory enhancement effects, building on prior reports of this effect induced by reward-conditioning\n13\nand fear-conditioning\n7\n."", ""reason"": ""The authors empirically retest reward-induced category-specific prospective memory enhancement in a post-conditioning phase in Experiments 1 and 2b using the 3x2 repeated-measures ANOVA design of Oyarzún et al. (2016, ref. 13), the only prior reward-conditioning study reporting selective prospective memory enhancement, and report finding no evidence for prospective memory enhancement. Reference 13 identifies Oyarzún et al. (2016) in Neurobiology of Learning and Memory (no DOI is printed in the source text). The only shipped row (1335) is Patil et al. (2016/2017, doi: 10.1101/lm.042978.116), an independent study by a different research group focusing on retroactive memory consolidation (target 0), so report equivalence does not apply. Luna's quote failed validation only due to stripped newlines; no prior adjudication file existed."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0068_84057613eb74c2bd"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Oyarzún, J. P., Packard, P. A., de Diego-Balaguer, R. & Fuentemilla, L."", ""year"": ""2016"", ""title"": ""Motivated encoding selectively promotes memory for future inconsequential semantically-related events."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2b"", ""evidence_quote"": ""In Experiment 2b, we incorporated a post-conditioning phase to examine prospective memory enhancement effects, building on prior reports of this effect induced by reward-conditioning\n13\nand fear-conditioning\n7\n."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Experiment 2b provided no evidence for prospective memory effects."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The prospective reward-enhancement finding is retested in the post-conditioning phases, with explicit absence of the effect in Experiments 1 and 2b. The fear-conditioning reference supplies related motivation rather than a retest of fear conditioning.""}"
10.1126/science.aac4716,9fed70371eeb17ff,58,,Social identity contingencies: how diversity cues signal threat or safety for African Americans in mainstream institutions,"Purdie-Vaughns, V., Steele, C. M., Davies, P. G., Ditlmann, R., & Crosby, J. R.",2008,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Purdie-Vaughns, V., Steele, C. M., Davies, P. G., Ditlmann, R., & Crosby, J. R."", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Social identity contingencies: how diversity cues signal threat or safety for African Americans in mainstream institutions"", ""year"": ""2008""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""9fed70371eeb17ff"", ""location"": ""Primary Project Report 97 (https://osf.io/download/3rxvs/), Introduction & Method, source_view.json lines 1048-1049; Confirmatory Analyses & References, lines 1055-1057; Master CSV (https://osf.io/download/fgjvw/) Study Num 97, lines 1162-1163; Appendix Table, p. 38, line 68"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Replication of “Social identity contingencies: How diversity cues signal threat or safety for African Americans in mainstream institutions” by V Purdie-Vaughns, CM Steele, PG Davies, R Ditlmann, JR Crosby (2008, Journal of Personality and Social Psychology). Kathleen Schmidt kes7z@virginia.edu Introduction In their original paper “Social identity contingencies: How diversity cues signal threat or safety for African Americans in mainstream institutions,” Purdie-Vaughns, Steele, Davies, Ditlmann, and Crosby (2008) suggest that people belonging to a group that is vulnerable to prejudice are especially sensitive to social identity contingencies—the set of potential “judgments, stereotypes, opportunities, restrictions, and treatments” related to social identity in an environment (Purdie-Vaughns et al. 2008). The researchers suggest that a given setting can produce expectations about social contingencies that affect personal comfort in that setting. In this replication of Purdie-Vaughns et al. (2008), I sought to reproduce the methodology used in the original paper as exactly as possible."", ""reason"": ""1. Empirical retesting: In Primary Project Report 97 (https://osf.io/download/3rxvs/, lines 1048-1057) and Master CSV Study Num 97 (lines 1162-1163), replicator Kathleen Schmidt collected new empirical data on Amazon Mechanical Turk (final analyzed N = 1,490 African American and White adults) to replicate Experiment 3 of Purdie-Vaughns et al. (2008), testing the 2 (participant race: African American or White) x 2 (fairness cue: high or low) between-subjects ANOVA interaction on institutional trust in a setting with low minority representation and a colorblind philosophy. Whereas the original study reported a significant interaction, F(1, 73) = 12.19, p = .001, partial eta^2 = .1431 (N = 90), the replication found a non-significant interaction in the opposite direction, F(1, 1486) = 2.03, p = .154, partial eta^2 = .001 (Note: the blind coder listed study_numbers as '2' from the Master CSV 'Replicated study number (R)' column, whereas the primary report narrative on line 1048 explicitly specifies 'this study is a replication of Experiment 3'). 2. Original work identity: The source explicitly cites 'Purdie-Vaughns, V., Steele, C. M., Davies, P. G., Ditlmann, R., & Crosby, J. R. (2008). Social identity contingencies: how diversity cues signal threat or safety for African Americans in mainstream institutions. Journal of personality and social psychology, 94(4), 615' (line 1057; Master CSV pages 615-30, line 1162). No original article DOI is printed in the source text (the OSF DOI 10.17605/OSF.IO/2R7NK on p. 38 is for the replication project), so doi is omitted. 3. Absence from shipped rows: All 93 supplied shipped rows for 10.1126/science.aac4716 were inspected; none represents Purdie-Vaughns et al. (2008, JPSP, 94(4), 615-630). Shipped rows sharing co-authors—row 1625 (Goff, Steele, & Davies, 2008, JPSP, 94(1), 91, 'The space between us: Stereotype threat and distance in interracial contexts', replicated in Report 81 / Study Num 87) and row 1907 (Crosby, Monin, & Richardson, 2008, Psychological Science, 19(3), 226, 'Where Do We Look During Potentially Offensive Behavior?', replicated in Report 48 / Study Num 132)—are distinct original articles with different designs and replication reports. 4. Non-duplication: Target 58 is unique among the 98 blind targets in codings/9fed70371eeb17ff.json. 5. Prior context: Prior Argon runs in prior_state_path timed out before emitting decisions; no prior adjudication exists for target 58."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0079_9fed70371eeb17ff"", ""target_index"": 58, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Purdie-Vaughns, V., Steele, C. M., Davies, P. G., Ditlmann, R., & Crosby, J. R."", ""year"": ""2008"", ""title"": ""Social identity contingencies: how diversity cues signal threat or safety for African Americans in mainstream institutions"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""2"", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Replication of “Social identity contingencies: How diversity cues signal threat or safety for African Americans in mainstream institutions” by V Purdie-Vaughns, CM Steele, PG Davies, R Ditlmann, JR Crosby (2008, Journal of Personality and Social Psychology). Kathleen Schmidt kes7z@virginia.edu"", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Confirmatory analyses did not replicate the original finding that fairness cues create more trust for Black but not White participants in an environment with low-minority representation. Exploratory analyses showed a pattern of results in the opposite direction: White participants responded to high fairness cues by feeling more trust to a greater degree than Black participants. The interaction between race and fairness cue did not impact identity contingent evaluations. Data Preparation Data were transposed into short form and cleaned by recoding variables into the appropriate formats."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Individual primary replication report: authors’ verdict and comparisons to this original, prioritizing an overall verdict when stated.""}"
10.1126/science.aac4716,9fed70371eeb17ff,63,,Anchor precision influences the amount of adjustment,"Janiszewski, C., D. Uy.",2008,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Janiszewski, C., D. Uy."", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Anchor precision influences the amount of adjustment"", ""year"": ""2008""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""9fed70371eeb17ff"", ""location"": ""Primary Project Report 10 (https://osf.io/download/ehjdm/), Results, Discussion & Reference, source_view.json line 222; Master CSV (https://osf.io/download/fgjvw/) Study Num 111, lines 1172-1173; Appendix Table, p. 37, line 66"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Confirmatory analysis A 2 X 2 ANOVA revealed a main effect of motivation, F(1,116) = 71.06, p < .001, = .38, indicating that people told to adjust by a large amount adjusted more (M = .27, SD = .39) than people told to adjust by a small amount (M = -.27, SD = .32). There was also a main effect of anchor precision, F(1,116) = 6.28, p = .014, = .05, indicating that people given the round anchor adjusted more (M = .08, SD = .48) than people given the precise anchor (M = -.08, SD = .40). There was no interaction between motivation and precision, F < 1. Discussion Summary of Replication Attempt These findings represent a partial replication of Janiszewski and Uy (2008). The central claim of their paper – that anchor precision influences the degree of adjustment was supported. Additionally, these data replicated the main effect of motivation observed in their study. There was no evidence of the reported interaction."", ""reason"": ""1. Empirical retesting: In Primary Project Report 10 (https://osf.io/download/ehjdm/, lines 219-222) and Master CSV Study Num 111 (lines 1172-1173), replicator Jesse Chandler collected new data from 126 undergraduate participants (N = 120 analyzed after excluding 6 who discussed answers) to replicate Study 4a of Janiszewski and Uy (2008) (report heading says 'Replication of Study 4b', while the body text on line 219 states 'Study 4a was selected for replication (rather than 4b)'). Using a 2 (anchor precision: round vs. precise) x 2 (motivation to adjust: small vs. large) ANOVA on standardized price-estimate adjustments, the replication reproduced the focal main effect of anchor precision, F(1, 116) = 6.28, p = .014, partial eta^2 = .05 (original F(1, 55) = 6.88, p_rep = .947, eta^2 = .02, N = 59) and the main effect of motivation, F(1, 116) = 71.06, p < .001, partial eta^2 = .38 (original F(1, 55) = 89.20, p_rep = .99, eta^2 = .60), but found no interaction, F < 1 (original F(1, 55) = 3.55, p_rep = .86). 2. Original work identity: The report reference (line 222) explicitly cites 'Janiszewski, C., D. Uy. 2008. Anchor precision influences the amount of adjustment. Psychological Science, 19,121–127.' (also listed in the Master CSV line 1172 as 'Precision of the anchor influences the amount of adjustment.,\""C Janiszewski, D Uy\"",PS,19,2,121-7'). No original article DOI is printed in the source text, so doi is omitted. 3. Absence from shipped rows: None of the 93 supplied shipped rows for 10.1126/science.aac4716 matches Janiszewski and Uy (2008) by author, title, or citation metadata. 4. Non-duplication: Target 63 is unique among the 98 blind targets in codings/9fed70371eeb17ff.json. 5. Prior context: Prior Argon runs in prior_state_path timed out without producing decisions; no prior adjudication exists for target 63."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0079_9fed70371eeb17ff"", ""target_index"": 63, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Janiszewski, C., D. Uy."", ""year"": ""2008"", ""title"": ""Anchor precision influences the amount of adjustment"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""4"", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Replication of Study 4b by Janiszewski & Uy (2008, Psychological Science) Jesse Chandler Princeton University"", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""These findings represent a partial replication of Janiszewski and Uy (2008). The central claim of their paper – that anchor precision influences the degree of adjustment was supported. Additionally, these data replicated the main effect of motivation observed in their study. There was no evidence of the reported interaction."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Individual primary replication report: authors’ verdict and comparisons to this original, prioritizing an overall verdict when stated.""}"
10.1126/science.aac4716,9fed70371eeb17ff,67,,Preschoolers' perspective taking in word learning: do they blindly follow eye gaze?,"Nurmsoo, E.; Bloom, P.",2008,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Nurmsoo, E.; Bloom, P."", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Preschoolers' perspective taking in word learning: do they blindly follow eye gaze?"", ""year"": ""2008""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""9fed70371eeb17ff"", ""location"": ""Primary Project Report 27 (https://osf.io/download/ictp5/), Confirmatory analysis & Discussion, source_view.json lines 366-367; Master CSV (https://osf.io/download/fgjvw/) Study Num 115, lines 1175-1176; Appendix Table, p. 38, line 67"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Confirmatory analysis The data from 20 participants were analyzed in order to attempt to replicate the original authors’ primary finding that older children performed at above-chance levels on “where” trials, answering an average of 1.41 out of 2 trials correctly, t(31) = 3.23, prep = .97. In our replication study, a one-sample t-test showed no such trend, M = 0.625, t(7) = -1.426, p = .197. Younger children (M = .833) also did not perform at above-chance levels on “where” trials, t(11) = -.804, p = .438. Similarly, neither the older (M = 1.125) or younger (M = 1.08) group performed at above-chance levels on “there” trials, t(7) = .357, p = .732 and t(11) = .364, p = .723, respectively. At one point during the planning of this replication, independent measures t-tests comparing younger versus older students on the number of correct “where” trial and “there” trial responses had been identified as the key finding. These analyses also showed no differences between younger and older children on “where” trials, t(18) = .627, p = .539, nor on “there” trials, t(18) = -1.04, p = .918. Discussion Summary of Replication Attempt Unlike in Nurmsoo and Bloom (2008), the confirmatory analysis for the current replication attempt did not reveal above-chance performance by older children on “where” trials. Thus, it is clear that we failed to replicate the original study’s finding."", ""reason"": ""1. Empirical retesting: In Primary Project Report 27 (https://osf.io/download/ictp5/, lines 363-367) and Master CSV Study Num 115 (lines 1175-1176), Benjamin Brown and colleagues tested N = 20 preschool children (n = 12 younger, ages 25-36 months; n = 8 older, ages 46-54 months) using the original authors' physical screen and novel object stimuli to replicate Study 1 of Nurmsoo and Bloom (2008). Whereas the original study found that older children selected the correct object above chance on 'where' trials, M = 1.41 out of 2, t(31) = 3.23, p_rep = .97, d = .571, the replication one-sample t-test found no above-chance performance in older children, M = 0.625, t(7) = -1.426, p = .197, or younger children, M = .833, t(11) = -.804, p = .438. 2. Original work identity: The original study is identified in Report 27 (line 363: 'Replication of Study 1 by Nurmsoo & Bloom (2008, Psychological Science)'), the Appendix table (p. 38, line 67: 'E Nurmsoo, P Bloom, Psychological Science'), and Master CSV Study Num 115 (lines 1175-1176: '115,Preschoolers' perspective taking in word learning: do they blindly follow eye gaze?,\""E Nurmsoo, P Bloom\"",PS,19,3,211-5'). No original article DOI is printed in the source text, so doi is omitted. 3. Absence from shipped rows: None of the 93 supplied shipped rows for 10.1126/science.aac4716 matches Nurmsoo and Bloom (2008) by author, title, or citation metadata. 4. Non-duplication: Target 67 is unique among the 98 blind targets in codings/9fed70371eeb17ff.json. 5. Prior context: Prior Argon runs in prior_state_path timed out without producing decisions; no prior adjudication exists for target 67."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0079_9fed70371eeb17ff"", ""target_index"": 67, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Nurmsoo, E.; Bloom, P."", ""year"": ""2008"", ""title"": ""Preschoolers' perspective taking in word learning: do they blindly follow eye gaze?"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""1"", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Replication of Study 1 by Nurmsoo & Bloom (2008, Psychological Science) Benjamin Brown, PhD; Kristina Brown, PhD Peter Attridge; Leah Behan; Heather Bentley; Grace Binion; Oliva Devitt; Sylvia Eboigbe; Melissa Vazquez bbrown6@ggc.edu"", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Unlike in Nurmsoo and Bloom (2008), the confirmatory analysis for the current replication attempt did not reveal above-chance performance by older children on “where” trials. Thus, it is clear that we failed to replicate the original study’s finding."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Individual primary replication report: authors’ verdict and comparisons to this original, prioritizing an overall verdict when stated.""}"
10.1126/science.aac4716,9fed70371eeb17ff,68,,Measuring the crowd within: Probabilistic representations within individuals,"Vul, E.; Pashler, H.",2008,full_text,same_author_year_siblings,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Vul, E.; Pashler, H."", ""doi"": ""10.1111/j.1467-9280.2008.02136.x"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Measuring the crowd within: Probabilistic representations within individuals"", ""year"": ""2008""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""9fed70371eeb17ff"", ""location"": ""Master CSV (https://osf.io/download/fgjvw/) Study Num 116, source_view.json lines 1176-1177; Primary Project Report 29 (https://osf.io/download/7kimb/, Steegen et al., 2014, Frontiers in Psychology 5:786), Abstract, Confirmatory Analysis & References, lines 384-386, 399-400, 404-406; Appendix Table, p. 38, line 67"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""116,Measuring the crowd within: Probabilistic representations within individuals.,\""E Vul, H Pashler\"",PS,19,7,645-7,https://osf.io/ivfu6/,\""Crowding, Humans, Models, Statistical\"",2,4,Vul,2785,MIT,Pashler,17561,UC San Diego,Steegen,5,University of Leuven,Vanpaemel,461,University of Leuven,122,6.42,4.08,2.35,2.35,1,Cognitive,1,Collected themselves,No expertise required,No opportunity for researcher expectations to influence results,No opportunity for lack of diligence to affect the results,2.67,4.33,0,0,0,0,0,1,1,0,W Vanpaemel,6-10-2013,5/23/14,J Cohoon,1,t(172) = 3.94,173,<.01,,2,dependent samples t-test,main effect,Averaging two guesses within one person provides a more accurate answer than either guess alone,d = 0.47,,38,50,61,Complete,48,0.89,1,X,t(139) = 4.02,140,0.001,same,2,dependent samples t-test,main effect,yes,0.99,d = .34,E Vul,,1,https://osf.io/8twa9/"", ""reason"": ""1. Empirical retesting: In Primary Project Report 29 (https://osf.io/download/7kimb/, Steegen, Dewitte, Tuerlinckx, & Vanpaemel, 2014, Frontiers in Psychology 5:786, lines 384-408) and Master CSV Study Num 116 (lines 1176-1177), the replication team collected new data from University of Leuven undergraduates in both an immediate condition (N = 471 analyzed) and a 3-week delayed condition (N = 140 analyzed) to replicate Vul and Pashler (2008). In the immediate condition, the mean MSE of the average of two guesses (M = 541, SD = 313) was significantly smaller than Guess 1 (M = 589, SD = 336), t(470) = 8.69, p < 0.001, d_z = 0.40, and Guess 2 (M = 615, SD = 351), t(470) = 10.26, p < 0.001, d_z = 0.47; in the delayed condition (the focal RPP comparison in Master CSV Study 116 against original t(172) = 3.94, p < .01, d = 0.47, N = 173), the mean MSE of the average of two guesses (M = 467, SD = 260) was significantly smaller than Guess 1 (M = 517, SD = 288), t(139) = 4.02, p < 0.001, d_z = 0.34, and Guess 2 (M = 589, SD = 327), t(139) = 8.48, p < 0.001, d_z = 0.72. (Note: the Master CSV quote is selected because the two-column PDF layout of Report 29 interleaves left and right columns across line breaks). 2. Original work identity: Both Master CSV Study 116 (lines 1176-1177) and Report 29 References (line 406) explicitly identify the original target as 'Vul, E., and Pashler, H. (2008). Measuring the crowd within: probabilistic representations within individuals. Psychol. Sci. 19, 645–647. doi: 10.1111/j.1467-9280.2008.02136.x'. 3. Absence from shipped rows: All 93 supplied shipped rows were checked. Shipped row 1890 shares first author E. Vul ('10.1111/j.1467-9280.2008.02046.x', 'Temporal Selection is Suppressed, Delayed, and Diffused During the Attentional Blink', Vul; Nieuwenstein; Kanwisher, 2008, Psychological Science 19(1), 55-61), but that is a completely different 2008 Psychological Science article replicated in Primary Report 30 / Master CSV Study 117 by Michael Barnett-Cowan (and already coded as blind target 69, input_row_index 1890, as also noted in prior_adjudication_path line 367). 4. Non-duplication: Target 68 is unique among the 98 blind targets in codings/9fed70371eeb17ff.json. 5. Prior context: Prior Argon runs in prior_state_path timed out without producing decisions; no prior adjudication exists for target 68."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0079_9fed70371eeb17ff"", ""target_index"": 68, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Vul, E.; Pashler, H."", ""year"": ""2008"", ""title"": ""Measuring the crowd within: Probabilistic representations within individuals"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""1"", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""HYPOTHESIS AND THEORY ARTICLE published: 28 July 2014 doi: 10.3389/fpsyg.2014.00786 Measuring the crowd within again: a pre-registered replication study Sara Steegen*, Laura Dewitte , Francis Tuerlinckx and Wolf Vanpaemel Research Group of Quantitative Psychology and Individual Differences, Faculty of Psychology and Educational Sciences, University of Leuven, Leuven, Belgium Edited by: According to the crowd within effect, the average of two estimates from one person Rolf Antonius Zwaan, Erasmus tends to be more accurate than a single estimate of that person. The effect implies University Rotterdam, Netherlands that the well documented wisdom of the crowd effect—the crowd’s average estimate Reviewed by: tends to be more accurate than the individual estimates—can be obtained within a single Diane Swick, VA Northern California Health Care System, USA individual."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Our replication attempt of the crowd within effect supports the the null hypothesis relative to the alternative hypothesis. In con- original finding by Vul and Pashler (2008) that averaging two trast to a p-value, the BF can provide evidence both in favor or guesses within one person provides a more accurate answer than against the null hypothesis. Therefore, in addition to p-values, either guess alone. This effect was found when the second guess we calculated BF’s for all tests, using a web-based Bayes factor was made immediately after the first guess (immediate condi- calculator8."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Individual primary replication report: authors’ verdict and comparisons to this original, prioritizing an overall verdict when stated. Two-column PDF extraction interleaves some prose; the explicit support statement is legible.""}"
10.1126/science.aac4716,9fed70371eeb17ff,71,,Errors Are Aversive: Defensive Motivation and the Error-Related Negativity,"Hajcak, G.; Foti, D.",2008,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Hajcak, G.; Foti, D."", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Errors Are Aversive: Defensive Motivation and the Error-Related Negativity"", ""year"": ""2008""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""9fed70371eeb17ff"", ""location"": ""Primary Project Report 34 (https://osf.io/download/82bwv/), Section 4.2 Statistical results, source_view.json lines 451-452; Report header, line 447; Master CSV (https://osf.io/download/fgjvw/) Study Num 120, lines 1180-1181; Appendix Table, p. 38, line 68"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""4.2 Statistical results A robust ERN was observed (mean amplitude = 6.44µV; SEM = .73) which was similar in magnitude to the original study (see Figure 1). This difference between ERP amplitudes following errors vs. correct responses was significant (t(40) = 8.69, p < .01). An error potentiated startle response was also apparent (see Figure 2), the magni- tude of which corresponded closely with the original study (mean difference between error and correct trials = 2.44µV; SEM = 1.17). This effect was significant. Paired t tests found that startle magnitude was greater following errors than following either subtype of correct trial, whether predictable, t(40) = 3.51, p < .01, or unpredictable t(28) = 2.08, p < .05. The main finding of the original study, a correlation between ERN and EPS am- plitude, did not reach the threshold for significance in our replication: r = -.25, p = .11, one-tailed. The original study reported a significant correlation: r = -.38, p < .05, one-tailed."", ""reason"": ""1. Empirical retesting: In Primary Project Report 34 (https://osf.io/download/82bwv/, lines 447-456) and Master CSV Study Num 120 (lines 1180-1181), replicators Melissa Lewis and Michael Pitts tested 51 subjects (final analyzed N = 43 after impedance/artifact exclusions) in an arrow flanker task with acoustic startle probes, measuring EEG error-related negativity (ERN) and EMG error-potentiated startle (EPS) to replicate Hajcak and Foti (2008). While the replication reproduced the ERN main effect, t(40) = 8.69, p < .01, and EPS main effects for predictable correct trials, t(40) = 3.51, p < .01, and unpredictable correct trials, t(28) = 2.08, p < .05, the focal zero-order correlation between ERN and EPS magnitude did not reach statistical significance, r = -.25, p = .11, one-tailed (N = 43), compared to the original study's r = -.38, p < .05, one-tailed (N = 31). 2. Original work identity: Report 34 (line 447) is titled 'Replication of “Errors Are Aversive” by Greg Hajcak & Dan Foti (2008, Psychological Science)', and Master CSV Study Num 120 (lines 1180-1181) gives the full title and citation metadata: '120,Errors Are Aversive: Defensive Motivation and the Error-Related Negativity,\""G Hajcak, D Foti\"",PS,19,2,103-8'. No original article DOI is printed in the source text, so doi is omitted. 3. Absence from shipped rows: None of the 93 supplied shipped rows for 10.1126/science.aac4716 matches Hajcak and Foti (2008) by author, title, or citation metadata. 4. Non-duplication: Target 71 is unique among the 98 blind targets in codings/9fed70371eeb17ff.json. 5. Prior context: Prior Argon runs in prior_state_path timed out without producing decisions; no prior adjudication exists for target 71."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0079_9fed70371eeb17ff"", ""target_index"": 71, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Hajcak, G.; Foti, D."", ""year"": ""2008"", ""title"": ""Errors Are Aversive: Defensive Motivation and the Error-Related Negativity"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""1"", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Replication of “Errors Are Aversive” by Greg Hajcak & Dan Foti (2008, Psychological Science) Melissa Lewis and Michael Pitts mlewis@alumni.reed.edu mpitts@reed.edu 1"", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""For this reason, we consider this to be a failure to replicate but with extremely similar results."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Individual primary replication report: authors’ verdict and comparisons to this original, prioritizing an overall verdict when stated.""}"
10.1126/science.aac4716,9fed70371eeb17ff,72,,The sunny side of fairness: Preference for fairness activates reward circuitry (and disregarding unfairness activates self-control circuitry),"Tabibnia, G.; Satpute, A. B.; Lieberman, M. D.",2008,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Tabibnia, G.; Satpute, A. B.; Lieberman, M. D."", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""The sunny side of fairness: Preference for fairness activates reward circuitry (and disregarding unfairness activates self-control circuitry)"", ""year"": ""2008""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""9fed70371eeb17ff"", ""location"": ""Primary Project Report 35 (https://osf.io/download/nxp9w/), Introduction, source_view.json lines 456-458; Overview of the Results & Neural Results, lines 461-464; Master CSV (https://osf.io/download/fgjvw/) Study Num 121, lines 1181-1182; Appendix Table, p. 38, line 68"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Replication of “The sunny side of fairness: Preference for fairness activates reward circuitry (and disregarding unfairness activates self-control circuitry)” by G Tabibnia, AB Satpute, MD Lieberman (2008, Psychological Science) Anastasia Rigney Taru Flagan Jennifer Beer beerutexas@gmail.com Introduction In their original paper “The sunny side of fairness: Preference for fairness activates reward circuitry (and disregarding unfairness activates self-control circuitry,” Tabibnia, Satpute, and Lieberman (2008) discuss the difficulties in separating the desire for fairness from the desire for material gain in the context of economic decision making. Accordingly, there had been little research on whether fair proposals produce positive emotional response due to its inherent fairness regardless of material gain. The original authors suggest that ultimatum games that require bilateral bargaining allow these motives to be tested independently. In this replication of Tabibnia et al. (2008), we sought to reproduce the methodology used in the original paper. The present work was conducted as part of the Reproducibility Project: Psychology which asks volunteers to replicate the final study in their chosen paper. Therefore, this is a direct replication of Experiment 2."", ""reason"": ""1. Empirical retesting: In Primary Project Report 35 (https://osf.io/download/nxp9w/, lines 456-464) and Master CSV Study Num 121 (lines 1181-1182), Anastasia Rigney, Taru Flagan, and Jennifer Beer conducted an fMRI replication (N = 24 analyzed out of 25 scanned on a 3T Siemens Skyra) of Experiment 2 of Tabibnia, Satpute, and Lieberman (2008). The replication reproduced the behavioral pattern of offer acceptance (higher acceptance for fair vs. unfair offers) and the focal neural findings: fair > unfair offers activated reward regions in the dorsal striatum (z = 3.23) and orbitofrontal cortex (z = 2.81 right, z = 3.84 left), and accepting subjectively unfair offers > baseline activated the left VLPFC (z = 4.92, p_rep > 0.99) and right LPFC extending to VLPFC (z = 5.43, p_rep > 0.99, d = 2.22), replicating the original right VLPFC activation (t(11) = 5.39, p_rep > 0.99, d = 3.25, N = 12), while auxiliary insula analyses (Planned analyses 3-5) did not replicate in the original direction. 2. Original work identity: Report 35 (line 456) and Master CSV Study Num 121 (lines 1181-1182) explicitly identify the original paper as 'The sunny side of fairness: Preference for fairness activates reward circuitry (and disregarding unfairness activates self-control circuitry)' by G Tabibnia, AB Satpute, MD Lieberman (2008, Psychological Science, 19(4), 339-347). No original article DOI is printed in the source text, so doi is omitted. 3. Absence from shipped rows: None of the 93 supplied shipped rows for 10.1126/science.aac4716 matches Tabibnia, Satpute, and Lieberman (2008) by author, title, or citation metadata. 4. Non-duplication: Target 72 is unique among the 98 blind targets in codings/9fed70371eeb17ff.json. 5. Prior context: Prior Argon runs in prior_state_path timed out without producing decisions; no prior adjudication exists for target 72."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0079_9fed70371eeb17ff"", ""target_index"": 72, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Tabibnia, G.; Satpute, A. B.; Lieberman, M. D."", ""year"": ""2008"", ""title"": ""The sunny side of fairness: Preference for fairness activates reward circuitry (and disregarding unfairness activates self-control circuitry)"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""2"", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Replication of “The sunny side of fairness: Preference for fairness activates reward circuitry (and disregarding unfairness activates self-control circuitry)” by G Tabibnia, AB Satpute, MD Lieberman (2008, Psychological Science) Anastasia Rigney Taru Flagan Jennifer Beer beerutexas@gmail.com"", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Summary of Planned Analysis #2: As in the original study, the replication study found that greater VLPFC activation was associated with trials where participants accepted offers they perceived to be unfair. Planned analysis 3: Accepted unfair > Rejected unfair Original Study: When unfair offers were Replication study: When unfair offers were accepted the left anterior insula was less accepted the left insula was more active active compared to when they were rejected compared to when they were rejected (x = -28, y = 8, z = -6), t(11) = 4.06, prep > (x = -34, y = 16, z = -2), z = 4.28, prep > 0.99. 0.99. Summary of Planned Analysis #3: When participants accepted rather than rejected their idiosyncratically-defined offers, the replication study found significantly increased activity in the insula. Planned analysis 4: Correlating parameter estimates from analyses #2 & #3 Original Study: Activity in the insula was Replication Study: Activity in the insula inversely correlated with activity in the was positively correlated with activity in the VLPFC (r = -0.77, prep = 0.99)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Individual primary replication report: authors’ verdict and comparisons to this original, prioritizing an overall verdict when stated.""}"
10.1017/jdm.2024.31,6dfd87d4b0761f76,1,10.1016/j.obhdp.2007.04.006,"""One of us"": Outstanding willingness to help save a single identified compatriot","Kogut, T., & Ritov, I.",2007,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Kogut, T., & Ritov, I."", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.obhdp.2007.04.006"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""\""One of us\"": Outstanding willingness to help save a single identified compatriot"", ""year"": ""2007""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""6dfd87d4b0761f76"", ""location"": ""Results (line 54); see also Introduction (lines 3, 28-29), Discussion (lines 69, 83, 85), and References (line 304)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""In our extension, we failed to find support for the findings by Kogut and Ritov (2007) that there is a higher likelihood of helping single victims over a group if they belong to the ingroup (vs. the outgroup)."", ""reason"": ""The paper explicitly designs and reports a pre-registered conceptual replication of Kogut and Ritov (2007) alongside its close replication of Kogut and Ritov (2005a) Study 2 by adding identified ingroup (American) and identified outgroup (Russian) conditions crossed with victim singularity (single child vs. group of eight children) in a 2 x 4 between-subjects design (N = 1,835), and directly tests and compares its results against Kogut and Ritov's (2007) finding that singularity and group belonging interact to produce higher willingness to help a single identified ingroup victim over a group (lines 3, 28-29, 37, 54, 69, 83, 85). The reference list (line 304) and Crossref metadata verify the original identity as Kogut, T., & Ritov, I. (2007), '\""One of us\"": Outstanding willingness to help save a single identified compatriot', Organizational Behavior and Human Decision Processes 104(2): 150-157, DOI 10.1016/j.obhdp.2007.04.006. The only shipped row (input_row_index 1473, DOI 10.1002/bdm.492) is Kogut and Ritov (2005a), 'The \""identified victim\"" effect: an identified group, or just a single individual?', Journal of Behavioral Decision Making 18(3): 157-167 (target_index 0), which did not manipulate ingroup/outgroup belonging (line 31). Because the 2007 article reports distinct experiments in a different journal two years later, report equivalence does not apply and this target is genuinely absent from the shipped rows. No prior adjudication or prior state file existed for this work."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0053_6dfd87d4b0761f76"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Kogut, T., & Ritov, I."", ""year"": ""2007"", ""title"": ""\""One of us\"": Outstanding willingness to help save a single identified compatriot"", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.obhdp.2007.04.006"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We sought to revisit this classic finding and re-examine the conditions which encourage or discourage helping intention. We report an independent close replication of the identified single victim effect demonstrated in Kogut and Ritov (2005a) Study 2 along with two extensions, testing the effect of group belonging as a conceptual replication of Kogut and Ritov (2007) and examining perceived responsibility (Erlandsson et al., 2015)."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In our extension, we failed to find support for the findings by Kogut and Ritov (2007) that there is a higher likelihood of helping single victims over a group if they belong to the ingroup (vs. the outgroup)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Explicit author verdict on the target effect.""}"
10.1177/2515245918810225,1c40038a928d872c,3,10.1037/a0015141,Liberals and conservatives rely on different sets of moral foundations,"Graham, J., Haidt, J., & Nosek, B. A.",2009,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 3, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""type"": ""replication"", ""doi"": ""10.1037/a0015141"", ""title"": ""Liberals and conservatives rely on different sets of moral foundations"", ""authors"": ""Graham, J., Haidt, J., & Nosek, B. A."", ""year"": ""2009"", ""outcome"": ""successful""}, ""quote"": ""The primary target of replication was the relationship between political ideology and the binding foundations. In the aggregate sample (N = 6,966), items that emphasized concerns for the in-group, authority, or purity were deemed more relevant for moral judgment by the political right than by the political left (r = .14, p = 6.05e -34 , d = 0.29, 95% CI = [0.25, 0.34], q = 0.15, 95% CI = [0.12, 0.17]). This result is consistent with the hypothesis that binding foundations are perceived as more morally relevant by members of the political right than by members of the political left. The overall effect size was smaller than the original (d = 0.29, 95% CI = [0.25, 0.34], vs. original d = 0.52)."", ""location"": ""The 28 Effects, Effect 4 (line 81)"", ""reason"": ""Graham, Haidt, and Nosek (2009, Study 1) is Effect 4 of the 28 replicated findings in Many Labs 2 and was omitted from the shipped rows; both primary and secondary moral-foundation tests replicated successfully.""}","{""authors"": ""Graham, J., Haidt, J., & Nosek, B. A."", ""year"": ""2009"", ""title"": ""Liberals and conservatives rely on different sets of moral foundations"", ""doi"": ""10.1037/a0015141"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""Study 1"", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""4"", ""evidence_quote"": ""4. Moral foundations of liberals versus conservatives (Graham, Haidt, & Nosek, 2009, Study 1). People on the political left (liberal) and political right (conservative) have distinct policy preferences and may also have different moral intuitions and principles. In Graham et al.'s (2009) Study 1, 1,548 participants across the ideological spectrum rated whether different concepts, such as \""purity\"" and \""fairness,\"" were relevant for deciding whether something was right or wrong. Items that emphasized concerns of harm or fairness (individualizing foundations) were deemed more relevant for moral judgment by the political left than by the political right (r = -.21, d = -0.43, 95% CI = [-0.55, -0.32]), whereas items that emphasized concerns for the in-group, authority, or purity (binding foundations) were deemed more relevant for moral judgment by the political right than by the political left (r = .25, d = 0.52, 95% CI = [0.40, 0.63]). 4 Participants rated the relevance to moral judgment of 15 items (3 for each foundation) in a randomized order on a 6-point scale from not at all relevant to extremely relevant.\nReplication. The primary target of replication was the relationship between political ideology and the binding foundations. In the aggregate sample (N = 6,966), items that emphasized concerns for the in-group, authority, or purity were deemed more relevant for moral judgment by the political right than by the political left (r = .14, p = 6.05e -34 , d = 0.29, 95% CI = [0.25, 0.34], q = 0.15, 95% CI = [0.12, 0.17]). This result is consistent with the hypothesis that binding foundations are perceived as more morally relevant by members of the political right than by members of the political left. The overall effect size was smaller than the original (d = 0.29, 95% CI = [0.25, 0.34], vs. original d = 0.52)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""This result is consistent with the hypothesis that binding foundations are perceived as more morally relevant by members of the political right than by members of the political left."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""fulltext"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The target-specific comparison determines the verdict; smaller effect size alone does not change agreement into a mixed outcome.""}"
10.1177/2515245918810225,1c40038a928d872c,17,10.1207/s15516709cog2605_4,Cultural preferences for formal versus intuitive reasoning,"Norenzayan, A., Smith, E. E., Kim, B. J., & Nisbett, R. E.",2002,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 17, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""type"": ""replication"", ""doi"": ""10.1207/s15516709cog2605_4"", ""title"": ""Cultural preferences for formal versus intuitive reasoning"", ""authors"": ""Norenzayan, A., Smith, E. E., Kim, B. J., & Nisbett, R. E."", ""year"": ""2002"", ""outcome"": ""mixed""}, ""quote"": ""Ultimately, just three effects (those originally reported by Huang et al., 2014;Knobe, 2003;and Norenzayan et al., 2002) showed significant evidence for moderation by WEIRDness after correction for multiple comparisons. However, for Norenzayan et al.'s effect, the cultural difference was the inverse of the original result, though we note that the original study did not have a theoretical commitment regarding the cultural difference in the condition effect that we tested."", ""location"": ""Results, Variation across samples and settings (line 168)"", ""reason"": ""Norenzayan et al. (2002, Study 2) is Effect 20 of the 28 replicated studies and was omitted from the shipped rows; the aggregate condition effect agreed with the original, but the authors explicitly state that the cultural difference was the inverse of the original result, yielding a mixed outcome.""}","{""authors"": ""Norenzayan, A., Smith, E. E., Kim, B. J., & Nisbett, R. E."", ""year"": ""2002"", ""title"": ""Cultural preferences for formal versus intuitive reasoning"", ""doi"": ""10.1207/s15516709cog2605_4"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""Study 2"", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""20"", ""evidence_quote"": ""20. Preferences for formal versus intuitive reasoning (Norenzayan, Smith, Kim, & Nisbett, 2002, Study 2). The way people living in the West think may be more rule based than the way people living in East Asia think. Fiftytwo European Americans (27 men, 25 women), 52 Asian Americans (28 men, 24 women), and 53 East Asians (27 men, 26 women) were randomly assigned to either a classification-judgment condition (decide \""which group the target object belongs to\""; two thirds of the sample) or a similarity-judgment condition (decide \""which group the target object is most similar to\""; one third of the sample).\nAll participants categorized targets into two alternative groups. Each stimulus set consisted of two targets and two groups of four exemplars each. Each of the two target stimuli was presented separately with the two groups. All the exemplars in each group had a particular feature in common with each other and with one of the targets but shared a family resemblance, and no single common feature, with the other target, in a counterbalanced design (see Fig. 1). When asked \""which group the target object belongs to,\"" European American and East Asian participants preferred to classify on the basis of a rule (M = 69% of responses for European Americans; M = 70% of responses for East Asians) rather than family resemblance, F(1, 100) = 44.40, p < .001, r = .55. When asked \""which group the target object is more similar to,\"" European Americans gave many more responses based on the unidimensional rule (M = 69%) than on family resemblance (M = 31%), t(17) = 3.68, p = .002, d = 1.65, 95% CI = [0.59, 2.67]. In contrast, East Asians gave fewer rule-based responses (M = 41%) than family-resemblance-based responses (M = 59%), t(17) = -2.09, p = .05, d = -0.93, 95% CI = [-1.85, 0.01]. The responses of Asian Americans were intermediate, with participants indicating no preference for the unidimensional rule (M = 46%) over family resemblance (M = 54%), t < 1.\nReplication. For the replication, we preregistered a plan to compare the percentage of rule-based responses between the belong-to and similar-to conditions. In the original study, European Americans showed no difference between these conditions (d = 0.00, 95% CI = [-0.15, 0.15]), but East Asians were more likely to give rulebased responses in the belong-to condition than in the similar-to condition (d = 0.67, 95% CI = [0.52, 0.81). Note that we planned a comparison between the experimental conditions, whereas Norenzayan et al. (2002) focused their analysis and theoretical interest on comparisons between cultural groups within each experimental condition.\nWe computed the percentage of rule-based responses for each participant and then tested whether the mean percentages for the two experimental conditions were equal, using a t test for independent samples. In the aggregate replication sample (N = 7,396), participants who were asked \""which group the target object belongs to\"" were more likely to classify on the basis of a rule (M = 64%, SD = 25%) than on the basis of family resemblance (M = 36%, SD = 25%), and participants who were asked \""which group the target object is more similar to\"" were more likely to classify on the basis of family resemblance (M = 56%, SD = 21%) than on the basis of a rule (M = 44%, SD = 21%). The likelihood of using a rule was higher in the belong-to condition compared with the similar-to condition, t(7227.59) = 37.05, p = 3.04e -275 , d = 0.86, 95% CI = [0.81, 0.91]. This pattern was in the same direction as the original aggregate result, and the effect size was somewhat larger: People were more likely to categorize on the basis of a rule when they considered what group the target belonged to and more likely to categorize on the basis of family resemblance when they considered what group the target was similar to. 5 Follow-up analyses. We identified a priori that this effect and the one reported by Tversky and Gati (1978) both involved similarity judgments and thus that the order of these study materials in Slate 2 might be particularly relevant. We tested whether Norenzayan et al.'s (2002) effect was moderated by whether its materials came before or after Tversky and Gati's and observed very weak moderation by task order, t(7392) = 2.34, p = .02, d = 0.05, 95% CI = [0.01, 0.10]."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""However, for Norenzayan et al.'s effect, the cultural difference was the inverse of the original result, though we note that the original study did not have a theoretical commitment regarding the cultural difference in the condition effect that we tested."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Mixed: the aggregate condition effect agrees with the original, but the authors explicitly describe the tested cultural difference as inverse to the original. The qualification about the original theoretical commitment does not erase that stated contrast. Neither comparison is an overall replication verdict."", ""additional_outcome_quotes"": [{""quote"": ""This pattern was in the same direction as the original aggregate result, and the effect size was somewhat larger: People were more likely to categorize on the basis of a rule when they considered what group the target belonged to and more likely to categorize on the basis of family resemblance when they considered what group the target was similar to."", ""section"": ""fulltext""}]}"
10.1177/2515245918810225,1c40038a928d872c,26,10.1027/1864-9335/a000178,"Investigating variation in replicability: A ""Many Labs"" replication project","Klein, R. A., Ratliff, K. A., Vianello, M., Adams, R. B., Jr., Bahník, Š., Bernstein, M. J., . . . Nosek, B. A.",2014,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 26, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""The order in which tasks are presented could moderate effect sizes. Across a 30-min session, effects may weaken if participants tire or if earlier procedures interfere with later procedures. We did not observe this pattern in prior Many Labs investigations with the same design (Ebersole et al., 2016;Klein et al., 2014a), but task order remains a potential moderator. Order of administration in the current project was randomized, so we were able to test this possibility directly."", ""location"": ""Results, Influence of task order (line 170)"", ""reason"": ""Inspection of the Methods, preregistered order analyses, Results, Discussion, and citations shows that Many Labs 2 diagnoses task order as an internal design check on its own 28 target replications rather than deliberately re-testing an empirical finding of Klein et al. (2014a; Many Labs 1). In the Methods (Table 1 Note, line 31), sequential administration in a 13- or 15-study slate is introduced as a procedural deviation from the 28 standalone original studies ('These differences included the fact that the studies were administered sequentially in a slate in the replication project. The order effect is evaluated directly in the Results section'). The a priori planned order analyses were specific to Many Labs 2's own materials: testing mutual order moderation between the two similarity-judgment tasks in Slate 2 (Effect 20, Norenzayan et al., 2002, and Effect 28, Tversky & Gati, 1978; lines 121, 149), evaluating first-in-slate administration for effects whose original authors predicted sensitivity to prior tasks (Alter et al., 2007; Giessner & Schubert, 2007; Miyamoto & Kitayama, 2002; Schwarz et al., 1991; lines 134, 171), and checking polynomial trends across positions 1 to 13/15 so as to confirm 'that task order cannot account for observation of null effects for any of the nonreplicated results' (line 171). In Discussion (line 185), the prediction of order interference or fatigue is cited to methodological commentaries (Ferguson, Carter, & Hassin, 2014; Kahneman, 2016; Schnall, 2014), and the authors explicitly treat the '51 total replication tests (28 reported here; 13 in Klein et al., 2014a;and 10 in Ebersole et al., 2016)' as parallel, additive tests on 51 distinct psychological effects rather than a replication of Klein et al. (2014a).""}","{""authors"": ""Klein, R. A., Ratliff, K. A., Vianello, M., Adams, R. B., Jr., Bahník, Š., Bernstein, M. J., . . . Nosek, B. A."", ""year"": ""2014"", ""title"": ""Investigating variation in replicability: A \""Many Labs\"" replication project"", ""doi"": ""10.1027/1864-9335/a000178"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Another possible moderating influence was task order. Participants completed their slate of 13 or 15 effects in a randomized order. It was possible that performance on tasks completed later in the sequence would be affected by tasks completed earlier in the sequence, either because of the specific content of the tasks or because of interference, fatigue, or other orderrelated influences (Ferguson, Carter, & Hassin, 2014;Kahneman, 2016;Schnall, 2014). Contrary to this prediction, we observed little evidence for systematic order effects for the 28 findings investigated. This replicates the lack of evidence for task-order effects observed in Many Labs 1 (Klein et al., 2014a) and Many Labs 3 (Ebersole et al., 2016)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""It was possible that performance on tasks completed later in the sequence would be affected by tasks completed earlier in the sequence, either because of the specific content of the tasks or because of interference, fatigue, or other orderrelated influences (Ferguson, Carter, & Hassin, 2014;Kahneman, 2016;Schnall, 2014). Contrary to this prediction, we observed little evidence for systematic order effects for the 28 findings investigated. This replicates the lack of evidence for task-order effects observed in Many Labs 1 (Klein et al., 2014a) and Many Labs 3 (Ebersole et al., 2016)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""This target is the earlier project's published lack of task-order effects, explicitly tested with new randomized-order data and described as replicated. It is not included merely as an earlier replication of the other substantive effects.""}"
10.1177/2515245918810225,1c40038a928d872c,27,,Many Labs 3: Evaluating participant pool quality across the academic semester via replication,"Ebersole, C. R., Atherton, O. E., Belanger, A. L., Skulborstad, H. M., Allen, J. M., Banks, J. B., . . . Brown, E. R.",2016,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 27, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""Contrary to this prediction, we observed little evidence for systematic order effects for the 28 findings investigated. This replicates the lack of evidence for task-order effects observed in Many Labs 1 (Klein et al., 2014a) and Many Labs 3 (Ebersole et al., 2016). Across 51 total replication tests (28 reported here; 13 in Klein et al., 2014a;and 10 in Ebersole et al., 2016), we have observed little evidence for reliable effects of task order."", ""location"": ""Discussion (line 185)"", ""reason"": ""Inspection of the Methods, preregistered order analyses, timeline, Results, Discussion, and citations confirms that Many Labs 2 does not re-test Ebersole et al. (2016; Many Labs 3) as an original target. First, Many Labs 2 issued its site invitation and collected data in 2014 (Method, line 29; Effect 16, line 106), two years before Ebersole et al. (2016) was published, and Ebersole et al.'s primary research question concerned participant pool quality across the academic semester (line 279). Second, in the Methods and preregistered protocol (Table 1 Note, line 31; lines 121, 134, 149, 170-171), task-order analyses were planned solely to evaluate whether sequential slate administration in Many Labs 2 moderated its own 28 target effects or explained any of its null replication outcomes ('these analyses confirm that the findings, in the aggregate, are robust to task order and, particularly, that task order cannot account for observation of null effects for any of the nonreplicated results', line 171). Third, when Discussion (line 185) states that the absence of systematic order effects across the 28 findings 'replicates the lack of evidence for task-order effects observed in Many Labs 1 (Klein et al., 2014a) and Many Labs 3 (Ebersole et al., 2016),' it immediately clarifies that these are '51 total replication tests (28 reported here; 13 in Klein et al., 2014a;and 10 in Ebersole et al., 2016)'—pooling three separate multi-lab projects' internal design checks rather than re-testing Ebersole et al.'s 10 effects.""}","{""authors"": ""Ebersole, C. R., Atherton, O. E., Belanger, A. L., Skulborstad, H. M., Allen, J. M., Banks, J. B., . . . Brown, E. R."", ""year"": ""2016"", ""title"": ""Many Labs 3: Evaluating participant pool quality across the academic semester via replication"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Another possible moderating influence was task order. Participants completed their slate of 13 or 15 effects in a randomized order. It was possible that performance on tasks completed later in the sequence would be affected by tasks completed earlier in the sequence, either because of the specific content of the tasks or because of interference, fatigue, or other orderrelated influences (Ferguson, Carter, & Hassin, 2014;Kahneman, 2016;Schnall, 2014). Contrary to this prediction, we observed little evidence for systematic order effects for the 28 findings investigated. This replicates the lack of evidence for task-order effects observed in Many Labs 1 (Klein et al., 2014a) and Many Labs 3 (Ebersole et al., 2016)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""It was possible that performance on tasks completed later in the sequence would be affected by tasks completed earlier in the sequence, either because of the specific content of the tasks or because of interference, fatigue, or other orderrelated influences (Ferguson, Carter, & Hassin, 2014;Kahneman, 2016;Schnall, 2014). Contrary to this prediction, we observed little evidence for systematic order effects for the 28 findings investigated. This replicates the lack of evidence for task-order effects observed in Many Labs 1 (Klein et al., 2014a) and Many Labs 3 (Ebersole et al., 2016)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""This target is the earlier project's published lack of task-order effects, explicitly tested with new randomized-order data and described as replicated. It is not included merely as an earlier replication of the other substantive effects.""}"
10.1525/collabra.310,f641e4b5cb3f280b,2,10.1016/j.jbusres.2019.01.027,"The 1 in 1,000,000: Context effects of how numbers cue different kinds of incidental environmental anchoring in marketing communications.","Koçaş, C., & Dogerlioglu-Demir, K.",2020,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Koçaş, C., & Dogerlioglu-Demir, K."", ""year"": ""2020"", ""title"": ""The 1 in 1,000,000: Context effects of how numbers cue different kinds of incidental environmental anchoring in marketing communications."", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.jbusres.2019.01.027"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2, 3"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Thus our procedure represents a close (near exact in Study 3) replication of the equivalent studies by Critcher and Gilovich (2008), Dogerlioglu-Demir and Koçaş (2015), and Koçaş and Dogerlioglu-Demir (2020) but nevertheless failed to detect an incidental anchoring effect."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Thus our procedure represents a close (near exact in Study 3) replication of the equivalent studies by Critcher and Gilovich (2008), Dogerlioglu-Demir and Koçaş (2015), and Koçaş and Dogerlioglu-Demir (2020) but nevertheless failed to detect an incidental anchoring effect."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true}"
10.1525/collabra.310,f641e4b5cb3f280b,3,10.1126/science.185.4157.1124,Judgment under uncertainty: Heuristics and biases.,"Tversky, A., & Kahneman, D.",1974,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Tversky, A., & Kahneman, D."", ""year"": ""1974"", ""title"": ""Judgment under uncertainty: Heuristics and biases."", ""doi"": ""10.1126/science.185.4157.1124"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""2, 3"", ""evidence_quote"": ""These items enabled a replication of the classic anchoring procedure designed by Tversky and Kahneman (1974)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In contrast to the incidental anchoring results, for these standard items robust anchoring effects were obtained (p < .001 in each case)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_confident"": true}"
10.1007/s40732-024-00597-x,7137b2b0d9b69364,0,10.1002/jeab.727,Effects of immediate-reinforcement training on delay discounting behavior in rats,"Fox, A. E.",2022,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Fox, A. E."", ""doi"": ""10.1002/jeab.727"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Effects of immediate-reinforcement training on delay discounting behavior in rats"", ""year"": ""2022""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""7137b2b0d9b69364"", ""location"": ""Abstract (lines 20-41); Introduction (lines 135-160); Discussion (lines 524-549); References (lines 687-691)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""A group of rats was exposed to an immediate (no-delay)-\n\nreinforcement intervention and subsequent delay discounting\n\nbehavior was modestly, but statistically signiﬁcantly, more\n\nimpulsive compared to a control group. This is consistent\n\nwith previous work (Fox, 2022; Fox etal., 2019; Renda\n\netal., 2018; Smith etal., 2022), and with the conclusion\nthat prolonged exposure to immediate reinforcement may\n\nhave just modest impacts on subsequent impulsive choice,\n\ncompared to relatively larger impacts of exposure to delayed\n\nreinforcement. This was true in the present experiment\n\ndespite double the number of subjects in each group and\n\ndouble the number of immediate reinforcement training tri-\n\nals, compared to previous research (Fox 2022; Fox etal.,\n\n2019)."", ""reason"": ""Omitted replication target (no prior adjudication file existed). Fox (2024) is titled a systematic replication of immediate reinforcement training on delay discounting behavior in rats and uses the author's prior FR 2 immediate-reinforcement training vs. maturation/handling control and posttest signaled-RIFI delay-discounting paradigm. In the Introduction (lines 135-160) and Discussion (lines 524-549), the author explicitly identifies Fox (2022) (10.1002/jeab.727) and Fox et al. (2019) (10.1016/j.beproc.2018.11.009) as the previous research whose sample size per group and number of immediate-reinforcement training trials were doubled (with a 50% longer intervention duration) to test the limits and generality of their reported results, and compares the observed modest increase in impulsive choice directly against them. Shipped row 1480 covers only Renda et al. (2018) (10.1002/jeab.432), leaving Fox (2022) omitted."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0056_7137b2b0d9b69364"", ""target_index"": 0, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Fox, A. E."", ""year"": ""2022"", ""title"": ""Effects of immediate-reinforcement training on delay discounting behavior in rats"", ""doi"": ""10.1002/jeab.727"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Experimental work investigating possible causal rela-\n\ntions between impulsive choice and extended experience\n\nwith immediate reinforcement is limited, but some work\n\nsuggests that experience with immediate reinforcement\n\nmay subsequently increase impulsive choice or blunt matu-\n\nrational decreases in impulsive choice in a delay-discounting\nparadigm (Fox, 2022; Fox etal., 2019; Renda etal., 2018;\n\nSmith etal., 2022). It was the goal of the work presented\n\nhere to expand on this body of research by testing eﬀects\n\nof an immediate reinforcement intervention that was 50%\n\nlonger in duration, included approximately twice as many\n\nimmediate reinforcement training trials, and employed twice\n\nthe sample, than previous work. In doing so, we hoped to\n\ntest the limits and generality of previously reported results."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""A group of rats was exposed to an immediate (no-delay)-\n\nreinforcement intervention and subsequent delay discounting\n\nbehavior was modestly, but statistically signiﬁcantly, more\n\nimpulsive compared to a control group. This is consistent\n\nwith previous work (Fox, 2022; Fox etal., 2019; Renda\n\netal., 2018; Smith etal., 2022), and with the conclusion\nthat prolonged exposure to immediate reinforcement may\n\nhave just modest impacts on subsequent impulsive choice,\n\ncompared to relatively larger impacts of exposure to delayed\n\nreinforcement. This was true in the present experiment"", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The introduction identifies the set of prior immediate-reinforcement findings whose generality is tested; the discussion explicitly reports consistency with every named member of that set. Modest magnitude is not framed as failure.""}"
10.1007/s40732-024-00597-x,7137b2b0d9b69364,1,10.1016/j.beproc.2018.11.009,Interventions aimed at changing impulsive choice in rats: Effects of immediate and relatively long delay to reward training,"Fox, A. E., Visser, E. J., & Nicholson, A. M.",2019,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Fox, A. E., Visser, E. J., & Nicholson, A. M."", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.beproc.2018.11.009"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Interventions aimed at changing impulsive choice in rats: Effects of immediate and relatively long delay to reward training"", ""year"": ""2019""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""7137b2b0d9b69364"", ""location"": ""Abstract (lines 20-41); Introduction (lines 135-160); Discussion (lines 524-549); References (lines 697-703)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""A group of rats was exposed to an immediate (no-delay)-\n\nreinforcement intervention and subsequent delay discounting\n\nbehavior was modestly, but statistically signiﬁcantly, more\n\nimpulsive compared to a control group. This is consistent\n\nwith previous work (Fox, 2022; Fox etal., 2019; Renda\n\netal., 2018; Smith etal., 2022), and with the conclusion\nthat prolonged exposure to immediate reinforcement may\n\nhave just modest impacts on subsequent impulsive choice,\n\ncompared to relatively larger impacts of exposure to delayed\n\nreinforcement. This was true in the present experiment\n\ndespite double the number of subjects in each group and\n\ndouble the number of immediate reinforcement training tri-\n\nals, compared to previous research (Fox 2022; Fox etal.,\n\n2019)."", ""reason"": ""Omitted replication target (no prior adjudication file existed). Along with Fox (2022) (target_index 0), Fox et al. (2019) (10.1016/j.beproc.2018.11.009) is a distinct primary empirical study by the same lab that is explicitly named in the Discussion (lines 541-549) as the previous research whose sample size and immediate-reinforcement training trials were doubled in this systematic replication, and whose finding of modestly increased impulsive choice after prolonged immediate reinforcement is retested and compared (lines 135-160, 524-549). It is absent from shipped_rows, which contains only Renda et al. (2018) (input_row_index 1480)."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0056_7137b2b0d9b69364"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Fox, A. E., Visser, E. J., & Nicholson, A. M."", ""year"": ""2019"", ""title"": ""Interventions aimed at changing impulsive choice in rats: Effects of immediate and relatively long delay to reward training"", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.beproc.2018.11.009"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Experimental work investigating possible causal rela-\n\ntions between impulsive choice and extended experience\n\nwith immediate reinforcement is limited, but some work\n\nsuggests that experience with immediate reinforcement\n\nmay subsequently increase impulsive choice or blunt matu-\n\nrational decreases in impulsive choice in a delay-discounting\nparadigm (Fox, 2022; Fox etal., 2019; Renda etal., 2018;\n\nSmith etal., 2022). It was the goal of the work presented\n\nhere to expand on this body of research by testing eﬀects\n\nof an immediate reinforcement intervention that was 50%\n\nlonger in duration, included approximately twice as many\n\nimmediate reinforcement training trials, and employed twice\n\nthe sample, than previous work. In doing so, we hoped to\n\ntest the limits and generality of previously reported results."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""A group of rats was exposed to an immediate (no-delay)-\n\nreinforcement intervention and subsequent delay discounting\n\nbehavior was modestly, but statistically signiﬁcantly, more\n\nimpulsive compared to a control group. This is consistent\n\nwith previous work (Fox, 2022; Fox etal., 2019; Renda\n\netal., 2018; Smith etal., 2022), and with the conclusion\nthat prolonged exposure to immediate reinforcement may\n\nhave just modest impacts on subsequent impulsive choice,\n\ncompared to relatively larger impacts of exposure to delayed\n\nreinforcement. This was true in the present experiment"", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The introduction identifies the set of prior immediate-reinforcement findings whose generality is tested; the discussion explicitly reports consistency with every named member of that set. Modest magnitude is not framed as failure.""}"
10.1007/s40732-024-00597-x,7137b2b0d9b69364,3,10.1016/j.beproc.2022.104584,Generalizability of time-based interventions: Effects of choice procedure and smaller-sooner delay,"Smith, T. R., Panfil, K., & Kirkpatrick, K.",2022,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Smith, T. R., Panfil, K., & Kirkpatrick, K."", ""year"": ""2022"", ""title"": ""Generalizability of time-based interventions: Effects of choice procedure and smaller-sooner delay"", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.beproc.2022.104584"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Experimental work investigating possible causal rela-\n\ntions between impulsive choice and extended experience\n\nwith immediate reinforcement is limited, but some work\n\nsuggests that experience with immediate reinforcement\n\nmay subsequently increase impulsive choice or blunt matu-\n\nrational decreases in impulsive choice in a delay-discounting\nparadigm (Fox, 2022; Fox etal., 2019; Renda etal., 2018;\n\nSmith etal., 2022). It was the goal of the work presented\n\nhere to expand on this body of research by testing eﬀects\n\nof an immediate reinforcement intervention that was 50%\n\nlonger in duration, included approximately twice as many\n\nimmediate reinforcement training trials, and employed twice\n\nthe sample, than previous work. In doing so, we hoped to\n\ntest the limits and generality of previously reported results."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""A group of rats was exposed to an immediate (no-delay)-\n\nreinforcement intervention and subsequent delay discounting\n\nbehavior was modestly, but statistically signiﬁcantly, more\n\nimpulsive compared to a control group. This is consistent\n\nwith previous work (Fox, 2022; Fox etal., 2019; Renda\n\netal., 2018; Smith etal., 2022), and with the conclusion\nthat prolonged exposure to immediate reinforcement may\n\nhave just modest impacts on subsequent impulsive choice,\n\ncompared to relatively larger impacts of exposure to delayed\n\nreinforcement. This was true in the present experiment"", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The introduction identifies the set of prior immediate-reinforcement findings whose generality is tested; the discussion explicitly reports consistency with every named member of that set. Modest magnitude is not framed as failure.""}"
10.1016/j.xjmad.2024.100075,cc4593dd95b8c649,1,,Attention bias to negative versus non-negative faces is related to negative affectivity in a transdiagnostic youth sample.,"Harrewijn, A., Abend, R., Naim, R., Haller, S. P., Stavish, C. M., Bajaj, M. A., et al.",2021,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Harrewijn, A., Abend, R., Naim, R., Haller, S. P., Stavish, C. M., Bajaj, M. A., et al."", ""year"": ""2021"", ""title"": ""Attention bias to negative versus non-negative faces is related to negative affectivity in a transdiagnostic youth sample."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Second, to replicate and extend previous findings of Harrewijn et al. [15], we hypothesized that attention bias towards negative-valence face stimuli as measured with eye-tracking would be associated with facets of negative affectivity."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Second, hypotheses that previous findings of Abend et al. [14] would be replicated were not supported, nor were hypotheses that previous findings of Harrewijn et al. [15] would be replicated."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Explicit overall verdict names this original as not replicated. Additional narrow-band associations and cautions about sample size do not override that verdict.""}"
10.1007/s11002-025-09771-9,c7f5a25a2191611b,1,10.1093/jcr/ucy061,Does time of day affect variety-seeking?,"Gullo, K., Berger, J., Etkin, J., & Bollinger, B.",2019,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Gullo, K., Berger, J., Etkin, J., & Bollinger, B."", ""year"": ""2019"", ""title"": ""Does time of day affect variety-seeking?"", ""doi"": ""10.1093/jcr/ucy061"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We propose a replication on the effect of the time of day on variety-seeking behavior, given the results from the two papers were different and there were some limitations of the previous research. For example, Roehm and Roehm (\n2004\n) used small samples of students and one product category (candy bars) that limits generalizability while Gullo et al. (\n2019\n) used households in their first study that could mask individual behavior."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Similar to Gullo et al. (2019), we found variety-seeking increases from the morning to the afternoon. Additionally, we found a post-lunch dip. Our results as such lend further support to the matching mechanism between variety-seeking and diurnal level proposed by Gullo et al. (2019) rather than the compensating mechanism suggested by Roehm and Roehm (2004)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The new UK sample confirms the increasing pattern reported by Gullo and contradicts the decreasing pattern reported by Roehm; the authors explicitly favor the matching mechanism.""}"
