10.1080/23273798.2016.1197954,3eb87368cec5640c,0,10.1037/a0013554,Do chinese readers obtain preview benefit from word n+2? Evidence from eye movements.,"Yang, J., Wang, S., Xu, Y., & Rayner, K.",2009,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Yang, J., Wang, S., Xu, Y., & Rayner, K."", ""doi"": ""10.1037/a0013554"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Do chinese readers obtain preview benefit from word n+2? Evidence from eye movements."", ""year"": ""2009""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""3eb87368cec5640c"", ""location"": ""Experiment 1, Discussion, page 18, lines 439-446; References, page 44, lines 1089-1091"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Experiment 1 was an attempt to replicate Yang et al.’s findings, and the analyses on\nthe regions examined by Yang et al. (i.e., words n, n+1, and n+2) provided a full replication.\nConsistent with their findings, we observed n+2 preview effects of 22ms on word n+2 in gaze\nduration when word n+1 was high-frequency. Yang et al. (2009) found a marginal (p=0.051)\neffect in gaze duration of 22ms, and no effect in other measures. Furthermore, Yang et al.\n(2012) did not find any effect of the word n+2 preview when a low-frequency word n+1 was\nused. Thus, the pattern of effects was replicated in the current experiment, and our results are\nconsistent with Yang et al.’s findings."", ""reason"": ""Experiment 1 explicitly re-tests the positive word n+2 preview benefit reported in Experiment 2 of Yang, Wang, Xu, and Rayner (2009) (doi:10.1037/a0013554) using its 20 high-frequency word n+1 stimuli, analysed as an independent subset alongside the 20 low-frequency word n+1 stimuli from Yang, Rayner, Li, and Wang (2012) (shipped row input_row_index 1497, doi:10.1007/s11145-010-9282-7). Shipped row 1497 represents only the 2012 low-frequency follow-up study; Yang et al. (2009) is a separate original publication with different authors, stimuli, experimental condition, and empirical finding, so report equivalence does not apply."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0028_3eb87368cec5640c"", ""target_index"": 0, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Yang, J., Wang, S., Xu, Y., & Rayner, K."", ""year"": ""2009"", ""title"": ""Do chinese readers obtain preview benefit from word n+2? Evidence from eye movements."", ""doi"": ""10.1037/a0013554"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1"", ""evidence_quote"": ""In addition to investigating whether idioms are processed as single lexical units during reading, we also attempted to replicate the findings of Yang et al. (2009) and Yang et al. (2012) (i.e. word n+2 preview effects are only observed in Chinese text when word n+1 is highly frequent) in the same testing session as our main study."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""This replication was successful, with us observing near identical effects to both Yang et al. studies when restricting our analysis to the same interest areas used in these studies."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors explicitly compare the tested finding with this original.""}"
10.1080/23273798.2016.1197954,3eb87368cec5640c,2,10.1037/xlm0000013,Preview benefit in English spaced compounds.,"Cutter, M. G., Drieghe, D., & Liversedge, S. P.",2014,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Cutter, M. G., Drieghe, D., & Liversedge, S. P."", ""year"": ""2014"", ""title"": ""Preview benefit in English spaced compounds."", ""doi"": ""10.1037/xlm0000013"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""In the current study we investigated whether a similar phenomenon occurs in Chinese reading; namely, that when word n+2 forms a multi-word unit with word n+1, parafoveal processing of word n+2 may occur to a larger extent than previously observed."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""It is worth considering the characteristics of our idioms which may have led to them not being processed as multi-word units in the parafovea, in contrast to the spaced compounds investigated by Cutter et al. (2014)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors explicitly compare the tested finding with this original.""}"
10.1080/23273798.2016.1197954,3eb87368cec5640c,3,,The eyes have it: An eye-movement study into the processing of formulaic sequences.,"Underwood, G., Schmitt, N., & Galpin, A.",2004,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Underwood, G., Schmitt, N., & Galpin, A."", ""year"": ""2004"", ""title"": ""The eyes have it: An eye-movement study into the processing of formulaic sequences."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""First of all, the terminal word of our items received shorter fixations when they were part of an idiom as opposed to part of a phrase. This replicates an effect that has previously been observed in English idioms (see Underwood, Schmitt, & Galpin, 2004) and translated Chinese idioms (see Carrol & Conklin, 2015)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""First of all, the terminal word of our items received shorter fixations when they were part of an idiom as opposed to part of a phrase. This replicates an effect that has previously been observed in English idioms (see Underwood, Schmitt, & Galpin, 2004) and translated Chinese idioms (see Carrol & Conklin, 2015)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors explicitly compare the tested finding with this original.""}"
