or adjudication file existed."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0068_846f605411d9c84d"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""undeterminable""}","{""authors"": ""Balsa, A., López Boo, F., Bloomfield, J., Cristia, A., Cid, A., Ferro, M. P., Valdés, R., & González, M. L."", ""year"": ""2023"", ""title"": ""Effect of crianza positiva e-messaging program on adult-child language interactions."", ""doi"": ""10.1017/bpp.2021.25"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Using an experimental design involving 39 early childhood centers (687 families), we find no effects of the intervention on the outcomes of interest. This finding contrasts with those found in a previous edition of the program in 2018, which showed improvements in parental involvement, in the quality of parent–child interaction, and in adult–child language patterns (Balsa in Child & Youth Care Forum 53:1–22, 2023a; Balsa in Behavioural Public Policy 7: 607–643, 2023c; Bloomfield in Review of the Economics of the Household 21:95–130, 2023)."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Using an experimental design involving 39 early childhood centers (687 families), we find no effects of the intervention on the outcomes of interest. This finding contrasts with those found in a previous edition of the program in 2018, which showed improvements in parental involvement, in the quality of parent–child interaction, and in adult–child language patterns (Balsa in Child & Youth Care Forum 53:1–22, 2023a; Balsa in Behavioural Public Policy 7: 607–643, 2023c; Bloomfield in Review of the Economics of the Household 21:95–130, 2023)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract explicitly contrasts the null intervention results with the improvements reported in this named set of three previous papers. This verdict covers each member of that set.""}"
10.1177/1948550616683017,ff70bf5ad942cddd,1,10.3102/0002831211410683,Implicit social cognitions predict sex differences in math engagement and achievement.,"Nosek, B. A., & Smyth, F. L.",2011,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Nosek, B. A., & Smyth, F. L."", ""year"": ""2011"", ""title"": ""Implicit social cognitions predict sex differences in math engagement and achievement."", ""doi"": ""10.3102/0002831211410683"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Nosek and Smyth (2011) compared the gender-science IAT and the explicit stereotype (measured with two self-reported items: Men are better at math than women are and Women can achieve as much as men in math) in predicting math-related outcomes. We re-analyzed that data and found only one variable, the difference between math and verbal SAT scores, that had reliably stronger relation with the IAT (r = .193, p < .001) than the explicit stereotype (r = .090, p = .004), Williams' t(1040) = 2.590, p = .010."", ""outcome"": """", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": ""not checked"", ""outcome_computational_quote"": ""To refute that possibility, we returned to previous research that found an advantage of the IAT, and tested whether the evidence for this advantage persists even when controlling for self-reported liking."", ""outcome_robustness"": ""robustness challenges"", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": ""Importantly, when we computed partial correlations between each measure and the SAT, partialling out shared variance with attitudes, there was no longer reliable evidence for an IAT advantage: The IAT/SAT relation (r =.049, p = .115) was not significantly better than the explicit stereotype/SAT relation (r = .017, p = .584), Williams' t(1040) < 1, p = .442."", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Analysis directly tests a covariate-adjusted specification; it does not report an audit of recovery of original numerical results. The IAT advantage disappears with attitude adjustment.""}"
10.1016/j.jesp.2015.10.012,2cc1b3af21a3e6fb,3,,,"De Fruyt, Van De Wiele, & Van Heeringen",2000,full_text,,error,obvious,"{""target_index"": 3, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""obvious"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""De Fruyt, Van De Wiele, & Van Heeringen"", ""year"": ""2000"", ""title"": ""Cloninger's Psychobiological Model of Temperament and Character and the Five-Factor Model of Personality"", ""doi"": """", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined""}, ""quote"": ""De Fruyt and colleagues (2000) investigated the relation between Cloninger’s \n\nTemperament and Character Dimensions (Cloninger, 1987) and the Big Five personality index.  \n\nThe researchers found that Cloninger-assessed persistence correlated with the Big Five trait \n\nconscientiousness, r(128) = .46, p < .001, d = 1.04, 95% CI = [.64, 1.43].   \n\nThe current study examined variation in persistence across the semester. To conceptually \n\nreplicate the relation between persistence and conscientiousness, we used the unsolvable \n\nanagram task, which has been used as a measure of persistence (e.g., Aspinwall & Richter, 1999; \n\nSommer & Baumeister, 2002).  In this task, participants are presented with a number of \n\nanagrams to unscramble.  Some anagrams are solvable, others are not.  Participants choose to \n\nstop working on the task whenever they would like.  Persistence is the amount of time spent on \n\nthe task before moving on to the next task.   \n\nUnlike the others, this is not a direct replication."", ""location"": ""Independent full primary original-specific Results/Methods/Discussion; original identity proof where named in integration/candidate_0015.parent.json"", ""reason"": ""ManyLabs3explicitly retests DeFruyt/VanDeWiele/VanHeeringen2000persistence–conscientiousness relationship with new behavioral anagram persistence and TIPIconscientiousness data. Different measures/clinical-versus-student samples make this conceptual, not a missing empirical relationship. Current reference extraction omits the full original title. Independent original publisher and original-author institutional record identify the exact2000three-author130-patientTCI/FFMpaper; the2006four-author personality-disorder prediction paper is a different report and not the cited identity. This named empirical pair is absent from ALL8shippedManyLabs3original identities. Full title is NEW independent parent original-source evidence, not repair of the rejected provider output or blind coding; original DOI not printed in current text is left empty.""}","{""authors"": ""De Fruyt, Van De Wiele, & Van Heeringen"", ""year"": ""2000"", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""De Fruyt and colleagues (2000) investigated the relation between Cloninger’s Temperament and Character Dimensions (Cloninger, 1987) and the Big Five personality index. The researchers found that Cloninger-assessed persistence correlated with the Big Five trait conscientiousness, r(128) = .46, p < .001, d = 1.04, 95% CI = [.64, 1.43]."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Across all replication studies (N = 3,193), there was little evidence for a relationship between conscientiousness measured with the TIPI and persistence measured with the unsolvable anagram task, r(3191) = .027, p = .134, 95% CI = [-0.008, 0.061], d = .05, 95% CI = [-.02, .12]."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""fulltext"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""No De Fruyt entry appears in the supplied reference list; author names and year are supplied by the effect heading, title and DOI unavailable. Behavioral conceptual replication has no reliable predicted correlation.""}"
10.1016/j.jesp.2015.10.012,2cc1b3af21a3e6fb,9,,Moral credentials and the expression of prejudice.,"Monin, B., & Miller, D. T.",2001,full_text,,error,obvious,"{""target_index"": 9, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""obvious"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Monin, B., & Miller, D. T."", ""year"": ""2001"", ""title"": ""Moral credentials and the expression of prejudice."", ""doi"": """", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined""}, ""quote"": ""For the replication design, we included only the “some” and “most” conditions.  In an \n\naggregate analysis of all replication studies (N = 3,134), there was a main effect of moral \n\ncredentials, F(1, 3130) = 17.01, p < .001, ηp² = .005, 95% CI = [.001, .012]) such that \n\nparticipants in the “most” condition (M = 4.44, SD = 0.94) favored a man more than participants \n\nin the “some” condition (M = 4.31, SD = 0.83).  There was a main effect of gender, F(l, 3130) = \n\n48.36, p < .001, but not a Gender × Credentials interaction, F(1, 3130) = 0.0004, p = .985, ηp² = \n\n.000.13  In sum, the replications showed a similar main effect of the predicted credentials \n\nmanipulation on making sexist judgments, but did not replicate the unexpected moderation by \n\ngender.14"", ""location"": ""Independent full primary original-specific Results/Methods/Discussion; original identity proof where named in integration/candidate_0015.parent.json"", ""reason"": ""The primary numbered Moral credentials section explicitly retests Monin/Miller2001Study1with only some/most conditions in a new aggregate sample, reproducing its predicted main effect and assessing the gender interaction. This is an actual empirical original, not a general moral-licensing citation. Its full printed title/authors/year identify a paper absent from all8shipped counterparts. Identity-only decision leaves outcome unknown rather than inferring a whole-study label from its component comparisons.""}","{""authors"": ""Monin, B., & Miller, D. T."", ""year"": ""2001"", ""title"": ""Moral credentials and the expression of prejudice."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""1"", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Monin and Miller (2001) tested whether participants were more willing to express prejudicial attitudes when their prior behavior provided evidence that they were non-prejudiced."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In sum, the replications showed a similar main effect of the predicted credentials manipulation on making sexist judgments, but did not replicate the unexpected moderation by gender.14"", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""fulltext"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": """"}"
10.1016/j.jesp.2015.10.012,2cc1b3af21a3e6fb,10,,Some participants may be better than others: Sustained attention and motivation are higher early in semester.,"Nicholls, M. E., Loveless, K. M., Thomas, N. A., Loetscher, T., & Churches, O.",2015,full_text,,error,obvious,"{""target_index"": 10, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""obvious"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Nicholls, M. E., Loveless, K. M., Thomas, N. A., Loetscher, T., & Churches, O."", ""year"": ""2015"", ""title"": ""Some participants may be better than others: Sustained attention and motivation are higher early in semester."", ""doi"": """", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined""}, ""quote"": ""The present study replicated and \n\nextended prior observations (Nicholls et al., 2014; Witt et al., 2011).  As the semester \n\nprogressed, participants reported slightly less effort and attention, were slightly more likely to \n\nfail an attention check, were slightly less conscientious, had slightly worse mood, had slightly \n\nhigher stress, and had slightly higher representation of men compared to women."", ""location"": ""Independent full primary original-specific Results/Methods/Discussion; original identity proof where named in integration/candidate_0015.parent.json"", ""reason"": ""Primary Discussion explicitly says the current study replicated and extended Nichollsattention/motivation and Wittpersonality/sex time-of-semester findings, then reports its own new-sample effort, attention, attention-check, conscientiousness and sex composition trends. Earlier Methods/Table1and later results specify these original-specific measured relationships; this is not merely generic concern about pool quality or methodology. Each full printed reference is a separate original paper, neither represented among all8shippedManyLabs3counterparts. Nicholls2014in-text/2015reference denotes the single cited paper, not another missing publication. These two actual empirical pairs are essential additional targets; outcome remains unknown in this identity-only decision.""}","{""authors"": ""Nicholls, M. E., Loveless, K. M., Thomas, N. A., Loetscher, T., & Churches, O."", ""year"": ""2015"", ""title"": ""Some participants may be better than others: Sustained attention and motivation are higher early in semester."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The present study replicated and extended prior observations (Nicholls et al., 2014; Witt et al., 2011). As the semester progressed, participants reported slightly less effort and attention, were slightly more likely to fail an attention check, were slightly less conscientious, had slightly worse mood, had slightly higher stress, and had slightly higher representation of men compared to women."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The present study replicated and extended prior observations (Nicholls et al., 2014; Witt et al., 2011). As the semester progressed, participants reported slightly less effort and attention, were slightly more likely to fail an attention check, were slightly less conscientious, had slightly worse mood, had slightly higher stress, and had slightly higher representation of men compared to women."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly identify these prior observations as replicated; Nicholls is dated 2014 in text and 2015 in the reference list.""}"
