10.1016/j.jesp.2020.103977,59d194699c6721bf,1,10.1038/scientificamerican0182-160,The psychology of preferences.,"Kahneman, D., & Tversky, A.",1982,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""The classic action-effect (Kahneman & Tversky, 1982) findings were that actions leading to a negative outcome are regretted more than inactions leading to the same negative outcomes. We added a regret measure to examine whether the action-effect findings would extend to situations of morality involving intended harmful behavior."", ""location"": ""discussion"", ""reason"": ""Kahneman and Tversky (1982) is cited as background literature on the action-effect to motivate adding a novel regret extension measure to the moral scenarios from Spranca et al. (1991), rather than as an original study directly re-tested by this replication.""}","{""authors"": ""Kahneman, D., & Tversky, A."", ""year"": ""1982"", ""title"": ""The psychology of preferences."", ""doi"": ""10.1038/scientificamerican0182-160"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The classic action-effect (Kahneman & Tversky, 1982) findings were that actions leading to a negative outcome are regretted more than inactions leading to the same negative outcomes. We added a regret measure to examine whether the action-effect findings would extend to situations of morality involving intended harmful behavior. Our findings were opposite to the expected action-effect omission-commission asymmetry with participants rating omissions as more regretted than commissions (d = 0.18 to 0.26)."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The classic action-effect (Kahneman & Tversky, 1982) findings were that actions leading to a negative outcome are regretted more than inactions leading to the same negative outcomes. We added a regret measure to examine whether the action-effect findings would extend to situations of morality involving intended harmful behavior. Our findings were opposite to the expected action-effect omission-commission asymmetry with participants rating omissions as more regretted than commissions (d = 0.18 to 0.26)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The added regret measure explicitly tests generalization of this published action-effect finding to moral scenarios and finds the opposite pattern.""}"
10.1037/xap0000366,257c330f3972ab87,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,"{""corrected_value"": null, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""257c330f3972ab87"", ""location"": ""Abstract (line 6)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Reassigning responsibility is the most prominent and best-replicated intervention against escalating commitment (i.e., the failure to withdraw from losing courses of action). This intervention is considered effective because it reduces reinvestments after negative feedback in decision scenarios with a single reinvestment decision. However, we argue that any intervention against escalating commitment should fulfill two additional criteria. The first is temporal stability, that is, the beneficial effects of the intervention need to persist beyond a single reinvestment decision. The second is specific effectiveness, that is, the intervention should reduce commitment only if the project continues to fail after an initial setback (structural failure) but not if it recovers and is ultimately profitable (temporary failure). To subject reassignment of responsibility to this critical test of effectiveness, we introduce a modification of the escalation paradigm that allows testing for temporal stability and differentiates between structural and temporary failure."", ""reason"": ""The available source text (10.1037/xap0000366, Schultze & Schulz-Hardt, 2022) consists only of the title, authors, DOI, and abstract. The abstract states that the paper empirically retests the intervention of reassigning responsibility against escalating commitment across two experiments using a modified escalation paradigm. However, the abstract contains no citations, author names, publication years, titles, or DOIs for the original study or studies establishing the responsibility-reassignment intervention or escalation paradigm. Consequently, primary source evidence is insufficient to establish whether the retested original is already represented by shipped row 1509 (Staw, 1976, 'Knee-deep in the big muddy: a study of escalating commitment to a chosen course of action', DOI 10.1016/0030-5073(76)90005-2) or whether an unshipped original paper is being retested."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0012_257c330f3972ab87"", ""target_index"": 0, ""verdict"": ""undeterminable""}","{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""To subject reassignment of responsibility to this critical test of effectiveness, we introduce a modification of the escalation paradigm that allows testing for temporal stability and differentiates between structural and temporary failure."", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": """", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Abstract reports challenges to temporal stability and specificity, but does not identify any original paper or assign comparisons to particular originals. Cannot attribute an outcome to an identifiable target. One blank entry denotes an unidentified target relationship, not a verified count of originals.""}"
10.1097/00041444-199322000-00006,2574f4c639b89397,0,,,,,abstract,,correct,argon,"{""corrected_value"": null, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""2574f4c639b89397"", ""location"": ""abstract"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""An association between a polymorphic ApaLI site within the porphobilinogen deaminase (PBGD) gene and schizophrenia has been reported recently. In order to replicate this finding we examined the ApaLI RFLP, which is located in intron 1 of the PBGD gene, in 93 unrelated patients who met the DSM-III-R criteria for schizophrenia versus 74 unrelated controls. There were no significant differences between the groups in allele frequencies or genotype counts."", ""reason"": ""Prior adjudication and state files are absent. The blind target has empty bibliographic fields solely because the supplied source text for Nöthen et al. (1993) is abstract-only and describes the recently reported association between the polymorphic ApaLI site in the porphobilinogen deaminase (PBGD) gene and schizophrenia without printing a citation. That single retested original finding is already represented by shipped row 1510 (Sanders et al., January 1993, Schizophrenia Research, 'Association between genetic variation at the porphobilinogen deaminase gene and schizophrenia', 10.1016/0920-9964(93)90019-f)."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0012_2574f4c639b89397"", ""target_index"": 0, ""verdict"": ""shipped_right""}","{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""An association between a polymorphic ApaLI site within the porphobilinogen deaminase (PBGD) gene and schizophrenia has been reported recently. In order to replicate this finding we examined the ApaLI RFLP, which is located in intron 1 of the PBGD gene, in 93 unrelated patients who met the DSM-III-R criteria for schizophrenia versus 74 unrelated controls."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""An association between a polymorphic ApaLI site within the porphobilinogen deaminase (PBGD) gene and schizophrenia has been reported recently. In order to replicate this finding we examined the ApaLI RFLP, which is located in intron 1 of the PBGD gene, in 93 unrelated patients who met the DSM-III-R criteria for schizophrenia versus 74 unrelated controls. There were no significant differences between the groups in allele frequencies or genotype counts."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Original reported association; new case-control comparison finds no significant allele/genotype differences. Abstract does not identify the prior paper, so citation fields remain empty.""}"
10.1080/20445911.2020.1805453,ed701519c48a420b,1,10.1080/20445911.2019.1659801,Anticipation-speciﬁc reliability and trial-to-trial carryover of anticipatory attentional bias for threat.,"Gladwin, T. E., Figner, B., & Vink, M.",2019,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Gladwin, T. E., Figner, B., & Vink, M."", ""year"": ""2019"", ""title"": ""Anticipation-speciﬁc reliability and trial-to-trial carryover of anticipatory attentional bias for threat."", ""doi"": ""10.1080/20445911.2019.1659801"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""The predicted within-subject eﬀects were found.\nFor Colour cues, attentional bias was drawn\ntowards the colour cue on which the target was presented in the previous trial. This replicated the previous ﬁnding on colour cues and carryover\n(Gladwin & Figner, 2019). Carryover was also found\nfor negative facial expressions, as in previous\nﬁndings for threatening stimuli (Gladwin et al.,\n2019; Gladwin & Figner, 2019)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The predicted within-subject eﬀects were found.\nFor Colour cues, attentional bias was drawn\ntowards the colour cue on which the target was presented in the previous trial. This replicated the previous ﬁnding on colour cues and carryover\n(Gladwin & Figner, 2019). Carryover was also found\nfor negative facial expressions, as in previous\nﬁndings for threatening stimuli (Gladwin et al.,\n2019; Gladwin & Figner, 2019)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Both cited findings are explicitly compared as confirmed by new samples and stimulus sets. Low individual-difference reliability does not invalidate the within-subject carryover finding; Study 2 also reports its replication.""}"
10.1016/j.eatbeh.2024.101899,f0eb6d5ebd63474b,0,,Negative emotional eating patterns among American university students: A replication study.,"Dixit U, He J, Whited M, Ellis JM, Zickgraf HF.",2023,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Dixit U, He J, Whited M, Ellis JM, Zickgraf HF."", ""year"": ""2023"", ""title"": ""Negative emotional eating patterns among American university students: A replication study."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Thus, our study filled these gaps by replicating and expanding on prior studies (Dixit et al., 2023; He et al., 2020) in a large sample of Chinese general adults."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In conclusion, this study replicated and extended prior research on the four patterns of negative emotional eating in American and Chinese university students (Dixit et al., 2023; He et al., 2020) with a Chinese sample from the general adult population."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Overall conclusion names both prior works as replicated.""}"
10.1111/psyp.12452,dda8e3b0fa809f21,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Replicating previous findings, conditional stimulus valence was not affected by instructed extinction."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Replicating previous findings, conditional stimulus valence was not affected by instructed extinction."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly say the absence of an instructed-extinction effect on stimulus valence replicated previous findings. This supports the unnamed target finding; it does not identify which cited paper supplied it.""}"
10.1177/01461672211052120,d6fc9c6efb321353,1,10.17759/exppsy.2017100102,The effects of alcohol on heart rate and evaluation of actions in moral dilemma.,"Arutyunova, K. R., Bakhchina, A. V., Krylov, A. K., & Alexandrov Yu, I.",2017,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Arutyunova, K. R., Bakhchina, A. V., Krylov, A. K., & Alexandrov Yu, I."", ""year"": ""2017"", ""title"": ""The effects of alcohol on heart rate and evaluation of actions in moral dilemma."", ""doi"": ""10.17759/exppsy.2017100102"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Subsequent laboratory experiments (Arutyunova et al., 2017; Francis et al., 2019) aimed to address this concern, and these studies did not find any significant effects of alcohol on moral judgments. However, the consumed doses of alcohol were relatively small in these experiments."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Despite these improvements, we failed to obtain any significant effect of alcohol on moral judgments."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The paper retests the prior experimental null effect with a higher dose and larger sample; its null result agrees.""}"
10.1177/01461672211052120,d6fc9c6efb321353,2,10.1007/s00213-019-05314-z,"Alcohol, empathy, and morality: Acute effects of alcohol consumption on affective empathy and moral decision-making.","Francis, K. B., Gummerum, M., Ganis, G., Howard, I. S., & Terbeck, S.",2019,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Francis, K. B., Gummerum, M., Ganis, G., Howard, I. S., & Terbeck, S."", ""year"": ""2019"", ""title"": ""Alcohol, empathy, and morality: Acute effects of alcohol consumption on affective empathy and moral decision-making."", ""doi"": ""10.1007/s00213-019-05314-z"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Subsequent laboratory experiments (Arutyunova et al., 2017; Francis et al., 2019) aimed to address this concern, and these studies did not find any significant effects of alcohol on moral judgments. However, the consumed doses of alcohol were relatively small in these experiments."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Despite these improvements, we failed to obtain any significant effect of alcohol on moral judgments."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The paper retests the prior experimental null effect with a higher dose and larger sample; its null result agrees.""}"
10.1016/j.physbeh.2010.12.015,6ed3253ec5d2e142,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,"{""corrected_value"": null, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""6ed3253ec5d2e142"", ""location"": ""Abstract"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""We have previously observed increased basal acoustic startle response (ASR) in Wistar rats exposed to stress or dexamethasone (DEX) in utero when tested during the light phase of the circadian rhythm, and decreased prepulse inhibition (PPI) in similar animals tested during the dark phase of the cycle. We speculated that this observation of increased basal startle might be influenced by diurnal phase. In the present study, adult female Sprague Dawley rats, stressed prenatally with DEX (200 μg/kg, gestational days 14-21) and postnatally by blood sampling under restraint, were tested for the ASR during both circadian phases (light and dark). Basal startle was increased in animals tested both during the light and the dark phases of the cycle. We hereby replicated our earlier findings in a new strain and laboratory, thus strengthening the validity of our model regarding prenatal stress effects on ASR in female offspring."", ""reason"": ""No prior adjudication or prior state file exists for this work. The supplied primary source text (6ed3253ec5d2e142.txt) contains only the title and abstract, without full text or a reference list. The abstract explicitly states that the authors replicated their earlier findings of increased basal acoustic startle response (ASR) in rats exposed prenatally to stress or dexamethasone (DEX) in a new strain and laboratory. Although shipped row input_row_index 1539 (Hougaard, Andersen, Kjær, Hansen, Werge, & Lund, 2005, 10.1016/j.devbrainres.2005.06.014, 'Prenatal stress may increase vulnerability to life events: Comparison with the effects of prenatal dexamethasone') is an earlier paper by overlapping authors (Hougaard, Kjær) comparing prenatal stress and prenatal DEX in rats, the abstract alone does not name citations and cannot resolve whether the unnamed target refers solely to shipped row 1539 or also includes an additional distinct original paper."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0054_6ed3253ec5d2e142"", ""target_index"": 0, ""verdict"": ""undeterminable""}","{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We have previously observed increased basal acoustic startle response (ASR) in Wistar rats exposed to stress or dexamethasone (DEX) in utero when tested during the light phase of the circadian rhythm, and decreased prepulse inhibition (PPI) in similar animals tested during the dark phase of the cycle."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We hereby replicated our earlier findings in a new strain and laboratory, thus strengthening the validity of our model regarding prenatal stress effects on ASR in female offspring."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Explicit overall replication verdict. Original bibliographic identity and number of original papers cannot be resolved from abstract; unnamed target represents the earlier basal-startle finding, not an inferred reference.""}"
10.1002/acp.4202,7739d78c26609886,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,"{""corrected_value"": null, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""7739d78c26609886"", ""location"": ""Abstract (line 5)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""To test these predictions, we experimentally manipulated misinformation availability (high vs. low) and presented double or single misinformation. However, double versus single misinformation presentation did not affect performance or interact with misinformation availability and participants frequently reported misinformation while fully detecting discrepancies between items."", ""reason"": ""No prior adjudication or state file exists for this work. The supplied primary source file (7739d78c26609886.txt) contains only the article title ('Double misinformation and eyewitness performance: An experimental replication' by Ewa Smołka and Hartmut Blank) and abstract; the introduction, methods, results, discussion, and reference list are not present. Although the title and abstract establish an empirical replication manipulating misinformation availability (high vs. low) and double vs. single misinformation presentation, the abstract never names or cites the prior study being retested, which is why the blind target has empty citation fields. Shipped row 1547 (Blank, Panday, Edwards, Skopicz-Radkiewicz, Gibson, & Reddy, 2022, 'Double misinformation: Effects on eyewitness remembering.', DOI 10.1016/j.jarmac.2021.08.001) shares authorship and topic and is very likely the original study being replicated, but the unresolved question is that the abstract-only source text does not provide the citation needed to verify whether target_index 0 is already represented by shipped row 1547 or retests a distinct absent original. (Note: Luna's duplicate_quote failed mechanical validation because it quoted the shipped row title, which is not present in the source text.)"", ""request_id"": ""luna_0058_7739d78c26609886"", ""target_index"": 0, ""verdict"": ""undeterminable""}","{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": """", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": """", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract reports null effects but neither identifies the prior finding nor states what that original found. Cannot compare results to an unidentified original; title alone establishes a replication attempt.""}"
10.1016/j.jenvp.2024.102289,d474c9df976cbc31,1,,"When worry about climate change leads to climate action: How values, worry and personal responsibility relate to various climate actions","Bouman, T., et al.",2020,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Bouman, T., et al."", ""year"": ""2020"", ""title"": ""When worry about climate change leads to climate action: How values, worry and personal responsibility relate to various climate actions"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The current study replicates and extends recent work (Bouman et al., 2020; Syropoulos & Markowitz, 2022) which found a meaningful and robust relationship between people's feelings of responsibility to reduce climate change, their worry about climate change, and their belief that climate change is caused by humans."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The current study replicates and extends recent work (Bouman et al., 2020; Syropoulos & Markowitz, 2022) which found a meaningful and robust relationship between people's feelings of responsibility to reduce climate change, their worry about climate change, and their belief that climate change is caused by humans. Importantly, these findings were consistently observed across 31 different European countries, and when controlling for crucial socio-demographic variables."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The publisher discussion preview explicitly places both named originals inside the replicated-and-extended set and states agreement on the relationship. This is independent evidence of a retested target rather than mere topical background.""}"
10.1027/1864-9335/a000178,9fae3aaf30064d5a,0,,Instructional manipulation checks: Detecting satisficing to increase statistical power.,"Oppenheimer, D. M., Meyvis, T., & Davidenko, N.",2009,full_text,same_author_year_siblings,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 0, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""type"": ""replication"", ""doi"": """", ""title"": ""Instructional manipulation checks: Detecting satisficing to increase statistical power."", ""authors"": ""Oppenheimer, D. M., Meyvis, T., & Davidenko, N."", ""year"": ""2009"", ""outcome"": ""successful""}, ""quote"": ""Oppenheimer et al. \n\n(2009; adapted from Thaler, 1985) asked participants to imagine that they have tickets to see \n\ntheir favorite football team play an important game, but that it is freezing cold on the day of \n\nthe game. Participants rated their likelihood of attending the game on a 9-point scale (1 = \n\ndefinitely stay at home, 9 = definitely go to the game). Participants were marginally more \n\nlikely to go to the game if they had paid for the ticket than if the ticket had been free."", ""location"": {""start_line"": 319, ""end_line"": 329, ""form_feed_page"": 7}, ""reason"": ""The paper directly re-tests the sunk cost scenario from Oppenheimer, Meyvis, and Davidenko (2009) as Effect 1 of its thirteen replicated effects and reports a successful replication (t = 10.83, p < .001), making this an omitted target."", ""quote_provenance"": {""kind"": ""new_independent_literal_primary_quotation"", ""inspection_result"": ""evidence_followup/integration/strict_literal_quote_inspection.result.json"", ""issue_index"": 170, ""source_path"": ""/home/lukas/.t3/worktrees/fred-data/audit-flora-blind-2026-10/audit/flora_blind_2026-10/texts/9fae3aaf30064d5a.txt"", ""source_sha256"": ""fc6d1d849b629280168577a39afce762d9d9337802e70fb325a21db68b250dfe"", ""raw_start_offset"": 15534, ""raw_end_offset"": 16026, ""scientific_verdict_unchanged"": true, ""reason"": ""Parent verified exact raw slice and semantic identity with the earlier accepted passage under its recorded display-normalisation policy. Independent primary-context inspector supported this passage. Original provider wording and whole prior decision retained in history; raw provider reply unchanged.""}}","{""authors"": ""Oppenheimer, D. M., Meyvis, T., & Davidenko, N."", ""year"": ""2009"", ""title"": ""Instructional manipulation checks: Detecting satisficing to increase statistical power."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Sunk costs (Oppenheimer, Meyvis, & Davidenko, 2009). Sunk costs are those that have already been incurred and cannot be recovered (Knox & Inkster, 1968). Oppenheimer et al. (2009; adapted from Thaler, 1985) asked participants to imagine that they have tickets to see their favorite football team play an important game, but that it is freezing cold on the day of the game. Participants rated their likelihood of attending the game on a 9-point scale (1 = definitely stay at home, 9 = definitely go to the game). Participants were marginally more likely to go to the game if they had paid for the ticket than if the ticket had been free."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""A large scale replication with 36 samples successfully replicated eleven of thirteen classic and contemporary effects in psychological science, some of which are well-known to be robust, and others that have been replicated infrequently or not at all. The original studies produced underestimates of some effects (e.g., anchoring-and-adjustment and allowed versus forbidden message framing), and overestimates of other effects (e.g., imagined contact producing willingness to interact with outgroups in the future). Two effects – flag priming influencing conservatism and currency priming influencing system justification – did not replicate."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The discussion gives an overall verdict of eleven successful effects and identifies the remaining two failures. This covers the named set. ""}"
10.1027/1864-9335/a000178,9fae3aaf30064d5a,5,,The current status of American public opinion.,"Hyman, H. H., & Sheatsley, P. B.",1950,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 5, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""type"": ""replication"", ""doi"": """", ""title"": ""The current status of American public opinion."", ""authors"": ""Hyman, H. H., & Sheatsley, P. B."", ""year"": ""1950"", ""outcome"": ""successful""}, ""quote"": ""In the original study, American participants answered two questions: \n\nwhether communist countries should allow American reporters in and allow them to report \n\nthe news back to American papers and whether America should allow communist reporters \n\ninto the United States and allow them to report back to their papers. Participants reported \n\nmore support for allowing communist reporters into America when that question was asked \n\nafter the question about allowing American reporters into the communist countries."", ""location"": {""start_line"": 427, ""end_line"": 437, ""form_feed_page"": 9}, ""reason"": ""The paper re-tests the norm of reciprocity effect from Hyman and Sheatsley (1950) as Effect 6 of the thirteen effects and successfully replicates it (χ2 = 135.3, p < .001), making this an omitted target."", ""quote_provenance"": {""kind"": ""new_independent_literal_primary_quotation"", ""inspection_result"": ""evidence_followup/integration/strict_literal_quote_inspection.result.json"", ""issue_index"": 171, ""source_path"": ""/home/lukas/.t3/worktrees/fred-data/audit-flora-blind-2026-10/audit/flora_blind_2026-10/texts/9fae3aaf30064d5a.txt"", ""source_sha256"": ""fc6d1d849b629280168577a39afce762d9d9337802e70fb325a21db68b250dfe"", ""raw_start_offset"": 20070, ""raw_end_offset"": 20585, ""scientific_verdict_unchanged"": true, ""reason"": ""Parent verified exact raw slice and semantic identity with the earlier accepted passage under its recorded display-normalisation policy. Independent primary-context inspector supported this passage. Original provider wording and whole prior decision retained in history; raw provider reply unchanged.""}}","{""authors"": ""Hyman, H. H., & Sheatsley, P. B."", ""year"": ""1950"", ""title"": ""The current status of American public opinion."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Norm of reciprocity (Hyman & Sheatsley, 1950). When confronted with a decision about allowing or denying the same behavior to an ingroup and outgroup, people may feel an obligation to reciprocity, or consistency in their evaluation of the behaviors (Hyman & Sheatsley, 1950). In the original study, American participants answered two questions: whether communist countries should allow American reporters in and allow them to report the news back to American papers and whether America should allow communist reporters into the United States and allow them to report back to their papers. Participants reported more support for allowing communist reporters into America when that question was asked after the question about allowing American reporters into the communist countries. In the replication, we changed the question slightly to ensure the “other country” was a suitable, modern target (North Korea). For international replication, the target country was determined by the researcher heading that replication to ensure suitability (see supplementary materials)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""A large scale replication with 36 samples successfully replicated eleven of thirteen classic and contemporary effects in psychological science, some of which are well-known to be robust, and others that have been replicated infrequently or not at all. The original studies produced underestimates of some effects (e.g., anchoring-and-adjustment and allowed versus forbidden message framing), and overestimates of other effects (e.g., imagined contact producing willingness to interact with outgroups in the future). Two effects – flag priming influencing conservatism and currency priming influencing system justification – did not replicate."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The discussion gives an overall verdict of eleven successful effects and identifies the remaining two failures. This covers the named set. ""}"
10.1027/1864-9335/a000178,9fae3aaf30064d5a,6,,Experiments in wording questions: II.,"Rugg, D.",1941,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 6, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""type"": ""replication"", ""doi"": """", ""title"": ""Experiments in wording questions: II."", ""authors"": ""Rugg, D."", ""year"": ""1941"", ""outcome"": ""successful""}, ""quote"": ""Rugg (1941) \n\nfound that respondents were less likely to endorse forbidding speeches against democracy \n\nthan they were to not endorse allowing speeches against democracy. Respondents in the \n\nUnited States were asked, in one condition, if the U.S. should allow speeches against \n\ndemocracy or, in another condition, whether the U.S. should forbid speeches against \n\ndemocracy. 62 percent of participants indicated “No” when asked if speeches against \n\ndemocracy should be allowed, but only 46 percent indicated “Yes” when asked if these \n\nspeeches should be forbidden. In the replication, the words “The United States” were \n\nreplaced with the name of the country the study was administered in."", ""location"": {""start_line"": 450, ""end_line"": 466, ""form_feed_page"": 10}, ""reason"": ""The paper re-tests Rugg (1941) on allowed versus forbidden question wording as Effect 7 of the thirteen effects and explicitly notes its successful replication (χ2 = 3088.7, p < .001), making this an omitted target."", ""quote_provenance"": {""kind"": ""new_independent_literal_primary_quotation"", ""inspection_result"": ""evidence_followup/integration/strict_literal_quote_inspection.result.json"", ""issue_index"": 172, ""source_path"": ""/home/lukas/.t3/worktrees/fred-data/audit-flora-blind-2026-10/audit/flora_blind_2026-10/texts/9fae3aaf30064d5a.txt"", ""source_sha256"": ""fc6d1d849b629280168577a39afce762d9d9337802e70fb325a21db68b250dfe"", ""raw_start_offset"": 20980, ""raw_end_offset"": 21675, ""scientific_verdict_unchanged"": true, ""reason"": ""Parent verified exact raw slice and semantic identity with the earlier accepted passage under its recorded display-normalisation policy. Independent primary-context inspector supported this passage. Original provider wording and whole prior decision retained in history; raw provider reply unchanged.""}}","{""authors"": ""Rugg, D."", ""year"": ""1941"", ""title"": ""Experiments in wording questions: II."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Allowed/Forbidden (Rugg, 1941). Question phrasing can influence responses. Rugg (1941) found that respondents were less likely to endorse forbidding speeches against democracy than they were to not endorse allowing speeches against democracy. Respondents in the United States were asked, in one condition, if the U.S. should allow speeches against democracy or, in another condition, whether the U.S. should forbid speeches against democracy. 62 percent of participants indicated “No” when asked if speeches against democracy should be allowed, but only 46 percent indicated “Yes” when asked if these speeches should be forbidden. In the replication, the words “The United States” were replaced with the name of the country the study was administered in."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""A large scale replication with 36 samples successfully replicated eleven of thirteen classic and contemporary effects in psychological science, some of which are well-known to be robust, and others that have been replicated infrequently or not at all. The original studies produced underestimates of some effects (e.g., anchoring-and-adjustment and allowed versus forbidden message framing), and overestimates of other effects (e.g., imagined contact producing willingness to interact with outgroups in the future). Two effects – flag priming influencing conservatism and currency priming influencing system justification – did not replicate."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The discussion gives an overall verdict of eleven successful effects and identifies the remaining two failures. This covers the named set. ""}"
10.1027/1864-9335/a000178,9fae3aaf30064d5a,7,,"Prestige, suggestion, and attitudes.","Lorge, I., & Curtis, C. C.",1936,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 7, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""type"": ""replication"", ""doi"": """", ""title"": ""Prestige, suggestion, and attitudes."", ""authors"": ""Lorge, I., & Curtis, C. C."", ""year"": ""1936"", ""outcome"": ""successful""}, ""quote"": ""Lorge and Curtis (1936) examined how an \n\nidentical quote would be perceived if it was attributed to a liked or disliked individual. \n\nParticipants were asked to rate their agreement with a list of quotations. The quotation of \n\ninterest was, “I hold it that a little rebellion, now and then, is a good thing, and as necessary \n\nin the political world as storms are in the physical world.” In one condition the quote was \n\nattributed to Thomas Jefferson, a liked individual, and in the other it was attributed to \n\nVladimir Lenin, a disliked individual. More agreement was observed when the quote was \n\nattributed to Jefferson than Lenin (reported in Moskowitz, 2004)."", ""location"": ""Primary original-specific empirical retest, raw chars[21823,22491); Methods/Results/Discussion/References and ALLshipped counterparts checked"", ""reason"": ""The paper re-tests the quote attribution effect of Lorge and Curtis (1936) as Effect 8 of the thirteen effects and successfully replicates it (t = 12.79, p < .001), making this an omitted target.""}","{""authors"": ""Lorge, I., & Curtis, C. C."", ""year"": ""1936"", ""title"": ""Prestige, suggestion, and attitudes."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Quote Attribution (Lorge & Curtis, 1936). The source of information has a great impact on how that information is perceived and evaluated. Lorge and Curtis (1936) examined how an identical quote would be perceived if it was attributed to a liked or disliked individual. Participants were asked to rate their agreement with a list of quotations. The quotation of interest was, “I hold it that a little rebellion, now and then, is a good thing, and as necessary in the political world as storms are in the physical world.” In one condition the quote was attributed to Thomas Jefferson, a liked individual, and in the other it was attributed to Vladimir Lenin, a disliked individual. More agreement was observed when the quote was attributed to Jefferson than Lenin (reported in Moskowitz, 2004). In the replication, we used a quote attributed to either George Washington (liked individual) or Osama Bin Laden (disliked individual)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""A large scale replication with 36 samples successfully replicated eleven of thirteen classic and contemporary effects in psychological science, some of which are well-known to be robust, and others that have been replicated infrequently or not at all. The original studies produced underestimates of some effects (e.g., anchoring-and-adjustment and allowed versus forbidden message framing), and overestimates of other effects (e.g., imagined contact producing willingness to interact with outgroups in the future). Two effects – flag priming influencing conservatism and currency priming influencing system justification – did not replicate."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The discussion gives an overall verdict of eleven successful effects and identifies the remaining two failures. This covers the named set. ""}"
10.1016/j.jsp.2024.101307,cf3cdf98cc2a333b,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The purpose of this research was to conduct a practice-based replication of Academic and Behavior Combined Support (ABC Support), a previously developed and experimentally evaluated supplemental intervention that merges a combined focus on reading fluency and academic engagement."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In concert with prior empirical findings on ABC Support, analyses revealed improvement from baseline to intervention for both reading and behavior outcomes, as well as from baseline to follow-up assessments."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly compare the new improvements with prior ABC Support findings as being in concert. The abstract does not identify the original bibliographic entry.""}"
10.1080/19345747.2024.2333734,865e547d45f851f6,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,"{""corrected_value"": null, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""865e547d45f851f6"", ""location"": ""Abstract"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""The On Track program, previously found to be effective in a researcher-driven and tightly controlled setting, was implemented in a larger sample of schools. To investigate the robustness of the original findings, specific aspects of the implementation were deliberately altered from the original study."", ""reason"": ""Prior adjudication and state files are absent (never previously adjudicated). The supplied source text is an abstract-only record (with no in-text citation or reference list) that explicitly reports a conceptual replication of a single prior study evaluating the 'On Track' early reading intervention in a researcher-driven setting and compares its effect sizes to that original try-out. The blind coder recorded this single target with empty bibliographic fields because the abstract omits the citation. Shipped row 1566 (Solheim, Frijters, Lundetrae, & Uppstad, 2018, 'Effectiveness of an early reading intervention in a semi-transparent orthography: A group randomised controlled trial', Learning and Instruction, doi:10.1016/j.learninstruc.2018.05.004) shares three authors (Solheim, Lundetrae, Uppstad) with the 2024 replication paper and tests an early reading intervention in a group randomized controlled trial, making it a strong candidate for the unnamed original try-out. However, neither the supplied abstract nor the Crossref metadata for 10.1016/j.learninstruc.2018.05.004 explicitly names the 'On Track' program or provides the replication's bibliography. The precise unresolved question is whether the unnamed original 'On Track' try-out study retested in 10.1080/19345747.2024.2333734 is identical to shipped row 1566 (Solheim et al., 2018) or refers to a different original report."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0069_865e547d45f851f6"", ""target_index"": 0, ""verdict"": ""undeterminable""}","{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The On Track program, previously found to be effective in a researcher-driven and tightly controlled setting, was implemented in a larger sample of schools. To investigate the robustness of the original findings, specific aspects of the implementation were deliberately altered from the original study."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Results indicated that the intervention significantly reduced the number of at-risk students by the end of first grade. However, effect sizes in the replication study were reduced compared to the original try-out."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Abstract states original program effective and corresponding beneficial intervention result, explicitly compares reduced sizes; reduced magnitude alone is not mixed. Reference citation is not in the abstract.""}"
10.24839/2325-7342.jn26.4.422,4b693b1501136919,1,10.1027/1864-1105/a000036,"“I really liked that movie”: Testing the relationship between trait empathy, transportation, perceived realism, and movie enjoyment.","Hall, A. E., & Bracken, C. C.",2011,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Hall, A. E., & Bracken, C. C."", ""doi"": ""10.1027/1864-1105/a000036"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""“I really liked that movie”: Testing the relationship between trait empathy, transportation, perceived realism, and movie enjoyment."", ""year"": ""2011""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""4b693b1501136919"", ""location"": ""Discussion (lines 659-665); see also Introduction (lines 143-148), The Present Study (lines 209-210), Results (lines 389-413), and References (lines 872-875)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""The correlation detected between trait empathy and narrative transportation supports and\nextends prior literature that has demonstrated\nsimilar results with films (Hall & Bracken, 2011;\nTaylor, 2015), as well as studies that have examined\nthe relationship between some components of trait\nempathy and narrative transportation into written\npieces (Bal & Veltkamp, 2013; Johnson, 2012)."", ""reason"": ""All three criteria for a genuine missing target are met: (1) the paper identifies the specific empirical finding of Hall and Bracken (2011) showing a positive correlation between multidimensional trait empathy and narrative transportation (lines 143-148), formulates Hypothesis 1 explicitly to test that correlation ('The first hypothesis was that trait empathy would be positively correlated with narrative transportation (Hall & Bracken, 2011; Taylor, 2015)', lines 209-210), empirically re-tests it in a new sample using the IRI and Narrative Transportation Scale in Results (r = .39, p < .001, lines 389-413), and in Discussion characterizes testing this relationship as its 'primary aim' (lines 640-642) and directly compares the observed correlation against Hall and Bracken (2011) (lines 659-662); (2) the reference list (lines 872-875) and verified Crossref metadata confirm the exact work: Hall, A. E., & Bracken, C. C. (2011), Journal of Media Psychology, 23(2), 90-99, DOI 10.1027/1864-1105/a000036; and (3) the sole shipped row (input_row_index '1577', doi_o '10.1016/j.poetic.2015.07.001') represents only Black and Barnes (2015a) (sibling blind target_index 0), an independent work testing genre differences on transportation. Prior adjudication and state files (7c6931f7...json and 44a1147d...json) are absent for this never-adjudicated work."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0036_4b693b1501136919"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Hall, A. E., & Bracken, C. C."", ""year"": ""2011"", ""title"": ""“I really liked that movie”: Testing the relationship between trait empathy, transportation, perceived realism, and movie enjoyment."", ""doi"": ""10.1027/1864-1105/a000036"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The first hypothesis was that trait empathy would be positively correlated with narrative transportation (Hall & Bracken, 2011; Taylor, 2015)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The correlation detected between trait empathy and narrative transportation supports and extends prior literature that has demonstrated similar results with films (Hall & Bracken, 2011; Taylor, 2015), as well as studies that have examined the relationship between some components of trait empathy and narrative transportation into written pieces (Bal & Veltkamp, 2013; Johnson, 2012)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Explicit discussion comparison settles this published finding.""}"
