that describing someone as a voter (noun) instead of one who votes (verb) increases turnout rates 11 to 14 points because the noun activates a person's social identity as a voter."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We find no evidence that this minor wording change increases turnout levels."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Abstract states the original positive noun-versus-verb turnout finding and this replication finds no evidence of the increase.""}"
10.1080/02699931.2020.1863187,67d37622e14f4554,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,"{""corrected_value"": null, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""67d37622e14f4554"", ""location"": ""Abstract"", ""method"": ""obvious"", ""quote"": ""Discrete emotions may affect memory above and beyond such dimensions, with recent research indicating that disgusting information is better remembered than frightening information. We initially sought to determine whether participants are sensitive to the effects of discrete emotions when predicting their future memory performance. Participants in Experiment 1 were more confident in their memory for emotional (both frightening and disgusting) images relative to neutral images, but confidence did not differ between frightening and disgusting images. However, because we did not replicate the mnemonic advantage of disgust, subsequent experiments were concerned with testing the replicability of this effect."", ""reason"": ""Parent independently read the complete available current abstract and preserved acquisition history. Three actual experiments retest disgust-versus-fear recall, but no original author, title, year or reference list is printed. The sole shipped Chapman2017 paper fits the topic; a shared generic finding does not independently establish that this is its exact original or exclude another disgust-memory report. Publisher access failed and the linked OSF contains data rather than the article. Retain actual original-identity access limit; do not infer a genuine omission or certify represented identity from the shipped entry alone."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0051_67d37622e14f4554"", ""target_index"": 0, ""verdict"": ""undeterminable""}","{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2, 3"", ""evidence_quote"": ""However, because we did not replicate the mnemonic advantage of disgust, subsequent experiments were concerned with testing the replicability of this effect."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Because metamemorial judgments sometimes eliminate memory effects, participants in Experiment 2 did not make such judgments. Even so, the effect did not replicate. The disgust advantage was ultimately replicated in Experiment 3, where participants completed a secondary task at encoding."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors explicitly report failures in Experiments 1 and 2 and replication in Experiment 3. The concluding statement calls the advantage replicable but less robust; the reported studies have conflicting results. Target citation is absent from the available abstract.""}"
10.1002/rrq.62,03263a7c5c7d8646,0,10.3102/01623737028004335,Effects of a voluntary summer reading intervention on reading achievement: Results from a randomized field trial.,"Kim, J. S.",2006,full_text,,error,obvious,"{""target_index"": 0, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""obvious"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": ""10.3102/01623737028004335"", ""title"": ""Effects of a voluntary summer reading intervention on reading achievement: Results from a randomized field trial."", ""authors"": ""Kim, J. S."", ""year"": ""2006"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication""}, ""quote"": ""We also compared the performance of control group students in moderate and high poverty\nschools in this study with the two studies of READS that we were attempting to replicate. As\nshown in Figure 5, in the initial studies, as in high poverty schools in this study, control group\nstudents lost ground in the summer, d = - .07 (Kim, 2006) and d = - .08 (Kim & White, 2008)."", ""location"": ""Independent raw primary benchmark/replication-purpose context; original identity evidence in strict_literal_parent_applied_0007.json"", ""reason"": ""The current independent school-district trial explicitly identifies TWO initial READS trials, Kim2006 and Kim/White2008, as the experiments it attempts to replicate. The only shipped counterpart is Kim/White2008. Current Introduction separately describes Kim2006 treatment/comparison and printed bibliography identifies its distinct single-author paper; independent original publisher/author-institution abstract confirms552students/10schools versus400children in the2008trial. Thus Kim2006is genuinely absent. The new literal passage establishes actual retesting/identity, not an outcome from control-group summer loss. Earlier failed outcome is withheld: overall full-sample failure and exploratory poverty-heterogeneity evidence must not be mistaken for an original-specific