s’ expectations are violated when an experimenter who ought to have a false belief reaches for an object in its true location (e.g. Onishi & Baillargeon, 2005; Song et al., 2008; Träuble et al., 2010)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Author verdict explicitly identifies this original; limitations do not replace that verdict.""}"
10.18718/81781.38,67732009dff134e5,1,10.1002/bdm.495,When Efortful Thinking Influences Judgmental Anchoring: Differential Effects of Forewarning and Incentives on Self-generated and Externally Provided Anchors,"Epley, N. & Gilovich, T.",2005,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": ""10.1002/bdm.495"", ""title"": ""When Efortful Thinking Influences Judgmental Anchoring: Differential Effects of Forewarning and Incentives on Self-generated and Externally Provided Anchors"", ""authors"": ""Epley, N. & Gilovich, T."", ""year"": ""2005"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""failed""}, ""quote"": ""Study 3 is a replication of Epley and Gilovich (2005, Study 2) and investigated the moderator\n      forewarning."", ""location"": {""start_line"": 166, ""end_line"": 167, ""form_feed_page"": 4}, ""reason"": ""In Study 3, the paper conducts a preregistered replication of a separate original article, Epley and Gilovich (2005, Study 2; DOI: 10.1002/bdm.495), which is an omitted target in the shipped dataset and resulted in a failed replication."", ""quote_provenance"": {""kind"": ""new_independent_literal_primary_quotation"", ""inspection_result"": ""evidence_followup/integration/strict_literal_quote_inspection.result.json"", ""issue_index"": 178, ""source_path"": ""/home/lukas/.t3/worktrees/fred-data/audit-flora-blind-2026-10/audit/flora_blind_2026-10/texts/67732009dff134e5.txt"", ""source_sha256"": ""62333059d42de54e295b06c29b50eb79f9dbdd304b1de6c994013e8a165e7844"", ""raw_start_offset"": 10725, ""raw_end_offset"": 10837, ""scientific_verdict_unchanged"": true, ""reason"": ""Parent verified exact raw slice and semantic identity with the earlier accepted passage under its recorded display-normalisation policy. Independent primary-context inspector supported this passage. Original provider wording and whole prior decision retained in history; raw provider reply unchanged.""}}","{""authors"": ""Epley, N. & Gilovich, T."", ""year"": ""2005"", ""title"": ""When Efortful Thinking Influences Judgmental Anchoring: Differential Effects of Forewarning and Incentives on Self-generated and Externally Provided Anchors"", ""doi"": ""10.1002/bdm.495"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""Study 2"", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""3"", ""evidence_quote"": ""In their Study 2, Epley and Gilovich (2005) found that warning participants that their adjustments\nfrom anchors were insufficient led to increases in adjustments from self-generated anchors but not\nfrom experimenter-provided anchors. We conducted a preregistered (https://osf.io/f5sj8) replica-\ntion of this study."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Thus, we deem\nthe replication an informative failure: There is evidence against the hypothesis that forewarning\nmoderates adjustment from self-generated anchors."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The moderators forming the central finding did not replicate. General anchoring effects are checks of the paradigm, not a successful replication of the moderator claim.""}"
10.1080/15248372.2018.1544140,bac6178abc69a5fe,1,10.1111/j.2044-835X.2011.02046.x,Where will the triangle look for it? Attributing false beliefs to a geometric shape at 17 months,"Surian, L., & Geraci, A.",2012,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": ""10.1111/j.2044-835X.2011.02046.x"", ""title"": ""Where will the triangle look for it? Attributing false beliefs to a geometric shape at 17 months"", ""authors"": ""Surian, L., & Geraci, A."", ""year"": ""2012"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""failed""}, ""quote"": ""Crucially, however, even after adding verbal narration, original ﬁndings could still not\nreliably be replicated, particularly in the Surian and Geraci task—much in line with\nprevious large-scale failed replication attempts of anticipatory looking false belief tasks\n(Kulke et al., 2018). A lack of looking patterns in line with false belief processing in the\nSurian and Geraci (2012) task may be because the eﬀect is fragile and hence diﬃcult to\nmeasure."", ""location"": {""start_line"": 546, ""end_line"": 551, ""form_feed_page"": 12}, ""reason"": ""The paper collects new eye-tracking data to re-test the anticipatory-looking false-belief task of Surian and Geraci (2012) with added verbal narration and concludes that its original findings could not be replicated, making it an omitted replication target with a failed outcome."", ""quote_provenance"": {""kind"": ""new_independent_literal_primary_quotation"", ""inspection_result"": ""evidence_followup/integration/strict_literal_quote_inspection.result.json"", ""issue_index"": 179, ""source_path"": ""/home/lukas/.t3/worktrees/fred-data/audit-flora-blind-2026-10/audit/flora_blind_2026-10/texts/bac6178abc69a5fe.txt"", ""source_sha256"": ""88a9726365107e07e64c726c45b4611aefd320c679bcc7d2bfd8ca9c56f53026"", ""raw_start_offset"": 35316, ""raw_end_offset"": 35770, ""scientific_verdict_unchanged"": true, ""reason"": ""Parent verified exact raw slice and semantic identity with the earlier accepted passage under its recorded display-normalisation policy. Independent primary-context inspector supported this passage. Original provider wording and whole prior decision retained in history; raw provider reply unchanged.""}}","{""authors"": ""Surian, L., & Geraci, A."", ""year"": ""2012"", ""title"": ""Where will the triangle look for it? Attributing false beliefs to a geometric shape at 17 months"", ""doi"": ""10.1111/j.2044-835X.2011.02046.x"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The rationale of the present study was, therefore, to explore whether existing successful\nvs. unsuccessful replications of AL FB tasks may be due to diﬀerences in verbal narration.\nTo this end, we followed up on our previous large-scale replication attempts of the two AL\ntasks by Southgate et al. (2007) and by Surian and Geraci (2012) (Kulke, Reiß, Krist, &\nRakoczy, 2017; Kulke et al., 2018, 2018)."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Crucially, however, even after adding verbal narration, original ﬁndings could still not\nreliably be replicated, particularly in the Surian and Geraci task—much in line with\nprevious large-scale failed replication attempts of anticipatory looking false belief tasks\n(Kulke et al., 2018)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Target-specific discussion verdict: only FB1 replicated for Southgate; original belief-based looking failed for Surian and Geraci.""}"
10.1177/0956797617747090,41a25a0b35cfc34c,0,,Eye movements reveal sustained implicit processing of others’ mental states.,"Schneider, D., Bayliss, A. P., Becker, S. I., & Dux, P. E.",2012,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Schneider, D., Bayliss, A. P., Becker, S. I., & Dux, P. E."", ""doi"": """", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Eye movements reveal sustained implicit processing of others’ mental states."", ""year"": ""2012""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""41a25a0b35cfc34c"", ""location"": ""Methods; Results (Study 1 and Study 2); References"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""As an additional test for order effects, Study 2 (n = 82) was designed to replicate the Schneider et al. (2012) paradigm in a single session to rule out more stringent multitest or order effects. The findings were comparable with the nonreplication described above (see Fig. 3)."", ""reason"": ""No prior adjudication or state files exist for this work. Studies 1 (n = 108) and 2 (n = 82) empirically retest the anticipatory-looking false-belief finding of Schneider, Bayliss, Becker, & Dux (2012) ('Eye movements reveal sustained implicit processing of others’ mental states.', Journal of Experimental Psychology: General, 141, 433–438; no DOI printed in source) using original stimuli and procedures, comparing the 2 x 2 Belief x Location ANOVA and planned contrasts at the no-object and object locations against the original findings. The sole shipped row (input_row_index 1877) is Southgate, Senju, & Csibra (2007; 10.1111/j.1467-9280.2007.01944.x), an independent study by different authors in a different journal using a different paradigm, and Schneider et al. (2012) is also distinct from all sibling targets (Surian & Geraci, 2012; Low & Watts, 2013; Senju et al., 2009), so report equivalence does not apply."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0031_41a25a0b35cfc34c"", ""target_index"": 0, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Schneider, D., Bayliss, A. P., Becker, S. I., & Dux, P. E."", ""year"": ""2012"", ""title"": ""Eye movements reveal sustained implicit processing of others’ mental states."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""In the original studies, 14-month-old children (Surian & Geraci, 2012), adults (Schneider et al., 2012), or both children from the age of 2 years and adults (Low & Watts, 2013; Senju et al., 2009; Southgate et al., 2007) revealed anticipatory-looking patterns suggestive of belief-based anticipation in both their first looks and overall looking time. The current study exactly replicated the methods described in the original articles, using original stimuli and protocols, which were generously shared with us by the authors (for details, see Supplement A in the Supplemental Material available online)."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The original results were thus only partially replicated: As in the original study, there was a significant Belief × Location interaction on looking times. But unlike in the original study, this was not due to the crucial difference in looking to the no-object location (participants looked longer to this location in the false-belief than in the truebelief condition)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors explicitly call these results partially replicated: the interaction agreed but the crucial no-object contrast did not; Study 2 produced the same pattern.""}"
10.1177/0956797617747090,41a25a0b35cfc34c,1,,Where will the triangle look for it? Attributing false beliefs to a geometric shape at 17 months.,"Surian, L., & Geraci, A.",2012,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Surian, L., & Geraci, A."", ""doi"": """", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Where will the triangle look for it? Attributing false beliefs to a geometric shape at 17 months."", ""year"": ""2012""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""41a25a0b35cfc34c"", ""location"": ""Methods; Results (Study 1); References"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Surian and Geraci (2012) paradigm. The main findings of the original Surian and Geraci (2012) study were as follows. There was no interaction effect of belief (false belief vs. true belief) and location (object vs. no object) on looking time. First saccades, however, were more often directed to the no-object location than to the object location in the false-belief condition, and vice versa in the true-belief condition. In the present study (n = 102), a 2 × 2 ANOVA on the proportion of looking time also showed no significant effects of belief, F(1, 201) = 2.02, p = .157, d = 0.20, BF10 = 0.079, and no interaction, F(1, 201) = 0.03, p = .869, d = 0.02, BF10 = 0.116, but a significant effect of object location, F(1, 201) = 28.20, p < .001, d = 0.75; specifically, participants looked significantly longer at the object location (M = .27, SD = .29, 95% CI = [.24, .30]), than the no-object location (M = .16, SD = .24, 95% CI = [.14, .19])."", ""reason"": ""No prior adjudication or state files exist for this work. Study 1 (n = 102) empirically retests the geometric-shape anticipatory-looking false-belief task of Surian & Geraci (2012) ('Where will the triangle look for it? Attributing false beliefs to a geometric shape at 17 months.', British Journal of Developmental Psychology, 30, 30–44; no DOI printed in source) using original stimuli and protocols, explicitly comparing both looking-time ANOVA results and first-saccade binomial tests against the original findings. The sole shipped row (input_row_index 1877, Southgate, Senju, & Csibra, 2007; 10.1111/j.1467-9280.2007.01944.x) and all sibling targets (Schneider et al., 2012; Low & Watts, 2013; Senju et al., 2009) are separate original studies by different authors using distinct paradigms, so report equivalence does not apply."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0031_41a25a0b35cfc34c"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Surian, L., & Geraci, A."", ""year"": ""2012"", ""title"": ""Where will the triangle look for it? Attributing false beliefs to a geometric shape at 17 months."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1"", ""evidence_quote"": ""In the original studies, 14-month-old children (Surian & Geraci, 2012), adults (Schneider et al., 2012), or both children from the age of 2 years and adults (Low & Watts, 2013; Senju et al., 2009; Southgate et al., 2007) revealed anticipatory-looking patterns suggestive of belief-based anticipation in both their first looks and overall looking time. The current study exactly replicated the methods described in the original articles, using original stimuli and protocols, which were generously shared with us by the authors (for details, see Supplement A in the Supplemental Material available online)."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The Kulke et al. present study thus replicated only the original negative findings but not the positive ones."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors explicitly distinguish replicated null findings from positive findings that did not replicate.""}"
10.1177/0956797617747090,41a25a0b35cfc34c,2,,Attributing false beliefs about object identity reveals a signature blind spot in humans’ efficient mind-reading system.,"Low, J., & Watts, J.",2013,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Low, J., & Watts, J."", ""doi"": """", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Attributing false beliefs about object identity reveals a signature blind spot in humans’ efficient mind-reading system."", ""year"": ""2013""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""41a25a0b35cfc34c"", ""location"": ""Methods; Results (Study 1, Summary of results, Studies 3a and 3b); References"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Table 2 summarizes the current results and contrasts them with the original findings. The current studies failed to replicate the previously observed full pattern of the studies of Southgate et al. (2007) and Senju et al. (2009), Surian and Geraci (2012), and Schneider et al. (2012). The only original results fully replicated here were those from the Low and Watts (2013) paradigm."", ""reason"": ""No prior adjudication or state files exist for this work, and a fresh intact primary-source passage is used instead of the Luna quote that repaired an unhyphenated word. Study 1 (n = 119), Study 3a (n = 36), and Study 3b (n = 19) empirically retest the location false-belief finding of Low & Watts (2013) ('Attributing false beliefs about object identity reveals a signature blind spot in humans’ efficient mind-reading system.', Psychological Science, 24, 305–311; no DOI printed in source) using original stimuli and follow-up control conditions, explicitly comparing differential looking scores and first saccades against the original findings. The sole shipped row (input_row_index 1877, Southgate, Senju, & Csibra, 2007; 10.1111/j.1467-9280.2007.01944.x) and all sibling targets (Schneider et al., 2012; Surian & Geraci, 2012; Senju et al., 2009) are separate original studies by different authors, so report equivalence does not apply."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0031_41a25a0b35cfc34c"", ""target_index"": 2, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Low, J., & Watts, J."", ""year"": ""2013"", ""title"": ""Attributing false beliefs about object identity reveals a signature blind spot in humans’ efficient mind-reading system."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 3a, 3b"", ""evidence_quote"": ""In the original studies, 14-month-old children (Surian & Geraci, 2012), adults (Schneider et al., 2012), or both children from the age of 2 years and adults (Low & Watts, 2013; Senju et al., 2009; Southgate et al., 2007) revealed anticipatory-looking patterns suggestive of belief-based anticipation in both their first looks and overall looking time. The current study exactly replicated the methods described in the original articles, using original stimuli and protocols, which were generously shared with us by the authors (for details, see Supplement A in the Supplemental Material available online)."", ""outcome"": ""statistically successful but flawed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In sum, the original pattern of belief-congruent looking could be reproduced only under conditions in which the belief congruency of the locations is confounded with additional factors, and therefore, this pattern might not reflect belief-based anticipation."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The original materials yielded the effect, but the target-specific main message is that removing confounds eliminated belief-congruent anticipation and invalidated its interpretation as implicit theory of mind.""}"
10.1177/0956797617747090,41a25a0b35cfc34c,4,,Mindblind eyes: An absence of spontaneous theory of mind in Asperger syndrome.,"Senju, A., Southgate, V., White, S., & Frith, U.",2009,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Senju, A., Southgate, V., White, S., & Frith, U."", ""doi"": """", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Mindblind eyes: An absence of spontaneous theory of mind in Asperger syndrome."", ""year"": ""2009""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""41a25a0b35cfc34c"", ""location"": ""Methods; Results (Study 1); References"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Southgate et al. (2007) and Senju et al. (2009) paradigm. In the original studies of Southgate et al. (2007) and Senju et al. (2009), the main finding was that participants generally showed more anticipatory looking to the belief-congruent than to the belief-incongruent location, with no significant differences between the False Belief 1 and False Belief 2 conditions. In contrast, in the present study (n = 54), the overall DLS did not significantly differ from zero (M = .16, SD = .65, 95% CI = [−.02, .35]), t(50) = 1.77, p = .083, d = 0.25, BF10 = 0.649."", ""reason"": ""No prior adjudication or state files exist for this work. Study 1 (n = 54 neurotypical adults) empirically retests the adult anticipatory-looking false-belief finding of Senju, Southgate, White, & Frith (2009) ('Mindblind eyes: An absence of spontaneous theory of mind in Asperger syndrome.', Science, 325, 883–885; no DOI printed in source) using the 6-s window to the windows from Senju et al. (2009) and explicitly compares overall and condition-specific differential looking scores and first saccades against the original findings of both Southgate et al. (2007) and Senju et al. (2009). Although shipped row 1877 covers Southgate, Senju, & Csibra (2007; 10.1111/j.1467-9280.2007.01944.x), which tested 2-year-olds in Psychological Science, Senju et al. (2009) is a separate empirical study testing adults in Science with additional authors (White & Frith), not an alternate report of the same experiment or of any other sibling target."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0031_41a25a0b35cfc34c"", ""target_index"": 4, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Senju, A., Southgate, V., White, S., & Frith, U."", ""year"": ""2009"", ""title"": ""Mindblind eyes: An absence of spontaneous theory of mind in Asperger syndrome."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1"", ""evidence_quote"": ""In the original studies, 14-month-old children (Surian & Geraci, 2012), adults (Schneider et al., 2012), or both children from the age of 2 years and adults (Low & Watts, 2013; Senju et al., 2009; Southgate et al., 2007) revealed anticipatory-looking patterns suggestive of belief-based anticipation in both their first looks and overall looking time. The current study exactly replicated the methods described in the original articles, using original stimuli and protocols, which were generously shared with us by the authors (for details, see Supplement A in the Supplemental Material available online)."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The overall original findings were not replicated, whereas a differential analysis of the False Belief 1 and False Belief 2 conditions suggests that only the False Belief 1 condition could be replicated."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors explicitly group this original with Southgate 2007 and state an overall nonreplication verdict. The neurotypical anticipatory-looking result is the tested finding, rather than the autism group contrast.""}"
10.17605/osf.io/82bwv,92e046b18547f955,1,,,"Riesel, Weinberg, Moran, & Hajcak",2013,full_text,,error,obvious,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Riesel, Anja; Weinberg, Anna; Moran, Tim; Hajcak, Greg"", ""year"": ""2013"", ""title"": ""Time Course of Error-Potentiated Startle and its Relationship to Error-Related Brain Activity"", ""doi"": """", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""92e046b18547f955"", ""location"": ""discussion, section 5.2, Additional Analyses"", ""method"": ""obvious"", ""quote"": ""Importantly, one of the original authors attempted to replicate this correlation result\nin a subsequent study (Riesel, Weinberg, Moran, & Hajcak, 2013, Journal of Psychophys-\niology). They found that the correlation between the ERN and EPS was only significant\nwhen assessed in a subgroup of participants who showed large ERNs (via a median split\nbased on ERN amplitude). Therefore, we attempted this same analysis strategy and per-\nformed a median split based on ERN amplitude and re-assessed the correlation between\nERN and EPS magnitude in this subgroup of participants (see Figure 4). Consistent with\nthe Riesel et al. (2013) study, we found a significant correlation between ERN and EPS\nin this subgroup of participants (r = -.63, p < .01)."", ""reason"": ""Parent independently verified0046original publisher-typeset nine-page primary report with exact title, four authors,2013issue27(2):51–59 and printedDOI10.1027/0269-8803/a000093. Current Section5.2 explicitly repeats that original high-ERN median-split correlation analysis in new participants, distinct from shipped1895Hajcak/Foti2008whole-sample finding. The earlier missing-title obstruction is resolved by new primary identity proof, without altering the blind coding or assigning an unrequested subgroup outcome. The DOI stays in provenance because the current text does not print it. PrimaryPDFcontrol-character signs remain untouched."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0073_92e046b18547f955"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Riesel, Weinberg, Moran, & Hajcak"", ""year"": ""2013"", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Therefore, we attempted this same analysis strategy and performed a median split based on ERN amplitude and re-assessed the correlation between ERN and EPS magnitude in this subgroup of participants (see Figure 4)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Consistent with the Riesel et al. (2013) study, we found a significant correlation between ERN and EPS in this subgroup of participants (r = -.63, p < .01)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Additional analysis explicitly re-tests the later published subgroup finding and reports agreement. The subsequent removal of an outlier is exploratory. Citation is inline only; title and DOI are absent.""}"
10.3389/fpsyg.2014.01276,3213132881105a19,1,10.1027/a000001,"Does cleanliness influence moral judgments? A direct replication of Schnall, Benton, and Harvey (2008).","Johnson D. J., Cheung F., Donnellan M. B.",2014a,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""It should be noted that these pilot results, albeit promising, were highly exploratory, and thus should not be interpreted as confirmatory evidence. Instead, these tentative results point to the need for a comprehensive examination of the role of response effort."", ""location"": ""introduction"", ""reason"": ""Johnson et al. (2014a) is an earlier replication attempt from which materials were obtained to replicate Schnall et al. (2008a) Experiment 1, and the brief mention of Pilot Study 1 refers to an exploratory subgroup analysis in the supplementary material that the author explicitly disclaims as non-confirmatory motivation rather than a central target re-test.""}","{""authors"": ""Johnson D. J., Cheung F., Donnellan M. B."", ""year"": ""2014a"", ""title"": ""Does cleanliness influence moral judgments? A direct replication of Schnall, Benton, and Harvey (2008)."", ""doi"": ""10.1027/a000001"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""1"", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""Pilot Study 1"", ""evidence_quote"": ""First, I explored JCD’s Experiment 1 data to identify a low response effort subsample (\nn\n= 57) based on percentage of correct responses on sentence unscrambling task and found tentative support for the cleanliness hypothesis [\nd\ncomposite\n= -0.41, 95% CI (-0.94, 0.13), see Supplementary Material, Pilot Study 1]."", ""outcome"": """", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""introduction"", ""outcome_computation"": ""not checked"", ""outcome_computational_quote"": ""First, I explored JCD’s Experiment 1 data to identify a low response effort subsample (\nn\n= 57) based on percentage of correct responses on sentence unscrambling task and found tentative support for the cleanliness hypothesis [\nd\ncomposite\n= -0.41, 95% CI (-0.94, 0.13), see Supplementary Material, Pilot Study 1]."", ""outcome_robustness"": ""robustness challenges"", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": ""First, I explored JCD’s Experiment 1 data to identify a low response effort subsample (\nn\n= 57) based on percentage of correct responses on sentence unscrambling task and found tentative support for the cleanliness hypothesis [\nd\ncomposite\n= -0.41, 95% CI (-0.94, 0.13), see Supplementary Material, Pilot Study 1]."", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Exploratory low-effort subgroup reanalysis tentatively qualified the published null; numerical reproduction was outside the stated subgroup analysis.""}"
10.1037/xhp0000331,2bba722926999fc3,1,10.1037/a0024248,Taking a hands-on approach: Apparent grasping ability scales the perception of object size.,"Linkenauger S. A., Witt J. K., & Proffitt D. R.",2011,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Linkenauger S. A., Witt J. K., & Proffitt D. R."", ""doi"": ""10.1037/a0024248"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Taking a hands-on approach: Apparent grasping ability scales the perception of object size."", ""year"": ""2011""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""2bba722926999fc3"", ""location"": ""Abstract, Line 1; Introduction, Line 2; General Discussion, Line 2; References, Line 10"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Linkenauger, Witt, and Proffitt (2011) found that the perceived size of graspable objects was scaled by perceived grasping capacity. However, it is possible that this effect occurred because object size was estimated on the same trial as grasping capacity. This may have led to a conflation of estimates of perceived action capacity and spatial properties. In 5 experiments, we tested Linkenauger et al.’s claim that right-handed observers overestimate the grasping capacity of their right hand relative to their left hand, and that this, in turn, leads them to underestimate the size of objects to-be-grasped in their right hand relative to their left hand."", ""reason"": ""Collier and Lawson (2017) empirically retest two distinct original articles by Linkenauger and colleagues: (1) Linkenauger, Witt, Bakdash, Stefanucci, and Proffitt (2009, Psychological Science, DOI 10.1111/j.1467-9280.2009.02447.x, 'Asymmetrical Body Perception'), which is represented in shipped_rows row 1931 and coded as target_index 0 (retested in Experiment 1 on perceived hand-size asymmetry and in the perceived grasping capacity tasks); and (2) Linkenauger, Witt, and Proffitt (2011, Journal of Experimental Psychology: Human Perception and Performance, 37, 1432-1441, DOI 10.1037/a0024248, 'Taking a hands-on approach: Apparent grasping ability scales the perception of object size.'), which is coded as target_index 1 and is the central study retested across Experiments 2-5 investigating whether right-handed observers' greater perceived grasping capacity for the right hand scales visually perceived object size of graspable blocks ('Thus we did not replicate the results of Linkenauger et al. (2011) because we failed to find the predicted influence of perceived action capacity on spatial perception'). Luna triage erred by conflating target_index 1 (Linkenauger et al., 2011) with shipped row 1931 (Linkenauger et al., 2009). Linkenauger et al. (2011) is a separate published study and is genuinely missing from shipped_rows. Prior adjudication and state files do not exist for this work."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0019_2bba722926999fc3"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Linkenauger S. A., Witt J. K., & Proffitt D. R."", ""year"": ""2011"", ""title"": ""Taking a hands-on approach: Apparent grasping ability scales the perception of object size."", ""doi"": ""10.1037/a0024248"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""2, 3, 4, 5"", ""evidence_quote"": ""We focused on the claim made by Linkenauger et al. (2011) that right-handed observers estimate the size of objects they intend to grasp in their right hand as smaller than objects they intend to grasp in their left hand because they perceive the grasping capacity of their right hand as greater than that of their left hand."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Thus we did not replicate the results of Linkenauger et al. (2011) because we failed to find the predicted influence of perceived action capacity on spatial perception."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Explicit overall nonreplication verdict for the focal object size finding; successful preliminary hand asymmetry checks do not override it.""}"
10.1371/journal.pone.0172636,1262c4f34ab4cc78,1,,"The retrospective gambler’s fallacy: Unlikely events, constructing the past, and multiple universes.","Oppenheimer DM, Monin B.",2009,full_text,,error,obvious,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""obvious"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Oppenheimer DM, Monin B."", ""year"": ""2009"", ""title"": ""The retrospective gambler’s fallacy: Unlikely events, constructing the past, and multiple universes."", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined""}, ""quote"": ""To help indicate the overall quality of our replication we included as a positive control an additional experiment with a well-defined effect size. Specifically, we used the retrospective gambler’s task [ 11 ]."", ""location"": ""Methods: Positive control; Results Table3; reference11"", ""reason"": ""Parent independently inspected new random assignment to the casino-dice positive-control experiment and the authors explicit detection of its expected retrospective gamblers effect in Table3. Reference11 identifies Oppenheimer/Monin2009. The sole shipped pair row1965 concerns Gervais/Norenzayan2012 religious-disbelief image priming, a different author team, published finding and task. This is an empirically tested published positive-control finding, not method reuse or an alternate report. Identity-only addition; outcome remains ungraded in this review.""}","{""authors"": ""Oppenheimer DM, Monin B."", ""year"": ""2009"", ""title"": ""The retrospective gambler’s fallacy: Unlikely events, constructing the past, and multiple universes."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""To help indicate the overall quality of our replication we included as a positive control an additional experiment with a well-defined effect size. Specifically, we used the retrospective gambler’s task [ 11 ]."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Our inability to detect a substantial decline in religious belief was not due to quality issues as we detected an expected effect in an additional positive control study with the same participants ( F (1,963) = 21.2, p < .001, Table 3 )."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_confident"": true}"
10.1525/collabra.32964,8b1c966efb3f2f97,1,10.1026//0033-3042.52.3.131,Effekte des generischen Maskulinums und alternativer Sprachformen auf den gedanklichen Einbezug von Frauen [Effects of the generic masculinum and of alternative forms of expression on the mental inclusion of women].,"Stahlberg, D., & Sczesny, S.",2001,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""The present high-powered and pre-registered study seeks to contribute to research on cognitive effects of masculine generics by replicating and extending one particular study, namely, a roughly 20-years old study on the effects of masculine generics in German speakers (Stahlberg et al., 2001, Study 2; Stahlberg & Sczesny, 2001, Study 3, is the same study)."", ""location"": ""Introduction, p. 2, lines 95-100"", ""reason"": ""The authors explicitly state that they are replicating one particular study and that Stahlberg & Sczesny (2001, Study 3) is the same study as Stahlberg et al. (2001, Study 2), making this blind target a duplicate of the original study already recorded in row 2002 rather than an omitted independent target.""}","{""authors"": ""Stahlberg, D., & Sczesny, S."", ""year"": ""2001"", ""title"": ""Effekte des generischen Maskulinums und alternativer Sprachformen auf den gedanklichen Einbezug von Frauen [Effects of the generic masculinum and of alternative forms of expression on the mental inclusion of women]."", ""doi"": ""10.1026//0033-3042.52.3.131"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""Study 3"", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The present high-powered and pre-registered study seeks to contribute to research on cognitive effects of masculine generics by replicating and extending one particular study, namely, a roughly 20-years old study on the effects of masculine generics in German speakers (Stahlberg et al., 2001, Study 2; Stahlberg & Sczesny, 2001, Study 3, is the same study)."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""That is, for both the experimental condition that produced the lowest inclusion of women (i.e., masculine generics) and the highest inclusion of women (capital-I), the number of women listed by participants in the present study and by participants two decades ago did not differ statistically. Although not a true experimental test, this convergence in absolute numbers indicates that the changes regarding the visibility and increased power of women in society did not lead to an overall increased cognitive accessibility of women. Only for masculine-feminine word pairs, our results differed from the original study. In our replication, significantly more women were listed in this condition than in the masculine generics condition, whereas this contrast was not significant in the original study."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors confirm agreement for masculine generics and capital-I but explicitly contrast the word-pair result with the original: a previously nonsignificant contrast becomes significant. The introductory confirmation of the focal effect does not override this named disagreement. The two cited papers report the same original experiment, so the same comparisons apply to both.""}"
10.1016/j.appet.2013.12.020,98ff6e79db78401a,0,10.1037/0022-3514.92.2.325,Self-control relies on glucose as a limited energy source. Willpower is more than a metaphor.,"Gailliot, M. T., Baumeister, R. F., DeWall, C. N., Maner, J. K., Plant, E. A., Tice, D. M., et al.",2007,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Gailliot, M. T., Baumeister, R. F., DeWall, C. N., Maner, J. K., Plant, E. A., Tice, D. M., et al."", ""doi"": ""10.1037/0022-3514.92.2.325"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Self-control relies on glucose as a limited energy source. Willpower is more than a metaphor."", ""year"": ""2007""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""98ff6e79db78401a"", ""location"": ""Experiment 1, p. 56 (line 229); Experiment 1 Discussion, p. 58 (line 361); Experiment 2 Discussion, p. 61 (line 559); General discussion, pp. 61-62 (lines 569, 575); References, p. 62 (line 579)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Experiment 1 was designed to replicate the counteracting effect of sugar consumption on ego depletion (Gailliot et al., 2007; Hagger et al., 2010). Following the protocol of Wang and Dvorak (2010), participants’ delay-discounting rates were assessed prior and after the consumption of a sweet drink containing either sugar or artiﬁcial sweetener."", ""reason"": ""Prior adjudication and state files are absent for this work. Both Experiment 1 and Experiment 2 (via the DG glucose-ingestion vs. SS stevia-rinse contrast on go/no-go performance, which does not use Wang and Dvorak's protocol) directly retest Gailliot et al. (2007) on whether glucose consumption counteracts ego depletion (and Experiment 1 additionally retests whether performing a self-control task lowers blood glucose levels as reported by Gailliot et al., 2007). The reference list explicitly identifies Gailliot et al. (2007), 'Self-control relies on glucose as a limited energy source. Willpower is more than a metaphor,' Journal of Personality and Social Psychology, 92(2), 325-336 (DOI: 10.1037/0022-3514.92.2.325). This target is a separate work from the sole shipped row (input_row_index 2004: Wang & Dvorak, 2010, Psychological Science, DOI: 10.1177/0956797609358096) and from sibling targets 2, 3, and 4."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0137_98ff6e79db78401a"", ""target_index"": 0, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Gailliot, M. T., Baumeister, R. F., DeWall, C. N., Maner, J. K., Plant, E. A., Tice, D. M., et al."", ""year"": ""2007"", ""title"": ""Self-control relies on glucose as a limited energy source. Willpower is more than a metaphor."", ""doi"": ""10.1037/0022-3514.92.2.325"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""8, 9"", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""The present study was designed to comply with the widespread call for replications by applying study protocols reported to results in substantial effects of sugar consumption (Gailliot et al., 2007; Wang & Dvorak, 2010) and sugar sensing (Hagger & Chatzisarantis, 2012; Molden et al., 2012) on ego depletion. The resulting observation that these procedures did not lead to the detection of the originally reported effects underscores the relevance of independent replications."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Two experimental designs involving highly reliable, prototypical self-control tasks and yielding more than sufﬁcient power failed to replicate the previously reported effects of glucose ingestion (Gailliot et al., 2007; Hagger et al., 2010) and glucose sensing (Hagger & Chatzisarantis, 2012; Molden et al., 2012)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly report failure to replicate this published glucose effect. Limitations are discussed, but their substantive overall judgment remains failure, rather than an inability to adjudicate.""}"
10.1016/j.appet.2013.12.020,98ff6e79db78401a,2,10.1177/0146167212459912,The sweet taste of success. The presence of glucose in the oral cavity moderates the depletion of self-control resources.,"Hagger, M. S., & Chatzisarantis, N. L.",2012,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Hagger, M. S., & Chatzisarantis, N. L."", ""doi"": ""10.1177/0146167212459912"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""The sweet taste of success. The presence of glucose in the oral cavity moderates the depletion of self-control resources."", ""year"": ""2012""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""98ff6e79db78401a"", ""location"": ""Experiment 2, p. 58 (lines 367, 371); Experiment 2 Discussion, p. 61 (line 567); General discussion, pp. 61-62 (lines 569, 575); References, p. 62 (line 579)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Rinsing one’s mouth with glucose solution did not signiﬁcantly alter ego depletion, neither when the solution was provided directly after the depletion task (reward drink), nor when it was provided directly before the second self-control task (signal drink). Hence, this experiment did not generate any evidence that sensing glucose in the oral cavity serves one of the two hypothesized psychological functions. Importantly, this implies that, despite applying a powerful research design, the present study could not replicate the ﬁndings presented by Hagger and Chatzisarantis (2012), Molden et al. (2012) and Sanders et al. (2012)."", ""reason"": ""Prior adjudication and state files are absent for this work. Experiment 2 directly retests the effect of oral glucose sensing (mouth rinsing with an 18% glucose solution vs. non-caloric sweetener for 10 seconds, measuring liquid volume before and after rinsing following Hagger & Chatzisarantis, 2012) on ego depletion, and explicitly concludes that it failed to replicate Hagger and Chatzisarantis (2012). The reference list identifies Hagger, M. S., & Chatzisarantis, N. L. (2012), 'The sweet taste of success. The presence of glucose in the oral cavity moderates the depletion of self-control resources,' Personality and Social Psychology Bulletin, 39(1), 28-42 (DOI: 10.1177/0146167212459912). This is a distinct work from shipped row 2004 (Wang & Dvorak, 2010) and from sibling targets 0, 3, and 4."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0137_98ff6e79db78401a"", ""target_index"": 2, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Hagger, M. S., & Chatzisarantis, N. L."", ""year"": ""2012"", ""title"": ""The sweet taste of success. The presence of glucose in the oral cavity moderates the depletion of self-control resources."", ""doi"": ""10.1177/0146167212459912"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""1, 2, 3, 4, 5"", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""The present study was designed to comply with the widespread call for replications by applying study protocols reported to results in substantial effects of sugar consumption (Gailliot et al., 2007; Wang & Dvorak, 2010) and sugar sensing (Hagger & Chatzisarantis, 2012; Molden et al., 2012) on ego depletion. The resulting observation that these procedures did not lead to the detection of the originally reported effects underscores the relevance of independent replications."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Importantly, this implies that, despite applying a powerful research design, the present study could not replicate the ﬁndings presented by Hagger and Chatzisarantis (2012), Molden et al. (2012) and Sanders et al. (2012)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly report failure to replicate this published glucose effect. Limitations are discussed, but their substantive overall judgment remains failure, rather than an inability to adjudicate.""}"
10.1016/j.appet.2013.12.020,98ff6e79db78401a,3,10.1177/0956797612439069,The motivational versus metabolic effects of carbohydrates on self-control.,"Molden, D. C., Hui, C. M., Noreen, E. E., Meier, B. P., Scholer, A. A., D’Agostino, P. R., & Martin, V.",2012,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Molden, D. C., Hui, C. M., Noreen, E. E., Meier, B. P., Scholer, A. A., D’Agostino, P. R., & Martin, V."", ""doi"": ""10.1177/0956797612439069"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""The motivational versus metabolic effects of carbohydrates on self-control."", ""year"": ""2012""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""98ff6e79db78401a"", ""location"": ""Introduction, p. 56 (line 135); Experiment 2, p. 58 (line 367); Experiment 2 Discussion, p. 61 (line 567); General discussion, pp. 61-62 (lines 569, 575); References, p. 63 (line 583)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""The present study was designed to comply with the widespread call for replications by applying study protocols reported to results in substantial effects of sugar consumption (Gailliot et al., 2007; Wang & Dvorak, 2010) and sugar sensing (Hagger & Chatzisarantis, 2012; Molden et al., 2012) on ego depletion. The resulting observation that these procedures did not lead to the detection of the originally reported effects underscores the relevance of independent replications."", ""reason"": ""Prior adjudication and state files are absent for this work. Experiment 2 directly retests the effect of oral carbohydrate/glucose rinsing and compares oral glucose sensing against glucose ingestion (explicitly including a fifth group that ingested the glucose solution on both occasions to compare with Molden et al., 2012), concluding that it failed to replicate Molden et al. (2012). The reference list identifies Molden, D. C., Hui, C. M., Noreen, E. E., Meier, B. P., Scholer, A. A., D'Agostino, P. R., & Martin, V. (2012), 'The motivational versus metabolic effects of carbohydrates on self-control,' Psychological Science, 23, 1130-1137 (DOI: 10.1177/0956797612439069). This is distinct from shipped row 2004 (Wang & Dvorak, 2010) and sibling targets 0, 2, and 4."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0137_98ff6e79db78401a"", ""target_index"": 3, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Molden, D. C., Hui, C. M., Noreen, E. E., Meier, B. P., Scholer, A. A., D’Agostino, P. R., & Martin, V."", ""year"": ""2012"", ""title"": ""The motivational versus metabolic effects of carbohydrates on self-control."", ""doi"": ""10.1177/0956797612439069"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""2, 3"", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""The present study was designed to comply with the widespread call for replications by applying study protocols reported to results in substantial effects of sugar consumption (Gailliot et al., 2007; Wang & Dvorak, 2010) and sugar sensing (Hagger & Chatzisarantis, 2012; Molden et al., 2012) on ego depletion. The resulting observation that these procedures did not lead to the detection of the originally reported effects underscores the relevance of independent replications."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Importantly, this implies that, despite applying a powerful research design, the present study could not replicate the ﬁndings presented by Hagger and Chatzisarantis (2012), Molden et al. (2012) and Sanders et al. (2012)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly report failure to replicate this published glucose effect. Limitations are discussed, but their substantive overall judgment remains failure, rather than an inability to adjudicate.""}"
