10.1080/1612197x.2016.1199580,9f0083cada5fd3bb,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,"{""corrected_value"": null, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""9f0083cada5fd3bb"", ""location"": ""Abstract"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Recent research on exercise with virtually present partners found that encouragement attenuated the Köhler effect, which boosts task motivation for weaker group members in conjunctive tasks. The current study replicated and extended previous research, comparing exclusive (e.g. “you can do it”) and inclusive encouragement (e.g. “we can do it”) as potential moderators of the Köhler effect."", ""reason"": ""No prior adjudication or state files exist for this work. The supplied source text is abstract-only and contains no in-text citation or reference list, so target_index 0 has empty bibliographic fields. Although the paper's title ('Is silence really golden? ...'), shared co-authors (D. L. Feltz and N. L. Kerr), and retested finding (attenuation of the Köhler motivation gain by partner encouragement in an active video game) strongly point to shipped input_row_index 2120 (Irwin, Feltz, & Kerr, 2013, 'Silence is Golden: Effect of Encouragement in Motivating the Weak Link in an Online Exercise Video Game', 10.2196/jmir.2551), the unresolved question from the abstract alone is the exact cited identity of the 'previous research' and whether it is identical to shipped row 2120 rather than an omitted original."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0078_9f0083cada5fd3bb"", ""target_index"": 0, ""verdict"": ""undeterminable""}","{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Recent research on exercise with virtually present partners found that encouragement attenuated the Köhler effect, which boosts task motivation for weaker group members in conjunctive tasks. The current study replicated and extended previous research, comparing exclusive (e.g. “you can do it”) and inclusive encouragement (e.g. “we can do it”) as potential moderators of the Köhler effect."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""A significant motivation gain was observed in all partnered conditions compared to the control, and exclusive encouragement from a virtually present partner attenuated motivation gains. However, inclusive encouragement did not, and encouragement from an audible trainer boosted exercise motivation over the control group."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Abstract states the previous finding that encouragement attenuated motivation gains and reports the corresponding attenuation under exclusive encouragement. Inclusive encouragement and audible trainer are extensions; no explicit contrast with the original is stated.""}"
10.2466/20.22.pms.114.1.185-188,29cbe0ff7f624a59,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,"{""corrected_value"": null, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""29cbe0ff7f624a59"", ""location"": ""Abstract (line 5); see also Title (line 1)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""One of the early studies supporting his model was conducted by Victorina Lefebvre. Over the years, other studies have been conducted, but not all have shown similar support as the original findings of Victorina Lefebvre. This study attempted to extend her findings in testing whether or not other ambiguous stimuli could be used in support of the model."", ""reason"": ""No prior adjudication or state file exists for this work. The supplied primary source (29cbe0ff7f624a59.txt) contains only the title ('Asymmetry in Human Cognition: Further Replication and Extension'), author list, and a five-sentence abstract, with no full text or reference list. Contrary to Luna's triage disposal as not_a_target, unseen full text cannot be used to exclude a paper whose title explicitly describes it as a 'Further Replication and Extension'. However, because only the abstract is available, all citation fields in the blind target are empty and the evidence cannot resolve the precise questions of: (1) whether the full text empirically retests a specific published finding in addition to testing new ambiguous stimuli, (2) what the source-backed bibliographic citation is for Victorina Lefebvre's study, and (3) whether under the codebook's replication-chain rule the retested target is already represented by shipped row 2121 (Anderson & Grice, 2009, 'Replicating a Simple Study of Asymmetry in Human Cognition', Perceptual and Motor Skills, 109(2), 577-580, doi_o: 10.2466/pms.109.2.577-580, by the same first author James A. Anderson) or is an unshipped work by Victorina Lefebvre."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0018_29cbe0ff7f624a59"", ""target_index"": 0, ""verdict"": ""undeterminable""}","{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""This study attempted to extend her findings in testing whether or not other ambiguous stimuli could be used in support of the model."", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract identifies an attempt to extend Victorina Lefebvre’s findings but supplies neither a unique bibliographic entry nor results. No target citation is guessed from background references.""}"
10.3758/s13414-015-0849-x,ae78ae9e5572ca14,1,10.3758/BF03193553,Asymmetric interference between sex and emotion in face perception.,"Atkinson, A. P., Tipples, J., Burt, D. M., & Young, A. W.",2005,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""Resembling the pattern seen in Experiment 1, Experiment\n2b\nfailed to replicate the symmetrical interaction between face sex and expression on expression and sex categorization seen for the larger picture set used in Experiment\n2a\n. Rather, an asymmetrical interaction was found, in that poser sex affected the categorization of emotional expressions, whereas expression did not affect the categorization of faces by sex. This pattern deviates from that seen in Experiment\n1b\n, in which no interaction was observed in either sex or expression categorization, but is consistent with the pattern of results reported in studies that had employed the Garner paradigm (Atkinson et al.,\n2005\n; Karnadewi & Lipp,\n2011\n)."", ""location"": ""Experiment 2b Results and discussion (lines 651-661)"", ""reason"": ""The paper's stated design and intent is to test whether Aguado et al.'s (2009) symmetrical interaction is moderated by stimulus set size (32 vs. 4 faces). Atkinson et al. (2005) is cited alongside Le Gal and Bruce (2002) and Karnadewi and Lipp (2011) only to motivate the negative expectation that Aguado et al.'s symmetrical interaction would be absent with four faces; the paper does not re-test Atkinson et al.'s empirical findings (either their orthogonal-vs.-control Garner interference or their post-hoc 2x2 orthogonal-condition ANOVA, which yielded no interaction at all), and instead explicitly evaluates Experiments 1b and 2b as failures to replicate the large-set symmetrical interaction while noting post-hoc consistency in Experiment 2b.""}","{""authors"": ""Atkinson, A. P., Tipples, J., Burt, D. M., & Young, A. W."", ""year"": ""2005"", ""title"": ""Asymmetric interference between sex and emotion in face perception."", ""doi"": ""10.3758/BF03193553"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1b, 2b"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Experiment\n2b\nwas designed to assess whether the same symmetrical pattern of results would emerge if a smaller set of individuals drawn from the new face set was used. On the basis of the results reported by Le Gal and Bruce (\n2002\n), Atkinson et al. (\n2005\n), Karnadewi and Lipp (\n2011\n), and in Experiment\n1b\n, no symmetrical interaction was expected."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Resembling the pattern seen in Experiment 1, Experiment\n2b\nfailed to replicate the symmetrical interaction between face sex and expression on expression and sex categorization seen for the larger picture set used in Experiment\n2a\n. Rather, an asymmetrical interaction was found, in that poser sex affected the categorization of emotional expressions, whereas expression did not affect the categorization of faces by sex. This pattern deviates from that seen in Experiment\n1b\n, in which no interaction was observed in either sex or expression categorization, but is consistent with the pattern of results reported in studies that had employed the Garner paradigm (Atkinson et al.,\n2005\n; Karnadewi & Lipp,\n2011\n)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors compare the small-set asymmetrical result to these published results as consistent; absence of symmetry is the selected claim tested.""}"
10.3758/s13414-015-0849-x,ae78ae9e5572ca14,2,10.1037/a0021333,The processing of invariant and variant face cues in the Garner Paradigm.,"Karnadewi, F., & Lipp, O. V.",2011,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 2, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""Aguado et al. (\n2009\n), on the other hand, found support for a symmetrical interaction, in which sex cues affected expression categorization, and vice versa. The present findings resolve this apparent contradiction by showing that stimulus set size is an important determinant of the nature of the Emotional Expression × Sex interaction. A symmetrical interaction will emerge if the number of different images used is large—32 in the present study—but not if the number of different images used is small—four in the present study. The latter finding is also consistent with the results of post-hoc analyses reported by Atkinson et al. and performed on our data (Karnadewi & Lipp,\n2011\n), in which no interaction emerged if the data from the Garner paradigm’s orthogonal conditions were analyzed in 2 × 2 factorial ANOVAs. These results resemble the results of Experiment\n1b\nand those of Le Gal and Bruce (\n2002\n)."", ""location"": ""General discussion (lines 669-677)"", ""reason"": ""Karnadewi and Lipp (2011) is not re-tested as a replication or reproduction target. Its contrast with Aguado et al. (2009) motivates manipulating set size within Aguado et al.'s paradigm to test whether the symmetrical interaction disappears at four faces, and both small-set experiments (1b and 2b) are explicitly framed and evaluated as failing to replicate Aguado et al.'s symmetrical interaction rather than testing Karnadewi and Lipp's asymmetrical Garner effect. The passing mention of a post-hoc 2x2 ANOVA on Karnadewi and Lipp's (2011) orthogonal condition in the Introduction is an incidental background check ruling out analytic differences, not a reproduction of Karnadewi and Lipp's reported Garner finding.""}","{""authors"": ""Karnadewi, F., & Lipp, O. V."", ""year"": ""2011"", ""title"": ""The processing of invariant and variant face cues in the Garner Paradigm."", ""doi"": ""10.1037/a0021333"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1b, 2b"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Experiment\n2b\nwas designed to assess whether the same symmetrical pattern of results would emerge if a smaller set of individuals drawn from the new face set was used. On the basis of the results reported by Le Gal and Bruce (\n2002\n), Atkinson et al. (\n2005\n), Karnadewi and Lipp (\n2011\n), and in Experiment\n1b\n, no symmetrical interaction was expected."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Resembling the pattern seen in Experiment 1, Experiment\n2b\nfailed to replicate the symmetrical interaction between face sex and expression on expression and sex categorization seen for the larger picture set used in Experiment\n2a\n. Rather, an asymmetrical interaction was found, in that poser sex affected the categorization of emotional expressions, whereas expression did not affect the categorization of faces by sex. This pattern deviates from that seen in Experiment\n1b\n, in which no interaction was observed in either sex or expression categorization, but is consistent with the pattern of results reported in studies that had employed the Garner paradigm (Atkinson et al.,\n2005\n; Karnadewi & Lipp,\n2011\n)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors compare the small-set asymmetrical result to these published results as consistent; absence of symmetry is the selected claim tested.""}"
10.3758/s13414-015-0849-x,ae78ae9e5572ca14,3,10.3758/BF03195789,Evaluating the independence of sex and expression in judgments of faces.,"Le Gal, P. M., & Bruce, V.",2002,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 3, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""Experiment\n1b\nfailed to replicate the pattern of results reported by Aguado et al. (\n2009\n), in that no interaction between poser sex and emotional expression was found if participants were presented repeatedly with a subset of the faces used by Aguado et al. and in Experiment\n1a\n. This evidence for independent processing of sex and emotion cues is consistent with the results reported by Le Gal and Bruce (\n2002\n)."", ""location"": ""Experiment 1b Discussion (lines 349-357)"", ""reason"": ""Le Gal and Bruce (2002) is not re-tested by this paper. The source never specifies Le Gal and Bruce's stimulus set or procedure, lumps it with Atkinson et al. (2005) and Karnadewi and Lipp (2011) despite their differing empirical results solely to motivate predicting the absence of Aguado et al.'s (2009) symmetrical interaction with four faces, and explicitly frames Experiment 1b as a failed replication of Aguado et al. (2009) whose null result is merely consistent with Le Gal and Bruce (2002).""}","{""authors"": ""Le Gal, P. M., & Bruce, V."", ""year"": ""2002"", ""title"": ""Evaluating the independence of sex and expression in judgments of faces."", ""doi"": ""10.3758/BF03195789"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1b, 2b"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Experiment\n2b\nwas designed to assess whether the same symmetrical pattern of results would emerge if a smaller set of individuals drawn from the new face set was used. On the basis of the results reported by Le Gal and Bruce (\n2002\n), Atkinson et al. (\n2005\n), Karnadewi and Lipp (\n2011\n), and in Experiment\n1b\n, no symmetrical interaction was expected."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Resembling the pattern seen in Experiment 1, Experiment\n2b\nfailed to replicate the symmetrical interaction between face sex and expression on expression and sex categorization seen for the larger picture set used in Experiment\n2a\n. Rather, an asymmetrical interaction was found, in that poser sex affected the categorization of emotional expressions, whereas expression did not affect the categorization of faces by sex. This pattern deviates from that seen in Experiment\n1b\n, in which no interaction was observed in either sex or expression categorization, but is consistent with the pattern of results reported in studies that had employed the Garner paradigm (Atkinson et al.,\n2005\n; Karnadewi & Lipp,\n2011\n)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Experiment 1b agrees with the original independence result; Experiment 2b instead has an asymmetrical interaction, explicitly contrasted with 1b. Outcome uncertainty concerns whether the targeted claim is complete independence or just absence of symmetry.""}"
10.1037/xhp0000180,1724e3b3976272af,1,10.1016/j.visres.2008.02.021,Intertrial target-feature changes do not lead to more distraction by singletons: Target uncertainty does.,"Lamy, D., & Yashar, A.",2008,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Lamy, D., & Yashar, A."", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.visres.2008.02.021"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Intertrial target-feature changes do not lead to more distraction by singletons: Target uncertainty does."", ""year"": ""2008""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""1724e3b3976272af"", ""location"": ""Results (Experiment 1, Intertrial analysis of target features), lines 432-437; References, lines 881-885"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Furthermore, RTs were shorter when the target shape was repeated\nthan when it changed (881 vs. 915 ms), F(1, 31) ⫽ 21.18, p ⬍\n.001, ␩p2 ⫽ 0.406, but there was no interaction with distractor\npresence (p ⫽ .505), confirming that repetition of the task-relevant\nshape feature did not affect interference from the distractor (Lamy\n& Yashar, 2008)."", ""reason"": ""In Experiment 1 Results (lines 422-437), the authors test whether repeating vs. changing the task-relevant target shape modulates interference from the salient distractor when distractor presence is randomly varied (test phase), finding a main effect of target shape repetition (881 vs. 915 ms, F(1, 31) = 21.18, p < .001) with no distractor-presence interaction (p = .505), and explicitly state this confirms that repetition of the task-relevant shape feature did not affect interference from the distractor (Lamy & Yashar, 2008). The only shipped row for this source work (input_row_index 2132) is Leber & Egeth (2006, 10.3758/bf03193824), which is a different paper and effect. Therefore, Lamy & Yashar (2008) is a genuinely absent replication-original pair."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0007_1724e3b3976272af"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Lamy, D., & Yashar, A."", ""year"": ""2008"", ""title"": ""Intertrial target-feature changes do not lead to more distraction by singletons: Target uncertainty does."", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.visres.2008.02.021"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Furthermore, RTs were shorter when the target shape was repeated than when it changed (881 vs. 915 ms), F(1, 31) ⫽ 21.18, p ⬍ .001, ␩p2 ⫽ 0.406, but there was no interaction with distractor presence (p ⫽ .505), confirming that repetition of the task-relevant shape feature did not affect interference from the distractor (Lamy & Yashar, 2008)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Furthermore, RTs were shorter when the target shape was repeated than when it changed (881 vs. 915 ms), F(1, 31) ⫽ 21.18, p ⬍ .001, ␩p2 ⫽ 0.406, but there was no interaction with distractor presence (p ⫽ .505), confirming that repetition of the task-relevant shape feature did not affect interference from the distractor (Lamy & Yashar, 2008)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The task-relevant target-shape repetition analysis explicitly confirms the original null moderation with randomly mixed distractor presence.""}"
10.1037/xhp0000180,1724e3b3976272af,2,10.1037/a0027629,"Top-down control of attention: It’s gradual, practice-dependent, and hierarchically organized.","Zehetleitner, M., Goschy, H., & Müller, H. J.",2012,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Zehetleitner, M., Goschy, H., & Müller, H. J."", ""doi"": ""10.1037/a0027629"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Top-down control of attention: It’s gradual, practice-dependent, and hierarchically organized."", ""year"": ""2012""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""1724e3b3976272af"", ""location"": ""Results (Experiment 1, Analysis of practice effects), lines 480-487; References, lines 1004-1009"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""The interaction\nof practice and distractor, F(1, 60) ⫽ 14.42, p ⬍ .001, ␩p2 ⫽ 0.194,\nwas modulated by the triple interaction of search group, practice\nand distractor, F(1, 60) ⫽ 9.09, p ⫽ .004, ␩p2 ⫽ 0.132, showing\nthat the distractor effect decreased from the first to the second half\nin the singleton group (117 vs. 77 ms), but not in the feature group\n(26 vs. 21 ms), which replicates the work of Zehetleitner et al.\n(2012)."", ""reason"": ""In Experiment 1 Results (lines 470-487), the authors analyze practice effects on distractor interference across the first and second halves of the block and find a significant three-way interaction of search group, practice, and distractor (F(1, 60) = 9.09, p = .004), showing that the distractor effect decreased from the first to the second half in the singleton group (117 vs. 77 ms) but not in the feature group (26 vs. 21 ms), explicitly stating that this 'replicates the work of Zehetleitner et al. (2012).' The only shipped row for this source work (input_row_index 2132) is Leber & Egeth (2006, 10.3758/bf03193824). Thus, Zehetleitner et al. (2012) is a genuinely absent replication-original pair."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0007_1724e3b3976272af"", ""target_index"": 2, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Zehetleitner, M., Goschy, H., & Müller, H. J."", ""year"": ""2012"", ""title"": ""Top-down control of attention: It’s gradual, practice-dependent, and hierarchically organized."", ""doi"": ""10.1037/a0027629"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1"", ""evidence_quote"": ""The interaction of practice and distractor, F(1, 60) ⫽ 14.42, p ⬍ .001, ␩p2 ⫽ 0.194, was modulated by the triple interaction of search group, practice and distractor, F(1, 60) ⫽ 9.09, p ⫽ .004, ␩p2 ⫽ 0.132, showing that the distractor effect decreased from the first to the second half in the singleton group (117 vs. 77 ms), but not in the feature group (26 vs. 21 ms), which replicates the work of Zehetleitner et al. (2012)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The interaction of practice and distractor, F(1, 60) ⫽ 14.42, p ⬍ .001, ␩p2 ⫽ 0.194, was modulated by the triple interaction of search group, practice and distractor, F(1, 60) ⫽ 9.09, p ⫽ .004, ␩p2 ⫽ 0.132, showing that the distractor effect decreased from the first to the second half in the singleton group (117 vs. 77 ms), but not in the feature group (26 vs. 21 ms), which replicates the work of Zehetleitner et al. (2012)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Practice analysis explicitly replicates the original decrease of interference in singleton search but not feature search.""}"
10.1037/xhp0000180,1724e3b3976272af,3,10.3758/s13423-012-0280-4,Experience-dependent attentional tuning of distractor rejection.,"Vatterott, D. B., & Vecera, S. P.",2012,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Vatterott, D. B., & Vecera, S. P."", ""doi"": ""10.3758/s13423-012-0280-4"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Experience-dependent attentional tuning of distractor rejection."", ""year"": ""2012""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""1724e3b3976272af"", ""location"": ""Results (Experiment 1, Analysis of practice effects), lines 492-502; References, lines 991-994"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Vatterott and Vecera (2012) showed that interference occurred\nin feature search in the initial 24 trials, but not in the following 24\ntrials. Because participants in the present study started with at least\n30 practice trials including both color distractors, it was unlikely\nthat this pattern would replicate in the present study. To be sure,\nwe ran the same ANOVA as in the previous section, but compared\nTrials 1–24 to Trials 25– 48 instead of the first to the second half\nof the block (with 240 trials each). A significant interaction of trial\nnumber and distractor presence would confirm rapid attentional\ntuning to the distractor, but this interaction was neither significant\nduring training (p ⫽ .638) nor during test (p ⫽ .229)."", ""reason"": ""In Experiment 1 Results (lines 492-502), the authors explicitly test whether the rapid attentional tuning pattern reported by Vatterott & Vecera (2012)—where distractor interference in feature search occurred in Trials 1-24 but disappeared in Trials 25-48—replicated in their dataset by running an ANOVA comparing Trials 1-24 to Trials 25-48, finding no significant interaction of trial number and distractor presence during training (p = .638) or test (p = .229). The only shipped row for this source work (input_row_index 2132) is Leber & Egeth (2006, 10.3758/bf03193824). Therefore, Vatterott & Vecera (2012) is a genuinely absent replication-original pair."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0007_1724e3b3976272af"", ""target_index"": 3, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Vatterott, D. B., & Vecera, S. P."", ""year"": ""2012"", ""title"": ""Experience-dependent attentional tuning of distractor rejection."", ""doi"": ""10.3758/s13423-012-0280-4"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Vatterott and Vecera (2012) showed that interference occurred in feature search in the initial 24 trials, but not in the following 24 trials. Because participants in the present study started with at least 30 practice trials including both color distractors, it was unlikely that this pattern would replicate in the present study. To be sure, we ran the same ANOVA as in the previous section, but compared Trials 1–24 to Trials 25– 48 instead of the first to the second half of the block (with 240 trials each). A significant interaction of trial number and distractor presence would confirm rapid attentional tuning to the distractor, but this interaction was neither significant during training (p ⫽ .638) nor during test (p ⫽ .229)."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Vatterott and Vecera (2012) showed that interference occurred in feature search in the initial 24 trials, but not in the following 24 trials. Because participants in the present study started with at least 30 practice trials including both color distractors, it was unlikely that this pattern would replicate in the present study. To be sure, we ran the same ANOVA as in the previous section, but compared Trials 1–24 to Trials 25– 48 instead of the first to the second half of the block (with 240 trials each). A significant interaction of trial number and distractor presence would confirm rapid attentional tuning to the distractor, but this interaction was neither significant during training (p ⫽ .638) nor during test (p ⫽ .229)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors expressly tested whether the original rapid tuning pattern replicated and found no corresponding interaction. They anticipated nonreplication after practice but did not call the attempt uninformative.""}"
10.1017/s1930297500008299,6f46281e9e4fbbbb,1,10.3758/BF03211332,Another look at reasons for choosing and rejecting.,"Wedell, D. H.",1997,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""To test the accentuation hypothesis, we conducted binary logistic mixed-eﬀects regression analysis. In this analysis, we included responses from Problem 1 to 7, as these problems shared the common procedure of choosing between two alternatives (binary choice set). We followed Wedell’s (1997) approach to calculate the overall proportion of subjects (across experimental conditions) preferring the enriched alternative for each of the seven problems, as a measure of the overall relative attractiveness of the enriched alternative."", ""location"": ""Section 3.5 Extension: Testing the accentuation hypothesis, p. 48"", ""reason"": ""Inspection of the title, abstract, introduction (Sections 1.1 and 1.2), method (Section 2.3), results (Section 3.5), discussion (Section 4.1), and reference list confirms that Shafir (1993) is the sole empirical target article re-tested in this replication. All eight decision scenarios and experimental stimuli were taken exclusively from Shafir (1993) (Section 2.3, p. 42). The paper never states any empirical finding, study, sample, or effect from Wedell (1997) to compare against; rather, it introduces Wedell (1997) as proposing a theoretical resolution (the accentuation hypothesis) of the conflicting findings between Shafir (1993) and Ganzach (1995) (Section 1.2, p. 40) and applies Wedell's (1997) analytical procedure—calculating overall enriched-option preference across conditions and running binary logistic mixed-effects regressions alongside new continuous attractiveness ratings—to the responses from Problems 1–7 of Shafir (1993) as an extension (Section 3.5, pp. 48–50; Section 4.1, p. 53). Under the codebook (Step 2 and Step 3 Rule 2), applying an analytical approach or testing agreement with a theoretical account on another target's stimuli without re-testing a stated published empirical finding of that work does not make Wedell (1997) a replication target.""}","{""authors"": ""Wedell, D. H."", ""year"": ""1997"", ""title"": ""Another look at reasons for choosing and rejecting."", ""doi"": ""10.3758/BF03211332"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We tested the competing theoretical assertion by Wedell (1997). Our results in support of this account suggest that the stimuli from the 1990s are still of relevance, at least for testing that account."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We tested the competing theoretical assertion by Wedell (1997). Our results in support of this account suggest that the stimuli from the 1990s are still of relevance, at least for testing that account."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true}"
10.1016/j.jecp.2015.12.009,eb5d4130a63b4193,0,,,Borst et al.,2015,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Borst et al."", ""year"": ""2015"", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""A previous study demonstrated that preventing mirror errors in reading requires the inhibition of the mirror generalization process in expert adult readers (Borst et al., 2015). Using the same experimental paradigm, the current study aimed at replicating this result in school-aged children."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Crucially, children required more time to determine that two animals facing opposite directions were identical when preceded by two letters that were lateral mirror images of one another (b/d) than when preceded by letters that were not mirror images of one another (f/t). Importantly, the amplitude of the negative priming effect did not vary with age. Our results suggest that overcoming mirror errors in reading, regardless of the reading proficiency of school-aged children, is rooted in the ability to inhibit the mirror generalization process."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract states the original inhibitory mechanism and reports the corresponding negative priming result in the new sample. The proposed age effect is an extension rather than a contradicted original result.""}"
10.31234/osf.io/prfm3,2480054a833f1b61,0,,,Kleiman and Rule,2013,abstract,,correct,argon,"{""corrected_value"": null, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""2480054a833f1b61"", ""location"": ""abstract"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Kleiman and Rule (2013) demonstrated that naive observers could categorize static images at above chance levels according to suicidality and that the faces of individuals who died by suicide were rated as more impulsive than control faces. We attempted to replicate these results with a larger sample and extended the original report by implementing a 2AFC paradigm that minimizes response bias."", ""reason"": ""Prior adjudication and state files are absent. The blind target ('Kleiman and Rule', 2013, coded from an abstract-only OSF preprint record without a reference list) is already represented by shipped row 2147 (Kleiman & Rule, 10.1177/1948550612466115, 'Detecting Suicidality From Facial Appearance', Social Psychological and Personality Science, Vol. 4, Issue 4, pp. 453–460). Crossref metadata confirms that 2012 is the OnlineFirst publication year (November 16, 2012) and 2013 is the print issue year for the same original paper."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0012_2480054a833f1b61"", ""target_index"": 0, ""verdict"": ""shipped_right""}","{""authors"": ""Kleiman and Rule"", ""year"": ""2013"", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Kleiman and Rule (2013) demonstrated that naive observers could categorize static images at above chance levels according to suicidality and that the faces of individuals who died by suicide were rated as more impulsive than control faces. We attempted to replicate these results with a larger sample and extended the original report by implementing a 2AFC paradigm that minimizes response bias."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We found that suicidality categorization was above-chance using the original testing paradigm, but performance dropped to chance in our 2AFC task. We found no evidence supporting differences in perceived impulsivity across categories in either paradigm."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Original paradigm reproduced suicidality classification; the alternative paradigm did not, and the original impulsivity difference was unsupported in both.""}"
10.3389/fpsyg.2016.01930,1afa9693a775a922,1,10.3758/s13414-013-0430-4,Continuous hand movement induces a far-hand bias in attentional priority.,Festman Y. Adam J. J. Pratt J. Fischer M. H.,2013,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,"{""file_key"": ""1afa9693a775a922"", ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""target_index"": 1, ""method"": ""obvious"", ""verdict"": ""undeterminable"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""We observed evidence for a directionality effect in the spatial gap discrimination task, as reflected in enhanced performance when moving toward as compared to moving away from the probe, which provides a replication of the previously established directionality effects in spatial tasks ( Festman et al., 2013a , b )."", ""location"": ""Independent parent primary context and original-report chain, integration/original_report_chain_0000.result.json"", ""reason"": ""Current article jointly attributes one retested directionality finding to2013aand2013b; original2013bexplicitly repeats2013afar-hand effect in a new sample before its static-left-hand extension. The codebook selects closest actual retested chain finding. Confirm2013b separately from shippedGozli2012, but do not list2013a again merely because it has another DOI/new original experiment. Current right-hand-response/left-in-lap protocol resemblesa, while joint prediction citesb. This leaves an actual closest-chain versus separate-target ambiguity after full original inspection; keepa unresolved rather than affirming a second genuinely absent pair or forcing a background exclusion.""}","{""authors"": ""Festman Y. Adam J. J. Pratt J. Fischer M. H."", ""year"": ""2013"", ""title"": ""Continuous hand movement induces a far-hand bias in attentional priority."", ""doi"": ""10.3758/s13414-013-0430-4"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Furthermore, on the basis of the direction effect of Festman et al. (2013a , b ), we predicted better discrimination when moving toward the probe in both tasks."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In addition, we obtained (weak) statistical evidence for a directionality effect, that is, enhanced performance when moving toward compared to away from the probe, but only in spatial gap discrimination."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Directionality was predicted in both tasks, and authors explicitly report it only in spatial discrimination. They call spatial directionality a replication of both Festman papers; aggregate across the two experiments is mixed.""}"
10.3389/fpsyg.2016.01930,1afa9693a775a922,2,10.3389/fpsyg.2013.00657,Both hand position and movement direction modulate visual attention.,Festman Y. Adam J. J. Pratt J. Fischer M. H.,2013,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Festman Y. Adam J. J. Pratt J. Fischer M. H."", ""doi"": ""10.3389/fpsyg.2013.00657"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Both hand position and movement direction modulate visual attention."", ""year"": ""2013""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""1afa9693a775a922"", ""location"": ""Discussion; Introduction; Results"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""We observed evidence for a directionality effect in the spatial gap discrimination task, as reflected in enhanced performance when moving toward as compared to moving away from the probe, which provides a replication of the previously established directionality effects in spatial tasks ( Festman et al., 2013a , b )."", ""reason"": ""No prior adjudication or state file exists for this work. The authors explicitly predict and empirically test the movement direction effect (enhanced discrimination when moving the right hand toward versus away from the probe while the left hand remains at rest) from Festman et al. (2013b, Frontiers in Psychology 4:657, DOI: 10.3389/fpsyg.2013.00657) across Experiment 1 (temporal gap discrimination) and Experiment 2 (spatial gap discrimination), explicitly stating in Results and Discussion that the spatial gap discrimination findings reproduce and provide 'a replication of the previously established directionality effects in spatial tasks ( Festman et al., 2013a , b )'. This work is absent from shipped row 2153 (Gozli, West & Pratt, 2012, DOI: 10.1016/j.cognition.2012.04.008), which tested static hand proximity effects, and is a distinct empirical paper from sibling target_index 1 (Festman et al., 2013a, DOI: 10.3758/s13414-013-0430-4), as described in the Introduction ('The interplay of near-hand and direction effects was examined in a subsequent study by Festman et al. (2013b) , in which the right hand moved below the display, while the left hand remained stationary on the side')."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0007_1afa9693a775a922"", ""target_index"": 2, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Festman Y. Adam J. J. Pratt J. Fischer M. H."", ""year"": ""2013"", ""title"": ""Both hand position and movement direction modulate visual attention."", ""doi"": ""10.3389/fpsyg.2013.00657"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Furthermore, on the basis of the direction effect of Festman et al. (2013a , b ), we predicted better discrimination when moving toward the probe in both tasks."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In addition, we obtained (weak) statistical evidence for a directionality effect, that is, enhanced performance when moving toward compared to away from the probe, but only in spatial gap discrimination."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Directionality was predicted in both tasks, and authors explicitly report it only in spatial discrimination. They call spatial directionality a replication of both Festman papers; aggregate across the two experiments is mixed.""}"
10.1353/eca.2016.0000,2050e50097e7846c,0,,How Elastic Are Preferences for Redistribution? Evidence from Randomized Survey Experiments.,"Kuziemko, Ilyana, Michael I. Norton, Emmanuel Saez, and Stefanie Stantcheva.",2013,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Kuziemko, Ilyana, Michael I. Norton, Emmanuel Saez, and Stefanie Stantcheva."", ""year"": ""2013"", ""title"": ""How Elastic Are Preferences for Redistribution? Evidence from Randomized Survey Experiments."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In reality, demand for\nincome redistribution in the United States has remained ﬂat by some mea-\nsures and decreased according to others (see Kuziemko and others 2013),\nas we document later in this paper.\n\nAcross the four measures, we are able\nto replicate the ﬁnding of previous literature showing no increase in sup-\nport for redistribution over this period of increased inequality."", ""outcome"": """", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": ""not checked"", ""outcome_computational_quote"": ""To ensure that our heterogeneous patterns\nare not driven by data or coding differences between our paper and previ-\nous work, we ﬁrst demonstrate that we can replicate the earlier ﬁnding of\nﬂat aggregate demand using our survey measures."", ""outcome_robustness"": ""robust"", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": ""Across the four measures, we are able\nto replicate the ﬁnding of previous literature showing no increase in sup-\nport for redistribution over this period of increased inequality."", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The earlier flat aggregate trend survives alternative measures and survey datasets. Exact original numerical estimates are not the object of the analysis.""}"
10.23668/psycharchives.4766,3617489aca51758c,0,10.5951/AT.32.5.0051,A study of math anxiety/math avoidance in preservice elementary teachers.,"Kelly, W. P., & Tomhave, W. K.",1985,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 0, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""The current study has two aims: The first aim is to conceptually replicate the finding of the meta-analysis by Hembree (1990) that math anxiety is higher for elementary school teachers than for students from other fields."", ""location"": ""introduction, p. 278"", ""reason"": ""The paper explicitly names Hembree (1990) as the target of its replication aim; Kelly & Tomhave (1985) is cited as supporting literature rather than an independent replication target.""}","{""authors"": ""Kelly, W. P., & Tomhave, W. K."", ""year"": ""1985"", ""title"": ""A study of math anxiety/math avoidance in preservice elementary teachers."", ""doi"": ""10.5951/AT.32.5.0051"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The current study replicated the finding that female elementary school teachers have a higher level of math anxiety in relation to female students from other fields (Hembree, 1990; Kelly & Tomhave, 1985; Szczygieł & Cipora, 2016)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The current study replicated the finding that female elementary school teachers have a higher level of math anxiety in relation to female students from other fields (Hembree, 1990; Kelly & Tomhave, 1985; Szczygieł & Cipora, 2016)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly compare their own result with this cited original finding. The stated agreement or disagreement determines this target’s outcome.""}"
