_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly compare their own result with this cited original finding. The stated agreement or disagreement determines this target’s outcome.""}"
10.23668/psycharchives.4766,3617489aca51758c,2,10.1080/1354060042000337084,"Mathematics anxiety, prior experience and confidence to teach mathematics among pre-service education students.","Brady, P., & Bowd, A.",2005,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 2, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""The current study has two aims: The first aim is to conceptually replicate the finding of the meta-analysis by Hembree (1990) that math anxiety is higher for elementary school teachers than for students from other fields. The replication is relevant because the math anxiety of elementary school teachers has consequences for their pupils and it is necessary because previous studies have reported divergent levels of math anxiety, mostly lacked a reference sample, and almost exclusively were based on pre-service teachers. The second aim addresses whether all elementary school teachers experience math anxiety by the consideration of gender, specialization, and experience."", ""location"": ""introduction, p. 278"", ""reason"": ""Brady & Bowd (2005) is cited as background literature and in post-hoc discussion comparisons for the second aim (moderators and teaching attitudes), not as a replication target.""}","{""authors"": ""Brady, P., & Bowd, A."", ""year"": ""2005"", ""title"": ""Mathematics anxiety, prior experience and confidence to teach mathematics among pre-service education students."", ""doi"": ""10.1080/1354060042000337084"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Among elementary school teachers, female teachers were found to express more math anxiety than male teachers. This corroborates gender effects on math anxiety in general (Hembree, 1990) as well as in particular for elementary school teachers (Brady & Bowd, 2005; Kelly & Tomhave, 1985)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Among elementary school teachers, female teachers were found to express more math anxiety than male teachers. This corroborates gender effects on math anxiety in general (Hembree, 1990) as well as in particular for elementary school teachers (Brady & Bowd, 2005; Kelly & Tomhave, 1985)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly compare their own result with this cited original finding. The stated agreement or disagreement determines this target’s outcome.""}"
10.23668/psycharchives.4766,3617489aca51758c,3,10.1111/j.1949-8594.2006.tb18073.x,Mathematics anxiety and preservice elementary teachers’ confidence to teach mathematics and science.,"Bursal, M., & Paznokas, L.",2006,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 3, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""The current study has two aims: The first aim is to conceptually replicate the finding of the meta-analysis by Hembree (1990) that math anxiety is higher for elementary school teachers than for students from other fields. The replication is relevant because the math anxiety of elementary school teachers has consequences for their pupils and it is necessary because previous studies have reported divergent levels of math anxiety, mostly lacked a reference sample, and almost exclusively were based on pre-service teachers. The second aim addresses whether all elementary school teachers experience math anxiety by the consideration of gender, specialization, and experience."", ""location"": ""introduction, p. 278"", ""reason"": ""Bursal & Paznokas (2006) is cited only as background and literature context regarding confidence in teaching math under the second aim, not as a replicated original study.""}","{""authors"": ""Bursal, M., & Paznokas, L."", ""year"": ""2006"", ""title"": ""Mathematics anxiety and preservice elementary teachers’ confidence to teach mathematics and science."", ""doi"": ""10.1111/j.1949-8594.2006.tb18073.x"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Nevertheless, our results were consistent with previous studies showing the relation of math anxiety to confidence (Brady & Bowd, 2005; Bursal & Paznokas, 2006), efficacy (Gresham, 2008; Jaggernauth & Jameson-Charles, 2015; Swars et al., 2006) and anxiety of teaching math (Ganley et al., 2019; Olson & Stoehr, 2019)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Nevertheless, our results were consistent with previous studies showing the relation of math anxiety to confidence (Brady & Bowd, 2005; Bursal & Paznokas, 2006), efficacy (Gresham, 2008; Jaggernauth & Jameson-Charles, 2015; Swars et al., 2006) and anxiety of teaching math (Ganley et al., 2019; Olson & Stoehr, 2019)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly compare their own result with this cited original finding. The stated agreement or disagreement determines this target’s outcome.""}"
10.23668/psycharchives.4766,3617489aca51758c,4,10.1177/2332858419839702,The construct validation of the math anxiety scale for teachers.,"Ganley, C. M., Schoen, R. C., LaVenia, M., & Tazaz, A. M.",2019,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 4, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""The current study has two aims: The first aim is to conceptually replicate the finding of the meta-analysis by Hembree (1990) that math anxiety is higher for elementary school teachers than for students from other fields. The replication is relevant because the math anxiety of elementary school teachers has consequences for their pupils and it is necessary because previous studies have reported divergent levels of math anxiety, mostly lacked a reference sample, and almost exclusively were based on pre-service teachers. The second aim addresses whether all elementary school teachers experience math anxiety by the consideration of gender, specialization, and experience."", ""location"": ""introduction, p. 278"", ""reason"": ""Ganley et al. (2019) is cited for background and literature comparison regarding specialization and teaching attitudes under the paper's second aim, not as a replication target.""}","{""authors"": ""Ganley, C. M., Schoen, R. C., LaVenia, M., & Tazaz, A. M."", ""year"": ""2019"", ""title"": ""The construct validation of the math anxiety scale for teachers."", ""doi"": ""10.1177/2332858419839702"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""As expected, math anxiety was lower both in German teachers majoring in math compared to those not majoring in math during their studies of elementary school education (Ganley et al., 2019; Malinsky et al., 2006; Porsch, 2017) and in Belgian teachers who specialized in math during secondary school compared to those who chose an educational trajectory containing less math (see the Appendix, Table A4)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""As expected, math anxiety was lower both in German teachers majoring in math compared to those not majoring in math during their studies of elementary school education (Ganley et al., 2019; Malinsky et al., 2006; Porsch, 2017) and in Belgian teachers who specialized in math during secondary school compared to those who chose an educational trajectory containing less math (see the Appendix, Table A4)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly compare their own result with this cited original finding. The stated agreement or disagreement determines this target’s outcome.""}"
