10.1002/jaba.613,b99fe2c5d5675e4b,1,10.1002/jaba.67,Effects of two variations of differential reinforcement on prompt dependency,"Cividini-Motta, C., & Ahearn, W. H.",2013,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Cividini-Motta, C., & Ahearn, W. H."", ""year"": ""2013"", ""title"": ""Effects of two variations of differential reinforcement on prompt dependency"", ""doi"": ""10.1002/jaba.67"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The current research sought to systematically replicate Smaby et al. (2007) and Cividini-Motta and Ahearn’s (2013) use of the MSA, speci ﬁ cally to evaluate the relative reinforcing ef ﬁ cacy of the individual components of a token economy in a brief assessment."", ""outcome"": ""descriptive only"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The current research sought to systematically replicate Smaby et al. (2007) and Cividini-Motta and Ahearn’s (2013) use of the MSA, speci ﬁ cally to evaluate the relative reinforcing ef ﬁ cacy of the individual components of a token economy in a brief assessment."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""introduction"", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors explicitly replicate use of the assessment in a token-economy application. Discussion confirms utility of the method but does not compare the specific earlier social-reinforcer or prompt-dependency effects. Methodological success alone is not a finding comparison.""}"
10.1152/jn.00412.2024,7b36096465800442,1,10.1016/j.neurobiolaging.2019.03.020,Internal model recalibration does not deteriorate with age while motor adaptation does.,"Vandevoorde K, Orban de Xivry JJ.",2019,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Vandevoorde K, Orban de Xivry JJ."", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.neurobiolaging.2019.03.020"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Internal model recalibration does not deteriorate with age while motor adaptation does."", ""year"": ""2019""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""7b36096465800442"", ""location"": ""Introduction, final paragraph (line 93); Materials and Methods, 'Statistical Analysis' (line 272); Results, 'Adaptation Does Not Significantly Deteriorate with Age' (lines 322-357); Discussion (lines 515-525); References, entry 27 (line 632)."", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Therefore, we set out to measure both implicit adaptation and its retention via spontaneous recovery in a single force-ﬁeld paradigm in both healthy young and older adults to test four different hypotheses, reﬂecting previous research: 1) implicit adaptation levels at the end of the adaptation period are similar across age groups (27), 2) spontaneous recovery is larger in young than in older participants (37), 3) implicit adaptation at the end of the adaptation period is correlated with the amount of spontaneous recovery (45), and 4) as suggested by Trewartha and colleagues, spontaneous recovery is related to explicit memory processes such as working memory capacity."", ""reason"": ""No prior adjudication or state files exist for this work. In 7b36096465800442 (Hermans et al., 2025), the authors explicitly design their force-field adaptation study in healthy young (n = 28) and older (n = 20) adults to empirically test four hypotheses reflecting prior research, where Hypothesis 1 is: '1) implicit adaptation levels at the end of the adaptation period are similar across age groups (27)'—citing Reference 27, Vandevoorde & Orban de Xivry (2019) (Neurobiol Aging 80: 138-153, DOI 10.1016/j.neurobiolaging.2019.03.020). They preregister and execute dedicated statistical tests for Hypothesis 1 (Analyses 1, 2, and 3 in Statistical Analysis and Results) and conclude in Results and Discussion that implicit adaptation does not deteriorate with age, confirming Vandevoorde & Orban de Xivry (2019). Only Reference 37 (Trewartha et al., 2014, DOI 10.1523/jneurosci.1489-14.2014, row 2324) was shipped. Note that Luna's mechanical quote validation error occurred only because Luna normalized author initials ('J-J.' instead of 'JJ.') and ligatures in the reference quotes; the substantive omission is fully verified in the source text."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0060_7b36096465800442"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Vandevoorde K, Orban de Xivry JJ."", ""year"": ""2019"", ""title"": ""Internal model recalibration does not deteriorate with age while motor adaptation does."", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.neurobiolaging.2019.03.020"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Therefore, we set out to measure both implicit adaptation and its retention via spontaneous recovery in a single force-ﬁeld paradigm in both healthy young and older adults to test four different hypotheses, reﬂecting previous research: 1) implicit adaptation levels at the end of the adaptation period are similar across age groups (27), 2) spontaneous recovery is larger in young than in older participants (37), 3) implicit adaptation at the end of the adaptation period is correlated with the amount of spontaneous recovery (45), and 4) as suggested by Trewartha and colleagues, spontaneous recovery is related to explicit memory processes such as working memory capacity."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Across age groups, we observed little difference in performance in this task. Both total adaptation and implicit adaptation were not impaired in older adults compared with their younger controls."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Hypothesis 1 explicitly re-tests reference 27: preserved implicit adaptation with age. New sample shows the same finding.""}"
10.1152/jn.00412.2024,7b36096465800442,2,10.1523/JNEUROSCI.5061-14.2015,Explicit and implicit processes constitute the fast and slow processes of sensorimotor learning.,"McDougle SD, Bond KM, Taylor JA.",2015,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""McDougle SD, Bond KM, Taylor JA."", ""doi"": ""10.1523/JNEUROSCI.5061-14.2015"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Explicit and implicit processes constitute the fast and slow processes of sensorimotor learning."", ""year"": ""2015""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""7b36096465800442"", ""location"": ""Introduction, final paragraph (line 93); Materials and Methods, 'Statistical Analysis' (line 286); Results, 'Spontaneous Recovery Directly Scales with Implicit Adaptation' (lines 439-443); Discussion (line 533); References, entry 45 (line 650)."", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Therefore, we set out to measure both implicit adaptation and its retention via spontaneous recovery in a single force-ﬁeld paradigm in both healthy young and older adults to test four different hypotheses, reﬂecting previous research: 1) implicit adaptation levels at the end of the adaptation period are similar across age groups (27), 2) spontaneous recovery is larger in young than in older participants (37), 3) implicit adaptation at the end of the adaptation period is correlated with the amount of spontaneous recovery (45), and 4) as suggested by Trewartha and colleagues, spontaneous recovery is related to explicit memory processes such as working memory capacity."", ""reason"": ""No prior adjudication or state files exist for this work. In 7b36096465800442 (Hermans et al., 2025), the authors explicitly state that their experiment tests four hypotheses reflecting prior research, where Hypothesis 3 is: '3) implicit adaptation at the end of the adaptation period is correlated with the amount of spontaneous recovery (45)'—citing Reference 45, McDougle, Bond, & Taylor (2015) (J Neurosci 35: 9568-9579, DOI 10.1523/JNEUROSCI.5061-14.2015). They test Hypothesis 3 directly via multilevel correlation in Analysis 5 (line 286), report a significant positive correlation in Results ('Spontaneous Recovery Directly Scales with Implicit Adaptation', r = 0.55, P < 0.001, lines 441-443, Fig. 6), and conclude in Discussion that 'This is also consistent with the ﬁndings of McDougle et al. (45)' (line 533). Reference 45 is a distinct original work from shipped row 2324 (Trewartha et al., 2014, DOI 10.1523/jneurosci.1489-14.2014). Luna's mechanical quote check failed only because Luna altered the citation year placement in the reference string; the literal source text confirms this is a genuine missing replication target."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0060_7b36096465800442"", ""target_index"": 2, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""McDougle SD, Bond KM, Taylor JA."", ""year"": ""2015"", ""title"": ""Explicit and implicit processes constitute the fast and slow processes of sensorimotor learning."", ""doi"": ""10.1523/JNEUROSCI.5061-14.2015"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Therefore, we set out to measure both implicit adaptation and its retention via spontaneous recovery in a single force-ﬁeld paradigm in both healthy young and older adults to test four different hypotheses, reﬂecting previous research: 1) implicit adaptation levels at the end of the adaptation period are similar across age groups (27), 2) spontaneous recovery is larger in young than in older participants (37), 3) implicit adaptation at the end of the adaptation period is correlated with the amount of spontaneous recovery (45), and 4) as suggested by Trewartha and colleagues, spontaneous recovery is related to explicit memory processes such as working memory capacity."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""This is consistent with the correlation between the amount of implicit adaptation during learning and the amount of spontaneous recovery (Fig. 6). This is also consistent with the ﬁndings of McDougle et al. (45)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Hypothesis 3 explicitly re-tests reference 45 linking implicit adaptation to spontaneous recovery; discussion explicitly agrees.""}"
10.1111/2041-210x.13602,a6ce639055c5e466,1,10.7554/eLife.46923,Consistent and correctable bias in metagenomic sequencing experiments.,"McLaren, M. R., Willis, A. D., & Callahan, B. J.",2019,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""McLaren, M. R., Willis, A. D., & Callahan, B. J."", ""year"": ""2019"", ""title"": ""Consistent and correctable bias in metagenomic sequencing experiments."", ""doi"": ""10.7554/eLife.46923"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We recovered Elbrecht et al.'s (2017)\nfinding that efficiency is higher when species evenness is higher\n(Figure 2; Figure S1), and we found that OTU sizes are a poor pre-\ndictor of input genomic DNA, which confirms the conventional wis-\ndom that OTU size is a poor predictor of species relative abundances\n(Figure S2; McLaren et al., 2019)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We recovered Elbrecht et al.'s (2017)\nfinding that efficiency is higher when species evenness is higher\n(Figure 2; Figure S1), and we found that OTU sizes are a poor pre-\ndictor of input genomic DNA, which confirms the conventional wis-\ndom that OTU size is a poor predictor of species relative abundances\n(Figure S2; McLaren et al., 2019)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true}"
10.1111/2041-210x.13602,a6ce639055c5e466,2,10.1128/Aem.05220-11,Barcoded primers used in multiplex amplicon pyrosequencing bias amplification.,"Berry, D., Ben Mahfoudh, K., Wagner, M., & Loy, A.",2011,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Berry, D., Ben Mahfoudh, K., Wagner, M., & Loy, A."", ""year"": ""2011"", ""title"": ""Barcoded primers used in multiplex amplicon pyrosequencing bias amplification."", ""doi"": ""10.1128/Aem.05220-11"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We also find no evidence for ‘tag bias’, which is the hypoth-\nesis that the sample-­\nidentifying nucleotide sequences attached to\nPCR primers might promote annealing to some template-­\nDNA se-\nquences over others, exacerbating taxonomic bias in PCR (e.g. Berry\net al., 2011; O'Donnell et al., 2016)."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We found no evidence for tag bias in PCR amplification. For instance,\nunder optimal PCR conditions (A & B), pairs using the same tags (A1/\nB1, A2/B2, A3/B3) and pairs using different tags (e.g. A1/B2, A2/\nB1, A3/B2, …) both generated almost identical NMDS ordinations\n(Figure 3). Under non-­\noptimal PCRs, we still found no evidence for\ntag bias, even though at higher annealing temperatures, some tag\nsequences might be more likely to aid primer annealing (Figures S3\nand S4). Note that we did not correct the p-­\nvalues for three tests,\nunderlining the lack of evidence for tag bias."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_confident"": true}"
10.1111/2041-210x.13602,a6ce639055c5e466,3,10.1371/journal.pone.0148698,Indexed PCR primers induce template-specific bias in large-scale DNA sequencing studies.,"O'Donnell, J. L., Kelly, R. P., Lowell, N. C., & Port, J. A.",2016,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""O'Donnell, J. L., Kelly, R. P., Lowell, N. C., & Port, J. A."", ""year"": ""2016"", ""title"": ""Indexed PCR primers induce template-specific bias in large-scale DNA sequencing studies."", ""doi"": ""10.1371/journal.pone.0148698"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We also find no evidence for ‘tag bias’, which is the hypoth-\nesis that the sample-­\nidentifying nucleotide sequences attached to\nPCR primers might promote annealing to some template-­\nDNA se-\nquences over others, exacerbating taxonomic bias in PCR (e.g. Berry\net al., 2011; O'Donnell et al., 2016)."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We found no evidence for tag bias in PCR amplification. For instance,\nunder optimal PCR conditions (A & B), pairs using the same tags (A1/\nB1, A2/B2, A3/B3) and pairs using different tags (e.g. A1/B2, A2/\nB1, A3/B2, …) both generated almost identical NMDS ordinations\n(Figure 3). Under non-­\noptimal PCRs, we still found no evidence for\ntag bias, even though at higher annealing temperatures, some tag\nsequences might be more likely to aid primer annealing (Figures S3\nand S4). Note that we did not correct the p-­\nvalues for three tests,\nunderlining the lack of evidence for tag bias."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_confident"": true}"
10.1080/02699206.2018.1504989,a3bfced90a859553,1,,Vocal Tract Control in Parkinson's Disease.,"Logemann, J. A. & Fisher, H. B.",1981,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""corrected_value"": null, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""a3bfced90a859553"", ""location"": ""Introduction, pages 3-4 (and Discussion, page 19)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""In Logemann et al (1978; 1981) two speech pathology/ phonetician experts evaluated 200 PwP showing a range of overall motor and speech severities. It is unclear in the 1978 study how many of the 200 actually entered the analyses for articulation, but from the 1981 study it appears clear it was only 90. Participants read eleven sentences. Each sentence elicited words focused on a different place and manner of consonant articulation (e.g. bilabial plosives; alveolar fricatives; palatal-alveolar affricates), aiming to cover all phonemes of English across a variety of syllable and word positions. The 1978 study reported outcomes according to whether a target sound was heard by the speech pathologists as misarticulated or not; the 1981 study examined the same data employing phonetic transcription."", ""reason"": ""1. Report equivalence with shipped row 2332: Read, Miller, & Kitsou (2018) test a single shared claim—that there is a posterior-to-anterior order of loss of speech sound accuracy in Parkinson's disease—jointly attributed to the linked reports of Logemann, Fisher, Boshes, & Blonsky (1978) (retained in shipped input_row_index 2332, 10.1044/jshd.4301.47, and matched by sibling target_index 0) and Logemann & Fisher (1981) (candidate target_index 1). 2. Positive primary-source evidence of shared underlying study/data: The primary text explicitly states on page 3 (and reiterates on page 19) that Logemann et al. (1978) and Logemann & Fisher (1981) are a 'pair of studies... in linked perceptual studies' in which the same two experts evaluated the exact same 200 PwP dataset reading the same 11 sentences ('The 1978 study reported outcomes according to whether a target sound was heard by the speech pathologists as misarticulated or not; the 1981 study examined the same data employing phonetic transcription'). Even the blind coder noted in coding reasoning that 'their original data were shared'. 3. Conclusion: Consistent with the pilot objections on multi-report studies of one underlying experiment/dataset (such as Ebbinghaus 1880/1885 and ENIGMA 2018/2021), Logemann & Fisher (1981) is a companion report of the same original study and finding already represented by shipped input_row_index 2332 (and sibling target_index 0), not an independent missing original study. 4. Prior adjudication: Neither prior_adjudication_path nor prior_state_path exists for this work."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0083_a3bfced90a859553"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""shipped_right""}","{""authors"": ""Logemann, J. A. & Fisher, H. B."", ""year"": ""1981"", ""title"": ""Vocal Tract Control in Parkinson's Disease."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Influential reports on speech changes in people with Parkinson’s disease (Logemann et al, 1978, 1981) reported a posterior to anterior pattern of loss of speech sound accuracy. These claims have never been examined. In a partial replication of Logemann et al’s work we examined whether posterior lingual sounds are most affected in people with Parkinson’s disease, followed by anterior lingual sounds then labial sounds."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""This study sought to re-examine the long propagated assumption that there is a posterior- anterior progression in articulatory breakdown in PD. The current data failed to uphold the assertion."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""fulltext"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Explicit overall conclusion rejects the common posterior-to-anterior assertion in both original papers.""}"
10.1111/j.1467-7687.2004.00322.x,7bc9ff8a5446a5aa,1,,,,,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,"{""corrected_value"": null, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""7bc9ff8a5446a5aa"", ""location"": ""Abstract (lines 25-30)"", ""method"": ""obvious"", ""quote"": ""Childers and Tomasello (2001) found that training 2 1/2 -year-olds on the English transitive construction greatly improves their\n                        performance on a post-test in which they must use novel verbs in that construction. In the current study, we replicated Childers\n                        and Tomasello’s finding, but using a much lower frequency of transitive verbs and models in training. We also used novel verbs\n                        that were of a different semantic class to our training verbs, demonstrating that semantic homogeneity is not crucial for gener-\n                        alization. We also replicated the finding that 4-year-olds are significantly more productive than 2 1/2 -year-olds with the transitive\n                        construction, with the new finding that this is also true for verbs of emission."", ""reason"": ""Parent independently read the complete current AbbotSmith/Lieven/Tomasello2004primary abstract, Introduction, own2.5/4yearage comparison, Discussion and references. The abstract expressly says it also replicated greater transitive productivity at4than2.5years. Its separate Childers/Tomasello2001training target is already represented by2341, while the broader age benchmark is routed through Tomasello2000review and generic prior developmental work, not a uniquely named empirical primary paper. Novel emission-verb content does not by itself erase the broader actual age comparison; conversely a review is not itself an empirical original and a general claim does not prove a separate missingpaper. Available source does not uniquely establish whether this anonymous target is an additional distinct published empirical original or already-covered developmental context. Retain actual original-identity/reporting ambiguity; do not certify absence or a new pair. This supersedes initial categorical exclusion based merely on review citation/generalbenchmark, keeping the initial reply and all spent attempts untouched."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0060_7bc9ff8a5446a5aa"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""undeterminable""}","{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We also replicated the finding that 4-year-olds are significantly more productive than 2 1/2 -year-olds with the transitive construction, with the new finding that this is also true for verbs of emission."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We also replicated the finding that 4-year-olds are significantly more productive than 2 1/2 -year-olds with the transitive construction, with the new finding that this is also true for verbs of emission."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The age finding is explicitly replicated, but the discussion attributes it only to the Tomasello (2000) review. No uniquely identifiable primary original paper is supplied for that age comparison; review excluded as target citation.""}"
