10.1111/bjso.12371,90ac410fbfe5387b,1,10.1371/journal.pone.0088852,The impact of weather on women’s tendency to wear red or pink when at high risk for conception,"Tracy, J. L., & Beall, A. T.",2014,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Tracy, J. L., & Beall, A. T."", ""doi"": ""10.1371/journal.pone.0088852"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""The impact of weather on women’s tendency to wear red or pink when at high risk for conception"", ""year"": ""2014""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""90ac410fbfe5387b"", ""location"": ""introduction, The Present Studies"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Having found inconclusive evidence in Study 1 for an effect of conception risk on the likelihood of wearing red or pink on relatively cold days, in Study 2 (Analysis I) we used data from a large sample collected online between February 2015 and May 2015 to more closely replicate Beall and Tracy (2013) and Tracy and Beall (2014)."", ""reason"": ""No prior adjudication or state files exist at the listed paths. Across Studies 1 and 2 (Analyses I and II), the authors explicitly conduct conceptual, close, and extended replications of both Beall and Tracy (2013) and Tracy and Beall (2014), specifically testing Tracy and Beall's (2014) empirical finding that outdoor temperature moderates the association between conception risk and wearing red or pink clothing (such that the effect emerges on cold days but not warm days). The reference list (and Crossref metadata) identifies this second target as Tracy, J. L., & Beall, A. T. (2014), 'The impact of weather on women’s tendency to wear red or pink when at high risk for conception', PLoS ONE, 9(2), e88852, DOI 10.1371/journal.pone.0088852. The sole shipped row (input_row_index 2359, DOI 10.1177/0956797613476045, sibling target 0) is the earlier Psychological Science paper (Beall & Tracy, 2013), which tested only the unmoderated main effect; as the source text explains, a subsequent failure to replicate that 2013 finding led the original authors to collect new cold- vs. warm-day data in Tracy and Beall (2014). Report equivalence therefore does not apply, and Tracy and Beall (2014) is genuinely absent from the shipped rows."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0072_90ac410fbfe5387b"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Tracy, J. L., & Beall, A. T."", ""year"": ""2014"", ""title"": ""The impact of weather on women’s tendency to wear red or pink when at high risk for conception"", ""doi"": ""10.1371/journal.pone.0088852"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Having found inconclusive evidence in Study 1 for an effect of conception risk on the likelihood of wearing red or pink on relatively cold days, in Study 2 (Analysis I) we used data from a large sample collected online between February 2015 and May 2015 to more closely replicate Beall and Tracy (2013) and Tracy and Beall (2014)."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The studies we presented here, which incorporated conceptual replications, close replications, and extended replications of the original studies reporting that women are more likely to wear red or pink at peak fertility (Beall & Tracy, 2013; Tracy & Beall, 2014), failed to reliably confirm the conjecture that women advertise their ovulatory status by wearing red, even on relatively cold days."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors give an overall failed-to-confirm verdict explicitly covering both original papers and all conceptual, close, and extended attempts. This overall verdict governs despite isolated supportive comparisons.""}"
