10.3389/fpsyg.2016.00630,9ae87d1f87fc5885,1,10.1162/jocn.2008.20099,Involuntary attentional capture is determined by task set: evidence from event-related brain potentials.,"Eimer, M., Kiss, M.",2008,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Eimer, M., Kiss, M."", ""doi"": ""10.1162/jocn.2008.20099"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Involuntary attentional capture is determined by task set: evidence from event-related brain potentials."", ""year"": ""2008""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""9ae87d1f87fc5885"", ""location"": ""Introduction, Electrophysiological Correlates of Suppression"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Previous studies have shown that cues in the target color capture attention, whereas neutral cues do not ( Eimer and Kiss, 2008 ; Lien et al., 2008 ). We hope to replicate this result in the present study."", ""reason"": ""No prior adjudication or state file existed for this work. In Experiment 1, the authors explicitly set out to replicate Eimer and Kiss (2008) by testing whether target-color (go) cues elicit an N2pc component and behavioral cueing benefits while neutral cues do not, and they directly compare and confirm this ERP capture finding in the Experiment 1 Results/Discussion and General Discussion. Shipped row 2426 (Anderson & Folk, 2012, 10.3758/s13414-012-0325-9) is a purely behavioral study of go/nogo cueing costs and is a distinct work from Eimer and Kiss (2008) and sibling targets."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0076_9ae87d1f87fc5885"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Eimer, M., Kiss, M."", ""year"": ""2008"", ""title"": ""Involuntary attentional capture is determined by task set: evidence from event-related brain potentials."", ""doi"": ""10.1162/jocn.2008.20099"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Previous studies have shown that cues in the target color capture attention, whereas neutral cues do not ( Eimer and Kiss, 2008 ; Lien et al., 2008 ). We hope to replicate this result in the present study."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Instead, we observed cueing benefits and an N2pc to cues in the go-color, confirming that response-relevant features capture attention ( Eimer and Kiss, 2008 ; Lien et al., 2008 )."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true}"
10.3389/fpsyg.2016.00630,9ae87d1f87fc5885,2,10.1037/0096-1523.34.3.509,Contingent attentional capture by top-down control settings: converging evidence from event-related potentials.,"Lien, M.-C., Ruthruff, E., Goodin, Z., Remington, R. W.",2008,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Lien, M.-C., Ruthruff, E., Goodin, Z., Remington, R. W."", ""doi"": ""10.1037/0096-1523.34.3.509"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Contingent attentional capture by top-down control settings: converging evidence from event-related potentials."", ""year"": ""2008""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""9ae87d1f87fc5885"", ""location"": ""Introduction, Electrophysiological Correlates of Suppression"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Previous studies have shown that cues in the target color capture attention, whereas neutral cues do not ( Eimer and Kiss, 2008 ; Lien et al., 2008 ). We hope to replicate this result in the present study."", ""reason"": ""No prior adjudication or state file existed for this work. Experiment 1 explicitly re-tests the ERP contingent-capture finding of Lien et al. (2008) that target-matching color cues elicit an N2pc and RT cueing benefits whereas neutral cues do not, confirming the result in the Experiment 1 Results/Discussion and General Discussion. Lien et al. (2008) is an independent study distinct from shipped row 2426 (Anderson & Folk, 2012) and from sibling targets Eimer and Kiss (2008) and Eimer et al. (2009)."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0076_9ae87d1f87fc5885"", ""target_index"": 2, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Lien, M.-C., Ruthruff, E., Goodin, Z., Remington, R. W."", ""year"": ""2008"", ""title"": ""Contingent attentional capture by top-down control settings: converging evidence from event-related potentials."", ""doi"": ""10.1037/0096-1523.34.3.509"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Previous studies have shown that cues in the target color capture attention, whereas neutral cues do not ( Eimer and Kiss, 2008 ; Lien et al., 2008 ). We hope to replicate this result in the present study."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Instead, we observed cueing benefits and an N2pc to cues in the go-color, confirming that response-relevant features capture attention ( Eimer and Kiss, 2008 ; Lien et al., 2008 )."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true}"
10.3389/fpsyg.2016.00630,9ae87d1f87fc5885,3,10.1037/a0015872,The roles of feature-specific task set and bottom-up salience in attentional capture: an ERP study.,"Eimer, M., Kiss, M., Press, C., Sauter, D.",2009,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Eimer, M., Kiss, M., Press, C., Sauter, D."", ""doi"": ""10.1037/a0015872"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""The roles of feature-specific task set and bottom-up salience in attentional capture: an ERP study."", ""year"": ""2009""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""9ae87d1f87fc5885"", ""location"": ""Experiment 1, Introduction"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Previously, Eimer et al. (2009) had observed an enhanced N2 component in response to color-cues that did not match the target cues. Thus, the most straightforward prediction is that an enhanced N2 occurs for neutral and nogo cues alike because both do not match the go-color."", ""reason"": ""No prior adjudication or state file existed for this work. Experiment 1 formulates a direct prediction from Eimer et al.'s (2009) spatial-cueing ERP finding of an enhanced anterior N2 to non-matching color cues relative to target-matching color cues, empirically tests cue-related ERPs at Fz for non-matching (neutral and nogo) versus matching (go) cues in Results, and explicitly compares the observed anterior negativity against Eimer et al. (2009) in the Discussion and General Discussion. This 2009 JEP:HPP article is distinct from shipped row 2426 (Anderson & Folk, 2012) and from the 2008 N2pc studies in targets 1 and 2."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0076_9ae87d1f87fc5885"", ""target_index"": 3, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Eimer, M., Kiss, M., Press, C., Sauter, D."", ""year"": ""2009"", ""title"": ""The roles of feature-specific task set and bottom-up salience in attentional capture: an ERP study."", ""doi"": ""10.1037/a0015872"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Previously, Eimer et al. (2009) had observed an enhanced N2 component in response to color-cues that did not match the target cues. Thus, the most straightforward prediction is that an enhanced N2 occurs for neutral and nogo cues alike because both do not match the go-color."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Of importance, Eimer et al. (2009) showed that the negativity at anterior electrodes was larger with non-matching than matching cues, suggesting that inhibition occurred when cues did not match the top–down set. Similarly, we observed a larger negativity for non-matching nogo cues. However, the negativity was the same for neutral cues, which suggests that the N2 does not reflect inhibition of nogo-stimuli, but rather activation of response-relevant stimuli (see Bruin et al., 2001 )."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true}"
10.1177/21674795231219742,f65b8567ad2e765d,0,,,Anderson; Raney,2018,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Anderson; Raney"", ""year"": ""2018"", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Anderson and Raney (2018) explored this in an experimental study ( n = 234) and found evidence of the Black criminal stereotype among sports fans. However, more research was needed to further explore this phenomenon. The current study employed a similar experimental design ( n = 603) in an attempt to (1) replicate their 2018 study to examine whether sports fans’ perceptions of criminal athletes have changed over the past several years, especially in light of the Black Lives Matter movement, and (2) determine whether the sport played by an alleged criminal athlete might be linked to judgments regarding race and crime in sports."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Our findings indicate that sports fandom still predicts stereotypical judgments."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract states the original found the Black criminal stereotype among sports fans and the replication finds fandom still predicts stereotypical judgments. Stronger alternative predictors do not contradict the stated replicated effect.""}"
