10.1177/147470491100900306,4a9d69d9cd99c0b1,0,,Changes in fat distribution (WHR) and body weight across the menstrual cycle.,"Kirchengast, S., and Gartner, M.",2002,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Kirchengast, S., and Gartner, M."", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Changes in fat distribution (WHR) and body weight across the menstrual cycle."", ""year"": ""2002""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""4a9d69d9cd99c0b1"", ""location"": ""The Current Study (lines 185-188); see also Results (lines 569-574), Discussion (lines 629-632), and References (lines 1091-1093)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""The  objective  of  the  current  research  was  to  provide  an  operational  replication  of \nthe finding that women’s faces are more attractive at high fertility (Roberts et al., 2004) and \nof  the  finding  that  women’s  WHRs  are  lower  at  high  fertility  (Kirchengast  and  Gartner, \n2002)."", ""reason"": ""All three criteria for a genuine missing target are met: (1) the paper explicitly conducts an operational replication of Kirchengast and Gartner's (2002) finding that women's waist-to-hip ratios (WHRs) are lower at high fertility, collecting new waist and hip measurements across high- and low-fertility cycle phases and testing the cycle-phase comparison in Results (lines 541-588, concluding 'Thus, we did not observe the same pattern reported in Kirchengast and Gartner (2002)'); (2) the reference list (lines 1091-1093) establishes the exact bibliographic identity: Kirchengast, S., and Gartner, M. (2002), 'Changes in fat distribution (WHR) and body weight across the menstrual cycle.', Collegium Anthropologicum, 26 (Suppl), 47-57 (no DOI printed); and (3) the sole shipped row (input_row_index '2437', doi_o '10.1098/rsbl.2004.0174') represents only Roberts et al. (2004) on facial attractiveness (sibling blind target_index 1), which is an independent work by different authors in Proceedings of the Royal Society of London B. Prior adjudication and state files (728e7bfe...json and 1d999eaf...json) are absent for this never-adjudicated work."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0036_4a9d69d9cd99c0b1"", ""target_index"": 0, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Kirchengast, S., and Gartner, M."", ""year"": ""2002"", ""title"": ""Changes in fat distribution (WHR) and body weight across the menstrual cycle."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The objective of the current research was to provide an operational replication of the finding that women’s faces are more attractive at high fertility (Roberts et al., 2004) and of the finding that women’s WHRs are lower at high fertility (Kirchengast and Gartner, 2002)."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We designed the current study to replicate two other potential cues of women’s ovulatory status: lower waist-to-hip ratios at high fertility and enhanced facial attractiveness at high fertility. We failed to replicate either effect."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Explicit overall failure verdict for both designated replication targets.""}"
10.1017/s1352465810000676,9b05053aa65fde14,1,,"The relationship between disgust sensitivity, anxiety and obsessions.","Thorpe, S. J., Patel, S. P. and Simonds, L. M.",2003,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Thorpe, S. J., Patel, S. P. and Simonds, L. M."", ""doi"": """", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""The relationship between disgust sensitivity, anxiety and obsessions."", ""year"": ""2003""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""9b05053aa65fde14"", ""location"": ""Results"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""The relationship between DS and OCI-R subscale scores was examined. Thorpe et al.\n(2003) found that DS was correlated with all OCI distress subscales, though most powerfully\nwith the washing distress subscale. In the present study, DS was significantly related to scores\non the washing, ordering and hoarding subscales but not to the three other subscale scores of\nneutralizing, checking and obsessing, although the latter two subtype scores were close to a\nsignificant relationship to DS."", ""reason"": ""No prior adjudication or state files exist for this work. Beyond extending the Jones and Menzies (1997) behavioural avoidance task, the authors explicitly re-test the published questionnaire findings of Thorpe, Patel, and Simonds (2003, Behaviour Research and Therapy, 41, 1397–1409) in dedicated Results subsections examining (1) the relationships and mediation pattern among health anxiety, disgust sensitivity, and OCI-R washing distress scores and (2) the correlations between disgust sensitivity and all OCI-R distress subscales, directly comparing their observed correlations and mediation results against Thorpe et al. (2003). The sole shipped row (input_row_index 2446, 10.1016/s0005-7967(97)00035-1) is Jones and Menzies (1997; target_index 0), an independent 1997 study by different authors that did not examine disgust sensitivity or health anxiety."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0076_9b05053aa65fde14"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Thorpe, S. J., Patel, S. P. and Simonds, L. M."", ""year"": ""2003"", ""title"": ""The relationship between disgust sensitivity, anxiety and obsessions."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""This paper therefore sets out to explore the posited relationship between disgust sensitivity,\nhealth anxiety, danger expectancies and cognitive and behavioural measures of hand washing,\nin vivo, within a non-clinical group. It builds specifically on the work by Jones and Menzies\n(1997) and Thorpe et al. (2003) and will do this by replicating the experimental methodology\ndescribed by Jones and Menzies (1997), by removing the elements that they found to\nbe redundant (responsibility and perfectionism), retaining those factors related to danger\nexpectancies concerning illness beliefs, and adding factors previously identified as being\nimplicated in self-reported levels of obsessional hand washing: namely, measures of self-\nreported disgust sensitivity and health anxiety (Thorpe et al., 2003)."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Health anxiety, for example, was not related to washing time but was related to the\nOCI-R washing subscale. However, our analysis showed that disgust sensitivity mediated the\nrelationship between health anxiety and OCI-R washing. This is different to previous findings\nby Thorpe et al. (2003) and may be due to the BAT heightening disgust sensitivity or other\n\f                        Disgust, danger expectancy and hand washing                           187\n\n\nsituational factors."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Named foundational study is retested with questionnaire and behavioural outcomes; health anxiety and disgust associations persist but the authors explicitly identify a different mediation pattern.""}"
10.1080/15248372.2015.1033525,a94c4cdacbb28b3b,1,,Representational change and children’s numerical estimation.,"Opfer, J. E., & Siegler, R. S.",2007,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Opfer, J. E., & Siegler, R. S."", ""year"": ""2007"", ""title"": ""Representational change and children’s numerical estimation."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""First, we wanted to directly replicate Siegler’s work (Opfer & Siegler, 2007)as closely as possible and thus chose to use the original positive number line and values used in those studies."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Our findings replicate those of Siegler and colleagues (e.g., Booth & Siegler, 2006) for positive numbers; specifically, we found evidence of a shift from logarithmic to linear representations for positive numbers between fourth and sixth grade (0–1,000 scale)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors state an overall replication verdict for the positive-number findings of Siegler and colleagues. Both cited papers are explicitly identified as work this study was designed to replicate.""}"
10.31234/osf.io/nwg7p,2fd219f965eadcec,1,10.1027/1618-3169.52.3.195,"Two-digit comparison - Decomposed, holistic, or hybrid?","Verguts, T., & De Moor, W.",2005,full_text,,error,obvious,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""obvious"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Verguts, T., & De Moor, W."", ""doi"": ""10.1027/1618-3169.52.3.195"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Two-digit comparison - Decomposed, holistic, or hybrid?"", ""year"": ""2005""}, ""quote"": ""Regarding modulations of the numerical effects due to linguistic markedness of the\n\ntask instruction, we expected to replicate the findings by Verguts and De Moor (2005)\n\nshowing comparable distance effects for marked and unmarked task instructions."", ""location"": ""Independent parent primary-source context and original publisher identity proof; integration/candidate_0016.parent.json"", ""reason"": ""The current primary study explicitly predicts and empirically tests comparable distance effects across pick-smaller and pick-larger instructions as a replication of Verguts/DeMoor2005. Own Results and Discussion compare both instruction sensitivity and overall response times to that original. This specific empirical comparison is distinct from the only shipped original, Bahnmueller2015row2458, and is absent from all supplied shipped identities. The printed original reference independently identifies authors, title, year and DOI. This identity-only decision leaves outcome unknown. The earlier expert sample-size and F-statistic claims are not adopted; the actual Methods starts with 53 newly collected participants, and no numerical claim is needed to establish this retest.""}","{""authors"": ""Verguts, T., & De Moor, W."", ""year"": ""2005"", ""title"": ""Two-digit comparison - Decomposed, holistic, or hybrid?"", ""doi"": ""10.1027/1618-3169.52.3.195"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Regarding modulations of the numerical effects due to linguistic markedness of the task instruction, we expected to replicate the findings by Verguts and De Moor (2005) showing comparable distance effects for marked and unmarked task instructions."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""However, in contrast to previous findings in single- and two-digit number comparison (Arend & Henik 2015; Verguts and De Moor, 2005), we did not detect reliable differences in overall response times in frequentist analyses. Although the Bayesian analysis supports the null model, the evidential value is relatively weak."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Distance invariance agrees (abstract and discussion report no numerical effect changes); expected overall latency contrast explicitly fails. No overall verdict for this target overrides the two comparisons.""}"
10.31234/osf.io/nwg7p,2fd219f965eadcec,2,10.1037/xlm0000135,Choosing the larger versus choosing the smaller: Asymmetries in the size congruity effect.,"Arend, I., & Henik, A.",2015,full_text,,error,obvious,"{""target_index"": 2, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""obvious"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Arend, I., & Henik, A."", ""doi"": ""10.1037/xlm0000135"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Choosing the larger versus choosing the smaller: Asymmetries in the size congruity effect."", ""year"": ""2015""}, ""quote"": ""However, in contrast to previous findings in single- and two-digit number comparison\n\n(Arend & Henik 2015; Verguts and De Moor, 2005), we did not detect reliable differences in\n\noverall response times in frequentist analyses."", ""location"": ""Independent parent primary-source context and original publisher identity proof; integration/candidate_0016.parent.json"", ""reason"": ""The current primary study explicitly predicts longer pick-smaller reaction times following Arend/Henik2015 and tests the prediction with its own instruction comparison. Discussion explicitly contrasts its overall response-time result with Arend/Henik, and a further bin analysis compares original-specific predictions. Thus this is an empirical conceptual retest rather than only a borrowed method. The printed reference identifies a separate original absent from the sole shipped Bahnmueller2015row2458. Identity-only decision leaves outcome unknown. The earlier expert sample-size and F-statistic claims are not adopted; the actual Methods starts with 53 newly collected participants, and no numerical claim is needed to establish this retest.""}","{""authors"": ""Arend, I., & Henik, A."", ""year"": ""2015"", ""title"": ""Choosing the larger versus choosing the smaller: Asymmetries in the size congruity effect."", ""doi"": ""10.1037/xlm0000135"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In line with previous reports (Arend & Henik, 2015; Verguts & De Moor, 2005), we expected prolonged reaction times when instructed to pick the smaller as compared to picking the larger number."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""However, in contrast to previous findings in single- and two-digit number comparison (Arend & Henik 2015; Verguts and De Moor, 2005), we did not detect reliable differences in overall response times in frequentist analyses."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Explicitly tests the prior overall reaction-time difference in a new number range; no difference found. Exploratory bin comparison also differs.""}"
10.1037/xhp0000483,6c17994fbe674096,1,10.3758/APP.72.2.326,What is top-down about contingent capture?,"Belopolsky, A. V., Schreij, D., & Theeuwes, J.",2010,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""We reexamined this claim using stimulus parameters from studies in which nonmatching color-cues had captured attention (Belopolsky et al., 2010; Noesen, Lien, & Ruthruff, 2014) to maximize possible effects of cue frequency. Hence, in the nonmatching cue condition of Experiment 3, green color singleton cues were presented in combination with red color singleton targets."", ""location"": ""Experiment 3, Introduction, p. 686"", ""reason"": ""Experiment 3 was designed to re-examine Folk and Remington's (2015) claim about color cues while borrowing stimulus parameters from Belopolsky et al. (2010); noting in the discussion that an unexpected baseline validity effect for frequent cues matches their prior finding does not make Belopolsky et al. (2010) a replication target.""}","{""authors"": ""Belopolsky, A. V., Schreij, D., & Theeuwes, J."", ""year"": ""2010"", ""title"": ""What is top-down about contingent capture?"", ""doi"": ""10.3758/APP.72.2.326"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""3"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Further, even the frequent green cues elicited validity effects in combination with red color singleton targets. This suggests that the bottom-up salience signal overrode the task-set for red color singletons. The finding replicates previous studies, in which targetnonmatching cues captured attention (Belopolsky et al., 2010; Noesen et al., 2014)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Further, even the frequent green cues elicited validity effects in combination with red color singleton targets. This suggests that the bottom-up salience signal overrode the task-set for red color singletons. The finding replicates previous studies, in which targetnonmatching cues captured attention (Belopolsky et al., 2010; Noesen et al., 2014)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors explicitly identify their nonmatching-cue capture result as replicating both named papers; these are targets in addition to the principal 2015 target.""}"
10.1037/xhp0000483,6c17994fbe674096,2,10.1080/20445911.2014.892112,An electrophysiological study of attention capture by salience: Does rarity enable capture?,"Noesen, B., Lien, M. C., & Ruthruff, E.",2014,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 2, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""We reexamined this claim using stimulus parameters from studies in which nonmatching color-cues had captured attention (Belopolsky et al., 2010; Noesen, Lien, & Ruthruff, 2014) to maximize possible effects of cue frequency. Hence, in the nonmatching cue condition of Experiment 3, green color singleton cues were presented in combination with red color singleton targets."", ""location"": ""Experiment 3, Introduction, p. 686"", ""reason"": ""Noesen et al. (2014) supplied stimulus parameters for Experiment 3's test of Folk and Remington's (2015) claim rather than serving as a target finding that the study set out to re-test.""}","{""authors"": ""Noesen, B., Lien, M. C., & Ruthruff, E."", ""year"": ""2014"", ""title"": ""An electrophysiological study of attention capture by salience: Does rarity enable capture?"", ""doi"": ""10.1080/20445911.2014.892112"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""3"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Further, even the frequent green cues elicited validity effects in combination with red color singleton targets. This suggests that the bottom-up salience signal overrode the task-set for red color singletons. The finding replicates previous studies, in which targetnonmatching cues captured attention (Belopolsky et al., 2010; Noesen et al., 2014)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Further, even the frequent green cues elicited validity effects in combination with red color singleton targets. This suggests that the bottom-up salience signal overrode the task-set for red color singletons. The finding replicates previous studies, in which targetnonmatching cues captured attention (Belopolsky et al., 2010; Noesen et al., 2014)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The explicit replication statement covers this target as well as Belopolsky et al.; later manipulations do not expressly contradict this target-specific finding.""}"
10.1002/1520-6629(199110)19:4<373::aid-jcop2290190409>3.0.co;2-j,6d95c6ae74f8dc7b,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,"{""corrected_value"": null, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""6d95c6ae74f8dc7b"", ""location"": ""Abstract (line 5)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""The present study tested the following hypotheses: (1) Stress has more of a negative impact on the life satisfaction of those under age 75 than those over age 75; (2) social support has more of a positive effect on life satisfaction in those under age 75 than those over age 75; (3) the buffering effect of social support is stronger in the under age 75 group than the over age 75 group. None of the three hypotheses was supported, contrary to prior research."", ""reason"": ""The supplied primary source contains only the title ('Stress, social support, and morale: Failure to replicate age effects'), author list (Robert J. Calsyn; Laurie A. Roades), and four-sentence abstract, with no full text or reference list. While the title and abstract establish an empirical replication re-testing three age-effect hypotheses regarding stress, social support, and life satisfaction in those under versus over age 75 ('contrary to prior research'), the abstract does not cite or name the specific prior work(s) tested. Consequently, the blind coder recorded an unnamed target placeholder (target_index 0). Shipped row 2467 (Gray & Calsyn, 1989, 'The relationship of stress and social support to life satisfaction: Age effects', Journal of Community Psychology 17(3): 214-219, DOI 10.1002/1520-6629(198907)17:3<214::aid-jcop2290170303>3.0.co;2-f) shares co-author Robert J. Calsyn, journal, and exact topic, and very likely corresponds to the unnamed target, but without the full text or reference list the primary evidence cannot settle whether Gray and Calsyn (1989) is the sole original work tested or whether another/additional prior study is re-tested. No prior adjudication or prior state file existed for this work."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0053_6d95c6ae74f8dc7b"", ""target_index"": 0, ""verdict"": ""undeterminable""}","{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The present study tested the following hypotheses: (1) Stress has more of a negative impact on the life satisfaction of those under age 75 than those over age 75; (2) social support has more of a positive effect on life satisfaction in those under age 75 than those over age 75; (3) the buffering effect of social support is stronger in the under age 75 group than the over age 75 group. None of the three hypotheses was supported, contrary to prior research."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The present study tested the following hypotheses: (1) Stress has more of a negative impact on the life satisfaction of those under age 75 than those over age 75; (2) social support has more of a positive effect on life satisfaction in those under age 75 than those over age 75; (3) the buffering effect of social support is stronger in the under age 75 group than the over age 75 group. None of the three hypotheses was supported, contrary to prior research."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract explicitly says none of the three tested age-effect hypotheses was supported, contrary to prior research; title explicitly frames failure to replicate.""}"
