10.1123/pes.2021-0082,5d3847715036d23b,1,10.1123/pes.2017-0240,The effect of maturation on performance during repeated sprints with self-selected versus standardized recovery intervals in youth footballers.,"Brownstein CG, Ball D, Micklewright D, Gibson NV.",2018,abstract,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Brownstein CG, Ball D, Micklewright D, Gibson NV."", ""doi"": ""10.1123/pes.2017-0240"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""The effect of maturation on performance during repeated sprints with self-selected versus standardized recovery intervals in youth footballers."", ""year"": ""2018""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""5d3847715036d23b"", ""location"": ""Abstract, line 5"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Conclusion: In contrast to the original studies, the present replication study demonstrated that self-selected recovery periods during a RSP leads to better repeated sprint performance compared with standardized recovery periods in youth soccer players. The better repeated sprint performance with individual recovery durations in RSP2 was achieved with less physiological and perceived effort."", ""reason"": ""Neither prior_adjudication_path nor prior_state_path exists on disk (never adjudicated). The source text (5d3847715036d23b.txt) explicitly states in the Abstract Purpose (line 5) that the study is 'Replicating the studies of Gibson et al and Brownstein et al to assess performance, and physiological, and perceived variables during a repeated sprint protocol (RSP) with standardized versus self-selected recovery in youth soccer players', and concludes 'In contrast to the original studies, the present replication study demonstrated that self-selected recovery periods during a RSP leads to better repeated sprint performance compared with standardized recovery periods in youth soccer players.' The publisher-deposited references (lines 8-9) identify both original empirical papers: Gibson et al. (2017, Pediatr Exerc Sci. 29(2):186-193, 10.1123/pes.2016-0130) and Brownstein et al. (2018, Pediatr Exerc Sci. 30(4):500-505, 10.1123/pes.2017-0240). Only Gibson et al. (2017) is in shipped_rows (row 2470, coded at target_index 0). Brownstein et al. (2018) (target_index 1) is a distinct empirical study published a year later that is also directly replicated here and is missing from the shipped dataset."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0042_5d3847715036d23b"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Brownstein CG, Ball D, Micklewright D, Gibson NV."", ""year"": ""2018"", ""title"": ""The effect of maturation on performance during repeated sprints with self-selected versus standardized recovery intervals in youth footballers."", ""doi"": ""10.1123/pes.2017-0240"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Replicating the studies of Gibson et al and Brownstein et al to assess performance, and physiological, and perceived variables during a repeated sprint protocol (RSP) with standardized versus self-selected recovery in youth soccer players."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In contrast to the original studies, the present replication study demonstrated that self-selected recovery periods during a RSP leads to better repeated sprint performance compared with standardized recovery periods in youth soccer players. The better repeated sprint performance with individual recovery durations in RSP2 was achieved with less physiological and perceived effort."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The conclusion explicitly contrasts the central performance result with the original studies as a set, covering both named targets.""}"
10.2307/2112261,8fee9ca43e9ad0e6,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,"{""corrected_value"": null, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""8fee9ca43e9ad0e6"", ""location"": ""abstract"", ""method"": ""obvious"", ""quote"": ""Longitudinal survey data on a national sample of youth, first sampled as high-school sophomores in 1955 and followed up in 1970, were employed in a replication and extension of the Blau and Duncan basic of the status attainment process."", ""reason"": ""Parent verified new original source/authoritative bibliography evidence0046. Shipped2474DOI10.2307/2092399is a1968book review, whereas the original empirical Blau/Duncan book is1967. A review-index title and book title cannot be treated as the same empirical report. The available current abstract says it re-tests Blau/Duncan status-attainment model but omits exact reference/title/year; current body is still inaccessible. Thus neither unique book-version binding nor a genuinely extra original can be certified. Supersede the raw expert represented certainty with an actual current-citation/report-identity limit. No false omission exported, no automatic correction to an uncited book."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0072_8fee9ca43e9ad0e6"", ""target_index"": 0, ""verdict"": ""undeterminable""}","{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Longitudinal survey data on a national sample of youth, first sampled as high-school sophomores in 1955 and followed up in 1970, were employed in a replication and extension of the Blau and Duncan basic of the status attainment process."", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": """", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract names the Blau and Duncan model as the object of replication. Its outcome wording concerns previous inquiries generally and does not identify which agreement or discrepancy belongs to this specific target.""}"
10.3389/fpsyg.2018.02662,d6d6e6eb9709b275,1,10.1086/674547,Environmental disorder leads to self-regulatory failure.,"Chae B., Zhu R.",2014,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Chae B., Zhu R."", ""year"": ""2014"", ""title"": ""Environmental disorder leads to self-regulatory failure."", ""doi"": ""10.1086/674547"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We also compared the effect of experimental conditions only considering the rotations that most closely resembled the task order in Vohs et al. (2013) and Chae and Zhu (2014) ."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Results in our study indicated that working at a messy desk did not result in significantly and reliably different creativity or executive functioning performance."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The original disorder effect was not found. The authors report no creativity or executive functioning differences, and explicitly compare the designs and discrepant results to this original.""}"
10.17705/1atrr.00061,5e2562976f8b03cf,1,,Examining the influence of demographic factors on internet users’ information privacy concerns.,"Zukowski, T., & Brown, I.",2007,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Zukowski, T., & Brown, I."", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Examining the influence of demographic factors on internet users’ information privacy concerns."", ""year"": ""2007""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""5e2562976f8b03cf"", ""location"": ""Section 4.2 Structural Model, lines 1295-1300"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Our replication of the analysis by Zukowski and Brown (2007), regarding covariate effects, only confirmed\nthe effects of gender on the privacy concerns in Japan. This finding is also reported by Tschersich et al.\n(2016). Specifically, a t-test for the gender shows a statistically significant difference in privacy concern\nlevels of men and women. Specifically, women are found to be, on average, more concerned with respect\nto their privacy. Different from our study, Zukowski and Brown (2007, p. 201) do not observe this statistically\nsignificant gender difference."", ""reason"": ""Neither prior_adjudication_path nor prior_state_path exists on disk (never adjudicated). In addition to replicating Malhotra, Kim, & Agarwal (2004) (shipped row 2480, 10.1287/isre.1040.0032, coded at target_index 0), the authors explicitly frame and execute an empirical replication of Zukowski & Brown (2007) ('Examining the influence of demographic factors on internet users’ information privacy concerns', Proceedings of the 2007 Annual Research Conference of the South African Institute of Computer Scientists and Information Technologists on IT Research in Developing Countries, pp. 197-204; References lines 1591-1594). Using new empirical survey data from N = 9,287 Japanese respondents, Section 3.3 (lines 498-500), Section 4.2 (lines 1261-1300), and Section 5 (lines 1379-1383) retest the covariate effects of demographic variables (age, gender, education, and income) on IUIPC and directly compare the Japanese empirical findings against Zukowski & Brown's (2007) original results. Zukowski & Brown (2007) is a distinct primary empirical work from Malhotra et al. (2004) and is completely absent from shipped_rows."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0043_5e2562976f8b03cf"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Zukowski, T., & Brown, I."", ""year"": ""2007"", ""title"": ""Examining the influence of demographic factors on internet users’ information privacy concerns."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Our replication of the analysis by Zukowski and Brown (2007), regarding covariate effects, only confirmed the effects of gender on the privacy concerns in Japan."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Even though the correlation of IUIPC with age is significant, we consider it negligible in magnitude (0.113). Zukowski and Brown again obtain a different result from what we observed in Japan by accepting their hypothesis of an effect of age. Similarly, our correlation of IUIPC with the education and personal income levels yields negligible values of 0.03, and -0.05 respectively. Zukowski and Brown, however, do identify an effect of education, but also reject the effect of income."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors explicitly call the covariate analysis a replication. Age and education differ, while absence of income effect agrees; gender also differs. Earlier IUIPC replications cited solely for context are not targets.""}"
10.1037/men0000497,2638a8bb6c2002e9,1,10.1037/men0000436,Masculine drinking norms and alcohol use in a national sample of NCAA male student-athletes.,"Zamboanga BL, Perrotte JK, Newins AR, Martin JL, Ford K, Wyrick DL, & Milroy JJ",2023,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Zamboanga BL, Perrotte JK, Newins AR, Martin JL, Ford K, Wyrick DL, & Milroy JJ"", ""doi"": ""10.1037/men0000436"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Masculine drinking norms and alcohol use in a national sample of NCAA male student-athletes."", ""year"": ""2023""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""2638a8bb6c2002e9"", ""location"": ""Introduction, Study Aims (lines 360-370); Discussion (lines 634-642, 732-740); References (lines 1075-1078)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Thus, we sought to replicate and extend prior work on \nthe MDNM using a large, multisite sample of college men. Our study aims are to investigate \nthe factor structure and item loadings of the MDNM, and examine the extent to which \nexcess and control are associated with alcohol use and negative drinking consequences \nover and above known demographic correlates of alcohol use including age, membership \nin a Greek fraternity and/or athletic involvement (e.g., Meilman et al., 1999; Merrill & \nCarey, 2016; Zhou & Heim, 2014) and general masculine norms (CMNI-29). Based on prior \nresearch (Perrotte et al., 2020; Zamboanga et al., 2023), we expected to find a two-factor \nsolution (i.e., excess and control) of the MDNM in this multisite sample of college men. \nWe also expected excess to be positively associated with alcohol use and negative drinking \nconsequences and control to be negatively related to both drinking outcomes."", ""reason"": ""The primary source (Zamboanga et al., 2025, DOI 10.1037/men0000497) collects new multisite survey data (N = 1,350 college men across 12 universities) to empirically retest the two-factor structure (excess and control) of the Masculine Drinking Norms Measure (MDNM) and its multivariate associations with alcohol use (AUDIT-C) and negative drinking consequences (B-YAACQ) over and above demographic covariates and CMNI-29 masculine norms, explicitly naming both Perrotte et al. (2020) and Zamboanga et al. (2023) as the prior MDNM studies being replicated and directly comparing the CFA factor structure, positive excess associations, and null control associations against Zamboanga et al. (2023) in the Discussion (lines 634-642, 732-740). The References section (lines 1075-1078) provides the complete citation and DOI: Zamboanga BL, Perrotte JK, Newins AR, Martin JL, Ford K, Wyrick DL, & Milroy JJ (2023), 'Masculine drinking norms and alcohol use in a national sample of NCAA male student-athletes.', Psychology of Men & Masculinities, 24(3), 261-268, DOI 10.1037/men0000436. The only shipped row for this work is input_row_index 2484 (Perrotte, Zamboanga, & Kearns, 2020, DOI 10.1037/men0000252, matched to sibling blind target 0), which is a distinct prospective study of Latino college men; Zamboanga et al. (2023) is a separate empirical study of NCAA male student-athletes and is genuinely omitted from the shipped dataset."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0012_2638a8bb6c2002e9"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Zamboanga BL, Perrotte JK, Newins AR, Martin JL, Ford K, Wyrick DL, & Milroy JJ"", ""year"": ""2023"", ""title"": ""Masculine drinking norms and alcohol use in a national sample of NCAA male student-athletes."", ""doi"": ""10.1037/men0000436"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Thus, we sought to replicate and extend prior work on the MDNM using a large, multisite sample of college men. Our study aims are to investigate the factor structure and item loadings of the MDNM, and examine the extent to which excess and control are associated with alcohol use and negative drinking consequences over and above known demographic correlates of alcohol use including age, membership in a Greek fraternity and/or athletic involvement (e.g., Meilman et al., 1999; Merrill & Carey, 2016; Zhou & Heim, 2014) and general masculine norms (CMNI-29). Based on prior research (Perrotte et al., 2020; Zamboanga et al., 2023), we expected to find a two-factor solution (i.e., excess and control) of the MDNM in this multisite sample of college men. We also expected excess to be positively associated with alcohol use and negative drinking consequences and control to be negatively related to both drinking outcomes."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Bivariate and multivariate findings indicated that the masculine drinking norm of control was not significantly associated with alcohol use and negative drinking consequences. These findings differ somewhat from the multivariate findings reported in Perrotte et al.’s (2020) prospective study where they found that control was inversely associated with negative consequences but not alcohol use. Although Zamboanga et al.’s (2023) multivariate findings with a large NCAA sample of male student-athletes indicated that control was negatively correlated with alcohol use and negative drinking consequences, they also found that the bivariate correlations between control and these outcome variables were small (r=−.05)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Factor structure and excess associations agree with this original, but authors explicitly contrast the null control associations with it. No overall replication verdict overrides these comparisons.""}"
10.23668/psycharchives.5757,e92d4f6567f6b8f4,1,10.1080/02699931.2016.1154817,The beneficial and detrimental effects of major depression on intuitive decision-making.,"Remmers, C., Topolinski, S., Buxton, A., Dietrich, D. E., & Michalak, J.",2017,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Remmers, C., Topolinski, S., Buxton, A., Dietrich, D. E., & Michalak, J."", ""year"": ""2017"", ""title"": ""The beneficial and detrimental effects of major depression on intuitive decision-making."", ""doi"": ""10.1080/02699931.2016.1154817"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The first hypothesis (H1) refers to the replica‐\nbility of recently found intuition deficits in depression (Remmers et al., 2015; Remmers et\nal., 2017). We hypothesized that patients with an acute episode of major depression are\nless able to intuitively discriminate semantic coherence from semantic incoherence in the\nJSCT than healthy controls."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Overall the current study did not replicate findings from previous research regarding\nintuitive  semantic  coherence  detection  deficits  in  depression."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true}"
10.23668/psycharchives.5757,e92d4f6567f6b8f4,2,10.1037/a0014678,The architecture of intuition: Fluency and affect determine intuitive judgments of semantic and visual coherence and judgments of grammaticality in artificial grammar learning.,"Topolinski, S., & Strack, F.",2009,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Topolinski, S., & Strack, F."", ""year"": ""2009"", ""title"": ""The architecture of intuition: Fluency and affect determine intuitive judgments of semantic and visual coherence and judgments of grammaticality in artificial grammar learning."", ""doi"": ""10.1037/a0014678"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""Experiment 8"", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Building up on basic research\n(Topolinski & Strack, 2009a; Experiment 8) showing that healthy subjects are more\nlikely to judge triads to be coherent that consist of positive as compared to negative\nwords, we expected that this effect would also emerge for depressed patients.\n\nResults did not reveal any effect of our fluency manipulation, and thus our hypothe‐\n\nses regarding fluency (H2) were not supported. As such, the current study could not\nreplicate previous results that bolstered the fluency model proposed by Topolinski and\nStrack (2009a, 2009b)."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Results did not reveal any effect of our fluency manipulation, and thus our hypothe‐\n\nses regarding fluency (H2) were not supported. As such, the current study could not\nreplicate previous results that bolstered the fluency model proposed by Topolinski and\nStrack (2009a, 2009b).\n\nThe results further showed that positive (vs. negative) valence triggered coherence\n\njudgments (H3a) and that this effect was – in contrast to our hypothesis (H3b) – not\nmoderated by depression."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true}"
10.23668/psycharchives.5757,e92d4f6567f6b8f4,3,10.1080/02699930802420745,The analysis of intuition: Processing fluency and affect in judgements of semantic coherence.,"Topolinski, S., & Strack, F.",2009,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Topolinski, S., & Strack, F."", ""year"": ""2009"", ""title"": ""The analysis of intuition: Processing fluency and affect in judgements of semantic coherence."", ""doi"": ""10.1080/02699930802420745"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Results did not reveal any effect of our fluency manipulation, and thus our hypothe‐\n\nses regarding fluency (H2) were not supported. As such, the current study could not\nreplicate previous results that bolstered the fluency model proposed by Topolinski and\nStrack (2009a, 2009b)."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Results did not reveal any effect of our fluency manipulation, and thus our hypothe‐\n\nses regarding fluency (H2) were not supported. As such, the current study could not\nreplicate previous results that bolstered the fluency model proposed by Topolinski and\nStrack (2009a, 2009b).\n\nOur findings also revealed a significant interaction between valence and coherence\n\nin the valence block (i.e., positive word triads that were coherent were most likely\nto be judged as coherent in both groups). This finding indicates that positivity (con‐\nveyed by positive valence in the current study) may lead to more accurate intuitive\njudgments and is in line with previous research showing that not only “tonic” positive\naffect (e.g., manipulated or freestanding positive mood; Balas et al., 2012; Bolte et al.,\n2003) but also “phasic” positive affect (induced by the activation of positively valenced\nmemory content) can strengthen the accuracy of coherence judgments (Topolinski &\nStrack, 2009b)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true}"
10.15616/bsl.2021.27.3.187,0a03c9acafa3d585,1,,Genome-wide association study of hematological and biochemical traits in a Japanese population.,"Kamatani Y, Matsuda K, Okada Y, Kubo M, Hosono N, Daigo Y, Nakamura Y, Kamatani N.",2010,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""rs9296095는 KARE 코호트 자료를 기반으로 한 이전 연 구에서도 발표되었던 SNP이었는데, 본 연구 결과에서 재 현성을 확인할 수 있었다(Oh et al., 2014). rs6141, rs210314, rs75080135, rs6490294, rs4148441은 본 연구에서 혈소판 수 치와 관련하여 한국인에서 처음으로 발굴된 SNP들이다."", ""location"": ""Results, p. 188"", ""reason"": ""The paper explicitly frames its replication around the prior Korean KARE cohort GWAS (Oh et al., 2014), whereas Kamatani et al. (2010) is cited only as general background and listed via a post-hoc GWAS Catalog database lookup of newly discovered Korean SNPs in Table 1.""}","{""authors"": ""Kamatani Y, Matsuda K, Okada Y, Kubo M, Hosono N, Daigo Y, Nakamura Y, Kamatani N."", ""year"": ""2010"", ""title"": ""Genome-wide association study of hematological and biochemical traits in a Japanese population."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In this study, it was confirmed whether significant SNPs were replicated in the HEXA (The Health Examinees) cohort. Table 1. Comparison of identified SNPs for platelet count (PLT) in GWAS catalog and HEXA cohort"", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""At the same time, it was confirmed that the direction of influence was the same according to the genotype. In conclusion, it can be seen that common SNPs are associated with the platelet count regardless of the cohort for Koreans."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors state genotype-adjusted direction agreement for the catalog comparison set; Table 1 places this source within that set. Overall author verdict governs despite some allele/direction ambiguities in the table.""}"
