 single shipped row (input_row_index 2487, Alfei et al., 2015, doi: 10.1101/lm.038513.115) is an unrelated midazolam study from a different laboratory, and Abrari et al. (2008) is an independent empirical work distinct from all other Table 2 propranolol studies."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0063_834b55f482c6e8a1"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Abrari, K., Rashidy-Pour, A., Semnanian, S., & Fathollahi, Y."", ""year"": ""2008"", ""title"": ""Administration of corticosterone after memory reactivation disrupts subsequent retrieval of a contextual conditioned fear memory: Dependence upon training intensity."", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.nlm.2007.07.005"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""To this aim, we set out to conceptually or directly replicate previous studies in which systemic, post-reactivation administration of midazolam (MDZ, see Table 1), propranolol (PROP, see Table 2), or cycloheximide (CYCLO; Haubrich et al., 2015) resulted in amnesia for contextual fear memories."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We can conclude that our findings are in stark contrast with the existing literature on post-reactivation amnesia in rats, in which systemic administration of MDZ (Table 1), PROP (Table 2), or CYCLO (Haubrich et al., 2015) has been shown to induce amnesia for contextual fear memories or other types of memories (Briggs & Olson, 2013; Dębiec & Ledoux, 2004; Flint & Marino, 2007; Milton, Lee, & Everitt, 2008; Przybyslawski et al., 1999; Robinson & Franklin, 2010; Taubenfeld et al., 2009)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly contrast their own results with this original or the target set containing it.""}"
