ss 20 conceptual replication experiments with systemic post-reactivation midazolam in Section 3.1. The reference list (lines 2065-2067) supplies the complete citation and DOI (10.1037/a0013273). The sole shipped row (input_row_index 2487, Alfei et al., 2015, DOI 10.1101/lm.038513.115) is a distinct original paper from a different laboratory, and no sibling target duplicates Zhang & Cranney (2008)."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0066_834b55f482c6e8a1"", ""target_index"": 21, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Zhang, S., & Cranney, J."", ""year"": ""2008"", ""title"": ""The role of GABA and anxiety in the reconsolidation of conditioned fear."", ""doi"": ""10.1037/a0013273"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""To this aim, we set out to conceptually or directly replicate previous studies in which systemic, post-reactivation administration of midazolam (MDZ, see Table 1), propranolol (PROP, see Table 2), or cycloheximide (CYCLO; Haubrich et al., 2015) resulted in amnesia for contextual fear memories."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We can conclude that our findings are in stark contrast with the existing literature on post-reactivation amnesia in rats, in which systemic administration of MDZ (Table 1), PROP (Table 2), or CYCLO (Haubrich et al., 2015) has been shown to induce amnesia for contextual fear memories or other types of memories (Briggs & Olson, 2013; Dębiec & Ledoux, 2004; Flint & Marino, 2007; Milton, Lee, & Everitt, 2008; Przybyslawski et al., 1999; Robinson & Franklin, 2010; Taubenfeld et al., 2009)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly contrast their own results with this original or the target set containing it.""}"
10.3389/fnbeh.2020.00128,cc4b8b7716b048a4,1,10.1093/scan/nsv059,Emotion regulation ability varies in relation to intrinsic functional brain architecture,Uchida M. Biederman J. Gabrieli J. D. E. Micco J. de Los Angeles C. Brown A. .,2015,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Uchida M. Biederman J. Gabrieli J. D. E. Micco J. de Los Angeles C. Brown A. ."", ""year"": ""2015"", ""title"": ""Emotion regulation ability varies in relation to intrinsic functional brain architecture"", ""doi"": ""10.1093/scan/nsv059"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""This investigation is inspired by the study of Uchida et al. ( 2015 ). Because there are important differences concerning the operationalization of the constructs and the experimental procedure between our study and the Uchida study (see Supplementary Table S24 ), the current investigation can be a conceptual replication at best."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Thus, we can only conclude that the findings of the original study could not conceptually be replicated, the results do not extend to a different reappraisal strategy nor arousal instead of affect outcomes."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true}"
10.1016/j.cognition.2020.104521,aef3bffe9de93fbe,1,10.1016/j.cognition.2014.06.011,Improving arithmetic performance with number sense training: An investigation of underlying mechanism,"Park, J., & Brannon, E. M.",2014,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Park, J., & Brannon, E. M."", ""year"": ""2014"", ""title"": ""Improving arithmetic performance with number sense training: An investigation of underlying mechanism"", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.cognition.2014.06.011"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""Experiment 1"", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2, 3, 4"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Consequently, we attempted more precise replications of Park & Brannon, 2013, 2014 in Experiment 3 (approximate arithmetic training compared to numeral ordering training) and a preregistered replication in Experiment 4 (approximate arithmetic training compared to approximate number comparison training)."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The original goal of the current study was to replicate the finding that non-symbolic approximate arithmetic training improves symbolic arithmetic fluency, and to build on this finding by probing the mechanism of the transfer effect. However, we were unable to replicate the original finding across four independent experiments."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Overall failure verdict applies to both explicitly re-tested Park and Brannon papers; the subsequent pooled analysis does not change it.""}"
10.1017/s1930297500004496,a335026fc212ccd8,1,10.1037/0022-3514.95.2.293,Why people are reluctant to tempt fate,"Risen, J. L., & Gilovich, T.",2008,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Risen, J. L., & Gilovich, T."", ""year"": ""2008"", ""title"": ""Why people are reluctant to tempt fate"", ""doi"": ""10.1037/0022-3514.95.2.293"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""2 (heading); 1 (results text)"", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""2a, 2b"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Our attempt to replicate the tempting fate effect reported in Risen and Gilovich (2008) was aimed at testing whether we could find a different magical thinking effect."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We thus think that the successful replication of the tempting fate effect adds credibility to the non-replication of the protection effect."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true}"
10.7717/peerj.4217,78b567dd3205b6ce,0,10.1016/j.bandl.2009.05.002,Atypical cerebral lateralisation in adults with compensated developmental dyslexia demonstrated using functional transcranial Doppler ultrasound,"Illingworth, S., & Bishop, D.",2009,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Illingworth, S., & Bishop, D."", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.bandl.2009.05.002"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Atypical cerebral lateralisation in adults with compensated developmental dyslexia demonstrated using functional transcranial Doppler ultrasound"", ""year"": ""2009""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""78b567dd3205b6ce"", ""location"": ""Conclusions (line 112)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Theories have proposed that disruption to the typical left hemisphere dominance for language may be a neurobiological correlate of language problems (e.g., Annett, 2002; Bishop, 2013; Crow et al., 1998), and empirical studies of very small samples have supported that view (Illingworth & Bishop, 2009; Whitehouse & Bishop, 2008). However, the present study did not replicate these findings, and we suggest that they are likely to have been false positives."", ""reason"": ""No prior adjudication or state file exists for this work. (1) Empirical retesting: Wilson & Bishop (2018, PeerJ, 10.7717/peerj.4217) use functional transcranial Doppler ultrasound (fTCD) in 263 twin children (107 DLD, explicitly including 10 children meeting criteria solely on literacy/dyslexia measures on the basis of Illingworth & Bishop, 2009 [Methods, line 45], and 156 TD controls) to retest whether language/literacy disorder is associated with reduced left-hemisphere cerebral lateralisation for language (Introduction, line 4), finding no between-groups difference in fTCD laterality index (t(242.63) = 1.32, p = .190; Results, line 78) and explicitly concluding that the study failed to replicate Illingworth & Bishop (2009) (Discussion, line 106; Conclusions, line 112). (2) Source-backed identity: Confirmed in References (line 167) and Crossref metadata as Illingworth, S., & Bishop, D. (2009), 'Atypical cerebral lateralisation in adults with compensated developmental dyslexia demonstrated using functional transcranial Doppler ultrasound', Brain and Language, 111(1), 61-65, DOI 10.1016/j.bandl.2009.05.002. (3) Absence and non-equivalence: The only shipped row is input_row_index 2493 (Bishop, 2005, 'Handedness and specific language impairment: A study of 6-year-old twins', Developmental Psychobiology, DOI 10.1002/dev.20062, corresponding to sibling target_index 2), which is a behavioural handedness study in 6-year-old twins rather than an fTCD cerebral lateralisation study in dyslexic adults. Target 0 is also independent of sibling targets 1 (Whitehouse & Bishop, 2008), 3 (Hill & Bishop, 1998), and 4 (Groen et al., 2012)."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0059_78b567dd3205b6ce"", ""target_index"": 0, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Illingworth, S., & Bishop, D."", ""year"": ""2009"", ""title"": ""Atypical cerebral lateralisation in adults with compensated developmental dyslexia demonstrated using functional transcranial Doppler ultrasound"", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.bandl.2009.05.002"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Theories have proposed that disruption to the typical left hemisphere dominance for language may be a neurobiological correlate of language problems (e.g., Annett, 2002; Bishop, 2013; Crow et al., 1998), and empirical studies of very small samples have supported that view (Illingworth & Bishop, 2009; Whitehouse & Bishop, 2008). However, the present study did not replicate these findings, and we suggest that they are likely to have been false positives."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Theories have proposed that disruption to the typical left hemisphere dominance for language may be a neurobiological correlate of language problems (e.g., Annett, 2002; Bishop, 2013; Crow et al., 1998), and empirical studies of very small samples have supported that view (Illingworth & Bishop, 2009; Whitehouse & Bishop, 2008). However, the present study did not replicate these findings, and we suggest that they are likely to have been false positives."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors explicitly identify the earlier finding being re-tested and report its nonreplication.""}"
10.7717/peerj.4217,78b567dd3205b6ce,1,10.1093/brain/awn266,Cerebral dominance for language function in adults with specific language impairment or autism,"Whitehouse, A., & Bishop, D.",2008,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Whitehouse, A., & Bishop, D."", ""doi"": ""10.1093/brain/awn266"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Cerebral dominance for language function in adults with specific language impairment or autism"", ""year"": ""2008""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""78b567dd3205b6ce"", ""location"": ""Conclusions (line 112)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Theories have proposed that disruption to the typical left hemisphere dominance for language may be a neurobiological correlate of language problems (e.g., Annett, 2002; Bishop, 2013; Crow et al., 1998), and empirical studies of very small samples have supported that view (Illingworth & Bishop, 2009; Whitehouse & Bishop, 2008). However, the present study did not replicate these findings, and we suggest that they are likely to have been false positives."", ""reason"": ""No prior adjudication or state file exists for this work. (1) Empirical retesting: The primary study retests Whitehouse & Bishop (2008), who reported right/bilateral fTCD language distribution and MCA blood-flow differences in adults with persisting specific language impairment (Introduction, line 4). In Results (lines 78, 82, 87, 88), the authors test continuous fTCD LI (t(242.63) = 1.32, p = .190), explicitly follow Whitehouse & Bishop (2008) to test mean percent change in left and right MCA blood flow (t(258.28) = 1.94, p = .053; t(260.97) = 1.72, p = .086), and test categorical right/bilateral vs. left laterality, concluding in Discussion (line 106) and Conclusions (line 112) that the study did not replicate Whitehouse & Bishop (2008). (2) Source-backed identity: Confirmed in References (line 206) and Crossref metadata as Whitehouse, A., & Bishop, D. (2008), 'Cerebral dominance for language function in adults with specific language impairment or autism', Brain, 131(12), 3193-3200, DOI 10.1093/brain/awn266. (3) Absence and non-equivalence: Distinct from the sole shipped row 2493 (Bishop, 2005, 10.1002/dev.20062, behavioural handedness in 6-year-old twins) and from sibling missing targets 0 (Illingworth & Bishop, 2009), 3 (Hill & Bishop, 1998), and 4 (Groen et al., 2012)."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0059_78b567dd3205b6ce"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Whitehouse, A., & Bishop, D."", ""year"": ""2008"", ""title"": ""Cerebral dominance for language function in adults with specific language impairment or autism"", ""doi"": ""10.1093/brain/awn266"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Theories have proposed that disruption to the typical left hemisphere dominance for language may be a neurobiological correlate of language problems (e.g., Annett, 2002; Bishop, 2013; Crow et al., 1998), and empirical studies of very small samples have supported that view (Illingworth & Bishop, 2009; Whitehouse & Bishop, 2008). However, the present study did not replicate these findings, and we suggest that they are likely to have been false positives."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Theories have proposed that disruption to the typical left hemisphere dominance for language may be a neurobiological correlate of language problems (e.g., Annett, 2002; Bishop, 2013; Crow et al., 1998), and empirical studies of very small samples have supported that view (Illingworth & Bishop, 2009; Whitehouse & Bishop, 2008). However, the present study did not replicate these findings, and we suggest that they are likely to have been false positives."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors explicitly identify the earlier finding being re-tested and report its nonreplication.""}"
10.7717/peerj.4217,78b567dd3205b6ce,3,10.1080/713754314,A reaching test reveals weak hand preference in specific language impairment and developmental co-ordination disorder,"Hill, E., & Bishop, D.",1998,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Hill, E., & Bishop, D."", ""doi"": ""10.1080/713754314"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""A reaching test reveals weak hand preference in specific language impairment and developmental co-ordination disorder"", ""year"": ""1998""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""78b567dd3205b6ce"", ""location"": ""Results, Main analysis (line 90)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""We also tested the hypothesis that children with DLD were less likely in the QHP task to reach across the midline with the right hand to a spatial position on the left side of the body, indicating weaker hand preference, as previously reported (Bishop, 2005; Hill & Bishop, 1998). A multilevel model was run with twin pair membership as a random effect, group as a between factor and spatial position as a within factor. There was a large main effect of spatial position, t(1,687.20) = 12.74, p < .001, with individuals being less likely to reach across the midline with the right hand to a spatial position on the left. However, there was no main effect of group, t(1,836.70) = 1.61, p = .108, and contrary to previous reports, no interaction between DLD status and spatial position, t(1,687.20) = 0.89, p = .376, indicating no between-groups differences in strength of hand preference."", ""reason"": ""No prior adjudication or state file exists for this work. (1) Empirical retesting: In Methods (line 51), Results (line 90), and Discussion (line 107), the authors explicitly attempt to replicate the finding of Hill & Bishop (1998) (alongside Bishop, 2005) that children with language impairment are less likely to reach across the midline with the right hand on the Quantified Hand Preference (QHP) task, finding no main effect of group (t(1,836.70) = 1.61, p = .108) and no group-by-spatial-position interaction (t(1,687.20) = 0.89, p = .376). (2) Source-backed identity: Confirmed in References (line 165) and Crossref metadata as Hill, E., & Bishop, D. (1998), 'A reaching test reveals weak hand preference in specific language impairment and developmental co-ordination disorder', Laterality: Asymmetries of Body, Brain and Cognition, 3(4), 295-310, DOI 10.1080/713754314. (3) Absence and non-equivalence: Although both Hill & Bishop (1998) and shipped row 2493 (Bishop, 2005, 'Handedness and specific language impairment: A study of 6-year-old twins', Developmental Psychobiology, DOI 10.1002/dev.20062, sibling target_index 2) examined QHP midline reaching, they are independent primary studies on distinct samples published seven years apart (children with SLI and developmental co-ordination disorder in 1998 vs. 6-year-old twins in 2005), not alternate reports of one study. Target 3 is also distinct from sibling targets 0, 1, and 4."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0059_78b567dd3205b6ce"", ""target_index"": 3, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Hill, E., & Bishop, D."", ""year"": ""1998"", ""title"": ""A reaching test reveals weak hand preference in specific language impairment and developmental co-ordination disorder"", ""doi"": ""10.1080/713754314"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In particular, our failure to replicate a between-groups difference in the probability of reaching across the midline with the right hand indicates that this may not be a marker of compromised neurodevelopment relevant to language disorder, as previously suggested (Bishop, 2005; Hill & Bishop, 1998)."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In particular, our failure to replicate a between-groups difference in the probability of reaching across the midline with the right hand indicates that this may not be a marker of compromised neurodevelopment relevant to language disorder, as previously suggested (Bishop, 2005; Hill & Bishop, 1998)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors explicitly identify the earlier finding being re-tested and report its nonreplication.""}"
10.7717/peerj.4217,78b567dd3205b6ce,4,10.1002/brb3.56,Does cerebral lateralization develop? A study using functional transcranial Doppler ultrasound assessing lateralization for language production and visuospatial memory,"Groen, M., Whitehouse, A., Badcock, N., & Bishop, D.",2012,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Groen, M., Whitehouse, A., Badcock, N., & Bishop, D."", ""doi"": ""10.1002/brb3.56"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Does cerebral lateralization develop? A study using functional transcranial Doppler ultrasound assessing lateralization for language production and visuospatial memory"", ""year"": ""2012""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""78b567dd3205b6ce"", ""location"": ""Results, Main analysis (line 102)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""In the second step of analysis, we tested the relationship between language and laterality from the opposite direction, using language measures as predictors, aiming to replicate the effects reported by Groen et al. (2012). We computed Spearman’s correlations between each language measure and the fTCD laterality index in the two replication samples. However, no uncorrected correlation replicated across samples, and no correlation within a sample survived a Holm-Bonferroni correction."", ""reason"": ""No prior adjudication or state file exists for this work. (1) Empirical retesting: In Introduction (line 6), Methods (line 53), and Results (line 102), the authors explicitly state a subsidiary aim to replicate the positive correlations between fTCD language laterality and language/reading measures (vocabulary and non-word reading) reported by Groen et al. (2012) in single-born children (n = 55), computing Spearman's correlations across two twin replication samples and finding that no correlation replicated across samples or survived Holm-Bonferroni correction. (2) Source-backed identity: Confirmed in References (line 160) and Crossref metadata as Groen, M., Whitehouse, A., Badcock, N., & Bishop, D. (2012), 'Does cerebral lateralization develop? A study using functional transcranial Doppler ultrasound assessing lateralization for language production and visuospatial memory', Brain and Behavior, 2(3), 256-269, DOI 10.1002/brb3.56. (3) Absence and non-equivalence: Distinct from the sole shipped row 2493 (Bishop, 2005, 10.1002/dev.20062, behavioural handedness in 6-year-old twins) and from sibling missing targets 0 (Illingworth & Bishop, 2009), 1 (Whitehouse & Bishop, 2008), and 3 (Hill & Bishop, 1998)."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0059_78b567dd3205b6ce"", ""target_index"": 4, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Groen, M., Whitehouse, A., Badcock, N., & Bishop, D."", ""year"": ""2012"", ""title"": ""Does cerebral lateralization develop? A study using functional transcranial Doppler ultrasound assessing lateralization for language production and visuospatial memory"", ""doi"": ""10.1002/brb3.56"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In the second step of analysis, we tested the relationship between language and laterality from the opposite direction, using language measures as predictors, aiming to replicate the effects reported by Groen et al. (2012). We computed Spearman’s correlations between each language measure and the fTCD laterality index in the two replication samples. However, no uncorrected correlation replicated across samples, and no correlation within a sample survived a Holm-Bonferroni correction."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In the second step of analysis, we tested the relationship between language and laterality from the opposite direction, using language measures as predictors, aiming to replicate the effects reported by Groen et al. (2012). We computed Spearman’s correlations between each language measure and the fTCD laterality index in the two replication samples. However, no uncorrected correlation replicated across samples, and no correlation within a sample survived a Holm-Bonferroni correction."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors explicitly identify the earlier finding being re-tested and report its nonreplication.""}"
10.1186/1748-7161-8-s2-o2,69b1f592e08ce7cf,1,10.1097/01.brs.0000216603.91330.6f,Association of estrogen receptor gene polymorphisms with susceptibility to adolescent idiopathic scoliosis,Wu J Qiu Y Zhang L Sun Q Qiu X He Y,2006,abstract,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Wu J Qiu Y Zhang L Sun Q Qiu X He Y"", ""doi"": ""10.1097/01.brs.0000216603.91330.6f"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Association of estrogen receptor gene polymorphisms with susceptibility to adolescent idiopathic scoliosis"", ""year"": ""2006""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""69b1f592e08ce7cf"", ""location"": ""Background and Purpose, lines 4-6; see also Methods, Results, Conclusions and discussion, and References, lines 8-12"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""It has been suggested that XbaI single nucleotide polymorphism (SNP) (A/G rs934099) in estrogen receptor 1 (ESR1) may be associated with curve severity in Japanese AIS patients[ 1 ] and with both curve severity and predisposition for AIS in Chinese patients[ 2 ]. However, replication studies have not confirmed these findings[ 3 ]. The role of the XbaI ESR1 polymorphism rs9340799 in AIS has never been described in Caucasian AIS patients. \n Purpose \n A genetic association study was performed to investigate the association between XbaI SNP in ESR1 and the predisposition for, or progression of, AIS in Caucasian patients."", ""reason"": ""Janusz et al. (2013, 10.1186/1748-7161-8-s2-o2) explicitly re-test both previously reported ESR1 XbaI (rs9340799) associations in a Caucasian sample of 286 female AIS patients and 116 healthy female controls: (1) association with curve severity/progression/operation rate first reported in Japanese AIS patients by Inoue et al. (2002, 10.1097/00007632-200211010-00009, shipped row 2495, target_index 0), and (2) association with both predisposition (case-control susceptibility) for AIS and curve severity reported in Chinese AIS patients by Wu et al. (2006, Spine 31(10):1131-1136, 10.1097/01.brs.0000216603.91330.6f, candidate target_index 1). Janusz et al. empirically test case-control allele/genotype frequencies (predisposition/susceptibility: p=0.63 and p=0.35) as well as progression velocity, mean Cobb angle, and surgery rate, concluding that 'No association between ESR1 XbaI polymorphism and AIS was found in the Caucasian population. None of the previously reported associations with curve severity, progression or operation rate could be confirmed.' Wu et al. (2006) is an independent primary study by a distinct research group in a distinct cohort testing the susceptibility claim directly evaluated here, and it is absent from the shipped rows (which contain only Inoue et al. 2002, row 2495). Both listed prior adjudication/state files (b7f058b4...json and 5626af31...json) are absent."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0135_69b1f592e08ce7cf"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Wu J Qiu Y Zhang L Sun Q Qiu X He Y"", ""year"": ""2006"", ""title"": ""Association of estrogen receptor gene polymorphisms with susceptibility to adolescent idiopathic scoliosis"", ""doi"": ""10.1097/01.brs.0000216603.91330.6f"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""It has been suggested that XbaI single nucleotide polymorphism (SNP) (A/G rs934099) in estrogen receptor 1 (ESR1) may be associated with curve severity in Japanese AIS patients[ 1 ] and with both curve severity and predisposition for AIS in Chinese patients[ 2 ]. However, replication studies have not confirmed these findings[ 3 ]. The role of the XbaI ESR1 polymorphism rs9340799 in AIS has never been described in Caucasian AIS patients."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""No association between ESR1 XbaI polymorphism and AIS was found in the Caucasian population. None of the previously reported associations with curve severity, progression or operation rate could be confirmed."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract tests the previously reported ESR1 XbaI associations in a new Caucasian sample and explicitly states that no association was found and none of the prior associations could be confirmed.""}"
10.3389/fnins.2019.00078,e22b91e77b92663e,0,10.1016/j.neuropsychologia.2015.12.030,Anodal-tDCS over the human right occipital cortex enhances the perception and memory of both faces and objects,"Barbieri, M., Negrini, M., Nitsche, M. A., and Rivolta, D.",2016,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Barbieri, M., Negrini, M., Nitsche, M. A., and Rivolta, D."", ""year"": ""2016"", ""title"": ""Anodal-tDCS over the human right occipital cortex enhances the perception and memory of both faces and objects"", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.neuropsychologia.2015.12.030"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Thus, in the current study we conducted two experiments where we sought to replicate recent findings (Romanska et al., 2015;Barbieri et al., 2016) reporting enhanced face perception following transcranial electrical stimulation techniques. The primary aim of Experiment 1 was to replicate enhancements in face perception following anodal tDCS compared to sham stimulation, which were observed with a medium effect size and group sample sizes of 16 (Barbieri et al., 2016). "", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In sum, the current results failed to replicate previous findings of enhanced face perception using anodal tDCS (Experiment 1) and high-frequency tRNS (Experiment 2) targeting the occipitotemporal cortex."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true}"
10.1111/head.12807,d81a6602f268754c,1,10.1097/AJP.0000000000000075,Molecular analysis of cluster headache,"Zarrilli F, Tomaiuolo R, Ceglia C, et al.",2015,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Zarrilli F, Tomaiuolo R, Ceglia C, et al."", ""year"": ""2015"", ""title"": ""Molecular analysis of cluster headache"", ""doi"": ""10.1097/AJP.0000000000000075"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The single nucleotide polymorphism (SNP) rs1126671 located in exon 7 of the ADH4 gene was associated with an increased risk for CH in two Italian studies, whereas rs1800759 located in the promoter region of ADH4 could only be associated with CH in the smaller case–control study. Thus, we performed a replication study on the ADH4 SNPs rs1126671 and rs1800759 in a large, Swedish case–control cohort in order to further investigate the possible contribution of ADH4 to CH."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The data did not support an association of the ADH4 SNPs rs1126671 and rs1800759 with CH."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors report no association for either variant, contrary to the associations reported by both cited Italian studies.""}"
10.1145/3416465.3416468,73f5176676324828,0,,Measuring cognitive load in introductory CS: Adaptation of an instrument.,"Morrison, Briana B., Dorn, Brian, and Guzdial, Mark",2014,full_text,,error,obvious,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Morrison, Briana B., Dorn, Brian, and Guzdial, Mark"", ""doi"": """", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Measuring cognitive load in introductory CS: Adaptation of an instrument."", ""year"": ""2014""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""73f5176676324828"", ""location"": ""Section 1 (Introduction, line 31); Section 4.1 (RQ1: Replication of Morrison et al.'s Findings, lines 63–72)"", ""method"": ""obvious"", ""quote"": ""Our study is opportunistic. We have a recent data set from an as-yet unpublished study, which enables us to replicate analyses from two earlier publications. We ask: RQ1 Morrison et al. [49] report good discriminant validity and internal reliability for a programming-domain adaptation of Leppink's [33] CL questionnaire, which is based on the threecomponent Old CLT. Do these findings replicate with a different cohort and a different programming tutorial?"", ""reason"": ""Parent independently verified the primary source and exact empirical relationship. Actual current is MeasuringTheCognitiveLoadOfLearningToProgram:AReplicationStudy, UKICER2020 DOI10.1145/3416465.3416468, not the2021ACMpaper/DOI stated in initialreason. Parent inspected cached primary running title/event header, independent noviceMTurksample307enrolled/175included, programming tutorial, RQ1and analyses repeatingMorrison2014three-factor CFA/EFA/reliability findings. These are empirical measurement findings explicitly tested with newdata, not merely using an instrument; Leppink supplies the instrument and is not another target. Shipped2500Jiang/Kalyuga2020two-factor model is separateRQ2. Primary source lacks frontmatter, but preserved coder Crossref title/assigned-author check and exact runningheader/Methods/References support identity; no invented author metadata or falsecurrentDOI adopted. Morrison/Dorn/Guzdial2014exactreference is genuine absent empirical original. Initial expert original-identity conclusion corroborated from primary evidence, while fabricated current author/DOI/sample/section claims are expressly rejected; immutable provider replies retained. This factual correction does not change blind coding or create a new model attempt."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0057_73f5176676324828"", ""target_index"": 0, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Morrison, Briana B., Dorn, Brian, and Guzdial, Mark"", ""year"": ""2014"", ""title"": ""Measuring cognitive load in introductory CS: Adaptation of an instrument."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""RQ1 Morrison et al. [49] report good discriminant validity and internal reliability for a programming-domain adaptation of Leppink's [33] CL questionnaire, which is based on the threecomponent Old CLT. Do these findings replicate with a different cohort and a different programming tutorial?"", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We found that the questions targeting each CL type aligned with the other questions targeting that type, which supports the claim that the questionnaire has internal reliability. Moreover, we confirm strong support for the questionnaire's discriminant validity-that is, the IL-targeting, EL-targeting, and GL-targeting items appear to be measuring three distinct constructs."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true}"
