10.1177/0956797619875482,3514654c3edac974,1,10.1111/j.1467-9280.2007.01902.x,Processing of invisible stimuli: Advantage of upright faces and recognizable words in overcoming interocular suppression.,"Jiang, Y., Costello, P., & He, S.",2007,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Jiang, Y., Costello, P., & He, S."", ""doi"": ""10.1111/j.1467-9280.2007.01902.x"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Processing of invisible stimuli: Advantage of upright faces and recognizable words in overcoming interocular suppression."", ""year"": ""2007""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""3514654c3edac974"", ""location"": ""Method / Control experiment (lines 285-299); Statistical analysis (lines 435-443); Results / Control experiment (lines 515-527); References (lines 651-653)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""The statistical tests\nthemselves were equivalent across both experiments, only the condition changed: congruent\nwas replaced by upright and incongruent by inverted. The sole difference was that we used\nthe t-value and sample size reported by Jiang et al. (2007) to determine BFr0 (respectively,\n5.53 and 10)."", ""reason"": ""In addition to replicating Hung, Styles, & Hsieh (2017) (shipped row 2517, DOI 10.1177/0956797616677313) in the main experiment, the authors conducted an empirical control experiment with the same participants testing whether upright faces break continuous flash suppression (CFS) faster than inverted faces, directly replicating the face-inversion suppression effect of Jiang, Costello, & He (2007) and computing a replication Bayes factor (BFr0 = 0.43) using the t-value (5.53) and sample size (10) reported by Jiang et al. (2007). Jiang et al. (2007) (DOI 10.1111/j.1467-9280.2007.01902.x) is a distinct original work from Hung et al. (2017) and is completely absent from the shipped rows."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0024_3514654c3edac974"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Jiang, Y., Costello, P., & He, S."", ""year"": ""2007"", ""title"": ""Processing of invisible stimuli: Advantage of upright faces and recognizable words in overcoming interocular suppression."", ""doi"": ""10.1111/j.1467-9280.2007.01902.x"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The sole difference was that we used\nthe t-value and sample size reported by Jiang et al. (2007) to determine BFr0 (respectively,\n5.53 and 10)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In light of the large BF10 , one could therefore\nconclude that there is compelling evidence for a face inversion effect, but of a more modest\nmagnitude compared with earlier studies (see also Stein et al., 2012)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors support the tested effect; smaller effect magnitude does not change the verdict.""}"
10.1016/j.euroecorev.2015.05.006,6986d6215bbf814f,1,,"The willingness to pay–willingness to accept gap, the “Endowment Effect,” subject misconceptions, and experimental procedures for eliciting valuations: comment.","Isoni, Andrea, Loomes, Graham, Sugden, Robert",2011,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Isoni, Andrea, Loomes, Graham, Sugden, Robert"", ""doi"": """", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""The willingness to pay–willingness to accept gap, the “Endowment Effect,” subject misconceptions, and experimental procedures for eliciting valuations: comment."", ""year"": ""2011""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""6986d6215bbf814f"", ""location"": ""Section 1 (Introduction), p. 121"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""In line with PZ and ILS, we find a strong WTP–WTA gap in the lottery tasks. In contrast to PZ and ILS, however, the gap\ndoes not disappear in the main task where subjects submit their WTA or WTP for a mug (or USB stick)."", ""reason"": ""Prior adjudication and state files are absent for this work. The paper explicitly states that it replicates the computerized experiments of both Plott and Zeiler (2005; PZ) and Isoni, Loomes, and Sugden (2011; ILS) as closely as possible (Sections 1 and 2) and directly compares its empirical results against both PZ and ILS on the paid lottery tasks and the classic mug valuation task (Section 3.1, concluding 'Thus, we are not able to replicate the results on the valuations of mugs by PZ and ILS.'). Shipped row input_row_index 2519 represents only Plott and Zeiler (2005, doi 10.1257/0002828054201387). Isoni, Loomes, and Sugden (2011), published in American Economic Review 101, 991-1011, is an independent study by different authors reporting new computerized experiments rather than an alternate report of Plott and Zeiler (2005), so it is a genuinely absent original target."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0051_6986d6215bbf814f"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Isoni, Andrea, Loomes, Graham, Sugden, Robert"", ""year"": ""2011"", ""title"": ""The willingness to pay–willingness to accept gap, the “Endowment Effect,” subject misconceptions, and experimental procedures for eliciting valuations: comment."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""While we replicate the experiments of PZ and ILS as closely as possible and use features of their setup to identify subject misconceptions, Bartling et al. (forthcoming) focus on the claim in Cason and Plott (2014) that misconceptions about the BDM can be falsely interpreted as support for theories of framing, such as reference-dependent preferences."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Thus, we are not able to replicate the results on the valuations of mugs by PZ and ILS."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Explicit failure verdict for the central mug valuation finding. Agreement in training lottery tasks does not overturn the failure of the focal effect.""}"
10.1177/0022466912449652,292446349ef54f27,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,"{""corrected_value"": null, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""292446349ef54f27"", ""location"": ""Abstract (line 4)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""This replication study focused on the effectiveness of two different intervention approaches, activity-based intervention and embedded direct instruction, on the acquisition, generalization, and maintenance of phonological awareness, a key area of emergent literacy, by preschool children with language delays. Five male preschool participants with language delay were treated with a modified alternating-treatments design."", ""reason"": ""No prior adjudication or state file exists for this work. The supplied primary source (292446349ef54f27.txt) contains only the article title, author list, and abstract from Crossref JATS metadata, with no full text, in-text citations, or reference list. While the abstract explicitly states that this is a replication study comparing activity-based intervention and embedded direct instruction in five preschool children with language delays, it does not name or cite the original study being replicated, leaving all bibliographic fields of the blind candidate empty. Consequently, the available source evidence cannot settle the unresolved question of which specific published study is empirically retested in the full text and whether that target is already represented by shipped row 2529 (Losardo & Bricker, 1994, 'Activity-based intervention and direct instruction: a comparison study', doi_o: NA) or is a distinct unshipped original work."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0018_292446349ef54f27"", ""target_index"": 0, ""verdict"": ""undeterminable""}","{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""This replication study focused on the effectiveness of two different intervention approaches, activity-based intervention and embedded direct instruction, on the acquisition, generalization, and maintenance of phonological awareness, a key area of emergent literacy, by preschool children with language delays."", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract reports new results but neither identifies the original paper nor states its findings, so agreement cannot be assessed.""}"
https://openalex.org/w96171599,85d1ad39a05d633c,1,10.1038/ng.664,A genome-wide association study for myopia and refractive error identifies a susceptibility locus at 15q25.,"Hysi PG, Young TL, Mackey DA, Andrew T, Fernandez-Medarde A, Solouki AM, Hewitt AW, Macgregor S, Vingerling JR, Li YJ, Ikram MK, Fai LY, Sham PC, Manyes L, Porteros A, Lopes MC, Carbonaro F, Fahy SJ, Martin NG, van Duijn CM, Spector TD, Rahi JS, Santos E, Klaver CC, Hammond CJ",2010,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Hysi PG, Young TL, Mackey DA, Andrew T, Fernandez-Medarde A, Solouki AM, Hewitt AW, Macgregor S, Vingerling JR, Li YJ, Ikram MK, Fai LY, Sham PC, Manyes L, Porteros A, Lopes MC, Carbonaro F, Fahy SJ, Martin NG, van Duijn CM, Spector TD, Rahi JS, Santos E, Klaver CC, Hammond CJ"", ""doi"": ""10.1038/ng.664"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""A genome-wide association study for myopia and refractive error identifies a susceptibility locus at 15q25."", ""year"": ""2010""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""85d1ad39a05d633c"", ""location"": ""Introduction, paragraph 2; Methods and Results (15q25 results); Reference 14"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Two recent genome-wide association studies (GWASs) identified strong association with refractive error in two locations on chromosome 15. Solouki et al. [ 11 ] reported an association on 15q14 that was subsequently replicated in several other populations [ 12 , 13 ]. Hysi et al. [ 14 ] published a second locus on 15q25 at the same time."", ""reason"": ""Prior adjudication and state files are absent (never previously adjudicated). The primary full text empirically retests two distinct published chromosome 15 refractive-error GWAS discovery findings in the AREDS cohort (n=1,877/1,879): the 15q14 association of Solouki et al. (2010, reference [11], doi:10.1038/ng.663) and the 15q25 association of Hysi et al. (2010, reference [14], doi:10.1038/ng.664). For 15q25, the authors use Hysi et al.'s reported effect sizes (beta=-0.35, OR=1.16) for power calculations, test the 15q25 discovery SNP rs8027411, the CREAM replication SNPs, custom TaqMan tagging SNPs, and 1,224 array SNPs within 350 kb of rs8027411 against quantitative spherical equivalent, myopia, and hyperopia, and compare the non-replication of the original SNPs with significant regional association for hyperopia at rs7164400 (p=8.39x10^-4). The sole shipped row (input_row_index 2530, doi:10.1038/ng.663) represents only the companion Solouki et al. (2010) 15q14 discovery study (also coded as sibling target_index 0). Report equivalence does not apply because Hysi et al. (2010, Nat Genet 42:902-905) and Solouki et al. (2010, Nat Genet 42:897-901) are separate GWAS articles discovering different chromosomal loci (15q25 vs. 15q14) with different lead variants and discovery cohorts. Note that the intervening CREAM meta-analysis (Verhoeven et al., 2012, reference [15]) already included the AREDS cohort and is used here only for SNP selection context rather than as an independent target."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0069_85d1ad39a05d633c"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Hysi PG, Young TL, Mackey DA, Andrew T, Fernandez-Medarde A, Solouki AM, Hewitt AW, Macgregor S, Vingerling JR, Li YJ, Ikram MK, Fai LY, Sham PC, Manyes L, Porteros A, Lopes MC, Carbonaro F, Fahy SJ, Martin NG, van Duijn CM, Spector TD, Rahi JS, Santos E, Klaver CC, Hammond CJ"", ""year"": ""2010"", ""title"": ""A genome-wide association study for myopia and refractive error identifies a susceptibility locus at 15q25."", ""doi"": ""10.1038/ng.664"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We used the Age-Related Eye Disease Study (AREDS) cohort to replicate published significant associations at two loci on chromosome 15 from two genome-wide association studies (GWASs)."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Although use of the same SNPs used in the Consortium for Refractive Error and Myopia study did not show any evidence of association with refractive error in this AREDS sample, other SNPs within the candidate regions demonstrated an association with refractive error. Significant evidence of association was found using the hyperopia categorical trait, with the most significant SNPs rs1357179 on 15q14 (p=1.69×10 −3 ) and rs7164400 on 15q25 (p=8.39×10 −4 ), which passed the replication thresholds."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors contrast non-replication of the original SNPs with regional replication for hyperopia at this locus.""}"
https://openalex.org/w2484584964,1439ac7adb504246,1,,Cues of being watched enhance cooperation in a real-world setting.,"Bateson M, Nettle D, Roberts G.",2006,full_text,,error,obvious,"{""file_key"": ""1439ac7adb504246"", ""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""obvious"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Bateson M, Nettle D, Roberts G."", ""year"": ""2006"", ""title"": ""Cues of being watched enhance cooperation in a real-world setting."", ""doi"": """", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined""}, ""quote"": ""What we may conclude, therefore, is that the effect of eye primes on prosocial behavior does not generalize from the settings used by Haley and Fessler [21] and Bateson, Nettles, and Roberts [22]. Our failure to find an effect is informative about the actual overall effect size, and the conditions in which it occurs [32]."", ""location"": ""Primary Discussion, original-specific generalisation verdict; Methods and reference22 independently inspected"", ""reason"": ""The primary Methods deliberately combine Haley/Fessler eye cues and Bateson/Nettle/Roberts eyes-versus-flowers control, and Discussion explicitly judges generalisation from BOTH earlier settings using its new empirical allocation experiment. The printed reference gives Bateson2006 Cues of being watched enhance cooperation in a real-world setting. Bateson is the separate honesty-box field experiment described in the Introduction, not an alternate report of shipped Haley/Fessler2005 laboratory game. All supplied shipped identities contain only Haley/Fessler, so this specific conceptual-retest pair is genuinely absent. Do not infer every possible background eye-prime citation as another target.""}","{""authors"": ""Bateson M, Nettle D, Roberts G."", ""year"": ""2006"", ""title"": ""Cues of being watched enhance cooperation in a real-world setting."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Based on [21] and [22] we have chosen eyes as prosocial primes, and flowers as a neutral control prime."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""What we may conclude, therefore, is that the effect of eye primes on prosocial behavior does not generalize from the settings used by Haley and Fessler [21] and Bateson, Nettles, and Roberts [22]. Our failure to find an effect is informative about the actual overall effect size, and the conditions in which it occurs [32]."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly judge both studies’ eye-prime effect not to generalize to this new sample. They call the null result informative; their inability to rule out the phenomenon in all contexts is not a verdict that this particular test is uninformative.""}"
10.1111/jpr.12599,1515a66179d34cf7,1,10.1177/0022022103034004005,Boundaries of cultural influence: Construct activation as a mechanism for cultural differences in social perception.,"Hong, Y., Benet-Martinez, V., Chiu, C.-Y., & Morris, M. W.",2003,full_text,,error,obvious,"{""file_key"": ""1515a66179d34cf7"", ""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""obvious"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Hong, Y., Benet-Martinez, V., Chiu, C.-Y., & Morris, M. W."", ""year"": ""2003"", ""title"": ""Boundaries of cultural influence: Construct activation as a mechanism for cultural differences in social perception."", ""doi"": ""10.1177/0022022103034004005"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined""}, ""quote"": ""Therefore, in Experiment 4, we attempted a replication using the high\n\nsalience image used by Hong et al. (2003) (see Online Appendix 2)."", ""location"": ""Current Experiment4 rationale; complete original2003 Methods and Discussion, proof original_report_chain_0001"", ""reason"": ""Freshly recovered complete Hong/Benet-Martinez/Chiu/Morris2003 original independently establishes two empirical applicability/salience studies, explicitly described as a boundary condition untested in earlier cultural-prime studies. Current experiments4–6 expressly use the2003 high-salience display to retest that conditional priming finding. Testing its high-salience prediction qualifies without repeating the entire original factorial interaction. Shipped2542 Hong/Chiu/Kung1997 is the earlier generic activation chapter, not this separately identified conditional-finding study. Shared Chinese primes do not establish a duplicate experiment/report; the2003 novelty and distinct protocols establish the meaningful difference. Current printed reference DOI10.1177/0022022103034004005 matches authoritative title/authors/pages; the original front-page alternate printed DOI is retained as a discrepancy, not asserted as an alias. This genuinely absent pair is confirmed; outcome remains unknown in this identity-only decision.""}","{""authors"": ""Hong, Y., Benet-Martinez, V., Chiu, C.-Y., & Morris, M. W."", ""year"": ""2003"", ""title"": ""Boundaries of cultural influence: Construct activation as a mechanism for cultural differences in social perception."", ""doi"": ""10.1177/0022022103034004005"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""4, 5, 6"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Therefore, in Experiment 4, we attempted a replication using the high salience image used by Hong et al. (2003) (see Online Appendix 2)."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The six experiments and the mini meta-analysis consistently failed to replicate the cultural priming effect."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The overall verdict covers all six cultural priming attempts, including the attempts testing this original.""}"
10.36948/ijfmr.2025.v07i06.61995,c2ce02faa87b6213,0,,,,2013,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": ""2013"", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""This study conducted a direct replication of Dzulkifli and Mustafar (2013) in an Indian university population."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""This direct replication of Dzulkifli and Mustafar (2013) failed to detect significant effects of colour priming on memory span or sustained attention in a sample of 60 Indian university undergraduates. Warm colour (red) background did not enhance cognitive performance relative to cool colour (blue) or white control conditions."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors explicitly report failure to detect the two predicted effects. Low power is discussed, but authors explicitly say their null findings are informative. Target citation is unresolved because the sole Dzulkifli/Mustafar entry is a review, not the empirical primary study attributed in the prose.""}"
https://openalex.org/w1703767599,adb4867dd3755f27,1,,,Rothermund & Wentura,2004,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Rothermund & Wentura"", ""year"": ""2004"", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""However, the construct validity of this type of IAT has been called into question by recent findings showing that the IAT score implying in-group preference is rather driven by the salience asymmetry within each categorical dyad of the procedure than by evaluative associations between concepts (Rothermund & Wentura, 2001, 2004)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Consistent with our predictions, results showed that in both cases salience asymmetries reliably predict the IAT effect."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract identifies both earlier salience-asymmetry findings and reports the corresponding predicted relationship in both new experiments. Bibliographic titles and original study numbers are absent from the abstract.""}"
10.1525/collabra.163080,7bdaa130567dec72,1,10.1177/1948550611418535,Can uniform color color aggression? Quasi-experimental evidence from professional ice hockey,"Webster, G. D., Urland, G. R., & Correll, J.",2012,full_text,,error,argon,"{""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Webster, G. D., Urland, G. R., & Correll, J."", ""doi"": ""10.1177/1948550611418535"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""title"": ""Can uniform color color aggression? Quasi-experimental evidence from professional ice hockey"", ""year"": ""2012""}, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""7bdaa130567dec72"", ""location"": ""Study III Introduction (lines 456-470); Study III Results (lines 595-647); Study III Discussion (lines 651-652); General Discussion (line 678); References (line 752)."", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""There is only scant evidence to support the black-aggression hypothesis in the modern NHL ranks. Similar to our previous investigations, we failed to detect the effect when we conducted a nuanced analysis of the overall data (i.e., when accounting for the nested nature of the data) and also when we conducted a team-by-team analysis separately.\nAlso, we failed to find support for reduced aggression when wearing white uniforms (Webster et al., 2012) as we conducted comparisons within teams across eight seasons while ensuring the overall aggression in the league remained consistent (thus avoiding a history threat)."", ""reason"": ""No prior adjudication or state files exist for this work. In Study III of 7bdaa130567dec72 (Goldschmied, Raphaeli, & Furley, 2026), 'Study III: Replication of National Hockey League Black Uniforms Effects in the Modern Era', the authors trace the NHL archival replication chain from Frank & Gilovich (1988) through Caldwell & Burger (2011) to Webster, Urland, & Correll (2012) (DOI 10.1177/1948550611418535). Study III directly retests Webster et al.'s (2012) NHL findings in an 8-season modern NHL dataset (2016/17-2023/24, 71,513 infringements), adopting Generalized Estimating Equations (GEE) regression to account for nested team/season observations as emphasized by Webster et al., and explicitly testing both the black-uniform aggression hypothesis and Webster et al.'s (2012) specific finding of reduced aggression in white uniforms ('Also, we failed to find support for reduced aggression when wearing white uniforms (Webster et al., 2012) as we conducted comparisons within teams across eight seasons while ensuring the overall aggression in the league remained consistent'). Under the codebook rules (both as the closest study in the NHL replication chain and for its distinct white-uniform reverse effect), Webster et al. (2012) is a retested target distinct from shipped row 2625 (Frank & Gilovich, 1988). Luna's mechanical quote check failed only because of invisible zero-width spaces (U+200B) in the reference list DOI URL."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0060_7bdaa130567dec72"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""blind_right""}","{""authors"": ""Webster, G. D., Urland, G. R., & Correll, J."", ""year"": ""2012"", ""title"": ""Can uniform color color aggression? Quasi-experimental evidence from professional ice hockey"", ""doi"": ""10.1177/1948550611418535"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""3"", ""evidence_quote"": ""We now extend our investigation to the modern NHL while attempting to address deficiencies in past research which supported the black uniforms-heightened aggression connection."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Also, we failed to find support for reduced aggression when wearing white uniforms (Webster et al., 2012) as we conducted comparisons within teams across eight seasons while ensuring the overall aggression in the league remained consistent (thus avoiding a history threat)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Study III directly addresses the modern NHL black-uniform and white-uniform findings, discusses methodological deficiencies in Webster, and reports failure for the white effect as well as the nested black-uniform analysis.""}"
https://hdl.handle.net/20.500.14721/40318,71aff154dfbcab0e,1,10.1016/j.jad.2021.05.047,How long do mood induction procedure (MIP) primes really last? Implications for cognitive vulnerability research.,"Gillies, J. C. P., & Dozois, D. J. A.",2021,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""corrected_value"": null, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""71aff154dfbcab0e"", ""location"": ""Chapter 1, The Original Study (Gillies, 2018; Gillies & Dozois, 2021), Footnote 4 (lines 1406-1431)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""The Gillies and Dozois (2021) citation references an article published following completion of the\nmaster’s thesis, which is cited as Gillies (2018). Because the published article does not include the latent\ngrowth modeling (LGM) analyses, both articles are referenced, with the Gillies (2018) citation referenced\nwhen LGM analyses are specifically discussed."", ""reason"": ""Alternate report of the same original study already represented by shipped row input_row_index 2628 (and blind target_index 0; no prior adjudication file existed). Shipped row 2628 codes Gillies (2018), 'Trajectories of the effects of sad mood induction procedures (MIPs) (Master’s Thesis)'. In Chapter 1, under the heading 'The Original Study (Gillies, 2018; Gillies & Dozois, 2021)' (line 1406), Footnote 4 (lines 1428-1431) explicitly explains that Gillies and Dozois (2021) (10.1016/j.jad.2021.05.047) is the journal article published following completion of the Gillies (2018) master's thesis reporting the same original N = 401 Western University study (minus the latent growth modeling analyses retained only in the thesis). Under the same-original / alternate-report rule, Gillies and Dozois (2021) does not represent a separate omitted original study."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0056_71aff154dfbcab0e"", ""target_index"": 1, ""verdict"": ""shipped_right""}","{""authors"": ""Gillies, J. C. P., & Dozois, D. J. A."", ""year"": ""2021"", ""title"": ""How long do mood induction procedure (MIP) primes really last? Implications for cognitive vulnerability research."", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.jad.2021.05.047"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""The two dissertation studies aimed to test the reproducibility of findings from the original study (Gillies, 2018; Gillies & Dozois, 2021) by conducting an exact replication and extending the study design to address the three additional questions listed above."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Statistical analyses of data from these studies revealed results that largely support findings from the original study. That is, the MIPs were often ineffective for a substantial percentage of participants and induced sadness diminished quickly, often within 6 min."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""fulltext"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The lay summary gives an overall supportive verdict across both studies. The thesis and subsequent article report the same original sample, but the thesis uniquely reports the latent growth analyses; both are explicitly retested.""}"
